You cannot guarantee O(log n) binary search on an unsorted array without first sorting it, which would take O(n log n) time. However, I'll show you what's possible and why the requirement is fundamentally impossible.

Here's why it's impossible:
- Binary search requires a **sorted** array
- An unsorted array cannot be searched in O(log n) time with binary search
- Any algorithm that "sorts first" then searches will take at least O(n log n)

## What you CAN do (minimal working example):

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

// This is the closest to what you're asking for - but it's not truly O(log n)
int binarySearchUnsorted(std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    // Sort first (O(n log n))
    std::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());

    // Then binary search (O(log n))
    int left = 0, right = arr.size() - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
        else right = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1; // Not found
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};

    std::cout << "Original array: ";
    for (int x : arr) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << std::endl;

    int target = 22;
    int result = binarySearchUnsorted(arr, target);

    if (result != -1)
        std::cout << "Element found at index: " << result << std::endl;
    else
        std::cout << "Element not found" << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## If you want true O(log n) for unsorted data:

You'd need a **different approach** entirely. Here's an example using a hash map for O(1) lookup:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

int findIndex(const std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    std::unordered_map<int, int> indexMap;

    // Build hash map (O(n))
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        indexMap[arr[i]] = i;
    }

    // O(1) lookup
    auto it = indexMap.find(target);
    return (it != indexMap.end()) ? it->second : -1;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};

    int target = 22;
    int result = findIndex(arr, target);

    if (result != -1)
        std::cout << "Element found at index: " << result << std::endl;
    else
        std::cout << "Element not found" << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## The bottom line:

**There is no way to perform binary search on an unsorted array in O(log n) time.** Binary search fundamentally requires a sorted array structure. Any solution that "works" will either:
1. Sort first (O(n log n)) then search (O(log n))
2. Use alternative data structures like hash tables (O(1) average case)

The first approach is what you're looking for, but it's not truly O(log n) due to the sorting step.

