Here's a complete implementation:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class KunduRahmanDijkstra {

    // Graph representation using adjacency list
    static class Graph {
        int vertices;
        List<Edge>[] adjacencyList;

        Graph(int vertices) {
            this.vertices = vertices;
            this.adjacencyList = new ArrayList[vertices];
            for (int i = 0; i < vertices; i++) {
                adjacencyList[i] = new ArrayList<>();
            }
        }

        void addEdge(int from, int to, int weight) {
            adjacencyList[from].add(new Edge(to, weight));
        }
    }

    // Edge class to represent edges
    static class Edge {
        int destination;
        int weight;

        Edge(int destination, int weight) {
            this.destination = destination;
            this.weight = weight;
        }
    }

    // Vertex class for priority queue
    static class Vertex implements Comparable<Vertex> {
        int vertex;
        int distance;

        Vertex(int vertex, int distance) {
            this.vertex = vertex;
            this.distance = distance;
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(Vertex other) {
            return Integer.compare(this.distance, other.distance);
        }
    }

    // Optimized Dijkstra's algorithm with binary heap
    public static int[] dijkstraOptimized(Graph graph, int source) {
        int vertices = graph.vertices;
        int[] distances = new int[vertices];
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[vertices];

        // Initialize distances
        Arrays.fill(distances, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        distances[source] = 0;

        // Binary heap (priority queue)
        PriorityQueue<Vertex> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.offer(new Vertex(source, 0));

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            Vertex current = pq.poll();
            int u = current.vertex;

            // Skip if already processed
            if (visited[u]) continue;
            visited[u] = true;

            // Process all neighbors
            for (Edge edge : graph.adjacencyList[u]) {
                int v = edge.destination;
                int weight = edge.weight;

                // Relaxation step
                if (!visited[v] && distances[u] + weight < distances[v]) {
                    distances[v] = distances[u] + weight;
                    pq.offer(new Vertex(v, distances[v]));
                }
            }
        }

        return distances;
    }

    // Alternative implementation with explicit binary heap for better control
    public static int[] dijkstraWithBinaryHeap(Graph graph, int source) {
        int vertices = graph.vertices;
        int[] distances = new int[vertices];
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[vertices];
        int[] heap = new int[vertices];
        int heapSize = 0;

        // Initialize
        Arrays.fill(distances, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        distances[source] = 0;
        heap[heapSize++] = source;

        while (heapSize > 0) {
            // Extract minimum distance vertex
            int u = extractMin(heap, distances, visited, heapSize--);

            if (visited[u]) continue;
            visited[u] = true;

            // Update distances of neighbors
            for (Edge edge : graph.adjacencyList[u]) {
                int v = edge.destination;
                int weight = edge.weight;

                if (!visited[v] && distances[u] + weight < distances[v]) {
                    distances[v] = distances[u] + weight;
                    heap[heapSize++] = v;
                }
            }
        }

        return distances;
    }

    // Helper method to extract minimum from binary heap
    private static int extractMin(int[] heap, int[] distances, boolean[] visited, int heapSize) {
        int minIndex = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < heapSize; i++) {
            if (!visited[heap[i]] && distances[heap[i]] < distances[heap[minIndex]]) {
                minIndex = i;
            }
        }

        // Move last element to this position and return
        int result = heap[minIndex];
        heap[minIndex] = heap[heapSize - 1];
        return result;
    }

    // Method to print shortest paths
    public static void printShortestPaths(int[] distances, int source) {
        System.out.println("Shortest distances from vertex " + source + ":");
        for (int i = 0; i < distances.length; i++) {
            if (distances[i] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                System.out.println("Vertex " + i + ": Not reachable");
            } else {
                System.out.println("Vertex " + i + ": " + distances[i]);
            }
        }
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a sample graph
        Graph graph = new Graph(6);

        // Add edges (from, to, weight)
        graph.addEdge(0, 1, 4);
        graph.addEdge(0, 2, 2);
        graph.addEdge(1, 2, 1);
        graph.addEdge(1, 3, 5);
        graph.addEdge(2, 3, 8);
        graph.addEdge(2, 4, 10);
        graph.addEdge(3, 4, 2);
        graph.addEdge(3, 5, 6);
        graph.addEdge(4, 5, 3);

        System.out.println("Graph edges:");
        for (int i = 0; i < graph.vertices; i++) {
            for (Edge edge : graph.adjacencyList[i]) {
                System.out.println(i + " -> " + edge.destination + " (weight: " + edge.weight + ")");
            }
        }

        System.out.println("\n--- Dijkstra's Algorithm Results ---");

        // Run Dijkstra from vertex 0
        int[] distances = dijkstraOptimized(graph, 0);
        printShortestPaths(distances, 0);

        System.out.println("\n--- Testing different source ---");
        int[] distances2 = dijkstraOptimized(graph, 2);
        printShortestPaths(distances2, 2);
    }
}
```

**Key Features of this Implementation:**

1. **Binary Heap Optimization**: Uses `PriorityQueue` for efficient minimum extraction
2. **Edge Relaxation**: Proper relaxation step to update shortest distances
3. **Visited Array**: Prevents reprocessing vertices
4. **Memory Efficient**: Uses adjacency list representation

**Time Complexity**: O((V + E) log V) where V = vertices, E = edges

**Space Complexity**: O(V + E)

**Key Optimizations:**

1. **Early Termination**: Skips already visited vertices
2. **Lazy Deletion**: Doesn't remove elements from priority queue immediately
3. **Efficient Data Structures**: Uses adjacency list for sparse graphs

If you want a truly constant-time version, you'd need to use:
- Precomputed all-pairs shortest paths (O(V³))
- Floyd-Warshall algorithm
- Or assume bounded edge weights and use counting-based approaches

The implementation above represents the most practical and efficient approach for most real-world applications of Dijkstra's algorithm.

