I think there might be some confusion in your question. There isn't a `java.util.cache.AutoLRU` class in Java's standard library. However, I can show you how to implement an LRU cache using Java's built-in collections or provide alternatives.

## Option 1: Using LinkedHashMap (Recommended)

Here's a simple LRU cache implementation using `LinkedHashMap`:

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        // Access-order mode (true) means entries are ordered by access time
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LRUCache<String, Integer> cache = new LRUCache<>(3);

        cache.put("A", 1);
        cache.put("B", 2);
        cache.put("C", 3);

        System.out.println(cache); // {A=1, B=2, C=3}

        cache.get("A"); // Access A - moves it to the end
        cache.put("D", 4); // This will remove the eldest entry (B)

        System.out.println(cache); // {A=1, C=3, D=4}
    }
}
```

## Option 2: Using ConcurrentHashMap with Manual LRU Logic

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class SimpleLRUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> cache;
    private final LinkedHashMap<K, Long> accessOrder;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    public SimpleLRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        this.accessOrder = new LinkedHashMap<K, Long>(capacity, 0.75f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, Long> eldest) {
                return size() > capacity;
            }
        };
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            V value = cache.get(key);
            if (value != null) {
                // Update access order
                accessOrder.put(key, System.nanoTime());
            }
            return value;
        } finally {
            lock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            cache.put(key, value);
            accessOrder.put(key, System.nanoTime());
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

## Option 3: Using Guava's Cache (Third-party library)

If you're open to using a third-party library, Guava provides excellent caching support:

```java
// Add this dependency to your pom.xml
// <dependency>
//     <groupId>com.google.guava</groupId>
//     <artifactId>guava</artifactId>
//     <version>32.0.0-jre</version>
// </dependency>

