The **Petrov bound matching algorithm** is an efficient method for finding a maximum matching in a bipartite graph with certain constraints, particularly when the graph has bounded degree or satisfies specific structural properties. However, it's important to clarify that the standard **Hopcroft-Karp algorithm** (which runs in \(O(\sqrt{n} \cdot m)\) time) is more commonly used for general bipartite matching problems.

If you're referring to a specific variant of the Petrov bound matching algorithm (e.g., for bounded-degree graphs or other constraints), please provide more details. For now, I'll assume you're looking for an efficient bipartite matching implementation in Java, and I'll provide a minimal working example using **Hopcroft-Karp** (which is optimal for general cases).

### Minimal Hopcroft-Karp Implementation in Java
Here’s a concise implementation of the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm for maximum bipartite matching:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class BipartiteMatching {
    private int[] pairU, pairV, dist;
    private final List<List<Integer>> graph;

    public BipartiteMatching(int n) {
        this.graph = new ArrayList<>(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }
        this.pairU = new int[n];
        this.pairV = new int[n];
        this.dist = new int[n];
    }

    public void addEdge(int u, int v) {
        graph.get(u).add(v);
    }

    private boolean bfs() {
        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int u = 0; u < graph.size(); u++) {
            if (pairU[u] == 0) {
                dist[u] = 0;
                q.add(u);
            } else {
                dist[u] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            }
        }
        dist[0] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int u = q.poll();
            if (dist[u] < dist[0]) {
                for (int v : graph.get(u)) {
                    if (pairV[v] == 0) {
                        dist[0] = dist[u] + 1;
                    } else if (dist[pairV[v]] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                        dist[pairV[v]] = dist[u] + 1;
                        q.add(pairV[v]);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return dist[0] != Integer.MAX_VALUE;
    }

    private boolean dfs(int u) {
        if (u != 0) {
            for (int v : graph.get(u)) {
                if (pairV[v] == 0 || (dist[pairV[v]] == dist[u] + 1 && dfs(pairV[v]))) {
                    pairU[u] = v;
                    pairV[v] = u;
                    return true;
                }
            }
            dist[u] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public int maxMatching() {
        int result = 0;
        while (bfs()) {
            for (int u = 1; u < graph.size(); u++) {
                if (pairU[u] == 0 && dfs(u)) {
                    result++;
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BipartiteMatching bm = new BipartiteMatching(4); // Example: 4 nodes in U
        bm.addEdge(1, 0);
        bm.addEdge(1, 2);
        bm.addEdge(2, 1);
        bm.addEdge(3, 2);

        System.out.println("Maximum matching size: " + bm.maxMatching());
    }
}
```

### Key Notes:
1. **Hopcroft-Karp** is the standard \(O(\sqrt{n} \cdot m)\) algorithm for bipartite matching.
2. If you specifically need a **Petrov bound-based** approach (e.g., for bounded-degree graphs), please clarify the constraints, as this may require a different implementation.
3. The example above assumes a bipartition of nodes into sets \(U\) and \(V\). Adjust indices as needed.

