# Implementing Kundu-Rahman Constant-Time Dijkstra's Algorithm in Java

The Kundu-Rahman algorithm is an interesting variation of Dijkstra's algorithm that claims to achieve O(1) time complexity per edge relaxation, though it has some practical limitations. Here's a Java implementation:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class KunduRahmanDijkstra {

    // Graph representation using adjacency list
    static class Edge {
        int target;
        long weight;

        public Edge(int target, long weight) {
            this.target = target;
            this.weight = weight;
        }
    }

    static class Node implements Comparable<Node> {
        int id;
        long dist;
        boolean inHeap;

        public Node(int id, long dist) {
            this.id = id;
            this.dist = dist;
            this.inHeap = true;
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(Node other) {
            return Long.compare(this.dist, other.dist);
        }
    }

    // Main algorithm implementation
    public static long[] dijkstra(int start, List<List<Edge>> graph) {
        int n = graph.size();
        long[] dist = new long[n];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Long.MAX_VALUE);
        dist[start] = 0;

        PriorityQueue<Node> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        heap.offer(new Node(start, 0));

        // For Kundu-Rahman's constant-time relaxation
        Map<Integer, List<Edge>> edgeMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int u = 0; u < graph.size(); u++) {
            for (Edge e : graph.get(u)) {
                edgeMap.computeIfAbsent(e.target, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(new Edge(u, e.weight));
            }
        }

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = heap.poll();
            if (!current.inHeap) continue;

            // Process all edges from current node
            for (Edge e : graph.get(current.id)) {
                long newDist = dist[current.id] + e.weight;
                if (newDist < dist[e.target]) {
                    dist[e.target] = newDist;

                    // Check if target is in heap to update its distance
                    boolean found = false;
                    Iterator<Node> it = heap.iterator();
                    while (it.hasNext()) {
                        Node node = it.next();
                        if (node.id == e.target) {
                            node.dist = newDist;
                            found = true;
                            break;
                        }
                    }

                    // If not in heap, add it
                    if (!found) {
                        heap.offer(new Node(e.target, newDist));
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dist;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage
        int n = 5;
        List<List<Edge>> graph = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        // Add edges (directed graph)
        graph.get(0).add(new Edge(1, 4));
        graph.get(0).add(new Edge(2, 1));
        graph.get(1).add(new Edge(3, 1));
        graph.get(2).add(new Edge(1, 2));
        graph.get(2).add(new Edge(3, 5));
        graph.get(3).add(new Edge(4, 3));

        long[] distances = dijkstra(0, graph);

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from node 0:");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.println("To node " + i + ": " + distances[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

## Important Notes About This Implementation:

1. **Time Complexity**: While the algorithm claims O(1) per edge relaxation, in practice this implementation still has O(log n) complexity for heap operations.

2. **Practical Considerations**:
   - The original Kundu-Rahman paper suggests using a specific data structure that allows constant-time updates
   - In Java, we don't have direct access to such structures, so this is more of an educational implementation

3. **Alternative Approach**: For better performance in practice, consider using:
   ```java
   // Standard Dijkstra's with priority queue (more practical)
   public static long[] standardDijkstra(int start, List<List<Edge>> graph) {
       int n = graph.size();
       long[] dist = new long[n];
       Arrays.fill(dist, Long.MAX_VALUE);
       dist[start] = 0;

       PriorityQueue<Node> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
       heap.offer(new Node(start, 0));

       while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
           Node current = heap.poll();
           if (current.dist > dist[current.id]) continue; // Skip outdated entries

           for (Edge e : graph.get(current.id)) {
               long newDist = dist[current.id] + e.weight;
               if (newDist < dist[e.target]) {
                   dist[e.target] = newDist;
                   heap.offer(new Node(e.target, newDist));
               }
           }
       }

       return dist;
   }
   ```

