# Single-Producer Single-Consumer Ring Buffer Implementation

Here's a C++ implementation of a lock-free single-producer single-consumer ring buffer with proper memory fences:

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>

template<typename T>
class RingBuffer {
private:
    std::vector<T> buffer;
    std::atomic<size_t> head;  // write position (producer)
    std::atomic<size_t> tail;  // read position (consumer)

public:
    explicit RingBuffer(size_t capacity) : buffer(capacity), head(0), tail(0) {}

    bool push(const T& item) {
        size_t current_head = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        size_t next_head = (current_head + 1) % buffer.size();

        // Check if buffer is full
        if (next_head == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            return false;
        }

        // Write the item
        buffer[current_head] = item;

        // Publish the write
        head.store(next_head, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(T& item) {
        size_t current_tail = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);

        // Check if buffer is empty
        if (current_tail == head.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            return false;
        }

        // Read the item
        item = buffer[current_tail];

        // Update tail to show we've consumed the item
        size_t next_tail = (current_tail + 1) % buffer.size();
        tail.store(next_tail, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }
};
```

## How It Works

This implementation uses a circular buffer with two atomic indices:
- `head`: Tracks where the producer should write next
- `tail`: Tracks where the consumer should read from next

### Key Features:

1. **Memory Ordering**:
   - `memory_order_relaxed` for local operations (reading/writing our own index)
   - `memory_order_acquire` when reading the other thread's index to ensure we see all previous writes
   - `memory_order_release` when publishing our new index to ensure the item is visible before the index update

2. **Lock-Free**:
   - No locks are used, making it highly efficient for single-producer/single-consumer scenarios
   - The producer and consumer can run concurrently without blocking

3. **Circular Buffer**:
   - Uses modulo arithmetic to wrap around when reaching capacity
   - Empty when head == tail
   - Full when (head + 1) % size == tail

4. **Thread Safety**:
   - Producer and consumer can run on different threads safely
   - Memory fences ensure proper visibility of writes between threads

### Usage Example:

```cpp
RingBuffer<int> buffer(1024);

// Producer thread:
int item = 42;
if (buffer.push(item)) {
    // Successfully pushed
}

// Consumer thread:
int received;
if (buffer.pop(received)) {
    // Successfully popped value 42
}
```

This implementation is optimal for the single-producer/single-consumer case where you don't need the full generality of a multi-producer/multi-consumer buffer.

