However, if your goal is to compute a maximum matching in a bipartite graph efficiently (which is a common problem), there are well-known algorithms like the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm that can achieve this in \(O(\sqrt{V}E)\) time, where \(V\) is the number of vertices and \(E\) is the number of edges. For general graphs, computing a maximum matching typically involves more complex algorithms like Edmonds' Blossom algorithm.

Given your request for an \(O(n)\) time complexity solution in Java, it's important to clarify that achieving \(O(n)\) for maximum matching in arbitrary or even bipartite graphs (with \(n\) representing the number of vertices or edges) is not generally possible with current algorithms unless there are very specific constraints on the graph structure (e.g., trees, certain types of sparse graphs).

Assuming you're working with a bipartite graph and looking for an efficient implementation, below is a minimal working example using the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm in Java. This algorithm does not run in \(O(n)\) time but is one of the most efficient known algorithms for this problem.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class HopcroftKarp {
    private static final int NIL = -1;
    private List<Integer>[] adj; // Adjacency list for the bipartite graph
    private int[] matchL, matchR; // Matchings for left and right sets
    private boolean[] visited; // Visited array for BFS

    public HopcroftKarp(int n) {
        // Assuming 0-based indexing for simplicity
        adj = new ArrayList[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            adj[i] = new ArrayList<>();
        }
        matchL = new int[n];
        matchR = new int[n];
        visited = new boolean[n];

        // Initialize matches to NIL (-1)
        Arrays.fill(matchL, NIL);
        Arrays.fill(matchR, NIL);
    }

    public void addEdge(int u, int v) {
        adj[u].add(v); // Assuming u is from the left set and v is from the right set
    }

    private boolean bfs() {
        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int u = 0; u < matchL.length; u++) {
            if (matchL[u] == NIL) { // If left vertex u is not matched
                visited[u] = true;
                q.add(u);
            } else {
                visited[u] = false;
            }
        }

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int u = q.poll();
            for (int v : adj[u]) {
                if (!visited[matchR[v]]) { // If the matched vertex of right vertex v is not visited
                    visited[matchR[v]] = true;
                    q.add(matchL[matchR[v]]);
                }
            }
        }

        // Returns true if there is an augmenting path
        for (int u = 0; u < matchL.length; u++) {
            if (matchL[u] == NIL && visited[u]) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(int u) {
        if (u != NIL) {
            for (int v : adj[u]) {
                if (!visited[matchR[v]]) {
                    visited[matchR[v]] = true;
                    if (dfs(matchL[matchR[v]])) { // Recursively try to find augmenting path
                        matchR[v] = u; // Update matching for right vertex v
                        matchL[u] = v; // Update matching for left vertex u
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            }

            visited[u] = false; // Mark as visited in this DFS call
            return false;
        }
        return true; // Base case: reached the end of an augmenting path
    }

    public int maximumMatching() {
        int matchCount = 0;
        // While there's an augmenting path, keep finding and adding it to the matching
        while (bfs()) {
            Arrays.fill(visited, false); // Reset visited array for next BFS
            for (int u = 0; u < matchL.length; u++) {
                if (matchL[u] == NIL && dfs(u)) { // If left vertex u is not matched and there's an augmenting path starting from u
                    matchCount++;
                }
            }
        }
        return matchCount;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5; // Example: 5 vertices on the left side (0 to 4)
        HopcroftKarp hk = new HopcroftKarp(n);

        // Adding edges (u, v) where u is from the left set and v is from the right set
        hk.addEdge(0, 0);
        hk.addEdge(1, 1);
        hk.addEdge(2, 2);
        hk.addEdge(3, 3);
        hk.addEdge(4, 4);

        System.out.println("Size of maximum matching: " + hk.maximumMatching());
    }
}
```

This example assumes a bipartite graph where the left set has `n` vertices (indexed from 0 to n-1) and edges are added between these vertices and vertices on the right side. The `maximumMatching` method returns the size of the maximum matching found.

