To achieve ACID transactions across two databases using the Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol, you'll typically rely on a transaction manager that coordinates between the participating databases. The 2PC protocol ensures that either all databases commit the transaction or none do, maintaining atomicity and consistency.

Below is a conceptual example in pseudo-code/Java-like syntax to illustrate how you might implement this. This example assumes you have access to APIs or libraries that can interact with your databases and support distributed transactions (e.g., JTA in Java EE environments). Note that the actual implementation details will vary based on the specific technologies and frameworks you're using.

### Pseudo-code for Two-Phase Commit Across Two Databases

