In functional programming and some imperative languages, recursion is a common pattern. However, naive recursive calls can lead to stack overflow if the recursion depth is too large because each call consumes stack space. Tail-call elimination is an optimization technique where the compiler recognizes that in certain tail positions (the last action of a function), it doesn't need to return to the caller after making the recursive call. Instead, it can jump directly to the beginning of the called function with updated arguments, reusing the current stack frame.

