To solve this problem, we need to implement a shared register that ensures wait-free consensus among three threads using only atomic read/write registers. The goal is for all threads to eventually agree on the same value without any thread being forced to wait indefinitely.

### Approach
The solution involves each thread repeatedly attempting to write its own value into a shared register until it successfully does so. Since each write operation is atomic, only one thread can succeed at a time. This ensures that after all threads have had their turn, the last successful write determines the final value in the register. Each thread continues writing its value until it has successfully done so once, ensuring that all threads eventually agree on the same value.

### Solution Code
```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>

std::atomic<int> shared_value{0};

void thread_function(int my_value) {
    while (true) {
        // Read the current value of the shared register
        int expected = shared_value.load();

        // Try to write the new value if it's different from what we expect
        // This loop ensures that only one thread will succeed in writing at a time
        while (expected != my_value) {
            // If someone else has already written our value, break out of the loop
            if (shared_value.load() == my_value) {
                break;
            }

            // Try to write the new value; if it fails (because another thread wrote), continue looping
            shared_value.store(my_value);
        }

        // If we've successfully written our value, exit the function
        if (shared_value.load() == my_value) {
            break;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    // Create three threads with their respective values
    std::thread t1(thread_function, 1);
    std::thread t2(thread_function, 2);
    std::thread t3(thread_function, 3);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    t3.join();

    return shared_value.load(); // The final agreed value
}
```

### Explanation
- **Atomic Operations**: Each thread uses atomic read and write operations to ensure that each write is visible to all other threads without any chance of data corruption.
- **No Locks or Condition Variables**: By relying on the atomicity of operations, we avoid using locks or condition variables, which would introduce waiting and potentially violate the wait-free requirement.
- **Infinite Loops with Atomic Writes**: Each thread enters an infinite loop where it repeatedly attempts to write its own value into the shared register. The atomic nature of these writes ensures that only one thread's write will succeed at any given time, preventing conflicts.
- **Consensus Through Last Write**: Since each thread continues writing until it successfully does so once, the last thread to write its value determines the final state of the shared register. All other threads will eventually read this value and recognize consensus.

This approach guarantees that all threads will reach a consistent state without any waiting, adhering to the constraints of using only atomic read/write operations.

