## 🔵 Approximate NN with LSH: O(1) Expected Query Time (C++ sketch)

Here’s how you’d do *approximate* exact-in-practice nearest neighbors:

### Key idea:
- Use hash functions that map nearby points to same bucket with high probability.
- For Euclidean space: **p-stable distributions** (e.g., Gaussian).
- Build multiple hash tables → query checks only one bucket per table.

```cpp
// Pseudocode sketch (not full production code)

struct LSH {
    size_t K, L;               // #projections per hash, #tables
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> A;  // random projections: K*L x d
    double w;                  // bin width

    LSH(size_t k, size_t l, size_t dim, double W)
        : K(k), L(l), A(k*l, std::vector<double>(dim)), w(W) {
        for (auto& v : A)
            for (double& x : v) x = normal_dist(rng); // N(0,1)

        for (size_t i = 0; i < k*l; ++i)
            for (size_t j = 0; j < dim; ++j)
                A[i][j] /= sqrt(1.0 / w); // scale by w
    }

    std::vector<uint64_t> hash(const Point& p) const {
        std::vector<uint64_t> hashes;
        for (size_t l_idx = 0; l_idx < L; ++l_idx) {
            uint64_t h = 0;
            for (size_t k_idx = 0; k_idx < K; ++k_idx) {
                size_t idx = l_idx*K + k_idx;
                double dot = 0.0;
                for (size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); ++i)
                    dot += A[idx][i] * p[i];
                // add random offset b ~ U[0,w]
                double b = uniform_dist(rng);
                size_t bin = static_cast<size_t>(std::floor((dot + b) / w));
                h ^= std::hash<size_t>{}(bin) << k_idx; // combine bits
            }
            hashes.push_back(h);
        }
        return hashes;
    }

    // Insert point with ID `id`
    void insert(const Point& p, size_t id) {
        for (auto h : hash(p))
            tables[h].push_back(id);
    }

    // Query: return candidate indices in O(L)
    std::vector<size_t> query(const Point& q) const {
        std::unordered_set<size_t> candidates;
        for (auto h : hash(q)) {
            if (tables.count(h)) {
                for (size_t id : tables.at(h))
                    candidates.insert(id);
            }
        }
        return {candidates.begin(), candidates.end()};
    }

  private:
    std::vector<std::unordered_map<uint64_t, std::vector<size_t>>> tables;
};
```

Then post-filter only those candidates with exact distance:

```cpp
auto candidates = lsh.query(q);
size_t best_idx = *std::min_element(candidates.begin(), candidates.end(),
    [&](size_t i, size_t j) {
        return dist(q, db[i]) < dist(q, db[j]);
    });
```

✅ Query time: **O(L)** → can be set to constant (e.g., \( L = 10 \))  
✅ Memory: ~\( O(n^{1+\rho}) \), with \( \rho < 1 \)  
❌ Approximate: may miss exact NN, but success probability can be tuned.

