It looks like there might be some confusion—**there is no such thing as an `inline_proof_blocks` language feature in Rust**, either in stable, beta, or nightly (as of 2024). Rust does **not** have built-in support for embedding compiler-checked mathematical proofs directly in code.

Rust has:
- `#[track_caller]`, `debug_assert!`, etc. for safety.
- External crates like [`witgen`](https://crates.io/crates/witgen), [`proptest`](https://docs.rs/proptest/latest/proptest/), or [`FStar`](https://fstar-lang.org/) integration (not native).
- Research efforts like [`RustHOL`](https://github.com/rust-hol) or [`CertiCoq`](https://certicoq.github.io/), but **no standard language-level proof feature**.

That said, here’s a *realistic* and *practical* example showing how to get close in idiomatic Rust using `#[cfg(test)]`, `static_assertions`, and property-based tests to embed **executable, compiler-checked assertions** (not full formal proofs):

```rust
//! Demonstrates "proof-like" verification in Rust:
//! - compile-time static assertions (`const` & `#[allow(clippy::let_unit_value)]`)
//! - property-based testing via `proptest`

use std::hint::unreachable_unchecked;
use static_assertions::const_assert;

/// A verified, simple invariant: 2 + 2 == 4 (checked at compile time)
pub const fn add_two_and_two() -> u32 {
    // This will fail to compile if the math is wrong (impossible here!)
    const_assert!(2 + 2 == 4);

    // SAFETY: We *know* this is safe because of `const_assert!`.
    // In practice, use `debug_assert!` or `assert!` instead.
    unsafe {
        debug_assert_eq!(2 + 2, 4); // runtime-only in debug builds
        4
    }
}

/// Verified property: reverse(reverse(xs)) == xs for arrays of known size.
pub fn reversed_is_involutive<const N: usize>(xs: &[u32; N]) -> [u32; N] {
    let rev1 = xs.iter().rev().copied().collect::<Vec<_>>();
    let rev2: [u32; N] = rev1.iter().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>().try_into()
        .expect("length preserved by reversals");

    // Compile-time check via `const` (if N ≤ 32, for example)
    if N <= 8 {
        const_assert!(true); // placeholder; real checks need const context
        debug_assert_eq!(rev2, *xs);
    }

    rev2
}

/// Property-based "proof" using `proptest`.
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use proptest::prelude::*;

    prop_compose! {
        fn vec_u32(max_val: u32, len in 0..16usize)
            (v in any::<[u32; 16]>().prop_map(move |arr| arr[..len].to_vec()))
            -> Vec<u32> {
            v
        }
    }

    proptest! {
        #[test]
        fn reverse_is_involutive(xs in vec_u32(42)) {
            let rev = xs.iter().rev().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>();
            let rev_rev = rev.iter().rev().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>();
            assert_eq!(rev_rev, xs);
        }
    }
}
```

