### ✅ Correct & Working Code Using `boost::lockfree::queue`

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <boost/lockfree/queue.hpp>

int main() {
    // Create a lock-free queue with capacity for 1024 elements
    boost::lockfree::queue<int> q(1024);

    if (!q.is_lock_free()) {
        std::cerr << "Warning: Queue is not lock-free!\n";
    }

    // Producer thread
    auto producer = std::thread([&q]() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
            while (!q.try_push(i)) { /* retry until success */ }
            std::cout << "Produced: " << i << '\n';
        }
    });

    // Consumer thread
    auto consumer = std::thread([&q]() {
        int val;
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
            while (!q.try_pop(val)) { /* retry until success */ }
            std::cout << "Consumed: " << val << '\n';
        }
    });

    producer.join();
    consumer.join();

    std::cout << "Queue is empty? " << (q.empty() ? "Yes" : "No") << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

> 🔔 **Important Note**: There is **no** `<boost/lockfree/perfect_queue.hpp>` in official Boost versions (v1.32–v1.85). This may be:
> - A typo or internal alias.
> - Confusion with [Boost's `queue`](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_84_0/libs/lockfree/doc/html/lockfree/queue.html) (SPSC/MPMC).
> - A custom header in your codebase.

If your tech lead insists on `perfect_queue`, ask for clarification. Otherwise, use the standard `boost::lockfree::queue`.

---

### 🛠 How It Works

`boost::lockfree::queue` is a **wait-free (Spsc)** or **lock-free (Mpmc)** bounded queue implemented using atomic operations and memory ordering primitives:

- **SPSC (Single-Producer Single-Consumer)** mode:
  - Uses separate producer/consumer tail/head indices.
  - Zero locks—only `std::atomic` operations (`compare_exchange_strong`, etc.).
  - Memory reclamation via deferred deletion or hazard pointers.

- **MPMC (Multi-Producer Multi-Consumer)** mode:
  - More complex: uses atomic compare-and-swap on a lock-free linked list of nodes.
  - May fall back to mutexes if the platform doesn't support required atomics.

#### Key APIs
| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `bool try_push(const T&)` | Attempts to enqueue (non-blocking). Returns `false` if full. |
| `bool try_pop(T&)` | Attempts to dequeue (non-blocking). Returns `false` if empty. |
| `push()`, `pop()` | Can be blocking in some modes (not recommended for performance-critical code). |
| `is_lock_free()` | Checks if the underlying implementation uses true lock-free atomics. |

> 📌 **Performance Tip**: Use `try_push`/`try_pop` with busy-waiting in tight loops—this avoids OS-level context switches.

