```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ParallelSumExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Generate a large list of integers
        int size = 10_000_000;
        List<Integer> numbers = new Random()
                .ints(size, 1, 100)   // 10 million random ints from [1, 100)
                .boxed()              // convert IntStream to Stream<Integer>
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Sequential sum (for comparison)
        long startSeq = System.nanoTime();
        long sumSequential = numbers.stream()
                                    .mapToLong(Integer::longValue)
                                    .sum();
        long timeSeq = System.nanoTime() - startSeq;

        // Parallel sum using .parallel()
        long startPara = System.nanoTime();
        long sumParallel = numbers.parallelStream()
                                  .mapToLong(Long::valueOf) // or mapToInt(i -> i).sum()
                                  .sum();                   // .sum() uses a numeric identity and associative combiner
        long timePara = System.nanoTime() - startPara;

        System.out.println("Sequential sum: " + sumSequential);
        System.out.println("Parallel sum:   " + sumParallel);
        System.out.printf("Seq time: %.3f ms, Para time: %.3f ms%n", 
                          timeSeq / 1_000_000.0, timePara / 1_000_000.0);
    }
}
```

✅ Key points:
- Use `.parallelStream()` or call `.parallel()` on a regular stream.
- `.sum()` on `IntStream`, `LongStream`, or `DoubleStream` is inherently parallel-friendly and uses efficient reduction under the hood (fork-join pool).
- Parallel streams use the common `ForkJoinPool` (`ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`), which by default has as many threads as your CPU cores.

⚠️ Caveats:
- Not all operations benefit from parallelism — it works best for large, independent, stateless, associative operations (like `sum`, `map`, `filter`, `reduce`).
- Small collections often run *slower* in parallel due to overhead.
- Avoid mutating shared state inside stream pipelines.

