- The standard API only provides [`HttpClient`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/20/docs/api/java/net/http/java/net/http/HttpClient.html), which supports **HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2** (via ALPN over TLS), but **not HTTP/3**.
- HTTP/3 is not part of the official Java standard library as of JDK 21 (released September 2023). Support may be added in future versions via [JEP 459](https://openjdk.org/jeps/459) (still draft/status: *Candidate*), but it’s **not implemented yet**.

✅ What you *can* do right now (to make an HTTP/2 request in Java):

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;

public class Http2ClientExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2) // Prefer HTTP/2; falls back to HTTP/1.1 if needed
                .build();

        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create("https://http2.golang.org/clock"))
                .GET()
                .build();

        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        System.out.println("Status: " + response.version() + " " + response.statusCode());
        System.out.println("Body: " + response.body());
    }
}
```

🔹 **Important notes**:
- HTTP/2 support requires:
  - Java ≥ JDK 9 (HTTP/2 support added in JDK 11+ as stable)
  - TLS 1.2+ with ALPN enabled (standard on modern JVMs when connecting to HTTPS servers supporting HTTP/2).
- For *actual* HTTP/3 (UDP-based, QUIC), you’ll need a third-party library like:
  - [QuicJ](https://github.com/quicj/quicj)
  - [quinn](https://github.com/dalekunk/quinn-jvm) (via JNI)
  - [OkHttp](https://square.github.io/okhttp/) (with HTTP/3 support via OkHttp-QUIC), used via interop.

If your deadline is urgent: **use `HttpClient` with `.version(HTTP_2)`** — it’s production-ready for HTTP/2. If you truly need HTTP/3, clarify whether the server actually *requires* QUIC (most still work fine over HTTP/2).

