Here’s a minimal example using [`ws-reliable`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ws-reliable) (a wrapper around `ws` that adds automatic reconnection and message queuing).

### 1. Install

```bash
npm install ws-reliable
```

> Note: This package does **not** include `ws`, so if you plan to use raw `WebSocket` elsewhere, you may also need `npm install ws`.

---

### 2. Basic usage (Node.js)

```js
// reliable-ws-example.js
const ReliableWS = require('ws-reliable'); // or: import ReliableWS from 'ws-reliable'; in ESM

// Create a new reliable WebSocket instance
// First arg: the URL, second arg: optional ws options (headers, etc.)
const socket = new ReliableWS('wss://echo.websocket.org', {
  // Options supported by ws-reliable:
  maxReconnectionDelay: 10000,       // max delay between reconnect attempts (ms)
  minReconnectionDelay: 1000,        // initial delay
  reconnectionDelayGrowFactor: 1.3,  // delay multiplier per attempt
  connectionTimeout: 4000,           // how long before declaring connect failed (ms)
  maxRetries: Infinity,              // -1 or Infinity for unlimited retries
  pingInterval: 25000,               // ms between pings (optional but recommended)
});

// Fired when the underlying WebSocket is open
socket.on('open', () => {
  console.log('✅ Connected reliably! Sending a message…');
  socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'hello', text: 'Hi server!' }));
});

// Fired on successful message send *to the underlying ws*
socket.on('sent', (data) => {
  console.log('📤 Sent data:', data);
});

// Fired when a message is received from the server
socket.on('message', (data) => {
  // `data` is Buffer (Node) or string (browser)
  const msg = typeof data === 'string' ? data : data.toString();
  console.log('📩 Received:', msg);
});

// Fired on connection error (e.g., DNS, TLS)
socket.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('⚠️ WebSocket error:', err.message || err);
});

// Fired when the socket is closed (manually or due to network issue)
socket.on('close', (code, reason) => {
  console.log('🔴 Closed. Code:', code, 'Reason:', reason);

  // ws-reliable will automatically try to reconnect unless you call socket.close() with forced = true
});

// Optional: handle reconnection attempts
socket.on('reconnecting', (attemptNumber, delay) => {
  console.log(`🔄 Reconnecting in ${delay} ms (attempt #${attemptNumber})`);
});
```

Run it:
```bash
node reliable-ws-example.js
```

You’ll see the reconnection logic in action if you temporarily disconnect your network or stop the server.

---

### 3. Sending and receiving typed messages (example)

```js
socket.on('open', () => {
  socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'ping', ts: Date.now() }));
});

socket.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data.toString());
  if (msg.type === 'pong') {
    console.log('⏱️ RTT:', Date.now() - msg.ts, 'ms');
  }
});
```

---

### 4. Manually closing (prevents reconnection)

```js
// This will close *and stop reconnecting*:
socket.close({ code: 1000, reason: 'User logged out' });

// Or if you want to force-close even during reconnect attempts:
socket.destroy(); // immediately kills connection and cancels all retries
```

---

### Notes

- In the browser, `ws-reliable` also works (it detects the environment). The API is identical—just import it from your bundler.
- If you need to pass custom HTTP headers on first connect (e.g., for auth), use the `options` parameter:

```js
const socket = new ReliableWS('wss://api.example.com', {
  headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer abc123' }
});
```

