Here’s how we can integrate the `tokio` crate into a Rust project and use it to run an asynchronous task.

### 1. Add `tokio` as a dependency

First, add this to your `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```

> The `"full"` feature enables all of Tokio’s functionality (runtime, macros, drivers), which is ideal for learning and development.

---

### 2. Write an async task

Below is a minimal example in `src/main.rs` that spawns and waits on an async task:

```rust
// src/main.rs
use tokio; // bring the crate into scope

#[tokio::main] // <-- this macro creates the async runtime
async fn main() {
    println!("Starting up...");

    // Spawn a task (non-blocking)
    let join_handle = tokio::spawn(async {
        println!("Task started!");
        tokio::time::sleep(tokio::time::Duration::from_secs(1)).await;
        println!("Task finished after 1 second.");
        "Hello from async!"
    });

    // Do something else in the meantime...
    println!("Main function continues while task runs...");

    // Wait for the task to complete (blocking at this call site)
    match join_handle.await {
        Ok(result) => println!("Task returned: {}", result),
        Err(e) => eprintln!("Task panicked: {}", e),
    }

    println!("All done!");
}
```

---

### ✅ Run it

```bash
cargo run
```

Expected output:

```
Starting up...
Main function continues while task runs...
Task started!
Task finished after 1 second.
Task returned: Hello from async!
All done!
```

