Go 1.35 doesn’t exist — **the latest stable Go version as of 2024 is Go 1.23**, and **arenas (including `arena.Reset`) are not yet part of the standard library**.

The `arena` package *is* being developed experimentally (see [Go issue #62159](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/62159) and [design doc](https://go.googlesource.com/proposal/+/refs/heads/master/design/62159-arena.md)), but as of Go 1.23 (released August 2024), **arenas remain a pre-release, behind the `gotip` or custom toolchain**, and there is *no* `arena.Reset()` API yet — only `arena.Free(arena)` to deallocate an entire arena.

### ⚠️ Important Clarification:
- ❌ There is **no `arena.Reset()`** in any public Go release.
- ✅ `arena.NewArena()` exists (under `GOEXPERIMENT=arenas`), and you can call `arena.Free(a)` to reclaim all memory at once — effectively resetting *for reuse*, but it’s not `Reset`, and it invalidates the arena (you must create a new one to reallocate).
- ❗ You cannot safely reuse an arena after freeing it *unless* you discard all existing pointers and start fresh.

---

### ✅ What You *Can* Do Today (Go 1.23+ with `GOEXPERIMENT=arenas`)

```go
// +build goexperiment.arenas

package main

import (
	"arena"
	"fmt"
)

func processWithArena() {
	// Create a new arena
	a := arena.NewArena()
	defer arena.Free(a) // ensures cleanup

	// Allocate within the arena (use unsafe or pointer tricks!)
	slice := arena.Make([]byte, 1024, a)
	copy(slice, "Hello arena!")
	fmt.Println(string(slice))

	// When done: Free releases ALL memory in the arena at once
}

func main() {
	// To use arenas, build with:
	//   GOEXPERIMENT=arenas go run -tags goexperiment.arenas .
	processWithArena()
}
```

To **reuse** an arena across multiple tasks (e.g., per-request), you must *not* `Free()` it in between — instead:

```go
type ReusableArena struct {
	a    arena.Arena
_used  bool // to guard reuse
}

func (ra *ReusableArena) Reset() {
	// ⚠️ You cannot actually "reset" an arena safely without leaking.
	// The only way is to Free() and create a new one — but that invalidates all pointers.
	ra.a = arena.NewArena()
	ra._used = true
}

func (ra *ReusableArena) Free() {
	if ra._used {
		arena.Free(ra.a)
		ra._used = false
	}
}
```

But crucially: **you cannot reuse the same `arena.Arena` instance after freeing it** — you must allocate a new one (as shown above with `.a = arena.NewArena()`).

---

