To solve this problem, we need to create a hash map using open addressing that ensures O(1) worst-case lookup time for any key distribution. This is achieved by using Cuckoo Hashing, which guarantees that each key will be found in one of two specific positions, allowing constant-time lookups.

### Approach
The approach involves using two distinct hash functions to determine potential slots for each key. Each key is mapped to two possible indices in the array. If both slots are occupied, we evict one key and reinsert it elsewhere. This method ensures that each lookup only needs to check a constant number of positions (specifically two), resulting in O(1) worst-case time complexity.

### Solution Code
```java
class HashMap {
    private static class Entry {
        int key;
        String value;

        Entry(int k, String v) {
            key = k;
            value = v;
        }

        public int getKey() {
            return key;
        }

        public String getValue() {
            return value;
        }
    }

    private int size;
    private int capacity;
    private Entry[] table;

    public HashMap() {
        this.capacity = 16; // Initial capacity
        this.size = 0;
        this.table = new Entry[capacity];
    }

    // Helper functions for hashing
    private int hash1(int key) {
        return (key & 0x7FFFFFFF) % capacity;
    }

    private int hash2(int key) {
        return ((key >> 16) | (key << 16)) % capacity;
    }

    public void put(int key, String value) {
        if (size >= 0.5 * capacity) { // Load factor threshold
            resize();
        }

        int index1 = hash1(key);
        int index2 = hash2(key);

        Entry entry1 = table[index1];
        Entry entry2 = table[index2];

        if (entry1 == null || entry1.getKey() == key) {
            table[index1] = new Entry(key, value);
            size++;
        } else if (entry2 == null || entry2.getKey() == key) {
            table[index2] = new Entry(key, value);
            size++;
        } else {
            // Evict one of the entries and reinsert
            Entry displacedEntry = entry1;
            table[index1] = new Entry(key, value);
            put(displacedEntry.getKey(), displacedEntry.getValue());
        }
    }

    public String get(int key) {
        int index1 = hash1(key);
        int index2 = hash2(key);

        if (table[index1] != null && table[index1].getKey() == key) {
            return table[index1].getValue();
        } else if (table[index2] != null && table[index2].getKey() == key) {
            return table[index2].getValue();
        }
        return null;
    }

    private void resize() {
        int newCapacity = capacity * 2;
        Entry[] newTable = new Entry[newCapacity];
        for (Entry entry : table) {
            if (entry != null) {
                put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
            }
        }
        this.table = newTable;
        this.capacity = newCapacity;
    }
}
```

### Explanation
- **Cuckoo Hashing**: This technique uses two hash functions to determine potential slots for each key. If both slots are occupied, the key causing the collision is displaced and reinserted elsewhere.
- **Resizing**: The hash map dynamically resizes when the load factor exceeds a threshold (e.g., 50%). Resizing ensures that the table can accommodate more entries while maintaining efficient operations.
- **Lookup Time**: Each lookup operation checks only two possible positions, resulting in O(1) worst-case time complexity.

This implementation efficiently handles any key distribution by ensuring each key is stored in one of its two hashed positions, providing constant-time lookups.

