To set up a reliable WebSocket connection using the `ws` npm package in JavaScript, follow these steps:

### Step 1: Install Required Packages

First, initialize your project and install the necessary packages.

```bash
npm init -y
npm install ws
```

### Step 2: Create the Server (index.js)

Set up an HTTP server and attach a WebSocket server to it.

```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const http = require('http');

// Create HTTP server
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Hello World\n');
});

// Attach WebSocket server to the HTTP server
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ server });

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
    console.log('Client connected');

    ws.on('message', (message) => {
        console.log(`Received: ${message}`);
        // Echo back the message
        ws.send(`Echo: ${message}`);
    });

    ws.on('close', () => {
        console.log('Client disconnected');
    });
});

server.listen(8080, () => {
    console.log('Server is running on port 8080');
});
```

### Step 3: Create the Client (client.html)

Create an HTML file with client-side WebSocket code.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>WebSocket Client</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>WebSocket Chat</h1>
    <input id="messageInput" type="text" placeholder="Type your message...">
    <button onclick="sendMessage()">Send</button>
    <div id="messages"></div>

    <!-- Include WebSocket client library -->
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/ws@8.5.0/dist/bundle.min.js"></script>
    <script>
        const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8080');

        // Connection events
        ws.onopen = () => {
            console.log("Connected to server");
        };

        ws.onclose = () => {
            console.log("Disconnected from server");
        };

        // Message handling
        function sendMessage() {
            const input = document.getElementById('messageInput');
            if (input.value.trim()) {
                ws.send(input.value);
                appendMessage('You: ' + input.value, 'right');
                input.value = '';
            }
        }

        ws.onmessage = (event) => {
            console.log("Received:", event.data);
            appendMessage('Server: ' + event.data, 'left');
        };

        function appendMessage(message, alignment) {
            const messagesDiv = document.getElementById('messages');
            const messageElement = document.createElement('div');
            messageElement.style.textAlign = alignment;
            messageElement.textContent = message;
            messagesDiv.appendChild(messageElement);
            messagesDiv.scrollTop = messagesDiv.scrollHeight; // Auto-scroll to bottom
        }

        // Handle errors
        ws.onerror = (error) => {
            console.error("WebSocket error:", error);
            // Optionally, attempt reconnect logic here
        };
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 4: Run the Server and Test

Start the server:

```bash
node index.js
```

Open `client.html` in a web browser. You should see messages sent to and received from the server.

### Enhancements for Reliability

1. **Reconnection Logic**: Implement automatic reconnection attempts on the client side if the connection drops.
2. **Heartbeats**: Use periodic pings to keep the connection alive, especially behind proxies or firewalls.
3. **Error Handling**: Add comprehensive error handling and logging.
