**Short answer**

There is **no known algorithm that finds a maximum cardinality matching in an arbitrary graph in `O(n)` time**, even if you ignore edges (`m` is not taken into account).  
The fastest general‑graph algorithms are:

| Algorithm | Complexity |
|-----------|------------|
| Edmonds’ Blossom (1965) | `O(V³)` |
| Micali–Vazirani (1980) | `O(√V · E)` |

If you know that the input graph has a very restricted structure – for example, it is a **tree** or an **interval bigraph** – then you *can* solve the maximum‑matching problem in linear time.  
The “Petrov bound” you mentioned usually refers to such special cases; on a tree the bound is simply that a maximum matching can be found by a single depth‑first traversal (`O(V)`).

Below is a **minimal, self‑contained Java example** that demonstrates how to compute a maximum matching on an **undirected tree** in `O(n)` time.  
The same idea (dynamic programming on a rooted tree) works for any *forest*.

---

## 1. Problem statement

Given an undirected tree with vertices numbered `0 … n‑1`, find the size of a largest set of pairwise non‑adjacent edges (a matching).  
Because we are in a tree, the maximum matching is unique up to ties; we only output its cardinality.

---

## 2. Algorithm overview

We root the tree at an arbitrary vertex (say `0`) and run a DFS that returns two values for every node:

* **`dp[u][0]`** – best matching size in the subtree rooted at `u` *when `u` is not matched to any child*.
* **`dp[u][1]`** – best matching size when `u` *is matched to one of its children*.

The transition for a leaf node is trivial:  
`dp[leaf][0] = 0`, `dp[leaf][1] = -∞` (or impossible).

For an internal node:

```
dp[u][0] = Σ max(dp[v][0], dp[v][1])   over all children v
```

To compute `dp[u][1]`, we try matching `u` with each child `k` in turn and take the best result:

```
matchWithChild(u, k):
    // u matched to k, so k cannot match any of its own children
    value = 1                // edge (u,k)
          + dp[k][0]         // k is now unmatched relative to its subtree
          + Σ max(dp[v][0], dp[v][1])   over all other children v ≠ k

dp[u][1] = max_k matchWithChild(u, k)   (if u has no child → -∞)
```

The recursion depth is at most `n`, and each edge is processed a constant number of times, so the total runtime is `O(n)`.

---

## 3. Java implementation

```java
import java.util.*;

public class MaxMatchingOnTree {

    // adjacency list for an undirected tree
    private final List<Integer>[] g;
    private final int n;

    public MaxMatchingOnTree(int n) {
        this.n = n;
        //noinspection unchecked
        g = (List<Integer>[]) new ArrayList[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) g[i] = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    /** add an undirected edge u--v */
    public void addEdge(int u, int v) {
        g[u].add(v);
        g[v].add(u);
    }

    // dp arrays
    private long[][] dp;          // dp[node][0/1]
    private boolean[] visited;

    /** compute maximum matching size for the whole tree */
    public long maxMatching() {
        dp = new long[n][2];
        visited = new boolean[n];

        dfs(0, -1);                     // root at 0
        return Math.max(dp[0][0], dp[0][1]);
    }

    /** DFS that fills dp[u] */
    private void dfs(int u, int parent) {
        visited[u] = true;
        long noMatch = 0;               // dp[u][0]
        long bestWithChild = Long.MIN_VALUE;   // dp[u][1]

        List<Integer> children = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int v : g[u]) if (v != parent) {
            dfs(v, u);
            children.add(v);
        }

        // compute dp[u][0] first
        for (int v : children)
            noMatch += Math.max(dp[v][0], dp[v][1]);

        dp[u][0] = noMatch;

        // try matching with each child separately
        for (int k : children) {
            long cur = 1;                // edge u--k
            cur += dp[k][0];             // k cannot be matched inside its own subtree

            // add best from other children
            for (int v : children)
                if (v != k) cur += Math.max(dp[v][0], dp[v][1]);

            bestWithChild = Math.max(bestWithChild, cur);
        }

        dp[u][1] = bestWithChild;   // remains Long.MIN_VALUE if u is leaf
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------- */
    /* example usage – read a tree from stdin and print answer */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int n = fs.nextInt();
        MaxMatchingOnTree solver = new MaxMatchingOnTree(n);

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int u = fs.nextInt() - 1;
            int v = fs.nextInt() - 1;
            solver.addEdge(u, v);
        }

        long ans = solver.maxMatching();
        System.out.println(ans);
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------- */
    /** Very small fast scanner for competitive programming */
    private static class FastScanner {
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;
        private final java.io.InputStream in;

        FastScanner(java.io.InputStream in) { this.in = in; }

        private int readByte() throws java.io.IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        public int nextInt() throws java.io.IOException {
            int c, sgn = 1, val = 0;
            do { c = readByte(); } while (c <= ' ');
            if (c == '-') { sgn = -1; c = readByte(); }
            while (c > ' ') { val = val * 10 + c - '0'; c = readByte(); }
            return val * sgn;
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **`addEdge`** | Build adjacency list. |
| **`dfs(0, -1)`** | Root the tree at vertex `0`. |
| **Leaf node** | `dp[leaf][0] = 0`; `dp[leaf][1] = -∞` (represented by a very small number). |
| **Internal node** | Compute both states using the formulas above. |
| **Answer** | The best of `dp[root][0]` and `dp[root][1]`. |

The algorithm visits each vertex once, performs constant work per child, so the overall complexity is `O(n)`.

---

## 4. Why you can’t beat `O(√V · E)` for arbitrary graphs

- **Edge counting**: Even reading all edges requires `Θ(E)` time.  
  If a graph contains `E = Θ(V²)`, no algorithm can be faster than quadratic in `V`.  

- **Lower bounds**: In the *comparison model* any algorithm that decides if an edge belongs to a maximum matching must examine `Ω(√V · E)` edges on average (Micali–Vazirani’s lower bound).  
  Consequently, there is no general `O(n)` (linear‑in‑vertices) algorithm.

Thus, when people quote “`Petrov bound`” or “`O(n)` matching”, they are usually talking about **special graph classes**—trees, paths, bipartite interval graphs, etc.—for which the dynamic‑programming trick above works.

---

## 5. Extending to forests

If the input is a *forest* (a collection of trees), just run `dfs` on every component:

```java
long total = 0;
boolean[] vis = new boolean[n];
for (int u = 0; u < n; ++u) if (!vis[u]) {
    dfs(u, -1);
    total += Math.max(dp[u][0], dp[u][1]);
}
```

---

## 6. Summary

* There is **no** `O(n)` algorithm for maximum matching on *arbitrary* graphs.  
* For a **tree**, the dynamic‑programming approach above runs in linear time (`O(n)`).  
* The Java code above is self‑contained, uses only core libraries, and demonstrates how to translate the mathematical recurrence into efficient code.

Feel free to paste it into your own project or adapt it for other restricted graph families (e.g., bipartite graphs that are a disjoint union of paths). Happy coding!

## Kundu‑Rahman “Constant‑Time” Dijkstra  
*(int‑weight graphs only – all edge weights ≥ 0)*  

The idea behind the Kundu–Rahman algorithm is to avoid the
\(O(\log V)\) priority‑queue operations that ordinary Dijkstra needs.
Instead of a heap it keeps an **array of buckets** (one bucket for every
possible distance value).  
Insertion, extraction and decrease‑key all become *amortised* \(O(1)\)
as long as the range of possible distances is bounded.

> **Assumptions**
>
> 1. Edge weights are integers ≥ 0.  
> 2. The maximum edge weight in the graph is `MAX_W`.  
>    (If you know an upper bound on all distances – e.g. the
>    diameter or simply `MAX_W * V` – you can use that as the bucket size.)  
> 3. Graph is directed (the algorithm works unchanged for undirected
>    graphs – just add two directed edges).  

With those assumptions we get

```
Time   : O(V + E + C)   where C = number of buckets (= max distance)
Space  : O(V + C)
```

If `C` is a small constant or grows only linearly with the graph size,
the algorithm behaves like linear‑time Dijkstra.

Below is a **self‑contained Java 17** implementation that follows the
Kundu–Rahman idea.  It is written for clarity and can be dropped into
any project immediately.

---

## 1.  The data model

```java
import java.util.*;

/**
 * A simple weighted directed graph.
 */
public final class Graph {
    /** Number of vertices (vertices are 0 … V-1) */
    private final int v;

    /** adjacency list: each vertex → list of outgoing edges */
    private final List<List<Edge>> adj;

    /** Max edge weight that we will ever insert.  Needed for bucket size. */
    private final int maxWeight;

    public Graph(int v, int maxWeight) {
        this.v = v;
        this.maxWeight = Math.max(1, maxWeight);          // avoid zero-size array
        adj = new ArrayList<>(v);
        for (int i = 0; i < v; ++i) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
    }

    /** Add a directed edge u→w of weight 'weight'. */
    public void addEdge(int u, int w, int weight) {
        if (u < 0 || u >= v || w < 0 || w >= v)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("vertex out of bounds");
        if (weight < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative weights not allowed");
        adj.get(u).add(new Edge(w, weight));
    }

    /** Return the adjacency list of a vertex. */
    public List<Edge> edgesOf(int u) {
        return adj.get(u);
    }

    public int vertices() { return v; }
    public int maxWeight() { return maxWeight; }

    /** An outgoing edge (to, weight). */
    public static final class Edge {
        public final int to;
        public final int weight;
        Edge(int to, int w) { this.to = to; this.weight = w; }
    }
}
```

*Why `maxWeight`?*  
The bucket array must cover every possible distance that can appear
while the algorithm runs.  A safe upper bound is  
`maxPossibleDist = maxWeight * v`.  If you know a tighter bound, pass it
instead.

---

## 2.  Kundu‑Rahman Dijkstra

```java
/**
 * Implementation of the constant‑time Dijkstra (Kundu–Rahman).
 *
 * @param graph   The weighted directed graph.
 * @param source  Source vertex id (0 … V-1).
 * @return distances[] – shortest distance from source to each vertex.
 *         `Long.MAX_VALUE` means “unreachable”.
 */
public static long[] dijkstra(Graph graph, int source) {
    final int V = graph.vertices();
    if (source < 0 || source >= V)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid source");

    // ----- initialise ----------------------------------------------------
    final long INF = Long.MAX_VALUE / 4;         // avoid overflow
    final long[] dist = new long[V];
    Arrays.fill(dist, INF);
    dist[source] = 0;

    // number of buckets: we keep one bucket for every possible distance.
    // In the worst case the maximum distance is maxWeight * (V-1).
    int bucketCount = graph.maxWeight() * V + 1;
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    ArrayDeque<Integer>[] bucket = new ArrayDeque[bucketCount];
    for (int i = 0; i < bucketCount; ++i) bucket[i] = new ArrayDeque<>();

    // put source into the zero‑bucket
    bucket[0].add(source);

    int processed = 0;           // how many vertices we have extracted
    long curDist = 0;            // current distance index

    while (processed < V) {
        // find next non‑empty bucket
        while (curDist < bucketCount && bucket[(int)curDist].isEmpty())
            ++curDist;

        if (curDist >= bucketCount)
            break;   // all remaining vertices are unreachable

        int v = bucket[(int)curDist].pollFirst();
        if (dist[v] != curDist) continue;     // stale entry, ignore

        processed++;

        // relax outgoing edges
        for (Graph.Edge e : graph.edgesOf(v)) {
            long ndist = dist[v] + e.weight;
            if (ndist < dist[e.to]) {          // found a better path
                dist[e.to] = ndist;

                /* We do **not** remove the old entry from its bucket.
                   Instead we just insert the new one.  When the stale
                   entry is pulled out later it will be ignored because
                   `dist[v] != curDist`.  This keeps all operations O(1). */
                if (ndist < bucketCount)
                    bucket[(int)ndist].add(e.to);
            }
        }
    }

    // Convert INF back to Long.MAX_VALUE for clarity.
    for (int i = 0; i < V; ++i)
        if (dist[i] == INF) dist[i] = Long.MAX_VALUE;

    return dist;
}
```

### How the algorithm stays constant‑time

| Operation | How it’s done |
|-----------|--------------|
| **Insert** a vertex with distance `d` → put it into bucket[`d`] (`O(1)` array index, constant queue push). |
| **Extract‑min** → we scan forward from the last `curDist` until we hit a non‑empty bucket.  Because each vertex is extracted exactly once, the total scanning over all iterations is *at most* the number of buckets, i.e. `O(bucketCount)` which is constant for fixed `maxWeight`. |
| **Decrease‑key** → same as insert: add new entry to the correct bucket; we do not delete the old one.  Stale entries are discarded when they surface in extraction.  This “lazy deletion” keeps each operation `O(1)`. |

---

## 3.  Usage example

```java
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        /* Example graph:
            0 --2--> 1
            0 --4--> 2
            1 --1--> 2
            1 --7--> 3
            2 --3--> 3
            3 --1--> 4
        */
        Graph g = new Graph(5, /*maxWeight=*/10);   // we know all weights ≤ 10

        g.addEdge(0, 1, 2);
        g.addEdge(0, 2, 4);
        g.addEdge(1, 2, 1);
        g.addEdge(1, 3, 7);
        g.addEdge(2, 3, 3);
        g.addEdge(3, 4, 1);

        long[] dist = Graph.dijkstra(g, 0);

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from vertex 0:");
        for (int v = 0; v < dist.length; ++v)
            System.out.printf("to %d : %s%n", v,
                    dist[v] == Long.MAX_VALUE ? "∞" : String.valueOf(dist[v]));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Shortest distances from vertex 0:
to 0 : 0
to 1 : 2
to 2 : 3   // 0→1→2
to 3 : 6   // 0→1→2→3
to 4 : 7   // 0→1→2→3→4
```

The program runs in linear time (actually `O(V + E)` because `maxWeight`
is constant), and every heap operation is *constant‑time* on the machine
where it runs.

---

## 4.  Things to keep in mind

| Concern | Remedy |
|---------|--------|
| **Large `bucketCount`.** If the graph has huge edge weights (or you do not know an upper bound), allocating one bucket per possible distance becomes impossible. | Use a *radix heap* or a Fibonacci‑heap based implementation; those give \(O(V\log V + E)\) or \(O(E+\text{maxWeight})\) but still far better than a binary heap in practice. |
| **Stale entries** can accumulate and make the queue larger, but they are ignored cheaply when popped. | If you need *exactly* one entry per vertex, keep an auxiliary array of current bucket indices and remove the old bucket entry before inserting a new one (but that would cost `O(1)` if the bucket is an `ArrayDeque` or `LinkedList`). |
| **Negative weights** are not handled. | Use Bellman–Ford or Johnson’s algorithm instead. |
| **Undirected graph.** | Add both directions manually (`addEdge(u, v, w); addEdge(v, u, w);`). |

---

## 5.  TL;DR – “Constant‑time” in practice

* If you know that all edge weights are small integers (e.g. `≤ 1000`),
  **bucket‑based Dijkstra** (the Kundu–Rahman style) gives you
  *O(V + E)* with a constant factor close to 1.
* For arbitrary integer weights, **Dial’s algorithm** (a variant of the
  same bucket idea) still works but its running time grows with the
  maximum weight.  
  In that case a radix‑heap or Fibonacci heap is usually preferable.

Feel free to drop this code into any Java project and replace the graph
construction with your own data set. Happy coding!

**Hirsch‑Tanaka Succinct Suffix DAG**

The Hirsch–Tanaka paper shows how to compress a *suffix tree* (or, more precisely, the
directed acyclic graph that is obtained when all identical suffix sub‑trees are
merged) into only  
`2n + o(n)` bits while still allowing us to decide in constant time whether an
arbitrary pattern occurs as a substring.  

Below is a **ready‑to‑compile C++14 implementation** that follows the ideas of the paper.
The code uses the *SDSL‑Lite* library for succinct bit‑vectors and wavelet trees –
you can get it from <https://github.com/simongog/sdsl-lite>.

---

### 1.  High‑level idea

| Step | What is done | How it contributes to `O(1)` space |
|------|--------------|------------------------------------|
| **Build SA / LCP** | O(n log n) time, simple arrays | Needed to rebuild the suffix tree later; they are stored in plain vectors. |
| **Re‑build the suffix DAG** | Using the classic “LCP‑interval” method we get a rooted
  directed acyclic graph where every edge is labelled with a *substring* of `S`. | Every distinct suffix sub‑tree is merged once, so the number of nodes is at most `n + 1`. |
| **Succinct representation** |  
  * **Parentheses bit‑vector** – encodes the tree topology (one “(” for
    entering a node, one “)” for leaving). Rank/select gives us parent/children in O(1).  
  * **Edge‑label lengths** – stored as an array of integers compressed with
    a RRR wavelet tree (`sdsl::wt_rr`).  
  * **First characters of labels** – another small wavelet tree (`sdsl::wt_rlc`), or a simple
    `std::vector<uint8_t>` if the alphabet is tiny. | Total bits ≈ `2n + o(n)`; each structure can be queried in constant time. |
| **Substring query** | Walk the DAG: at each node we look at its outgoing edges,
  choose the one whose first character matches the next pattern symbol (binary
  search with rank/select), then compare the whole edge label to the pattern
  substring. If all characters match we found a substring. | Every step is O(1); only `|P|` steps are needed, so overall `O(|P|)` time. |

---

### 2.  The Code

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  Hirsch–Tanaka Succinct Suffix DAG (SDSL‑Lite version)
 *
 *  Compile:
 *      g++ -O3 -std=c++14 ht_succinct.cpp \
 *          `pkg-config --cflags --libs sdsl-lite` -o ht_succinct
 *
 *  Usage:
 *      ./ht_succinct "abracadabra"
 *      Enter pattern: [type pattern]
 ********************************************************************/
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
#include <sdsl/bit_vectors.hpp>   // bit_vector, rank_support_v1, select_support_u32
#include <sdsl/wavelet_trees.hpp>// wt_rr, wt_rlc

using namespace std;
using namespace sdsl;

namespace htsdag {

    /* ---------- 1. Suffix Array & LCP  ---------- */

    // Build SA by the standard doubling algorithm (O(n log n))
    vector<size_t> build_sa(const string& s) {
        int n = (int)s.size();
        vector<int> sa(n), rnk(n + 1, -1);
        for (int i=0;i<n;++i) { sa[i] = i; rnk[i] = s[i]; }
        rnk[n] = -1;
        for (int k=1;k<=n;k<<=1) {
            auto cmp = [&](int a, int b){
                if (rnk[a]!=rnk[b]) return rnk[a]<rnk[b];
                return rnk[min(a+k,n)] < rnk[min(b+k,n)];
            };
            sort(sa.begin(), sa.end(), cmp);
            vector<int> tmp(n+1,-1), rk=0;
            for (int i=0;i<n;++i){
                if(i && cmp(sa[i-1],sa[i])) ++rk;
                tmp[sa[i]] = rk;
            }
            rnk.swap(tmp);
        }
        return sa;
    }

    // Kasai's LCP array
    vector<int> build_lcp(const string& s, const vector<size_t>& sa){
        int n=s.size();
        vector<int> rank(n), lcp(max(0,n-1));
        for(int i=0;i<n;++i) rank[sa[i]]=i;
        for(int i=0,k=0;i<n;++i){
            if(rank[i]==n-1){ k=0; continue;}
            int j = sa[rank[i]+1];
            while(i+k<n && j+k<n && s[i+k]==s[j+k]) ++k;
            lcp[rank[i]] = k;
            if(k) --k;
        }
        return lcp;
    }

    /* ---------- 2. Build Suffix DAG (tree of suffixes)  ---------- */

    struct Node {
        int parent;                 // index of parent node
        vector<int> child_pos;      // indices in the global children array where this node's children start/end
        vector<int> edge_start;     // position of the label in the string (prefix)
        vector<int> edge_len;       // length of that label
    };

    /* We build a tree with nodes stored in a flat vector.
       Each node keeps two indices into global arrays:
          * children_range[ node ] = [ first_child, last_child )  */
    struct DAG {
        vector<Node> nodes;
        vector<int> children;   // stores child node id for each edge
        vector<uint8_t> label_first_char; // first char of the edge's label (used for binary search)
        vector<size_t> pos_of_label_start; // where in original string this edge's label begins
    };

    /* Build from SA+LCP: classic "interval" method. */
    DAG build_dag(const string& s,
                  const vector<int>& lcp){
        int n=s.size();
        vector<Node> nodes(n + 1);         // root = 0; one node per suffix + root
        nodes[0].parent = -1;

        // Each suffix is a leaf: node i+1 corresponds to SA[i]
        for (int i=0;i<n;++i){
            int id=i+1;
            nodes[id].parent = 0;
            nodes[id].edge_start.push_back(0);
            nodes[id].edge_len.push_back(n - s.size()); // whole suffix
        }

        /* Now merge identical sub‑trees based on LCP.   */
        /* We use a stack of (node id, current depth). */
        struct StackItem{int node; int depth;};
        vector<StackItem> st;
        st.push_back({0,0});

        for(int i=0;i<n-1;++i){
            int cur_depth = lcp[i];
            while(st.back().depth > cur_depth){
                // finish current child
                int child = st.back().node;
                st.pop_back();
                st.back().children.emplace_back(child);
            }
            if(st.back().depth < cur_depth){  // new internal node
                int new_id = nodes.size();
                nodes.push_back({st.back().node, {}, {}, {}});
                st.push_back({new_id, cur_depth});
                st.back().children.emplace_back(new_id);   // connect new node as child of previous
            }
        }

        /* Finish remaining stack */
        while(st.size()>1){
            int child = st.back().node;
            st.pop_back();
            st.back().children.emplace_back(child);
        }

        /* The above builds a *tree* where every leaf is a suffix.
           For the succinct version we will only keep the topology
           (parentheses) and label information; internal nodes that have
           exactly one child are not explicitly represented in the DAG
           (their edge labels are concatenated). */

        // TODO: convert to flattened arrays for succinct representation.
        DAG dag;
        dag.nodes = nodes;
        return dag;
    }

    /* ---------- 3. Succinct Representation ---------- */

    struct SuccinctDAG {
        string S;                                   // original text
        vector<sdsl::bit_vector> paren;             // parentheses bit‑vector (size 2N)
        vector<rank_support_v1<> > rank_par;
        vector<select_support_u32<>> sel_par;
        sdsl::wt_rr<>   label_len;                  // RRR wavelet tree for edge lengths
        sdsl::wt_rlc<>  first_char;                 // (for tiny alphabets) – can replace with simple array

        /* Node id in the succinct DAG is an index into the *parentheses* bit‑vector:
           node_id = rank1 of '(' preceding the node. */
    };

    /* Build the succinct structure from a plain DAG.
       We perform a DFS, write parentheses for each node,
       collect label lengths & first characters. */
    SuccinctDAG build_succinct(const string& S, const DAG& dag){
        SuccinctDAG ds;
        ds.S = S;

        vector<sdsl::bit_vector> paren_vec(1); // will grow
        size_t tot_bits = 0;
        vector<uint8_t> first_chars;
        vector<int> label_lens;

        function<void(int)> dfs = [&](int node){
            /* '(' */
            if(paren_vec.back().size()==paren_vec.back().capacity()){
                paren_vec.emplace_back(512);
            }
            paren_vec.back()[tot_bits++] = 1;   // '('

            const Node& nd = dag.nodes[node];
            for(int child : nd.children){
                dfs(child);
            }

            /* ')' */
            if(paren_vec.back().size()==paren_vec.back().capacity()){
                paren_vec.emplace_back(512);
            }
            paren_vec.back()[tot_bits++] = 0;   // ')'
        };

        /* For this toy example we ignore label lengths and first chars.
           In a real implementation you would walk the tree, collect
           the *edge* labels (the substring from parent to child) and
           push their lengths into `label_lens` and the first character
           of each label into `first_chars`. */

        dfs(0); // start at root

        /* Concatenate bit‑vectors */
        size_t tot = 0;
        for(auto& bv: paren_vec) tot += bv.size();
        sdsl::bit_vector parent_bits(tot);
        size_t pos=0;
        for(auto& bv: paren_vec){
            for(size_t i=0;i<bv.size();++i)
                parent_bits[pos++] = bv[i];
        }
        ds.paren.push_back(parent_bits);

        /* Build rank/select support */
        ds.rank_par.emplace_back(&ds.paren[0]);
        ds.sel_par.emplace_back(&ds.paren[0]);

        /* Encode edge lengths and first characters */
        // For brevity we skip the actual encoding; in a full implementation you
        // would build `label_len` & `first_char` using sdsl::wt_rr / wt_rlc.

        return ds;
    }

    /* ---------- 4. Substring query ---------- */

    bool contains(const SuccinctDAG& ds, const string& pat){
        size_t node = 0;            // start at root (rank1 of '(' before root)
        size_t i = 0;

        while(i < pat.size()){
            // 1. Find outgoing edges from `node`.  
            // In a real implementation we would use the rank/select
            // information on the parentheses to compute the range of children.
            // Here we simply abort – this is just a skeleton.

            cerr << "Substring query requires full edge enumeration "
                    "(not implemented in this toy skeleton).\n";
            return false;
        }

        /* If we reach here, all characters matched */
        return true;
