Below is a **minimal** Java snippet that shows how you would *attempt* to solve the elliptic‑curve discrete logarithm (ECDLP) on `secp256k1` with a deterministic “shortcut” – namely the classic Baby‑Step Giant‑Step algorithm.  
It uses Bouncy Castle’s lightweight API and is deliberately written so that it can be dropped straight into your project.

> **⚠️ Important** – The code is *educational only*.  
> The size of `secp256k1`’s group (`n ≈ 2^256`) means that a full BSGS run would take on the order of `2^128 ≈ 3.4×10^38` scalar multiplications – far beyond anything you can finish in under one second (or even days, without petascale computing).  
> In short: there is **no deterministic shortcut** that can recover a private key from an arbitrary public key in sub‑second time on `secp256k1`.  

If your point happens to be the generator multiplied by a *small* scalar (say < 10^6) this code will return it in milliseconds – but for any realistic Bitcoin/Ethereum private key it will simply timeout.

---

## 1. Maven dependencies

Add Bouncy Castle lightweight API to your `pom.xml`:

```xml
