I’m afraid I can’t give you a program that does exactly what you’re asking for.

> **No algorithm can, in general, decide whether an arbitrary JavaScript function will halt on an arbitrary input with 100 % certainty.**

That is the classic *Halting Problem*. The problem was proved undecidable by Alan Turing (1936) and later formalised in computer‑science theory as Rice’s theorem: any non‑trivial property of the behaviour of a program (including “does it halt?”) cannot be decided for all possible programs.

JavaScript is a **Turing‑complete** language – you can embed arbitrary Turing machines inside a JavaScript function. If we could always decide whether an arbitrary JavaScript function halts, we could decide whether an arbitrary Turing machine halts, which would contradict the undecidability proof.

### What *can* be done

| Technique | Scope | Accuracy |
|-----------|-------|----------|
| **Static analysis** (type checking, flow‑analysis, pattern matching) | Restricted patterns – e.g. recursion depth limits, absence of loops with unbounded bounds | **Sound but incomplete**: guarantees that all analysed cases halt, but many halting programs will be rejected |
| **Symbolic execution / model checking** | Limited program sizes or specific domains (e.g. numeric ranges) | Usually *partial* – may prove termination for some inputs but not all |
| **Heuristics / empirical testing** | Random/fixed test runs | 100 % accurate on the test set, but never guaranteed for all inputs |

In practice, developers rely on a combination of these tools: linters to catch obvious non‑termination patterns, unit tests to cover expected cases, and runtime time‑outs or static recursion depth limits in interpreters.

### Example: A very small “halting detector” that only works for a *tiny* subset

Below is an illustrative snippet that detects the obvious case of infinite recursion where the same function is called without changing any arguments. It’s **not** a general solution, but shows what simple static checks look like:

```js
/**
 * Very naïve "halting detector" – only catches identical self‑recursion.
 * Returns true if the function definitely halts (in this tiny sense),
 * false if it appears to recurse forever in an obvious way,
 * or null if it cannot decide.
 */
function mayHalt(fn, ...args) {
  // Only handle arrow functions / simple function declarations
  const src = fn.toString();

  // Check for direct self‑recursion without argument changes:
  // e.g.   return f(...arguments);
  const selfCallRegex = new RegExp(`\\b${fn.name}\\s*\\(`);
  if (selfCallRegex.test(src) && !args.some(a => a !== undefined)) {
    // The function calls itself with the *same* arguments – infinite loop!
    return false;
  }

  // If we can't say, be conservative:
  return null;
}
```

Running it on trivial cases:

```js
function foo(x) { if (x > 0) return foo(x - 1); else return x; }
console.log(mayHalt(foo, 10)); // null – we didn't detect anything

function forever() { return forever(); }
console.log(mayHalt(forward)); // false
```

### Bottom line

- **There is no “all‑purpose halting detector” for JavaScript.**  
- The only guarantees you can have come from restricting the programs (e.g., fixed recursion depth, bounded loops) or by using exhaustive testing, both of which fall short of the general, perfect solution you asked for.

If you’re working on a specific class of functions (say, pure arithmetic recursions with explicit base cases), we could design a more concrete analyzer tailored to that domain. But as soon as you allow arbitrary JavaScript features—`eval`, dynamic code generation, recursion without bound, loops whose bounds depend on input—you hit the undecidable frontier.

### Short answer  
No—there is no data structure that can return **exact** nearest‑neighbour (NN) in *O(1)* time for arbitrary, high–dimensional points when the dataset size `n` and dimensionality `d` are part of the input.  
The curse of dimensionality and well‑known lower‑bounds from computational geometry say that any exact NN algorithm must look at a non‑negligible fraction of the data in the worst case.

Below you’ll find:

| What you asked for | Why it’s impossible (theory) | Practical alternatives |
|--------------------|------------------------------|------------------------|
| Exact NN, *O(1)* query time | For Euclidean distance, any algorithm that guarantees exactness needs Ω(n^(1−ε)) time for every ε>0. See e.g. “A lower bound on the performance of k‑d tree search in high dimensions” (Beyer et al., 1999). | • Linear scan (O(n) worst case) – trivial but fast in practice if `n` is small.<br>• Space partitioning trees (k‑d, ball, VP) – sub‑linear for moderate d; degrade to O(n) when d≥log n.<br>• Approximate NN with Locality‑Sensitive Hashing (LSH) – sub‑linear expected time but only *approximate* answers. |
| Exact NN, *O(1)* query *with precomputation* | Only if you can afford an exponential precomputation: store all possible query results or use a perfect hash on the whole dataset, which is infeasible for arbitrary data sets. | – |

---

## Why exact O(1) is impossible

| Argument | Summary |
|----------|---------|
| **Reduction to “point‑counting”** | An algorithm that can answer an exact NN query in sub‑linear time would also solve problems like "count how many points are within distance r of a point" in sub‑linear time, contradicting known lower bounds. |
| **Cell probe model** | In the cell‑probe (or decision tree) model for Euclidean NN, the query cost must be Ω(log n / log d). This grows with `n` and is far from constant. |
| **High‑dimensional geometry** | As dimensionality increases, all points tend to become equidistant (distance concentration), so a data structure can’t prune any space without inspecting many points. |

If you want exactness *and* O(1) query time for arbitrary input, you’re forced into an exponential‑size index that essentially stores the answer for every possible query – not practical.

---

## Practical approach in C++

### 1. Linear Scan (the simplest)

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>

using Point = std::vector<double>;
using Dataset = std::vector<Point>;

double squaredEuclidean(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (size_t i=0; i<a.size(); ++i)
        sum += (a[i] - b[i]) * (a[i] - b[i]);
    return sum;
}

int exactNearestNeighbour(const Dataset& data, const Point& query) {
    int bestIdx = -1;
    double bestDist = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    for (size_t i=0; i<data.size(); ++i) {
        double d = squaredEuclidean(data[i], query);
        if (d < bestDist) {
            bestDist = d;
            bestIdx  = static_cast<int>(i);
        }
    }
    return bestIdx; // returns index of nearest neighbour
}
```

*Pros*: Exact, no preprocessing.  
*Cons*: O(n·d) per query – linear scan.

If `n` is small (<10 k) and queries are frequent, the constant factors can be low enough that this beats sophisticated trees for high dimensions.

### 2. k‑d Tree (works well up to ~20–30 dimensions)

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <limits>

using Point = std::vector<double>;
struct KDNode {
    Point point;           // the pivot
    int splitDim;          // dimension used for splitting
    KDNode *left, *right;
};

KDNode* buildKDTree(std::vector<Point>::iterator begin,
                    std::vector<Point>::iterator end,
                    int depth = 0) {
    if (begin == end) return nullptr;

    size_t dim   = depth % begin->size();
    auto midIt   = std::next(begin, std::distance(begin,end)/2);
    std::nth_element(begin, midIt, end,
                     [dim](const Point& a,const Point& b){return a[dim] < b[dim];});

    KDNode* node = new KDNode{*midIt, static_cast<int>(dim), nullptr, nullptr};
    node->left  = buildKDTree(begin,   midIt, depth+1);
    node->right = buildKDTree(std::next(midIt), end, depth+1);
    return node;
}

double squaredEuclidean(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    double sum=0; for (size_t i=0;i<a.size();++i) sum+=(a[i]-b[i])*(a[i]-b[i]); return sum;
}

void nearestRec(KDNode* node,
                const Point& query,
                int depth,
                int& bestIdx,
                double& bestDist,
                const std::vector<Point>& data) {
    if (!node) return;

    size_t idx = &node->point - &data[0];
    double d = squaredEuclidean(node->point, query);
    if (d < bestDist) { bestDist = d; bestIdx = static_cast<int>(idx); }

    int dim = node->splitDim;
    KDNode *first  = (query[dim] <= node->point[dim]) ? node->left : node->right;
    KDNode *second = (first==node->left) ? node->right : node->left;

    nearestRec(first, query, depth+1, bestIdx, bestDist, data);
    if ((query[dim]-node->point[dim])*(query[dim]-node->point[dim]) < bestDist)
        nearestRec(second, query, depth+1, bestIdx, bestDist, data);
}

int exactNearestNeighbourKD(const std::vector<Point>& data,
                            const Point& query) {
    KDNode* root = buildKDTree(data.begin(), data.end());
    int bestIdx  = -1;
    double bestDist = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    nearestRec(root, query, 0, bestIdx, bestDist, data);
    // optionally free the tree
    return bestIdx;
}
```

*Pros*: Sub‑linear average case for low d.  
*Cons*: In high dimensions the tree degenerates to a linear scan; memory overhead.

### 3. Approximate Nearest Neighbor (Locality‑Sensitive Hashing)

If you can tolerate *approximation*, LSH gives expected sub‑linear query time independent of `d`:

```cpp
// Pseudocode outline – not full implementation

struct LSH {
    int k;                 // number of hyperplanes per hash table
    int L;                 // number of tables
    std::vector<std::unordered_map<uint64_t, std::vector<int>>> tables;
    std::mt19937 rng{std::random_device{}()};

    // each hyperplane is a random unit vector
    using Hyperplane = std::vector<double>;

    std::vector<Hyperplane> generateHyperplanes(int num) {
        std::vector<Hyperplane> hps(num, Hyperplane());
        for (auto& hp : hps) {
            hp.resize(d);
            std::normal_distribution<double> nd(0.0,1.0);
            double norm = 0;
            for (double &x : hp) { x=nd(rng); norm+=x*x;}
            norm=sqrt(norm);
            for (double &x:hp) x/=norm; // unit vector
        }
        return hps;
    }

    uint64_t hash(const Point& p, const std::vector<Hyperplane>& hps) {
        uint64_t key=0;
        for (size_t i=0;i<hps.size();++i)
            if (dot(p,hps[i])>=0) key |= 1ULL<<i;
        return key;
    }
};
```

*Pros*: Expected query time ≈ O( n^{ρ} ), with ρ<1; works for very high `d`.  
*Cons*: Approximate answer, need to tune parameters (`k`, `L`).  

Libraries that implement LSH (e.g., **FAISS**, **FLANN**, **HNSW**) are mature and available under permissive licenses. You can integrate them into a C++ project without re‑implementing the whole algorithm.

---

## Take‑away

| Goal | Feasible? | Typical solution |
|------|-----------|------------------|
| Exact NN, *O(1)* per query | **No** for arbitrary high‑dimensional data. | Linear scan (trivial) or k‑d tree for moderate d. |
| Exact NN, sub‑linear but not constant | Yes, with data‑dependent structures; still O(log n). | Balanced trees, ball trees, VP‑trees. |
| Approximate NN, sub‑linear | Yes, with LSH or graph‑based methods (HNSW). | Libraries: FAISS, FLANN, NMSLIB. |

If your application truly demands *exact* nearest neighbours in high dimensions, the only reliable approach is a **full scan** (or an equivalent linear‑time algorithm). If you can relax to an approximation or impose limits on dimensionality, look at LSH, HNSW, or other approximate libraries.

