Below is a *conversation‑style* walk‑through of the design and an actual implementation that you can drop straight into your code base.  
The key take‑away: **A deterministic PRNG with only O(1) state cannot have a mathematically infinite period** – there are only finitely many possible states, so once the generator has exhausted them it must cycle back to the start.  What we can do is push that cycle length to an astronomically large number and make collisions practically impossible by using a cryptographic hash of a counter.

---

## 1. Theoretical limits

| State size (bits) | Max possible distinct states |
|-------------------|-----------------------------|
| 32‑bit            | 2³² ≈ 4 G                   |
| 64‑bit            | 2⁶⁴ ≈ 18 E18                |
| 128‑bit           | 3.4×10³⁸                     |

With a single counter of N bits you can at most generate `2^N` distinct outputs before the counter wraps around – that is your absolute theoretical upper bound.

If we take `N = 64`, that’s ~1.8 × 10¹⁹ different numbers.  
Even if every number were generated in *one nanosecond*, it would still take more than 600 years to finish the cycle.

So, for all practical purposes the sequence will never repeat (unless you hit an actual hash collision, which is astronomically unlikely).  That’s why we’ll call it a “guaranteed infinite period” **in practice**.

---

## 2. Design

We keep only one field:

```text
counter : u64   // 0 .. 2^64-1  (wraps after that)
```

The generator outputs the *hash* of the counter combined with a secret “salt”.  
That salt can be user‑supplied, or it can be derived from the process start time – any value that is not reset between calls.

Because we never modify the state other than incrementing `counter`, the implementation is O(1) in both space and time per call.

### Hash function

A cryptographic hash guarantees a low probability of collision.  
We’ll use a fast, well‑known hash from the public domain – **xxHash64** – because it gives us:

* 64‑bit output (fits into a `u64`)
* very high throughput (≈ 1 cycle per byte on modern CPUs)
* deterministic and independent of input size

If you’re in an environment where xxHash is not available, you can swap it out for any other fast hash – e.g., Murmur3 128‑bit truncated to 64 bits.

---

## 3. Implementation (Rust)

> **Why Rust?**  
> Rust gives us zero‑cost abstraction and guarantees about safety.  
> If you prefer C/C++/Python, the same logic applies – only the syntax changes.

```rust
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// lib.rs
// ------------------------------------------------------------
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

/// A stateless (O(1) state) PRNG with a practically infinite period.
///
/// The generator internally keeps a single 64‑bit counter that is
/// incremented on every call.  
/// Each output value is the xxHash64 of the current counter *plus* a
/// per‑process salt that never changes during the lifetime of the process.
/// 
/// Because xxHash64 has a collision probability of 2⁻¹²⁸ (for a 128‑bit
/// variant) and we truncate to 64 bits, collisions are astronomically
/// unlikely. The only theoretical repeat occurs when `counter` wraps,
/// i.e. after ~1.8×10¹⁹ calls.
pub struct InfinitePRNG {
    counter: AtomicU64,
    salt: u64,
}

impl InfinitePRNG {
    /// Create a new PRNG instance.
    ///
    /// If you pass `None`, the generator will pick an internal salt
    /// based on the process start time (seconds + nanoseconds).
    pub fn new(salt: Option<u64>) -> Self {
        let default_salt = {
            // use system uptime in nanoseconds as a seed
            let now = SystemTime::now()
                .duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)
                .expect("Clock moved backwards");
            now.as_secs() ^ now.subsec_nanos() as u64
        };
        Self {
            counter: AtomicU64::new(0),
            salt: salt.unwrap_or(default_salt),
        }
    }

    /// Produce the next 64‑bit pseudo‑random value.
    ///
    /// # Complexity
    ///
    /// *Time*: `O(1)` – a few integer ops + one hash call.  
    /// *Space*: `O(1)` – only two 64‑bit integers are stored.
    pub fn next_u64(&self) -> u64 {
        let cnt = self.counter.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        // Combine counter and salt; the xor is trivial
        let input = cnt ^ self.salt;
        // xxHash64 of the 8‑byte integer
        self.hash_uint64(input)
    }

    /// Return a random `f32` in the range [0.0, 1.0).
    pub fn next_f32(&self) -> f32 {
        let bits = self.next_u64() >> 9; // keep 23‑bit mantissa
        const INV: f32 = 1.0 / (1u32 << 23) as f32;
        bits as f32 * INV
    }

    /// Return a random `f64` in the range [0.0, 1.0).
    pub fn next_f64(&self) -> f64 {
        let bits = self.next_u64() >> 11; // keep 53‑bit mantissa
        const INV: f64 = 1.0 / (1u64 << 53) as f64;
        bits as f64 * INV
    }

    /// Hash an arbitrary u64 value with xxHash64.
    fn hash_uint64(&self, input: u64) -> u64 {
        // The Rust crate `twox-hash` implements xxhash64.
        use twox_hash::XxHash64;
        let mut hasher = XxHash64::with_seed(0);
        hasher.write_u64(input);
        hasher.finish()
    }
}
```

