## 1. Why a 32‑bit key is **not** “unbreakable”

| Key size | Key space   | Time to brute force (≈ 2³⁰ ≈ 10⁹ ops/second) |
|----------|-------------|----------------------------------------------|
| 32 bits  | 4 294 967 296 possibilities | ~13 minutes          |
| 128 bits | 3.4 × 10³⁸      | practically impossible   |

A **32‑bit key** can be tried in less than a few hours on a single CPU, so no cryptographic routine (AES or otherwise) using that size can claim to be unbreakable.  
If you truly want “unbreakability”, use at least 128‑bit keys with AES‑128, or even larger if you have the performance budget.

