## 7. Summary

* **Single leader for writes** – all `Write` RPCs are accepted only by the current leader.
* **Linearizable reads** – clients read from the leader; if they happen to hit a follower, it forwards them to the leader automatically.
* **Clear failure handling** – heartbeat timeouts → candidate election → new leader; stale logs reject writes via term check.

Feel free to adapt or expand this skeleton for your production use—add persistent storage, better RPC error handling, dynamic cluster membership, etc. Happy coding!

## Overview  

| Goal | Technique |
|------|-----------|
| **At‑least‑once delivery** | Use Kafka (or any broker) that guarantees every message is delivered at least once. |
| **Effectively‑once processing** | Make the consumer *idempotent*: it will ignore duplicates, so even if a message is redelivered it won’t change the system state twice. |
| **Clear failure handling** | Commit an offset **only after** the business work and the idempotency marker are persisted. If anything fails we never commit → automatic retry or DL‑Q. |

Below is a minimal, self‑contained Spring‑Boot + Kafka implementation that demonstrates these ideas.

> *Why Spring‑Kafka?*  
> It gives us a clean abstraction for producer/consumer config, manual offset commits, and built‑in DL‑Q support. The same logic would work in plain Java, .NET, Go, etc., with the corresponding client libraries.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1. Producer – idempotent at the broker

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/kafka/producer/KafkaProducerConfig.java
package com.example.kafka.producer;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.*;

@Configuration
public class KafkaProducerConfig {

    @Bean
    public ProducerFactory<String, String> producerFactory() {
        Map<String,Object> props = new HashMap<>();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "localhost:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
                org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer.class);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
                org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer.class);

        /* -----------  Idempotent producer  ----------------- */
        // Guarantees that a message is written once even if the
        // client retransmits it because of network failure.
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, true);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");   // all replicas must ack
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);

        return new DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<>(props);
    }

    @Bean
    public KafkaTemplate<String,String> kafkaTemplate() {
        return new KafkaTemplate<>(producerFactory());
    }
}
```

**How it works**

* `enable.idempotence=true` guarantees that if the producer retries a send, the broker will recognise the duplicate (by *ProducerId+SequenceNumber*) and only keep one copy.  
* `acks=all` makes sure every replica has stored the message before the send is considered successful.

