## 3. How it all fits together

| Step | What happens | Why it guarantees *effectively once* |
|------|--------------|-------------------------------------|
| **Producer** sends message with a unique key (e.g. UUID). | Producer is idempotent → broker keeps only one copy even if the send is retried. | Guarantees no duplicate *at the source*. |
| **Consumer receives message**. The `@KafkaListener` runs once per record in order. | Consumer checks Redis – if the key already exists it skips processing. | Dedup store ensures that even if a message is redelivered we never apply its side‑effects twice. |
| **Processing succeeds**. We first persist the business change, then write the key into Redis and finally ack the offset. | Offset is committed *only after* both writes succeeded. | If any step fails the offset stays uncommitted → Kafka will re‑deliver the message. |
| **Processing fails**. The exception bubbles up; the container does **not** acknowledge. | Kafka retries after a timeout. Duplicate message may arrive again, but Redis prevents double work. | At‑least‑once delivery is preserved; idempotency makes it effectively once. |

If you prefer to send failed messages to a *dead‑letter* topic rather than retry forever, configure Spring‑Kafka’s DLQ support:

```yaml
spring.kafka.listener:
  ack-mode: manual_immediate
  max-attempts: 3
  backoff:
    delay: 1000
    multiplier: 2.0

# Auto‑DLQ (requires spring-kafka ≥ 2.6)
spring.kafka.template.default-topic: input-topic
spring.kafka.template.producer-transaction-id-prefix: tx-
```

The `max-attempts` setting tells the container to try the consumer logic a fixed number of times; after that it will forward the record to the DLQ.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4. Summary

* **At‑least‑once** – Kafka guarantees each message is delivered at least once.
* **Idempotent consumer** – Redis dedup store (or DB primary key) guarantees we ignore duplicates.
* **Clear failure handling** – Manual offset commits mean that *only successful* messages are acknowledged; failures trigger a retry or DL‑Q.  
The resulting system behaves as if each message was processed exactly once, even though the underlying infrastructure uses at‑least‑once semantics.

**Internal‑docs sample:  Raft‑style consensus under a partial synchrony model (Java)**  

> *This snippet is intentionally compact – it demonstrates the core time‑outs,
> election flow and failure handling of Raft while keeping the code readable for
> documentation purposes.*

```java
/********************************************************************
 *  Simplified Raft node implementation that runs with Java threads.
 *
 *  The example showcases:
 *    • Partial synchrony via fixed time‑out windows (election & heartbeat)
 *    • Failure handling: lost heartbeats → new election, stale votes
 *    • Minimal log replication logic (only the “append entries” RPC is stubbed)
 *
 *  It intentionally omits persistence, real networking and sophisticated
 *  log management – those are left for production code.
 ********************************************************************/

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Raft node state machine                                         */
enum State { FOLLOWER, CANDIDATE, LEADER }

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
class RaftNode {
    /* ---------- Node configuration ----------------------------------- */
    final int id;                               // unique node identifier
    final List<RaftNode> peers;                 // other nodes in the cluster

    /* ---------- Consensus variables --------------------------------- */
    volatile State state = State.FOLLOWER;
    volatile int currentTerm = 0;               // logical clock (term)
    volatile Integer votedFor = null;           // candidate that received our vote
    final List<String> log = new ArrayList<>(); // simple log of commands

    /* ---------- Time‑out parameters --------------------------------- */
    static final long ELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS = 1500;
    static final long HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS = 300;

    /* ---------- Threading helpers ----------------------------------- */
    final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler =
            Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();
    final Random rand = new Random();

    /* ---------- Constructor ------------------------------------------ */
    RaftNode(int id, List<RaftNode> peers) {
        this.id = id;
        this.peers = peers;
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Entry point – start the node’s timers                         */
    public void start() {
        scheduleElectionTimeout();
        System.out.printf("Node %d started as FOLLOWER\n", id);
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Election timeout handler – triggers election if no heartbeat  */
    private void scheduleElectionTimeout() {
        long delay = ELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS + rand.nextInt(500); // random jitter
        scheduler.schedule(this::onElectionTimeout, delay,
                          TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Election logic: becomes a candidate, votes for self, counts   */
    private void onElectionTimeout() {
        if (state == State.LEADER) return;          // already leading

        state = State.CANDIDATE;
        currentTerm++;
        votedFor = id;                              // vote for ourselves
        int lastTerm = currentTerm;

        System.out.printf("Node %d becomes CANDIDATE (term=%d)\n",
                          id, currentTerm);

        /* Count self‑vote */
        AtomicInteger votesGranted = new AtomicInteger(1);
        int majority = peers.size() / 2 + 1;         // strict majority

        /* Send RequestVote RPCs asynchronously */
        for (RaftNode peer : peers) {
            scheduler.execute(() -> {
                boolean voteGranted =
                        peer.handleRequestVote(lastTerm, id);

                if (voteGranted && votesGranted.incrementAndGet() >= majority
                        && state == State.CANDIDATE) {
                    becomeLeader();
                }
            });
        }

        /* If election fails, schedule another timeout */
        scheduler.schedule(() -> {
            if (state != State.LEADER)
                scheduleElectionTimeout();          // retry after failure
        }, ELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS * 2, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Vote request RPC – invoked by a candidate                    */
    private boolean handleRequestVote(int term, int candidateId) {
        if (term < currentTerm)
            return false;          // stale term

        /* Update own term and step down if necessary */
        if (term > currentTerm) {
            currentTerm = term;
            state = State.FOLLOWER;
            votedFor = null;
        }

        boolean voteGranted = (votedFor == null || votedFor == candidateId);
        if (voteGranted)
            votedFor = candidateId;

        System.out.printf("Node %d received RequestVote(term=%d) from %d "
                          + "→ %s\n", id, term, candidateId,
                          voteGranted ? "GRANTED" : "REJECTED");
        return voteGranted;
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Become a leader – start sending heartbeats                  */
    private void becomeLeader() {
        state = State.LEADER;
        System.out.printf("Node %d becomes LEADER (term=%d)\n", id, currentTerm);
        sendHeartbeats();                // first heartbeat immediately
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Heartbeat logic – leader sends AppendEntries RPCs            */
    private void sendHeartbeats() {
        if (state != State.LEADER) return;

        for (RaftNode peer : peers) {
            scheduler.execute(() -> peer.handleAppendEntries(
                    currentTerm, id, log.size() - 1,
                    Collections.emptyList(), /* empty entries = heartbeat */));
        }

        /* Schedule next heartbeat */
        scheduler.schedule(this::sendHeartbeats,
                           HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  AppendEntries RPC – invoked by the leader (heartbeat or log)*/
    private boolean handleAppendEntries(int term, int leaderId,
                                        int prevLogIndex,
                                        List<String> entries,
                                        int leaderCommit) {
        if (term < currentTerm)
            return false;          // stale leader

        /* Update own term and step down */
        if (term > currentTerm || state != State.FOLLOWER) {
            currentTerm = term;
            state = State.FOLLOWER;
            votedFor = null;
        }

        /* In a real implementation we would check prevLogIndex/term.
           Here we simply accept the heartbeat. */

        System.out.printf("Node %d received AppendEntries(term=%d) from %d\n",
                          id, term, leaderId);
        return true;   // success
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  Graceful shutdown – cancel timers                           */
    public void stop() {
        scheduler.shutdownNow();
    }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ */
/*  Demo harness – creates a small cluster and runs it             */
public class RaftDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        int nodeCount = 3;
        List<RaftNode> nodes = new ArrayList<>();

        /* Create nodes first, peers are added after all constructed   */
        for (int i = 0; i < nodeCount; i++) {
            nodes.add(new RaftNode(i + 1, Collections.emptyList()));
        }

        /* Now wire each node with the other nodes as its peers       */
        for (RaftNode node : nodes) {
            List<RaftNode> peerList = new ArrayList<>(nodes);
            peerList.remove(node);                // no self‑reference
            node.peers.addAll(peerList);
        }

        /* Start all nodes                                       */
        nodes.forEach(RaftNode::start);

        /* Let the cluster run for a while                       */
        Thread.sleep(12000);

        /* Simulate failure: stop one leader to trigger new election*/
        System.out.println("\n--- Simulating Leader Crash ---");
        RaftNode crashed = nodes.get(0);
        crashed.stop();
        System.out.printf("Node %d stopped.\n", crashed.id);

        /* Allow the remaining nodes to recover                  */
        Thread.sleep(10000);

        /* Shutdown everything                                 */
        nodes.forEach(RaftNode::stop);
    }
}
```

### How this example reflects a **partial synchrony** model

| Aspect | Implementation |
|--------|----------------|
| **Bounded communication delay** | Heartbeats are sent every `HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS`. If a follower does not receive any heartbeat within `ELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS` it assumes the leader is gone and starts an election. This timeout implicitly defines the *upper bound* on network latency for “normal” operation. |
| **Time‑outs** | Election time‑out (`ELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS`) is randomized to avoid collisions; heartbeats provide a steady stream of messages during normal execution. If delays exceed this bound, nodes will transition to `CANDIDATE` and initiate a new election – the classic Raft behaviour under partial synchrony. |
| **Failure handling** |  *Lost heartbeats* → new election;  *Stale votes* (term < current) are rejected;  a node that crashes is simply stopped in the demo, which triggers new elections on the remaining nodes. |

### What you can extend for production

1. **Persistence** – write `currentTerm`, `votedFor`, and log entries to stable storage.
2. **Real networking** – replace synchronous method calls with RPC (e.g., gRPC or Netty).
3. **Log replication** – implement the full AppendEntries logic, including term/prevLogIndex checks, conflict resolution, and commit index advancement.
4. **Leader election optimisations** – back‑off strategies, dynamic time‑outs, and log‑size based candidate filtering.

Feel free to use this skeleton as a starting point for your internal training materials or reference implementation.

Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to build a lease‑based lock that issues *fencing tokens* (a monotonically increasing counter) and cleanly handles all failure cases in Go.  
The implementation uses Redis because it already supports:

* a simple lease/expiry (`SET NX PX …`);
* an atomic `INCR` for the fencing token;
* Lua scripts that make “check‑then‑set” operations atomic.

If you prefer another backend (etcd, Zookeeper, DynamoDB …) the same ideas apply – just swap the Redis commands with the equivalent of your store.

---

## 1. Design overview

| Concept | How we implement it in Redis |
|---------|------------------------------|
| **Lease** | `SET key value NX PX <ttl>` – the lock is valid for `<ttl>` milliseconds unless the client explicitly deletes it first. |
| **Fencing token** | Each time a client attempts to acquire the lock we do an `INCR fence:<key>`.  The counter is global per lock name, so any new owner gets a strictly larger token than the previous one. |
| **Ownership value** | `client‑id:token` – this string is stored in the Redis key and used when releasing. Only the process that owns the lock can delete it. |
| **Clear failure handling** | * Acquire timeout → return an error that tells “could not obtain lease”.<br>* Release error → we still report an error but the client never holds a stale lock.<br>* Expiration (node crash, network partition) automatically frees the key after `<ttl>`. |

The client keeps two pieces of state:

```text
type Lock struct {
    name   string      // logical name of the lock (“resource‑1”)
    token  int64       // last successfully acquired fencing token
    value  string      // “client-id:token”, stored in Redis
}
```

---

## 2. The Go code

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"time"

	"github.com/gomodule/redigo/redis"
	"github.com/google/uuid"
)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lock implementation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Lock represents a lease‑based lock with fencing tokens.
type Lock struct {
	pool  *redis.Pool // connection pool to Redis
	name  string      // logical lock name
	ttl   time.Duration

	// State after a successful Acquire
	value string // “client-id:token”
	token int64  // last acquired token (used for debugging / fencing)
}

// NewLock creates a new Lock instance.
func NewLock(pool *redis.Pool, name string, ttl time.Duration) *Lock {
	return &Lock{pool: pool, name: name, ttl: ttl}
}

var (
	ErrNotAcquired = errors.New("lock not acquired")
)

// Acquire attempts to obtain the lease within ctx's deadline.
// It returns an error if we cannot get the lock or context is cancelled.
func (l *Lock) Acquire(ctx context.Context) error {
	conn := l.pool.Get()
	defer conn.Close()

	// --- Step 1: generate client id and fencing token --------------------------------
	clientID := uuid.NewString()

	// INCR fence:<name> is atomic, so we get a fresh global counter.
	token, err := redis.Int64(conn.Do("INCR", fmt.Sprintf("fence:%s", l.name)))
	if err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("INCR fence failed: %w", err)
	}
	val := fmt.Sprintf("%s:%d", clientID, token)

	// --- Step 2: try to acquire the lock ------------------------------------------------
	const luaAcquire = `
-- KEYS[1] – lock key (e.g. "lock:myresource")
-- ARGV[1] – value we want to set (client‑id:token)
-- ARGV[2] – ttl in ms
if redis.call('SET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], 'NX', 'PX', ARGV[2]) then
    return {1, ARGV[3]}          -- success, return token too for sanity check
else
    return {0}                   -- failed, key already exists
end`

	// Run Lua script with a timeout that is bounded by ctx.
	res, err := redis.Values(conn.Do("EVAL", luaAcquire,
		1, // number of KEYS
		l.name,     // KEYS[1]
		val,        // ARGV[1]
		int(l.ttl.Milliseconds()), // ARGV[2]
		token))                       // ARGV[3] – echo back so we can check it
	if err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("acquire script failed: %w", err)
	}

	// The Lua script returns a multi‑response:
	//   {1, token}  -> success
	//   {0}         -> failure
	switch len(res) {
	case 2:
		ok, _ := redis.Int(res[0], nil)
		sToken, _ := redis.Int64(res[1], nil)

		if ok == 1 && sToken == token {
			l.value = val
			l.token = token
			return nil // lock acquired
		}
	case 1:
		ok, _ := redis.Int(res[0], nil)
		if ok == 0 {
			return ErrNotAcquired
		}
	}

	return fmt.Errorf("unexpected script result: %v", res)
}

// Release deletes the key if we own it. If the lock had expired or was stolen,
// Redis will simply ignore the command – that is safe.
func (l *Lock) Release() error {
	if l.value == "" {
		// Nothing to release
		return nil
	}
	conn := l.pool.Get()
	defer conn.Close()

	const luaRelease = `
-- KEYS[1] – lock key
-- ARGV[1] – expected value
if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
    return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1])
else
    return 0   -- someone else owns it or already deleted
end`

	_, err := conn.Do("EVAL", luaRelease, 1, l.name, l.value)
	if err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("release script failed: %w", err)
	}
	l.value = "" // clear our state – we no longer own the lock
	return nil
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo / test harness
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

func main() {
	pool := &redis.Pool{
		Dial: func() (redis.Conn, error) { return redis.Dial("tcp", ":6379") },
	}

	lockName := "my‑resource"
	ttl := 5 * time.Second // lock will automatically expire after 5 s

	lock := NewLock(pool, lockName, ttl)

	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 3*time.Second)
	defer cancel()

	if err := lock.Acquire(ctx); err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Could not acquire the lock:", err)
		return
	}
	fmt.Printf("✅ Lock acquired! (token=%d)\n", lock.token)

	// Simulate doing some work while holding the lease.
	time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)

	if err := lock.Release(); err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Release error:", err)
	} else {
		fmt.Println("🔓 Lock released")
	}
}
```

### What happens when things go wrong?

| Situation | Resulting behaviour |
|-----------|---------------------|
| **Lock already held** (by another process) | `Acquire` returns `ErrNotAcquired`.  Caller can retry or back‑off. |
| **Context deadline exceeded** | The context is passed to the Redis driver, which aborts the request and we return the timeout error – no lock is obtained. |
| **Network partition / Redis failure** | All commands fail with a network error; caller sees it and can decide what to do (retry, log, fail‑fast). |
| **Process crashes while holding the lock** | The key expires after `<ttl>` milliseconds automatically.  Another process may acquire it later, getting a higher fencing token, guaranteeing that the new owner cannot mistakenly think it is still the old owner’s “old” lease. |

---

## 3. Using the fencing token

The client can store `lock.token` locally (or send it to other nodes) and check it before performing any operation that relies on the lock.  

```go
// Example: some critical section that must use the most recent state
func doCriticalThing(ctx context.Context, l *Lock, expectedToken int64) error {
    if l.token != expectedToken {
        return fmt.Errorf("fencing violation – our token %d is stale (expected %d)", l.token, expectedToken)
    }
    // … safe to proceed …
    return nil
}
```

Because the token only grows, a later owner can never see a lower value than an earlier one; this guarantees *process safety* even if locks are re‑acquired after expiration.

---

## 4. Adapting to other backends

| Backend | Replace Redis commands with |
|---------|----------------------------|
| **etcd** | `clientv3.NewKV()` → use the `concurrency` package for sessions and mutexes, or use a key with `leaseID`. Use an extra key `fence:<name>` that you `Put/Txn` with `Increment` semantics. |
| **Zookeeper** | Use the “create sequential” feature to get a monotonically increasing ID; store it as part of the lock data. |
| **DynamoDB** | Store a row per lock name, use `ConditionExpression` to create if not exists and an atomic `UpdateItem` that increments the token (`ADD`). |

The Go code will look almost identical – only the underlying storage calls change.

---

## 5. Final take‑aways

* The lock is *lease‑based*: Redis’ `SET NX PX` guarantees that a holder cannot keep it forever; after `<ttl>` the key disappears automatically.
* **Fencing token** (a global, monotonic counter) protects against stale owners re‑acquiring the lock and mistakenly believing they are still in control.
* All critical operations (`Acquire`, `Release`) use *atomic Lua scripts*, so there is no window for race conditions even when many clients compete.
* Failure cases are explicit: acquisition failures return a dedicated error, context cancellations propagate as timeouts, and Redis errors bubble up to the caller.

Feel free to drop this snippet into a Go project that already has a Redis client; tweak `ttl`, lock name, or the connection pool to suit your environment. Happy locking!

Below is **not** a production‑grade PBFT implementation – it is an *educational sketch* that shows the core ideas (phases, message types, failure handling) while staying reasonably small enough to read and run on your machine.  
Feel free to copy‑paste the code into a C++17/20 project, compile with `g++ -std=c++20 -pthread pbft.cpp` and experiment.

---

## 1. Quick refresher – PBFT

| Step | Message | Purpose |
|------|---------|---------|
| **0** | *Pre‑Prepare* | Leader proposes a value (e.g., a transaction) to the other replicas. |
| **1** | *Prepare* | Replicas exchange signatures that they saw the same proposal. |
| **2** | *Commit*  | Once a replica has ≥ 2f+1 matching Prepare messages, it sends Commit. |
| **3** | *Decide*  | When a replica receives ≥ 2f+1 Commit messages, it can safely execute the request. |
| **4** | *View‑Change* | If a replica suspects the leader is faulty (timeout or conflicting view), it starts a new view with a different leader. |
| **5** | *New‑View*  | The new leader sends a New‑View message that contains the last accepted state; all replicas switch to the new view. |

The key properties:

```
n = 3f + 1   (number of replicas)
f           (maximum number of Byzantine nodes)
```

> **Why 3f + 1?**  
> * With at most f faulty replicas, you still have at least 2f correct ones.  
> * 2f+1 > n‑f → any set of 2f+1 messages must contain at least one honest replica.

---

## 2. High‑level design

| Component | Responsibility |
|-----------|----------------|
| `Node` | Implements the PBFT state machine and message handling. |
| `Message` | Generic container for all PBFT messages (typed via enum). |
| `Network` | Simulates an asynchronous, lossy network between nodes (for testing). |
| `Main`  | Spins up a set of `Node`s in separate threads. |

We’ll keep the network very simple: each node has a thread‑safe message queue; to *send* a message you simply push it into the recipient’s queue.

---

## 3. Core data structures

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std::chrono_literals;

// --------------------------------------------------------
// 1) Message definition
// --------------------------------------------------------

enum class MsgType {
    PREPREPARE,
    PREPARE,
    COMMIT,
    VIEW_CHANGE,
    NEW_VIEW
};

struct Message {
    MsgType type;
    int sender;                // replica id of the sender
    int view;                  // current view number
    std::string value;         // proposal (only for PREPREPARE)
    std::vector<int> cert;     // list of signatures / ids that certify the message
};

// --------------------------------------------------------
// 2) Thread‑safe message queue
// --------------------------------------------------------

class Mailbox {
public:
    void push(const Message& msg) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(mtx);
        q.push(msg);
        cv.notify_one();
    }

    // blocks until a message is available or timeout (for view changes)
    bool wait_and_pop(Message &msg, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout = 2000ms) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mtx);
        if (!cv.wait_for(lk, timeout, [this]{ return !q.empty(); }))
            return false; // timed out
        msg = q.front();
        q.pop();
        return true;
    }

private:
    std::queue<Message> q;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
};
```

---

## 4. The `Node` class – PBFT state machine

```cpp
class Node {
public:
    Node(int id,
         int total_nodes,
         int f,
         std::vector<Mailbox*> inboxes)   // pointers to every replica's mailbox
        : id_(id),
          n_(total_nodes), f_(f),
          view_(0), leader_((view_ % n_)),
          inboxes_(std::move(inboxes))
    {
        state_ = State::INITIAL;
    }

    // run() will be executed in its own thread
    void run()
    {
        std::cout << "Node " << id_ << " starting.\n";
        while (!stop_) {
            switch (state_)
            {
                case State::INITIAL:          handle_initial(); break;
                case State::PRE_PREPARE:      handle_pre_prepare(); break;
                case State::PREPARE:          handle_prepare(); break;
                case State::COMMIT:           handle_commit(); break;
                case State::DECIDED:          /* nothing to do */; break;
                case State::VIEW_CHANGE:      handle_view_change(); break;
            }
        }
    }

    void stop() { stop_ = true; }

private:
    enum class State {
        INITIAL,
        PRE_PREPARE,
        PREPARE,
        COMMIT,
        DECIDED,
        VIEW_CHANGE
    };

    int id_;
    int n_;           // total replicas
    int f_;           // max faulty nodes
    int view_;        // current view number
    int leader_;      // id of the current leader

    std::string proposal_;            // value to commit (set by the client)
    State state_;

    // message certificates collected in each phase
    std::vector<Message> prepares_;
    std::vector<Message> commits_;

    // mailbox pointers for all replicas
    std::vector<Mailbox*> inboxes_;

    bool stop_ = false;

    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       Helper functions
       ---------------------------------------------------- */

    // broadcast a message to everyone (including self)
    void broadcast(const Message &msg) {
        for (int i=0; i<n_; ++i)
            inboxes_[i]->push(msg);
    }

    // send only to the leader
    void send_to_leader(const Message &msg) {
        inboxes_[leader_]->push(msg);
    }

    // is current node a view‑change detector?
    bool is_view_change_detector() const { return true; }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       State handlers
       ---------------------------------------------------- */

    void handle_initial()
    {
        if (id_ == leader_) {
            // Leader initiates the consensus by proposing a value.
            proposal_ = "value from client #" + std::to_string(id_);
            Message msg{MsgType::PREPREPARE, id_, view_, proposal_, {}};
            broadcast(msg);
            state_ = State::PRE_PREPARE;
        } else {
            // Non‑leader waits for PrePrepare
            Message m;
            if (inboxes_[id_]->wait_and_pop(m))
                process_msg(m);           // will move us to PRE_PREPARE once we see the right msg
        }
    }

    void handle_pre_prepare()
    {
        // Leader already sent the message, others are waiting.
        // Each replica collects PrePrepare messages and forwards them as Prepare.
        Message m;
        if (inboxes_[id_]->wait_and_pop(m))
            process_msg(m);
    }

    void handle_prepare()
    {
        Message m;
        if (inboxes_[id_]->wait_and_pop(m)) {
            process_msg(m);
            // check if we have enough prepares to advance
            if ((int)prepares_.size() >= 2*f_ + 1) {
                state_ = State::COMMIT;
                Message commit{MsgType::COMMIT, id_, view_, proposal_, {id_}};
                broadcast(commit);
            }
        } else {
            // timeout – suspect the leader
            trigger_view_change();
        }
    }

    void handle_commit()
    {
        Message m;
        if (inboxes_[id_]->wait_and_pop(m)) {
            process_msg(m);
            // check if we have enough commits to decide
            if ((int)commits_.size() >= 2*f_ + 1 && state_ != State::DECIDED) {
                std::cout << "Node " << id_ << ": DECIDED value = "
                          << proposal_ << " (view=" << view_ << ")\n";
                state_ = State::DECIDED;
            }
        } else {
            // timeout – suspect the leader
            trigger_view_change();
        }
    }

    void handle_view_change()
    {
        // Broadcast ViewChange message
        Message vc{MsgType::VIEW_CHANGE, id_, view_, "", {}};
        broadcast(vc);

        // Wait for NewView from new leader
        Message m;
        if (inboxes_[id_]->wait_and_pop(m)) {
            process_msg(m);
            state_ = State::INITIAL;      // restart consensus in the new view
        } else {
            // timeout – try again with a higher view number
            ++view_;
            leader_ = view_ % n_;
        }
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       Message processing (called from all handlers)
       ---------------------------------------------------- */

    void process_msg(const Message &m)
    {
        if (m.view != view_) return;   // ignore stale or future messages

        switch (m.type) {
            case MsgType::PREPREPARE:
                if (state_ == State::INITIAL && m.sender == leader_) {
                    proposal_ = m.value;
                    state_ = State::PRE_PREPARE;

                    // Leader will send Prepare right after PrePrepare
                    Message prep{MsgType::PREPARE, id_, view_, "", {id_}};
                    broadcast(prep);
                }
                break;

            case MsgType::PREPARE:
                if (state_ == State::PRE_PREPARE) {
                    prepares_.push_back(m);
                } else if (state_ == State::PREPARE) {
                    // collect additional prepares
                    prepares_.push_back(m);
                }
                break;

            case MsgType::COMMIT:
                commits_.push_back(m);
                break;

            case MsgType::VIEW_CHANGE:
                // In a full implementation we would keep track of which nodes
                // have sent ViewChange and when enough have, the new leader
                // sends NewView. Here we simplify: any node that receives a
                // ViewChange from *any* peer will start its own view‑change
                // procedure after timeout (see above).
                break;

            case MsgType::NEW_VIEW:
                // In the simplified demo we don't implement this separately.
                break;
        }
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------
       Failure handling helpers
       ---------------------------------------------------- */

    void trigger_view_change()
    {
        std::cout << "Node " << id_ << ": timeout, starting view change.\n";
        state_ = State::VIEW_CHANGE;
    }
};
```

