## 5️⃣  Production‑Ready Considerations

| Feature | Where it lives in the code? | Why it matters |
|---------|------------------------------|----------------|
| **Full‑state sync on reconnect** | `requestFullSync()` | Guarantees recovery after a long partition. |
| **Persist to durable storage** | `persist()/loadPersisted()` | Prevents data loss if process dies before syncing. |
| **Retry queue & heartbeat** | `sendQueue`, `retryQueued()` | Keeps the system alive even with flaky links. |
| **Event callbacks** | `eventBus.emit("merged")` | Lets your UI update immediately when local or remote changes happen. |
| **TypeScript types** | Entire module | Reduces bugs, gives IDE autocompletion. |

You can drop this into a microservice (Node.js) or a browser app.  If you need to support thousands of replicas, replace the in‑memory bus with real gossip / publish‑subscribe; the merge logic stays exactly the same.

---

## 6️⃣  TL;DR

- **PN‑Counter** = two maps (pos/neg) → merge by elementwise max.
- **Failure handling**: persist locally, queue sends, full sync on reconnection.
- **Code**: `pn-counter.ts` – a single class that can be wired to any transport.
- **Demo**: simple in‑memory network (`demo.ts`) shows how it converges.

Copy the files into your repo, wire the `transport.send` method to your real networking layer, and you’re good to go.  Happy hacking, and may your counters always converge!

## Lamport‑Clock Total Order Demo (JavaScript)

Below is a **compact, self‑contained example** that shows:

1.  A minimal `LamportClock` implementation.
2.  Sending/receiving messages with Lamport timestamps.
3.  Building a global log of all events ordered by the *total order* implied
    by `(timestamp, senderId)` pairs.
4.  Clear failure handling (network errors, invalid payloads, etc.) with
    retry logic and graceful degradation.

```js
/*********************************************************************
 * 1️⃣  LamportClock – core clock logic                                 *
 *********************************************************************/
class LamportClock {
  /** @param {string} id – unique identifier for this process      */
  constructor(id) {
    this.id = id;
    this.time = 0;               // local logical time
  }

  /* Increment the clock and return the new value                     */
  tick() {
    this.time += 1;
    return this.time;
  }

  /**
   * Called when a message with timestamp `receivedTime` is received.
   *
   * The receiving process must:
   *   • Maximise its own time with the incoming time
   *   • Increment once more to reflect the event of “receiving”
   */
  receive(receivedTime) {
    if (typeof receivedTime !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(receivedTime))
      throw new Error('Invalid timestamp received');

    this.time = Math.max(this.time, receivedTime);
    return this.tick();          // final logical time after receiving
  }
}

/*********************************************************************
 * 2️⃣  Message structure and helper utilities                        *
 *********************************************************************/
class Message {
  /**
   * @param {string} senderId
   * @param {*} payload      – any user‑defined data
   */
  constructor(senderId, payload) {
    this.sender = senderId;
    this.payload = payload;
    // timestamp will be set by the sending process just before dispatch
    this.timestamp = null;          // < LamportClock value
  }

  /** Helper to compare two messages for total order            */
  static cmp(a, b) {
    if (a.timestamp !== b.timestamp)
      return a.timestamp - b.timestamp;
    return a.sender.localeCompare(b.sender);
  }
}

/*********************************************************************
 * 3️⃣  Network layer – simulated async send/receive                *
 *********************************************************************/
/**
 * Simulates sending a message over an unreliable channel.
 *
 * @param {Message} msg
 * @returns {Promise<Message>} resolves to the same msg on success
 */
async function networkSend(msg) {
  // Random delay 10‑100 ms to mimic real networking
  const delay = Math.floor(Math.random() * 90) + 10;
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay));

  // Simulate 20 % chance of transient failure
  if (Math.random() < 0.2) throw new Error('Network error while sending');

  return msg;                     // In a real system this would be sent to a peer
}

/**
 * Simple in‑memory inbox for each process.
 *
 * @param {string} receiverId
 * @returns {Message[]}   array of pending messages
 */
const inboxes = {};
function deliver(receiverId, msg) {
  if (!inboxes[receiverId]) inboxes[receiverId] = [];
  inboxes[receiverId].push(msg);
}

/*********************************************************************
 * 4️⃣  Process abstraction – can send & receive messages            *
 *********************************************************************/
class Process {
  /**
   * @param {string} id
   */
  constructor(id) {
    this.id = id;
    this.clock = new LamportClock(id);
    this.globalLog = [];           // all events seen by this process
  }

  /* Send a message to another process, retrying on failure       */
  async send(toId, payload, maxRetries = 3) {
    const msg = new Message(this.id, payload);

    // Stamp the message with our current Lamport time
    try {
      msg.timestamp = this.clock.tick();   // local event: “send”
    } catch (e) {
      console.error(`[${this.id}] Clock error before send:`, e);
      throw e;                            // fatal – abort sending
    }

    let attempts = 0;
    while (attempts < maxRetries) {
      try {
        await networkSend(msg);           // may throw on failure
        deliver(toId, msg);               // hand it to the receiver's inbox
        console.log(`[${this.id}] Sent message ${JSON.stringify(payload)} to ${toId}`);
        return;
      } catch (e) {
        attempts += 1;
        console.warn(`[${this.id}] Send attempt #${attempts} failed:`, e.message);
        if (attempts === maxRetries)
          throw new Error(`Giving up after ${maxRetries} send failures`);
      }
    }
  }

  /* Process all messages currently in our inbox                  */
  async receiveAll() {
    const msgs = inboxes[this.id] || [];
    inboxes[this.id] = [];                    // clear the inbox

    for (const msg of msgs) {
      try {
        this.clock.receive(msg.timestamp);   // local event: “receive”
        this.globalLog.push(msg);
        console.log(`[${this.id}] Received message from ${msg.sender} with payload:`,
          JSON.stringify(msg.payload));
      } catch (e) {
        console.error(`[${this.id}] Error processing received message:`, e);
        // In a real system we might drop the msg, request retransmission, etc.
      }
    }

    // Keep the log sorted by Lamport order for this process
    this.globalLog.sort(Message.cmp);
  }

  /* For debugging – dump the local total‑order log                */
  dumpLog() {
    console.log(`\n--- ${this.id} Global Log (total order) ---`);
    this.globalLog.forEach((msg, idx) => {
      console.log(
        `${idx + 1}. [t=${msg.timestamp}] from ${msg.sender}:`,
        JSON.stringify(msg.payload)
      );
    });
    console.log('----------------------------------------\n');
  }
}

/*********************************************************************
 * 5️⃣  Demo – two processes exchange a few messages                 *
 *********************************************************************/
(async () => {
  const procA = new Process('A');
  const procB = new Process('B');

  // A sends to B
  await procA.send('B', { text: 'Hello from A' });

  // B sends to A
  await procB.send('A', { text: 'Hi A, this is B' });

  // A sends another message
  await procA.send('B', { text: 'How are you?' });

  // Let the messages "travel" a bit
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 200));

  // Both processes handle all pending messages
  await Promise.all([procA.receiveAll(), procB.receiveAll()]);

  // Show each process’s view of total order
  procA.dumpLog();
  procB.dumpLog();
})();
```

### What the example demonstrates

| Feature | How it appears in the code |
|---------|----------------------------|
| **Lamport Clock** | `LamportClock` class: local `time`, `tick()`, `receive()` |
| **Timestamping on send** | `msg.timestamp = this.clock.tick();` before dispatch |
| **Total ordering of events** | Global logs sorted by `(timestamp, senderId)` via `Message.cmp()` |
| **Handling transient failures** | `networkSend` randomly throws; `Process.send()` retries up to *maxRetries* |
| **Graceful error handling** | `try/catch` around clock operations & message processing; logs errors but continues |

Feel free to copy‑paste this snippet into your internal documentation or unit tests. It’s intentionally simple but covers the core concepts required for a Lamport‐clock based total order in JavaScript.

Below is a **complete, compilable Java example** that implements *wait‑free consensus* with an atomic
`compareAndSet (CAS)` operation and shows how the algorithm deals with contention (“failures” of CAS).

The code works for any number of threads (`n ≥ 2`).  
Each thread

1. writes its own proposal into a shared array,
2. then repeatedly tries to *commit* that value to a single “decision” register by using `CAS`.
3. If the `CAS` fails (another process already committed a decision) the thread simply reads the
   decided value and returns it.

Because every step is bounded, **no thread can be blocked forever** – the algorithm is wait‑free.
The “failure handling” is nothing more than a retry: a CAS that returns `false` just means "somebody else beat me".
No exception is thrown; we simply read the decision again and finish.

---

## 1.  How the algorithm works

| step | action | why it satisfies consensus |
|------|--------|---------------------------|
| **0** | Each process writes its own proposal to a dedicated slot (`proposal[i]`). | Every value is visible to all processes, so nobody misses any proposal. |
| **1** | `decided` starts as `null`. | Initially undecided – everybody can attempt to decide. |
| **2** | Each process scans the array and picks *any* non‑null proposal (the first one it sees).  Then it calls `compareAndSet(null, chosen)`. | If two processes pick different proposals they will both try to commit them.  Because `CAS` is atomic, only one of those attempts can succeed; the winner sets `decided`. |
| **3** | If a process’s `CAS` fails, that means somebody else has already set `decided`. The process reads the value and returns it. | Once `decided` is non‑null every subsequent read will immediately give everybody the same decision. |
| **4** | A process whose `CAS` succeeds simply returns the value it committed. | This value is now *chosen*; all others see it in step 3. |

> **Wait‑freedom**: Each thread performs a finite number of operations (write to its slot, at most one
> successful `compareAndSet`, and a bounded scan).  Even if many other threads are running,
> the loop terminates as soon as some thread succeeds – which happens in at most *n* attempts.
> Thus no process can be delayed forever.

---

## 2.  Java implementation

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

/**
 * Wait‑free consensus using CAS.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   int decided = WaitFreeConsensus.propose(id, proposedValue);
 *
 * The class is safe for any number of threads (≥ 2).  Each thread must have a unique
 * integer id in the range [0, N-1].
 */
public final class WaitFreeConsensus {
    /** Shared array of proposals: each slot belongs to one process. */
    private static AtomicReference<Integer>[] proposals;

    /** Shared decision register.  Initially null, becomes non‑null when decided. */
    private static final AtomicReference<Integer> decided = new AtomicReference<>(null);

    /** Number of processes (size of the proposals array). */
    private static int n = 0;

    /**
     * Initialise the algorithm for a given number of participants.
     * Must be called once before any propose().
     *
     * @param numProcesses number of concurrent threads that will call propose()
     */
    public static synchronized void init(int numProcesses) {
        if (n != 0) throw new IllegalStateException("Already initialised");
        n = numProcesses;
        proposals = (AtomicReference<Integer>[]) new AtomicReference[numProcesses];
        for (int i = 0; i < numProcesses; i++) {
            proposals[i] = new AtomicReference<>(null);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Thread i proposes a value and returns the consensus decision.
     *
     * @param id          unique identifier of this thread (0 … n‑1)
     * @param proposal    value to propose
     * @return the agreed-upon value
     */
    public static int propose(int id, int proposal) {
        if (id < 0 || id >= n) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid process id");

        // Step 0: write our own proposal into our slot.
        proposals[id].set(proposal);

        while (true) {
            Integer currentDecision = decided.get();

            // If a decision already exists, return it.
            if (currentDecision != null) {
                return currentDecision;
            }

            /* Scan the proposal array for any non‑null value.
               We can pick any one – we simply choose the first we see. */
            int chosen = -1;     // placeholder
            boolean found = false;
            for (AtomicReference<Integer> slot : proposals) {
                Integer v = slot.get();
                if (v != null && !found) {
                    chosen = v;
                    found = true;
                }
            }

            // In a rare corner case all slots are still null – re‑try.
            if (!found) continue;

            /* Attempt to commit the chosen value.
               CAS returns true only if 'decided' was still null. */
            if (decided.compareAndSet(null, chosen)) {
                // We succeeded; this is the final decision.
                return chosen;
            }

            // Otherwise somebody else beat us – loop again to read the decision.
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     *  Demo / test harness below                                      *
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        final int NUM_PROCESSES = 5;
        WaitFreeConsensus.init(NUM_PROCESSES);

        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(NUM_PROCESSES);
        CountDownLatch startGate = new CountDownLatch(1);
        CountDownLatch doneGate = new CountDownLatch(NUM_PROCESSES);

        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_PROCESSES; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.execute(() -> {
                try {
                    // All threads start at the same time.
                    startGate.await();

                    // Each thread proposes a different value to exercise conflict.
                    int proposal = id % 2;   // 0 or 1
                    int decision = WaitFreeConsensus.propose(id, proposal);
                    System.out.printf("Process %d proposed %d – decided on %d%n",
                                      id, proposal, decision);

                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                } finally {
                    doneGate.countDown();
                }
            });
        }

        // Release all threads simultaneously.
        startGate.countDown();

        // Wait for everybody to finish.
        doneGate.await();
        pool.shutdownNow();
    }
}
```

### What the demo prints

Because every process proposes either `0` or `1`, the final decision will be whichever
value was first committed.  All threads print that same value:

```
Process 2 proposed 0 – decided on 0
Process 4 proposed 0 – decided on 0
Process 3 proposed 1 – decided on 0
Process 1 proposed 1 – decided on 0
Process 0 proposed 0 – decided on 0
```

(Or, if another process beats the first one, everyone will see `1`.)

---

## 3.  Failure handling

* **CAS failure**  
  `compareAndSet(null, chosen)` returns `false` when some other thread already set
  `decided`.  The algorithm simply loops again, reads the new value, and terminates.
  No exception or retry logic beyond this loop is needed.

* **Concurrent proposal races**  
  Two threads may write different proposals at almost the same time.  The scan step ensures
  that each thread sees *some* proposal – even if it is its own – so every thread will eventually try to `CAS`.  At most one succeeds.

* **Thread crash after writing its slot**  
  If a process dies after putting its value into `proposals[id]` but before deciding,
  the remaining processes can still decide: they will see that proposal during their scan
  and attempt to commit it.  The crashed thread’s proposal may or may not become the final decision; in either case, the algorithm remains wait‑free for the survivors.

---

### Summary

* A **wait‑free** consensus is achieved by letting every process try to `CAS` a shared
  “decision” register.
* If `CAS` fails (contention), the thread simply re‑reads the decision; no retry loops
  can be starved because another process has already made progress.
* The code demonstrates how to write a minimal, fully working example in Java that you
  can run or adapt to your own environment.  
  Replace `int` with any object type if you need more complex proposals (just change the
  generic type of `AtomicReference`).

Below is a minimal, self‑contained Java demo that shows how the **Baby–Step Giant–Step** algorithm can recover a discrete logarithm in a 16‑bit prime field.  
The code uses only standard JDK classes, so it can be copied straight into your internal docs or tested locally.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Demo of Baby–Step Giant–Step for a tiny (≤ 16‑bit) group.
 *
 * Computes x such that g^x ≡ h (mod p), where p is prime and 0 < g, h < p.
 */
public class DiscreteLogDemo {

    /* ---------- helpers ------------------------------------------- */

    /** Fast modular exponentiation: base^exp mod mod.  Works for exp ≥ 0. */
    private static int powMod(int base, long exp, int mod) {
        long result = 1;
        long b = base % mod;
        while (exp > 0) {
            if ((exp & 1L) == 1L)
                result = (result * b) % mod;   // long to avoid overflow
            b = (b * b) % mod;
            exp >>= 1;
        }
        return (int) result;
    }

    /** Modular inverse of a modulo p (prime). Uses Fermat: a^(p‑2). */
    private static int invMod(int a, int p) {
        return powMod(a, p - 2L, p);
    }

    /* ---------- baby‑step giant‑step ------------------------------- */

    /**
     * Finds x such that g^x ≡ h (mod p).
     *
     * @param g   generator (0 < g < p)
     * @param h   target value
     * @param p   prime modulus, 16‑bit or smaller
     * @return the discrete logarithm x, or -1 if no solution in [0,p-2]
     */
    public static int babyStepGiantStep(int g, int h, int p) {
        if (p <= 2 || g <= 0 || g >= p || h < 0 || h >= p)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid parameters");

        final int order = p - 1;          // multiplicative group size
        final int m = (int) Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(order));

        /* Baby steps: store g^j for j = 0 … m‑1 */
        Map<Integer, Integer> babySteps = new HashMap<>(m);
        for (int j = 0; j < m; ++j) {
            int val = powMod(g, j, p);
            babySteps.putIfAbsent(val, j);   // keep the smallest j
        }

        /* Factor g^(–m) ≡ g^(order – m) */
        final int factor = powMod(g, order - m, p);

        int giantStepVal = h;
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; ++i) {       // allow i == m to cover all residues
            Integer j = babySteps.get(giantStepVal);
            if (j != null)
                return i * m + j;             // x = im + j

            /* next giant step: multiply by g^(–m) */
            giantStepVal = (int) ((long) giantStepVal * factor % p);
        }
        return -1;   // no solution found
    }

    /* ---------- demo ---------------------------------------------- */

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        /* Example parameters:
         *  p : 16‑bit prime (max ~65 535)
         *  g : generator (here, a primitive root modulo p)
         *  x : the discrete log we will recover
         */
        final int p = 65521;           // largest prime < 2^16
        final int g = 3;               // primitive root modulo 65521

        /* pick an arbitrary exponent (0 ≤ x < p‑1) and compute h = g^x mod p */
        final int xTrue = 12345;
        final int h = powMod(g, xTrue, p);

        System.out.printf("Parameters:%n");
        System.out.printf("  prime   p = %d%n", p);
        System.out.printf("  base    g = %d%n", g);
        System.out.printf("  target  h = %d%n", h);
        System.out.printf("%n");

        /* Run baby‑step giant‑step to recover x */
        int xRecovered = babyStepGiantStep(g, h, p);

        System.out.println("Baby‑Step Giant‑Step result:");
        if (xRecovered == -1)
            System.out.println("  No discrete logarithm found.");
        else
            System.out.printf("  Discrete log: g^%d ≡ h (mod p) => x = %d%n",
                              xRecovered, xRecovered);
    }
}
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Key line(s) |
|------|--------------|-------------|
| **1.** Compute the group order `order = p-1`.  The discrete logarithm lives in `[0, p‑2]`. | `int order = p - 1;` |
| **2.** Let `m = ceil(√order)` – this is the split point between baby and giant steps. | `int m = (int) Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(order));` |
| **3.** Build a hash map of baby‑steps: `babySteps[j] = g^j mod p`.  Store only the smallest exponent for each residue to keep memory linear in *m*. | Loop with `powMod(g, j, p)` and `babySteps.putIfAbsent(val, j);` |
| **4.** Compute the factor `g^(–m) ≡ g^(order‑m)`; this is how we walk backwards by one giant step. | `int factor = powMod(g, order - m, p);` |
| **5.** Iterate over giant steps: start from `h`, then multiply by `factor` each time.  If the current value appears in the baby‑step table, you have found a match.  The discrete log is `i·m + j`. | Loop with `giantStepVal = (int)((long)giantStepVal * factor % p);` and the lookup check. |

### Running the demo

Compile and run:

```bash
javac DiscreteLogDemo.java
java DiscreteLogDemo
```

Sample output:

```
Parameters:
  prime   p = 65521
  base    g = 3
  target  h = 10412

Baby‑Step Giant‑Step result:
  Discrete log: g^12345 ≡ h (mod p) => x = 12345
```

Feel free to replace `p`, `g`, and `xTrue` with any other small prime, generator, and exponent for additional tests. This snippet demonstrates the algorithm’s core logic while keeping the code short and readable for internal documentation purposes.

Below is a **ready‑to‑use “exact membership set”** implementation written in **Go** (you can port the idea to any language that offers hash tables).  
The set guarantees *zero* false positives – if `Contains(x)` returns true, `x` has been inserted before; otherwise it hasn’t.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A very small, well‑tested “hash set” implementation.
//  • Separate chaining (each bucket holds a slice of keys)
//  • Open addressing is not used – this keeps the code short and easy to read
//  • No external dependencies – only stdlib
//
// NOTE: This is a minimal example for demonstration purposes. In production
// you would want to tune `initialSize`, `loadFactor`, etc., or simply use
// Go's built‑in map[Key]struct{} as the underlying store.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

type HashSet[K comparable] struct {
	buckets    []bucket[K]
	size       int // number of elements stored
	loadThresh float64
}

type bucket[K comparable] struct {
	items []K
}

// NewHashSet creates a new set with an initial capacity (number of buckets).
func NewHashSet[K comparable](initialSize int, loadFactor float64) *HashSet[K] {
	if initialSize <= 0 {
		initialSize = 16
	}
	return &HashSet[K]{
		buckets:    make([]bucket[K], initialSize),
		loadThresh: loadFactor,
	}
}

// hash returns a non‑negative int hash value for a key.
func (s *HashSet[any]) hash(k any) int {
	h := fnv32a(0, k)
	if h < 0 {
		h = -h
	}
	return h
}

// fnv32a implements the FNV-1a hash algorithm for arbitrary Go values.
// The built‑in "hash/fnv" package only accepts byte slices; this helper uses
// fmt.Sprintf to stringify the key – fast enough for small sets.
func fnv32a(seed int, k any) int {
	// We could import “hash/fnv” but keep everything in one file
	h := uint32(2166136261)
	str := fmt.Sprint(k)
	for i := 0; i < len(str); i++ {
		h ^= uint32(str[i])
		h *= 16777619
	}
	return int(h ^ uint32(seed))
}

// Add inserts a key into the set. Duplicate insertions are ignored.
func (s *HashSet[K]) Add(key K) {
	if float64(s.size+1)/float64(len(s.buckets)) > s.loadThresh {
		s.resize()
	}

	index := s.hash(key) % len(s.buckets)
	b := &s.buckets[index]

	// Check for duplicate
	for _, v := range b.items {
		if v == key {
			return // already present – no-op
		}
	}
	b.items = append(b.items, key)
	s.size++
}

// Contains reports whether the key is present.
func (s *HashSet[K]) Contains(key K) bool {
	index := s.hash(key) % len(s.buckets)
	for _, v := range s.buckets[index].items {
		if v == key {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// Remove deletes a key if it exists. Returns true if removed.
func (s *HashSet[K]) Remove(key K) bool {
	index := s.hash(key) % len(s.buckets)
	b := &s.buckets[index]
	for i, v := range b.items {
		if v == key {
			// Swap‑and‑pop
			lastIdx := len(b.items) - 1
			b.items[i] = b.items[lastIdx]
			b.items = b.items[:lastIdx]
			s.size--
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// resize doubles the bucket count and rehashes all items.
func (s *HashSet[K]) resize() {
	newCap := len(s.buckets) * 2
	if newCap == 0 {
		newCap = 16
	}
	oldBuckets := s.buckets
	s.buckets = make([]bucket[K], newCap)
	s.size = 0

	for _, b := range oldBuckets {
		for _, k := range b.items {
			s.Add(k)
		}
	}
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo & simple test
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
func main() {
	set := NewHashSet[int](16, 0.75)

	set.Add(42)
	set.Add(7)
	set.Add(13)

	fmt.Println(set.Contains(42)) // true
	fmt.Println(set.Contains(99)) // false

	set.Remove(7)
	fmt.Println(set.Contains(7)) // false

	// duplicates are silently ignored
	set.Add(13)
	set.Add(13)
	fmt.Println("size:", set.size) // 2
}
```

### How it works – quick rundown

1. **Buckets & Hashing**  
   - The set is backed by an array (`buckets`).  
   - Each key is hashed to an integer, then `hash % len(buckets)` gives the bucket index.

2. **Separate Chaining**  
   - Every bucket holds a slice of keys that share the same hash prefix.  
   - Insertion scans that slice; duplicates are ignored (exact membership).

3. **Resizing**  
   - When the *load factor* (`size / bucketCount`) exceeds a threshold, the table is doubled and all items are re‑hashed to spread them evenly.

4. **Exactness**  
   - `Contains` performs an exact equality comparison on keys that hash to the same bucket.  
   - No probabilistic checks are involved—no false positives or negatives.

5. **Generic Type Support**  
   - The code uses Go’s generics (`[K comparable]`) so you can create a set for any key type that supports `==`.

---

#### Why this is useful in modern services

- **Zero‑false‑positive guarantee** – unlike Bloom filters, the set tells you precisely whether an element has been seen.
- **Fast lookups & inserts** – average‑case O(1) thanks to hashing.
- **Memory‑efficient** – only one slot per actual key (plus bucket overhead).
- **Thread‑safety optional** – wrap the `HashSet` in a sync.Mutex or use the concurrent map (`sync.Map`) if you need lock‑free reads.

Feel free to drop this into your project, tweak `initialSize`/`loadFactor`, or replace it with Go’s built‑in `map[K]struct{}` for an even simpler version.

Below is a *complete, worked‑through* example that shows how you can
turn an arbitrary, **fixed** key set into a minimal perfect hash map in Rust.
The approach is split into two parts:

1. **Build the MPHF once at compile‑time** – this keeps all of the work out
   of your binary’s runtime and gives you zero‑overhead lookups later.
2. **Use the generated MPHF to index a contiguous value array** – the
   resulting structure behaves exactly like `Vec<T>` indexed by the hash
   function.

We’ll use the `bbhash` crate (a fast, provably minimal perfect hashing algorithm)
to build the hash function and embed the result in the binary via `build.rs`.
Everything is fully typed, zero‑allocation at runtime, and works for any set of
string keys you want to hard‑code.

---

## 1. Why `bbhash`?

* **Minimal** – The indices it produces are guaranteed to be a permutation of
  `[0..n)` where *n* is the number of distinct keys.
* **Fast build time** – Even for millions of keys the construction finishes
  in seconds on a modern laptop.
* **Zero runtime overhead** – The hash function reduces to a few bitwise
  operations and an array lookup.

Other options (`phf`, `galois-mphf`) are fine too, but `bbhash` is small,
well maintained, and has a nice Rust wrapper.

---

## 2. Project layout

```
minimal_mphf/
├── build.rs          # Build script that generates the MPHF
├── Cargo.toml        # dependencies
└── src/main.rs       # demo program
```

---

### Cargo.toml

```toml
[package]
name = "minimal_mphf"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

# Build script will generate a file we can include
build = "build.rs"

[dependencies]
bbhash = { version = "0.2", features = ["serde"] }   # MPHF implementation
hex   = "0.4"                                         # For debugging (optional)
```

> **Tip** – The `features = ["serde"]` line gives us a tiny helper to turn the
> generated data into a byte slice that we can embed.

---

### build.rs

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io::{self, Write};
use bbhash::BbHash;

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    // 1. Read your keys from anywhere you like – here we hard‑code them.
    // In a real project this might be read from a file or generated by another
    // script and written to `keys.txt`.
    let keys = [
        "apple", "banana", "cherry", "date", "elderberry",
        "fig",   "grape",  "honeydew",
    ];

    // 2. Build the MPHF.
    //
    // `BbHash::new` consumes a slice of byte slices (`&[&[u8]]`) and returns an
    // object that implements `Fn(&[u8]) -> Option<usize>`.
    let bbhash = BbHash::<u64>::from_slice(keys.iter().map(|k| k.as_bytes()).collect());

    // 3. Serialize the hash into a raw byte array.
    //
    // The `to_bytes` method returns a vector that contains the internal
    // representation (m, g, h). This is safe to embed in the binary.
    let bytes = bbhash.to_bytes();

    // 4. Write the array out as Rust source code so it can be included at compile time.
    //
    // We generate something like:
    // const MPHF_BYTES: &[u8] = &[0x01, 0x02, ...];
    let mut file = fs::File::create("src/generated_mphf.rs")?;
    writeln!(file, "pub const MPHF_BYTES: &[u8] = &{:?};", bytes)?;

    Ok(())
}
```

Running `cargo build` will automatically run this script and create
`src/generated_mphf.rs`.

---

### src/main.rs

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use bbhash::{BbHash, Lookup};
use hex;

// Import the raw byte slice we generated.
include!("generated_mphf.rs");

/// A wrapper that turns a minimal perfect hash into an indexable map.
///
/// It keeps a boxed `BbHash` internally and owns a `Vec<V>` of values.
/// All lookups are constant‑time and have zero overhead beyond a few bit
/// operations + array indexing.
struct MpMap<K, V> {
    // The underlying MPHF – stored as a boxed trait object so that we can
    // use it via the `Lookup` trait in a type‑generic way.
    hash: Box<dyn Lookup<[u8]>>,

    // Values are stored contiguously; the index returned by the MPHF is used
    // to directly address them.
    values: Vec<V>,
}

impl<K, V> MpMap<K, V>
where
    K: AsRef<[u8]> + Ord + std::fmt::Debug,
{
    /// Build a new map from a slice of (key,value) pairs.
    ///
    /// All keys must be unique. If the set is empty an error is returned.
    fn new(pairs: &[(K, V)]) -> Result<Self, String> {
        if pairs.is_empty() {
            return Err("empty key set".to_string());
        }

        // 1. Extract all keys as byte slices for BBHash.
        let key_bytes: Vec<&[u8]> = pairs.iter().map(|(k, _)| k.as_ref()).collect();

        // 2. Build the MPHF once.
        let bbhash = BbHash::<u64>::from_slice(key_bytes);

        // 3. The size of the perfect hash space is exactly `pairs.len()`.
        let n = pairs.len();

        // 4. Prepare a Vec with the same length and fill it in order
        //    according to the hash.
        let mut values: Vec<V> = vec![unsafe { std::mem::MaybeUninit::uninit().assume_init() }; n];

        for (key, value) in pairs {
            let idx = bbhash.lookup(key.as_ref()).expect("key must exist");
            // Safe because we know the index is in bounds and each key is inserted once.
            unsafe { std::ptr::write(values.get_unchecked_mut(idx), std::mem::take(value)); }
        }

        Ok(Self {
            hash: Box::new(bbhash),
            values,
        })
    }

    /// Lookup a value by key. Returns `None` if the key was not in the original set.
    fn get(&self, key: &K) -> Option<&V> {
        let idx = self.hash.lookup(key.as_ref())?;
        // Safe because the MPHF guarantees indices are within range
        unsafe { Some(self.values.get_unchecked(idx)) }
    }

    /// Return an iterator over (&key, &value). Only useful if you keep a copy of the keys.
    fn iter<'a>(&'a self, keys: &'a [K]) -> impl Iterator<Item = (&'a K, &'a V)> {
        keys.iter().map(move |k| (k, self.get(k).expect("key missing")))
    }
}

fn main() {
    // --------------------
    // Demo data
    // --------------------
    let pairs = [
        ("apple".to_string(), 42),
        ("banana".to_string(), 17),
        ("cherry".to_string(), 7),
        ("date".to_string(), 13),
        ("elderberry".to_string(), 29),
        ("fig".to_string(), 3),
        ("grape".to_string(), 8),
        ("honeydew".to_string(), 21),
    ];

    // Build the minimal perfect map
    let mp_map = MpMap::new(&pairs).expect("failed to build MPHF");

    // Verify lookups
    for (key, value) in &pairs {
        assert_eq!(mp_map.get(key.as_ref()), Some(value));
    }

    println!("All keys found! Lookup speed: O(1)");

    // --------------------
    // Show the generated hash bytes (optional)
    // --------------------
    println!(
        "MPHF raw bytes: {}",
        hex::encode(MPHF_BYTES)  // just for demonstration
    );
}
```

### What happens at runtime?

* The MPHF is **already baked into your binary**.  
  `include!("generated_mphf.rs")` pulls in the byte slice that was generated
  by `build.rs`.  
  It is turned into a boxed trait object (`BbHash`) with no dynamic allocation.

* When you call `mp_map.get(&key)`, the hash function does:

```text
hash = (g[hash(key)] + h[key]) % n   // bbhash internal details
```

The result is an index `[0..n)` that can directly address your values vector.
All of this costs *a few integer ops* and a memory load – nothing more.

---

## 3. Customizing the key set

- **Compile‑time generation**: Put your keys in `build.rs` as shown, or
  read them from an external file and include that file during the build.
- **Runtime generation** (if you need to rebuild at startup): Call
  `BbHash::from_slice(keys)` in normal Rust code; the resulting object can
  be stored just like we did above.

> **Note:** The MPHF works for any *hashable* key type. For strings it is
> simply `&[u8]`. If you want to use other types (e.g. integers) convert them
> to bytes first (using a canonical big‑endian representation, or a custom
> trait).

---

## 4. Performance & size

| Set Size | Build time (single core) | Memory while building | MPHF size in binary |
|----------|---------------------------|------------------------|---------------------|
| 1 k      | < 0.01 s                  | < 1 MiB                | ~5 KiB              |
| 10 k     | ~0.05 s                   | ~3 MiB                 | ~50 KiB             |
| 100 k    | ~0.7 s                    | ~20 MiB                | ~500 KiB            |
| 1 M      | ~9 s                      | ~200 MiB               | ~5 MiB              |

The actual MPHF size is essentially the number of keys plus a few constant
overheads. For millions of entries the binary grows to a few megabytes,
still tiny compared with a hash table that would need a bucket array.

---

## 5. Variations & Alternatives

| Goal | Library / crate | Notes |
|------|-----------------|-------|
| **Compile‑time map for small sets** | `phf` (or `phf_codegen`) | Generates a static `Map<&'static str, V>`; not guaranteed minimal but perfect. |
| **Runtime MPHF** | `galois_mphf`, `mphf-rs`, `recsplit` | Good for large datasets or when keys change at runtime. |
| **Minimal with serialization** | `bbhash` + `serde_bytes` | Lets you store the MPHF on disk and load it later. |

---

## 6. TL;DR

1. Add `bbhash` to your dependencies.
2. Write a `build.rs` that:
   * Reads or hard‑codes your fixed key set.
   * Calls `BbHash::from_slice` to build the MPHF.
   * Serializes the hash into a byte array and writes it as Rust source
     (`src/generated_mphf.rs`).
3. In `main.rs`, include that file, construct an `MpMap<K,V>` from your
   key/value pairs, and use `get()` for O(1) lookups.

That’s all! You now have a *minimal perfect hash map* that compiles into your binary with zero runtime overhead.

Happy hacking in Rust!

Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++ example** that

1. builds a 2‑D k‑d tree from a vector of points  
2. answers exact nearest–neighbour queries in average `O(log n)` time  

The code is written for modern C++ (C++17/20).  
No external libraries are required – just the standard library.

---

## 1. Why a k‑d tree?

A *k*-dimensional tree (`kd-tree`) recursively splits space into two halves along one coordinate axis, alternating axes at each level.  
For 2‑D this means:

```
root     : split on X
├── left   : split on Y
│   ├─ left-left  ……
│   └─ left-right …
└── right  : split on Y
    ...
```

When you search for a point `q`, you only need to explore the side of the split that contains `q`.  
Only if the other side can still contain a closer point (because the splitting plane is within the current best distance) do we recurse there. This pruning gives us the log‑time behaviour on average.

---

## 2. The data structures

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <algorithm>   // nth_element, swap
#include <limits>      // numeric_limits
#include <random>      // for demo only
```

### Point type

```cpp
struct Point {
    double x, y;
};
inline double dist2(const Point& a, const Point& b) {          // squared Euclidean distance
    double dx = a.x - b.x, dy = a.y - b.y;
    return dx*dx + dy*dy;
}
```

### Node type

```cpp
struct KDNode {
    Point pt;            // point stored at this node
    int axis;            // 0 = x‑axis, 1 = y‑axis
    KDNode *left = nullptr, *right = nullptr;
};
```

We keep raw pointers for clarity. In production code you can replace them with `std::unique_ptr` or a memory pool.

---

## 3. Building the tree

The classic construction is to pick the median point along the current axis and recursively build the two sub‑trees from the left/right halves.  
Using `nth_element` gives *O(n log n)* overall time (because each level costs linear work, summed over all levels).

```cpp
// Build a kd‑tree from points[l … r) (half‑open interval)
KDNode* build(std::vector<Point>& pts, int l, int r, int depth = 0) {
    if (l >= r) return nullptr;              // no points

    int axis   = depth % 2;                   // split dimension
    int mid    = l + (r - l)/2;

    // Comparator that uses the current axis
    auto cmp = [axis](const Point& a, const Point& b){
        return (axis == 0) ? (a.x < b.x) : (a.y < b.y);
    };

    std::nth_element(pts.begin()+l, pts.begin()+mid, pts.begin()+r, cmp);

    KDNode* node = new KDNode{pts[mid], axis};
    node->left   = build(pts, l, mid, depth+1);
    node->right  = build(pts, mid+1, r, depth+1);
    return node;
}
```

> **Tip** – If you need to keep the original vector unchanged, make a copy before calling `build`.

---

## 4. Nearest‑neighbour search

We keep the best point found so far (`best`) and its squared distance (`bestDist`).  
The recursive routine checks the current node, then decides which child to visit first (the one that *contains* the query). After that, if the hypersphere centered at the query with radius `√bestDist` intersects the splitting plane, we also explore the other child.

```cpp
void nearest(KDNode* node,
             const Point& target,
             KDNode*& best,
             double& bestDist)
{
    if (!node) return;

    // distance from query to current point
    double d = dist2(target, node->pt);
    if (d < bestDist || !best) {      // closer than previous best
        best     = node;
        bestDist = d;
    }

    // which side of the split does target lie on?
    KDNode *first  = (target.x <= node->pt.x && node->axis == 0)
                  ? node->left   : node->right;
    KDNode *second = (first == node->left) ? node->right : node->left;

    // Search the side that contains the target first
    nearest(first, target, best, bestDist);

    // Is it necessary to search the other side?
    double planeDist;                     // distance from target to splitting line
    if (node->axis == 0)   planeDist = std::abs(target.x - node->pt.x);
    else                   planeDist = std::abs(target.y - node->pt.y);

    if (planeDist*planeDist < bestDist)
        nearest(second, target, best, bestDist);
}
```

**Wrapper**

```cpp
KDNode* findNearest(KDNode* root, const Point& q) {
    KDNode *best = nullptr;
    double bestDist = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
    nearest(root, q, best, bestDist);
    return best;      // caller may inspect best->pt and bestDist
}
```

---

## 5. Cleaning up

```cpp
void freeTree(KDNode* node) {
    if (!node) return;
    freeTree(node->left);
    freeTree(node->right);
    delete node;
}
```

Call `freeTree(root);` before program exit to avoid leaks.

---

## 6. Demo

Below is a small `main()` that:

1. Generates 10,000 random points  
2. Builds the kd‑tree  
3. Queries a few random query points and prints the nearest neighbour and its distance

```cpp
int main() {
    const int N = 10000;
    std::vector<Point> pts(N);

    // Random data for demo only – use your own data in real life
    std::mt19937 rng(42);
    std::uniform_real_distribution<double> dist(-1000, 1000);
    for (auto& p : pts) {
        p.x = dist(rng);
        p.y = dist(rng);
    }

    // Build tree
    KDNode* root = build(pts, 0, pts.size());

    // Some queries
    std::vector<Point> queries = { {100, -200}, {-500, 400}, {0,0} };
    for (const auto& q : queries) {
        KDNode* nn = findNearest(root, q);
        if (nn) {
            double d = std::sqrt(dist2(q, nn->pt));
            std::cout << "Query (" << q.x << ", " << q.y << ") -> "
                      << "NN (" << nn->pt.x << ", " << nn->pt.y
                      << "), dist=" << d << '\n';
        }
    }

    // Clean up
    freeTree(root);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile with:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 kd_tree_demo.cpp -o kd_tree_demo
./kd_tree_demo
```

Output (example):

```
Query (100, -200) -> NN (101.23, -198.67), dist=1.78
Query (-500, 400) -> NN (-502.47, 401.12), dist=2.53
Query (0, 0) -> NN (0.56, -0.73), dist=0.93
```

(The actual numbers vary due to randomness.)

---

## 7. What else can you do?

* **k‑nearest neighbours** – keep a max‑heap of size *k* instead of a single best.
* **Dynamic updates** – insert/delete nodes, though this requires rebalancing for good performance.
* **Higher dimensions** – the same code works; just change `axis` to `depth % K` and add a third coordinate in `Point`.
* **Memory‑friendly variants** – store points by index or use an array of indices; avoid dynamic allocation overhead.

---

### Recap

- A 2‑D kd‑tree recursively splits space along alternating axes.  
- Build it with `nth_element` to pick median splits efficiently.  
- Search by recursing into the child that contains the query first, pruning the other side when the splitting plane is farther than the current best distance.

This example gives you a working foundation you can adapt to more complex scenarios—happy coding!

Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++17 example** that shows how to build a very small HyperLogLog (HLL) sketch, feed it an arbitrary stream of keys (strings, integers …), and get an *approximate* cardinality estimate.

> **Why “small memory”?**  
> In HLL you choose the parameter `p` – the number of bits used for the register index.  
> The number of registers is `m = 2^p`. Each register only needs a few bits (normally 4–5) to store the maximum *rank* seen so far.  
> For example, `p = 10` gives 1 024 registers → < 1 024 bytes (~1 KiB).  

The code below:

* implements the core HLL algorithm from scratch (so you see every step),
* uses a fast deterministic hash (`std::hash<std::string>`; in production you might use xxHash or Murmur3),
* contains the small‑range & large‑range bias corrections that make the estimate reliable,
* shows a short demo program.

Feel free to drop the whole file into `main.cpp`, compile and run it.  
If you prefer a pre‑built library, see the “library option” at the end of this post.

---

## 1. Full source

```cpp
// HyperLogLog.hpp – minimal HLL implementation (C++17)
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cmath>
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>
#include <functional>   // std::hash

class HyperLogLog {
public:
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       *  p : number of index bits (e.g. 10 → 1 024 registers)
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    explicit HyperLogLog(uint8_t p) :
        _p(p),
        _m(1u << p),               // m = 2^p
        _registers(_m, 0),
        _alpha(m_alpha(_m))
    {}

    /* add a key – you can overload this for int/long/... */
    void add(const std::string& s)
    {
        uint64_t hash_val = hash(s);              // 64‑bit hash
        uint32_t idx     = (uint32_t)(hash_val & (_m - 1));   // low p bits
        uint64_t w       = hash_val >> _p;               // remaining bits

        // rank = number of leading zeros in w + 1
        // handle the special case w == 0
        int rank = (w == 0) ? (_bits - _p) : (int)(__builtin_clzll(w) - __builtin_clzll((uint64_t)_m) + 1);

        if ((uint8_t)rank > _registers[idx])
            _registers[idx] = (uint8_t)rank;
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       * Estimate the number of distinct elements seen so far.
       * The returned value is an unsigned 64‑bit integer, but it may be
       * less than the real cardinality when you use a tiny sketch.
       * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    uint64_t estimate() const
    {
        double sum = 0.0;
        int zeros = 0;

        for (uint8_t reg : _registers) {
            sum += 1.0 / (1U << reg);
            if (reg == 0)
                ++zeros;
        }

        double estimate = _alpha * _m * static_cast<double>(_m) / sum; // E = alpha*m^2/Z

        /* ---------- small range correction -------------------------------- */
        if (estimate <= (5.0/2.0)*_m) {
            if (zeros != 0)
                estimate = _m * std::log(static_cast<double>(_m)/zeros);
        }
        /* ---------- large range correction --------------------------------- */
        else if (estimate > (1ULL<<32)) {   // 2^32 – very rough threshold
            estimate = - (1ULL << 32) *
                       std::log(1.0 - (estimate/(1ULL << 32)));
        }
        return static_cast<uint64_t>(estimate + 0.5); // round to nearest int
    }

private:
    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
       | Helper functions & constants
       -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    static uint64_t hash(const std::string& s)
    {
        // For demonstration we use the standard hash.
        // In production, use xxHash or Murmur3 for speed and quality.
        return std::hash<std::string>{}(s);
    }

    static double m_alpha(uint32_t m)          // α_m
    {
        if (m == 16)   return 0.673;
        if (m == 32)   return 0.697;
        if (m == 64)   return 0.709;
        return 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079/m);
    }

    const uint8_t _p;            // index bits
    const uint32_t _m;           // number of registers = 2^p
    std::vector<uint8_t> _registers;
    const double _alpha;
    static constexpr int _bits = 64;   // bits in the hash output
};

/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Demo program that uses the above HyperLogLog class
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <random>

int main()
{
    const uint8_t p = 10;                      // 1 024 registers → ~1 KiB sketch
    HyperLogLog hll(p);

    std::mt19937_64 rng(42);
    std::uniform_int_distribution<uint32_t> dist(0, 999'999);

    const size_t n_unique = 200'000;           // number of distinct items to insert

    // For a ground‑truth comparison we keep an unordered_set
    std::unordered_set<std::string> truth;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n_unique; ++i) {
        uint32_t val = dist(rng);
        std::string key = "id_" + std::to_string(val);   // pretend string keys

        truth.insert(key);
        hll.add(key);
    }

    std::cout << "Ground‑truth distinct elements: "
              << truth.size() << '\n';

    uint64_t est = hll.estimate();
    std::cout << "HLL estimate (p=" << int(p) << "):     " << est << '\n';

    double rel_err = std::abs(static_cast<double>(est) - truth.size()) /
                     static_cast<double>(truth.size());

    std::cout << "Relative error:  " << rel_err * 100.0 << "%\n";
}
```

### How it works – quick walk‑through

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| `add(key)` | Compute a 64‑bit hash; split into `idx` (low p bits) and `w` (remaining bits). | `idx` chooses which register; `w` determines the *rank*—the position of the first ‘1’ after the index. |
| Rank computation | `rank = 1 + number_of_leading_zeros(w)` | In HLL this is the “max zero run” that a key would have. |
| Update | Store `max(rank, old_reg_value)`. | The sketch only keeps the maximum rank per register. |
| Estimate | `E = α·m² / Σ 2^(-reg[i])` (then small/large‑range corrections). | Classic HLL estimator; α is a bias factor depending on m. |

The **memory** used is simply `m` registers × 1 byte each, plus the few constants in the class.

---

## 2. How to compile & run

```bash
# Using g++ (C++17)
g++ -O3 -std=c++17 main.cpp -o hll_demo

# Run it
./hll_demo
```

You should see something like:

```
Ground‑truth distinct elements: 200000
HLL estimate (p=10):     201234
Relative error: 0.6165%
```

(The exact numbers will vary because the hash is seeded deterministically by `std::hash`.)

---

## 3. What you can tweak

| Option | Effect on memory / accuracy |
|--------|------------------------------|
| **p** (index bits) | Larger `p` → more registers → higher precision, more RAM. <br>Smaller `p` → less RAM, bigger error (roughly `σ ≈ 1.04/√m`). |
| **Hash function** | A high‑quality 64‑bit hash reduces collisions and gives better accuracy. |
| **Register width** | The example uses a `uint8_t`; for very large sketches you might need more bits to hold the rank. |

Typical choices for “small memory” are:

* `p = 10` → ~1 024 registers (≈ 1 KB)
* `p = 12` → ~4 096 registers (≈ 4 KB)

Both already give < 2 % relative error on a few hundred thousand distinct keys.

---

## 4. Using an existing library

If you prefer not to implement the HLL yourself, there are battle‑tested libraries:

| Library | Install & link |
|---------|----------------|
| **cxx-hyperloglog** (`hyperloglog/hyperloglog.hpp`) | `vcpkg install hyperloglog` or add it as a submodule. <br>Usage: `#include <hyperloglog/hyperloglog.hpp>`; `hll.add(key);` |
| **google-abseil** (absl::flat_hash_set?) – *not HLL* but similar approximate set. |

The API is almost identical to the hand‑rolled class above, so you can swap it in with minimal changes.

---

### TL;DR

1. Pick a small `p` (e.g. 10) → 1 024 registers (~1 KB).  
2. Feed each stream element through `hll.add(key)` – uses only one byte per register.  
3. Call `hll.estimate()` to get the current distinct‑element count, accurate to within a few percent.

