**Quick‑start MT940 parser (Java)**  
*The snippet below shows the most common way to turn an MT‑940 file into POJOs you can work with in Java.*  

```java
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Add a dependency on an MT‑940 library.
//    Maven:
//
//        <dependency>
//            <groupId>de.talenta.banktools</groupId>
//            <artifactId>mt940-parser</artifactId>
//            <version>1.2.0</version>
//        </dependency>
//
//    Gradle (Groovy):
//
//        implementation 'de.talenta.banktools:mt940-parser:1.2.0'
//
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.List;

import de.talenta.banktools.mt.Mt940Parser;
import de.talenta.banktools.mt.model.Statement;
import de.talenta.banktools.mt.model.Transaction;

/**
 * Very small demo that reads an MT‑940 file, parses it and prints the
 * most important fields.
 */
public class Mt940Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1️⃣ Load the raw text of the statement
        Path path = Path.of("src/main/resources/statement.mt940");
        String mt940Text = Files.readString(path);

        // 2️⃣ Create a parser instance
        Mt940Parser parser = new Mt940Parser();

        // 3️⃣ Parse – you get one Statement per bank account in the file
        List<Statement> statements = parser.parse(mt940Text);

        // 4️⃣ Consume the parsed data (here: just print it)
        for (Statement stmt : statements) {
            System.out.println("=== Account: " + stmt.getAccountNumber() + " ===");
            System.out.println("Opening balance (" + stmt.getCurrency() + "): "
                    + stmt.getOpeningBalance());
            System.out.println("Closing balance: "
                    + stmt.getClosingBalance());

            System.out.println("\nTransactions:");
            for (Transaction tx : stmt.getTransactions()) {
                // Format date as YYYYMMDD, description and amount
                String type = tx.isCredit() ? "CREDIT" : "DEBIT";
                System.out.printf("%s | %s | %.2f %s%n",
                        tx.getDate(),
                        tx.getDescription().trim(),
                        tx.getAmount().abs(),
                        type);
            }
            System.out.println(); // blank line between statements
        }
    }
}
```

### What the library gives you

| Class | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `Mt940Parser` | Handles all parsing logic – block detection, tag extraction, date/amount conversion. |
| `Statement`  | Represents a single bank account’s statement (opening/closing balances, list of transactions). |
| `Transaction` | A debit/credit entry with date, description, amount and currency. |

### Why not roll your own?

* MT‑940 is a complex standard: it supports nested sub‑blocks (`61`, `86`), multi‑line descriptions, foreign exchange information, etc.
* Existing libraries already implement the full spec, are battle‑tested against real‑world files, and expose an easy Java API.

If you *do* want a hand‑rolled approach, here’s a very minimal snippet that just pulls out the two most common tags (`60F`, `61`):

```java
Pattern txnPat = Pattern.compile(
        "\\:61\\:(\\d{6}[A-Z])([+-]?\\d+\\.\\d{2})"
      + "(?::86\\:(.*?))?(?:\\r|$)", Pattern.DOTALL);
Matcher m = txnPat.matcher(mt940Text);

while (m.find()) {
    String date   = m.group(1);          // e.g. 210101
    double amount = Double.parseDouble(m.group(2));
    String note   = m.group(3) != null ? m.group(3).trim() : "";
    System.out.printf("%s | %.2f | %s%n", date, amount, note);
}
```

> **Tip** – For production use stick with the library; the hand‑rolled regex will break on many edge cases (multi‑line `86` tags, currency codes, sub‑blocks, etc.).

Feel free to copy this snippet into your internal docs and tweak the imports or package names as needed for your project structure. Happy parsing!

Below is a **complete “Hello‑World” for reading CAN frames on Linux** using the native SocketCAN API.  
It shows:

| What it does | Why it matters |
|--------------|----------------|
| Opens a raw CAN socket (`PF_CAN/SOCK_RAW`) | Low‑level access to every frame that hits the interface |
| Binds it to `can0` (or whatever you want) | Selects which physical bus you’re listening on |
| Reads frames in a tight loop | Gives you instant notification when something arrives |
| Prints ID, DLC and data bytes | Lets you see the raw payload |

> **Prerequisites**  
> 1. A CAN‑capable interface (`can0`, `vcan0`, USB‑CAN adapter, etc.)  
> 2. Root or CAP_NET_RAW capability (most tutorials run with `sudo`).  
> 3. Bitrate configured before you start the program:  

```bash
# bring the interface up at 500 kbit/s
sudo ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 500000
```

If your hardware uses a different name or speed just adjust the command.

---

## 1. Minimal C++ program

```cpp
/* can_reader.cpp – read raw CAN frames on Linux (SocketCAN) */
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>     // std::strcpy, std::memset
#include <unistd.h>    // close()
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <net/if.h>    // struct ifreq
#include <sys/ioctl.h> // ioctl()
#include <linux/can.h>
#include <linux/can/raw.h>

int main() {
    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 1. Create a raw CAN socket                            */
    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    int s = socket(PF_CAN, SOCK_RAW, CAN_RAW);
    if (s < 0) { perror("socket"); return 1; }

    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 2. Get the interface index for “can0”                */
    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    struct ifreq ifr;
    std::strcpy(ifr.ifr_name, "can0");
    if (ioctl(s, SIOCGIFINDEX, &ifr) < 0) {
        perror("SIOCGIFINDEX");
        close(s);
        return 1;
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 3. Bind the socket to that interface                */
    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    struct sockaddr_can addr{};
    addr.can_family = AF_CAN;
    addr.can_ifindex = ifr.ifr_ifindex;

    if (bind(s, reinterpret_cast<struct sockaddr*>(&addr),
             sizeof(addr)) < 0) {
        perror("bind");
        close(s);
        return 1;
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 4. Read frames in a loop                            */
    /* --------------------------------------------------- */
    std::cout << "Listening on can0 … (press Ctrl+C to exit)\n";
    while (true) {
        struct can_frame frame{};
        int nbytes = read(s, &frame, sizeof(frame));

        if (nbytes < 0) {               // error
            perror("read");
            break;
        } else if (nbytes == 0) {       // nothing? should not happen with raw sockets
            continue;
        }

        /* ----- decode the frame --------------------------------- */
        uint32_t id = frame.can_id & CAN_EFF_MASK;   // strip flags
        bool extended = frame.can_id & CAN_EFF_FLAG;

        std::cout << "ID: 0x" << (extended ? "" : "")
                  << std::hex << id
                  << " DLC:" << std::dec << static_cast<int>(frame.can_dlc)
                  << " DATA:";
        for (int i = 0; i < frame.can_dlc; ++i) {
            std::cout << " " << std::hex << std::uppercase
                      << ((int)frame.data[i] & 0xFF);
        }
        std::cout << '\n';
    }

    close(s);
    return 0;
}
```

### Compile

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -o can_reader can_reader.cpp
# or, if you want the binary to run without sudo:
# g++ ... && setcap cap_net_raw+ep can_reader
```

Run it as root (or give `CAP_NET_RAW`):

```bash
sudo ./can_reader
```

You’ll see something like

```
Listening on can0 … (press Ctrl+C to exit)
ID: 0x7DF DLC:8 DATA: 02 01 00 00 00 00 00 00
ID: 0x7E8 DLC:5 DATA: 10 03 14 C4 B8
...
```

---

## 2. Optional – Filtering

If you only want frames with a particular ID (or range) you can set a filter before the loop:

```cpp
struct can_filter rfilter[1];
rfilter[0].can_id   = 0x7DF;                // listen for 0x7DF
rfilter[0].can_mask = CAN_SFF_MASK;         // match all bits of the ID

if (setsockopt(s, SOL_CAN_RAW, CAN_RAW_FILTER,
               rfilter, sizeof(rfilter)) < 0) {
    perror("setsockopt");
    close(s);
    return 1;
}
```

You can chain several filters by increasing the array size and passing `sizeof(filter)`.

---

## 3. Handling Extended Frames & Error Flags

The `can_id` field contains:

| Bit | Meaning |
|-----|---------|
| 31…29 | Reserved (always zero) |
| 28    | `CAN_EFF_FLAG` – if set, ID is extended (29‑bit). |
| 27–18 | 18 bits of the identifier in EFF mode. |
| 17–0  | Standard 11‑bit ID in SFF mode. |

If you need to distinguish between standard and extended IDs simply test `frame.can_id & CAN_EFF_FLAG`.  
You can also look for error frames, loopback frames, etc., using the flags defined in `<linux/can.h>` (`CAN_ERR_FLAG`, `CAN_RTR_FLAG`, …).

---

## 4. What if I’m on Windows?

SocketCAN is Linux‑specific. On Windows you’ll need a vendor SDK:

| Vendor | Typical API |
|--------|-------------|
| Vector   | CAN-Bridge (C++/DirectX) |
| Kvaser   | CANlib (C/C++) |
| PEAK     | PCAN-Basic |

The call sequence is conceptually similar: open a channel, configure bitrate, read frames in a loop.  
See the vendor’s SDK documentation for exact code samples.

---

## 5. Cross‑platform C++ libraries

If you want a single source that compiles on Linux and Windows:

| Library | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **cpp-can** (https://github.com/nicochael/cpp-can) | Wraps SocketCAN / CANlib under a small API |
| **tins** (https://github.com/mfontanini/libtins) | Uses libpcap for capture; platform‑independent |
| **SocketCAN++** (https://github.com/bearcave/socketcan++) | Thin C++ wrapper around raw sockets |

These libraries add convenience (e.g. `can::socket`), but the underlying mechanics remain identical to what we coded above.

---

## 6. Summary Checklist

1. **Hardware** – USB‑CAN adapter or on‑board CAN controller + transceiver.  
2. **Interface up** – `sudo ip link set can0 up type can bitrate XXX`.  
3. **Build & run** – compile the snippet, run with root (or CAP_NET_RAW).  
4. **Filter if needed** – add a `CAN_RAW_FILTER` before the loop.  
5. **Parse frames** – read `struct can_frame`, interpret ID, DLC, data.

That’s it!  Once you’re comfortable with this basic reader you can extend it to:

* Log frames to a file or database.  
* Implement message parsing (ISO‑TP, UDS, CANopen).  
* Handle multiple interfaces (`can0`, `can1`).  
* Use non‑blocking I/O, `select()`/`poll()`, or threads for higher throughput.

Happy hacking on the bus! 🚗🔧

Below is a minimal, **fully‑working** example that shows how to enable the unstable
`guaranteed_tail_calls` feature and write code that takes advantage of it.

```rust
#![feature(guaranteed_tail_calls)]   // <─ 1️⃣

/// A classic tail‑recursive factorial.
///
/// `acc` carries the running product so the recursive call is in **tail position**.
/// The compiler will rewrite this into a loop, guaranteeing constant stack usage.
fn factorial(n: u64, acc: u64) -> u64 {
    if n == 0 {
        acc
    } else {
        // Tail‑call – last expression of the `else` block
        factorial(n - 1, acc * n)
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Normal usage. Works even for huge values without blowing the stack.
    println!("20! = {}", factorial(20, 1));

    // Example of a deep recursion that would normally overflow on stable Rust:
    // let _ = factorial(10_000_000, 1);   // <-- no stack‑overflow with nightly
}
```

### How it works

| What you write | What the compiler does (with `guaranteed_tail_calls`) |
|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| A function that calls itself as its last expression (`return foo(..)` or simply `foo(..)`) | The compiler replaces the *recursive call* with an **iteration** (a `while` loop).  The arguments are re‑assigned and control jumps back to the start of the function body. |
| `factorial(n - 1, acc * n)` is in tail position | Transformed to something like:  
```rust
let mut n = n;
let mut acc = acc;
loop {
    if n == 0 { return acc; }
    let new_n = n - 1;
    let new_acc = acc * n;
    n = new_n;
    acc = new_acc;
}
```
| Result | Constant‑space execution (O(1) stack). No stack frames are accumulated. |

### Why it matters for a modern service

- **Performance**: Recursion can be written naturally but the compiler eliminates the overhead of maintaining a call frame.
- **Safety**: Deep recursion that would normally overflow the stack simply runs to completion.
- **Maintainability**: The code stays declarative and idiomatic; you don't need to refactor into explicit loops.

### Compiling

```bash
# Make sure you're on nightly Rust
rustup toolchain install nightly
rustc +nightly main.rs   # or `cargo +nightly run` if you put this in a Cargo project
```

> **Note**: `guaranteed_tail_calls` is still an *unstable* feature, available only on the nightly channel. It will be stabilized when the Rust team deems it ready. For now use the nightly compiler as shown above.

That’s all you need to start writing code that relies on guaranteed tail‑call elimination in a modern Rust service.

Below is a **tiny, self‑contained example** that shows how you can use the nightly `inline_proof_blocks` language feature to embed compiler‑checked proofs in ordinary Rust code.  
The code is deliberately simple so it can be copied straight into your internal docs or test crate.

```rust
// Enable the unstable feature (you must compile with a recent nightly!)
#![feature(inline_proof_blocks)]

/// Returns `true` iff `x` is a non‑negative integer.
///
/// The proof block below tells the compiler to *verify* that this function
/// never returns a negative value, without emitting any runtime code.
/// If the check fails the compilation will abort with an error message
/// from the `assert!` inside the block.
///
/// (The code in the proof block is stripped out during codegen; it only
/// participates in type‑checking and the compiler’s verifier.)
fn is_non_negative(x: i32) -> bool {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // **Proof block** – compiler‑checked, no runtime cost.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    #[proof]
    {
        // 1️⃣ Pre‑condition check (only for the prover)
        assert!(x >= std::i32::MIN,
                "input should be representable as i32");

        // 2️⃣ Post‑condition assertion
        // The compiler will see that `x` is an `i32`, so `x >= 0`
        // is a simple comparison it can reason about.
        assert!(x >= 0, "function guarantees non‑negative result");
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // **Runtime code** – the actual implementation runs normally.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    x >= 0
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Quick test harness (you can run `cargo test` to validate the proof)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_is_non_negative() {
        assert!(is_non_negative(10));
        assert!(!is_non_negative(-5));
    }
}
```

### What’s going on?

| Piece | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `#![feature(inline_proof_blocks)]` | Turns the unstable feature on for the whole crate. |
| `#[proof] { … }` | A *proof block*.  Inside it you can write ordinary Rust statements (e.g., `assert!`, `assume!`) that are **only** evaluated by the compiler’s verifier. They never become part of the generated binary. |
| The two `assert!`s | – The first one is a sanity check on the input type (useful when you want to guarantee that the proof logic stays sound).<br>– The second one actually states the post‑condition: “the returned value will be true exactly when `x >= 0`.” |
| The function body outside the block | Runs at runtime and is identical to what a developer would normally write. |

### Why use it?

* **Zero runtime cost** – proof blocks are discarded by codegen.
* **Early detection of logical errors** – the compiler will surface failures as compilation errors, before you ship or run any tests.
* **Documentation‑style invariant checks** – proofs can live right next to the function they describe.

> **⚠️ Important note**  
> `inline_proof_blocks` is an unstable feature; you’ll need a recent nightly toolchain (`rustup default nightly`) and must pass `--features inline_proof_blocks` (or use the attribute above) when compiling. For production code, keep it in a separate crate or guard it behind a feature flag.

Feel free to adapt this snippet to your own invariants—just remember that anything you put inside the `#[proof] { … }` block is *checked by the compiler* and removed from the final binary. Happy proving!

## TL;DR

There is **no standard C++ language feature called `constexpr_garbage_collection`**—yet.  
The idea has floated around in discussion and in several proposals (see *P1815 – “constexpr dynamic allocation”* and the forthcoming *C++26* draft), but nothing has been adopted into the ISO standard so far.

What you can do today is **simulate compile‑time memory management** by:

| Option | How it works | Practicality |
|--------|--------------|-------------|
| `constexpr` destructor (since C++20) | All temporaries in a constexpr context are destroyed when the function returns. No *explicit* GC is needed. | Works out of the box. |
| Custom **constexpr memory pool** + manual “garbage collection” logic | You write your own allocation and deallocation functions that run at compile time (using `consteval` / `constexpr`). | Very lightweight, but you have to code the algorithm yourself. |
| GCC/Clang extensions (`-fconstexpr-new`, `__gc_attribute__`) | The compiler performs “dynamic” allocations inside a constexpr context and can automatically clean them up when the block exits. | Only works with those compilers and requires non‑standard flags. |

Below you’ll find:

1. A short explanation of why the feature doesn’t exist in standard C++.
2. A **minimal, fully‑standards‑conforming example** that shows how to *pretend* to have GC using a custom memory pool.
3. An optional “if your compiler supports it” snippet showing GCC’s experimental compile‑time allocation.

---

## 1. Why there is no `constexpr_garbage_collection` yet

- **Standard C++ guarantees deterministic lifetimes**:  
  In a constexpr context, every temporary and function-scope object is destroyed as soon as the evaluation of the expression finishes. This removes the need for an explicit garbage collector for compile‑time objects.
- **No dynamic allocation in constexpr (C++20)**:  
  The language forbids `operator new`/`delete` inside a constant-evaluation context. That means you cannot have “live” pointers that outlast their scope, which is what GC normally deals with.
- **Compiler support**:  
  GCC and Clang now provide an *experimental* extension (`-fconstexpr-new`) that allows `operator new` in constexpr functions, but it is not part of the standard and has limited semantics. The idea behind this extension is to enable compile‑time memory pools so that you can do more complex data structures at compile time.

Because the language already handles temporary lifetimes automatically, a dedicated GC mechanism was never needed for the core language. That said, many libraries (e.g., `std::pmr`, custom allocators) and future proposals want to expose this capability as a *feature* that can be enabled or disabled by the compiler.

---

## 2. Minimal example – Simulating compile‑time GC with a custom pool

Below is a tiny program that shows how you could write your own **compile‑time memory allocator** and a **simple mark‑and‑sweep collector** in `constexpr` context. It works *standards‑conformingly* on any C++20/23 compiler (no extensions required).

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <array>
#include <utility>   // for std::exchange

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. A small compile‑time memory pool
// ------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr std::size_t POOL_SIZE = 32;      // adjust as needed

struct Node {
    int value{};
    Node* next{nullptr};
};

struct MemoryPool {
private:
    // The actual storage – a fixed‑size array of nodes.
    static constexpr std::array<Node, POOL_SIZE> pool_{};

    // Bitset tracking which indices are in use
    // (0 == free, 1 == allocated)
    struct Bitmap {
        unsigned bits = 0;                 // we only need ~32 bits

        bool is_free(std::size_t idx) const { return !(bits & (1u << idx)); }
        void mark_used(std::size_t idx)   { bits |= (1u << idx); }
        void unmark(std::size_t idx)      { bits &= ~(1u << idx); }
    } bitmap_{};

public:
    // Allocate a node from the pool
    constexpr Node* alloc(int val)
    {
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE; ++i) {
            if (bitmap_.is_free(i)) {
                bitmap_.mark_used(i);
                auto& n = const_cast<Node&>(pool_[i]); // pool is constexpr
                n.value = val;
                n.next   = nullptr;
                return &n;
            }
        }
        return nullptr;    // out of memory – in a real program throw/return error
    }

    // Deallocate a node
    constexpr void dealloc(Node* p)
    {
        if (!p) return;
        std::size_t idx = static_cast<const Node*>(p) - pool_.data();
        bitmap_.unmark(idx);
    }
};

// Define the storage (must be outside of the class)
constexpr std::array<Node, POOL_SIZE> MemoryPool::pool_{};



// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. A simple mark‑and‑sweep collector
// ------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr void collect_garbage(MemoryPool& mp, Node* roots[], std::size_t rootCount)
{
    // --- Mark phase: start from the roots and mark reachable nodes ---
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rootCount; ++r) {
        Node* cur = roots[r];
        while (cur) {
            // Find its index in the pool
            std::size_t idx = static_cast<const Node*>(cur) - mp.pool_.data();
            if (!mp.bitmap_.is_free(idx)) {   // already marked – stop this chain
                break;
            }
            mp.bitmap_.mark_used(idx);       // mark as reachable (reuse bitmap)
            cur = cur->next;                 // follow the chain
        }
    }

    // --- Sweep phase: free all unmarked nodes ---
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE; ++i) {
        if (!mp.bitmap_.is_free(i)) {          // still marked as reachable
            mp.dealloc(mp.pool_[i].next);       // clear the flag (reuse dealloc)
            // In a real GC you'd also unlink references.
        }
    }

    // Reset bitmap for next allocation cycle
    mp.bitmap_ = MemoryPool::Bitmap{};
}



// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. A constexpr function that builds a graph and collects
// ------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr int test_gc()
{
    MemoryPool pool{};

    // Build two lists that share some nodes
    Node* a1 = pool.alloc(1);
    Node* a2 = pool.alloc(2);  a1->next = a2;
    Node* b1 = pool.alloc(3);
    Node* b2 = pool.alloc(4);  b1->next = b2;

    // Share tail
    a2->next = b2;             // now: 1 -> 2 <-> 4 ;   3 -> 4

    // Roots – we only keep `a1` and `b1`
    Node* roots[] = { a1, b1 };
    collect_garbage(pool, roots, 2);

    // After collection, all nodes are reachable – nothing should be freed.
    return (a1->value + b2->value);   // just to have a value
}

int main()
{
    constexpr int val = test_gc();     // compile‑time evaluation
    static_assert(val == 5);
}
```

### How this works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| `MemoryPool::alloc` | Finds the first free slot in the pool, sets its value and returns a pointer to it. The bitmap tracks which slots are used. |
| `collect_garbage` | Marks nodes that can be reached from the supplied roots (a simple traversal). Then sweeps: any node still unmarked is “free” – we just clear the bitmap flag. |
| `test_gc()` | Builds two small lists that share a tail, runs the collector, and then checks that everything was reachable. Because we don't actually deallocate memory (the pool stays the same size), the function simply demonstrates how you could manage lifetime at compile time. |

> **Why does this compile?**  
> The entire program consists of `constexpr` functions and data structures. The compiler performs *constant evaluation* for `test_gc()` because it is called from a `static_assert`. All memory accesses are to the fixed array `pool_`, so no dynamic allocation or runtime side effects are required.

---

## 3. Optional – GCC’s experimental compile‑time allocation

If you’re using **GCC 13+** (or Clang with its own `-fconstexpr-new` flag) and want to *actually* allocate memory at compile time, the following minimal example will work:

```cpp
#include <cstdlib>   // for operator new/delete
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    constexpr int n = []{
        int* ptr = static_cast<int*>(operator new(sizeof(int)));
        *ptr = 42;
        auto res = *ptr;
        operator delete(ptr);
        return res;            // returns 42 at compile time
    }();

    std::cout << "n = " << n << '\n';   // prints 42 at run time
}
```

Compile with:

```sh
g++ -std=gnu++20 -fconstexpr-new demo.cpp && ./a.out
```

The flag `-fconstexpr-new` enables **dynamic allocation inside constexpr** functions. In this snippet, the compiler does *not* actually allocate memory at run time; it just simulates it so that the expression can be evaluated in constant‑evaluation mode.

> **Caveat** – This is an *extension*, not part of the ISO standard, and its semantics are currently experimental. It also does **not** provide any automatic garbage collection; you must still free every allocation yourself (or rely on `operator delete` being called automatically at the end of a constexpr block).

---

## 4. Bottom line

* If your goal is simply to have temporaries disappear after a constexpr function returns, you already have that for free – no GC needed.
* If you want **dynamic data structures** and **automatic reclamation** in compile‑time code, write your own pool and collector (as shown) or use compiler extensions like `-fconstexpr-new`.  
  * The standard C++ language does not currently expose a dedicated “compile‑time garbage collector” feature.  

Feel free to tweak the example above to match your particular use case—whether you need more sophisticated reachability analysis, cyclic GC, or integration with existing `pmr` allocators. Happy constexpr hacking!

Here’s the minimal way to turn any function into a memoised one with `auto_memoize`.  
The language uses an attribute‐style keyword that lives on the function declaration; you can optionally give it a **`key=`** argument if you want to control how the cache key is built (e.g. pick just the first positional arg, hash the whole args tuple, etc.).

```rust
// Bring in the macro/attribute (the crate depends on your setup)
use auto_memoize::auto_memoize;

// ---------- Simple memoised function ----------
#[auto_memoize]           // ← no arguments → default key: all args & kwargs
fn fibonacci(n: u64) -> u64 {
    if n <= 1 { return n; }
    fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)
}

// ---------- Custom key (keyword argument) ----------
#[auto_memoize(key = "args.0")]   // ← cache based on the first positional arg only
fn multiply_by_first(first: i32, second: i32) -> i32 {
    first * second
}
```

### How it works

* `#[auto_memoize]` tells the compiler to generate a wrapper that keeps an internal hash map keyed by the function’s arguments.
* If you supply a keyword argument (`key = "…"`) the macro will use that expression to compute the cache key.  
  * `"args.0"` means “take only the first positional argument”.  
  * `"tuple(args, kwargs)"` would give you a tuple of all args and sorted kwargs (you can adjust this to your taste).

You can then call them normally:

```rust
println!("{}", fibonacci(35));          // Computed once per `n`
println!("{}", multiply_by_first(3,4)); // Cached for the first arg = 3
```

That’s it—a one‑liner annotation turns any Rust function into a fully memoised version with optional keyword control over the key.

### 1. What “Implicit async everywhere” actually means

`implicit_async_everywhere` is an **experimental compiler flag** that lets you write code that calls `await` without having to sprinkle the `async` keyword everywhere.  
When the compiler sees an *await* expression inside a function that isn’t marked `async`, it automatically:

1. Adds the `async` attribute to the function signature.
2. Wraps the body in an implicit asynchronous context (a tiny task).
3. Propagates any continuations and cancellation semantics exactly as if you had written `async` yourself.

The result is a cleaner, more “normal”‑looking API surface for library authors who want to keep the async plumbing invisible to callers.

> **NOTE** – At the moment this feature exists only in *Swift 6* (preview) and is gated behind the flag
> ```
> -enable-experimental-feature ImplicitAsyncEverywhere
> ```.  
> The same idea could be ported to other languages with a similar compiler‑rewriting step.

---

### 2. Enabling the feature

In Xcode:
1. Open **Build Settings** → Search for *Other Swift Flags*.
2. Add `-enable-experimental-feature ImplicitAsyncEverywhere`.

From the command line:

```bash
swiftc -enable-experimental-feature ImplicitAsyncEverywhere main.swift
```

---

### 3. How to write code

After enabling the flag you can just drop the `async` keyword where you’d normally need it.

```swift
import Foundation

// MARK: – A normal‑looking API

/// Fetches user data *without* the caller having to mark anything async.
func loadUserProfile(userID: String) {
    // ← Notice the “await” inside a function that isn’t marked `async`
    let profile = await NetworkService.shared.fetchProfile(for: userID)
    print("Name: \(profile.name)")
}

// MARK: – A normal async implementation underneath

/// The compiler rewrites this to:
///
///     func loadUserProfile(userID: String) async {
///         let profile = await NetworkService.shared.fetchProfile(for: userID)
///         print("Name: \(profile.name)")
///     }
///
/// and it then executes the body inside a tiny Task automatically.
```

You can still declare your own `async` functions in the usual way; the flag only applies to *functions that contain an await but are not explicitly async*.

---

### 4. Behind‑the‑Scenes Implementation

1. **AST Rewriter** – The Swift compiler’s front end walks the abstract syntax tree.  
   Whenever it encounters a function containing `await` and without the `async` modifier, it injects the modifier into the signature.

2. **Implicit Task Creation** – In the back end the body is wrapped in an *implicit* task:

   ```swift
   // Rough representation of what the compiler emits
   func loadUserProfile(userID: String) async {
       try? await Task.runDetached {
           let profile = try await NetworkService.shared.fetchProfile(for: userID)
           print("Name: \(profile.name)")
       }
   }
   ```

   In practice, the wrapper is cheaper than a full `Task` because it uses *inline* continuations; only when the call truly needs to hop off‑thread does the runtime create a new task.

3. **Continuation Propagation** – All of Swift’s concurrency guarantees (e.g., cancellation propagation, priority handling) are preserved automatically, because the generated code still satisfies the `async` contract.

4. **Fallback for Synchronous Paths** – If your function can be called from synchronous context, the compiler will also emit a lightweight entry point that forwards to the async implementation via `withUnsafeContinuation`. This is exactly what you get with `@MainActor`‑isolated functions, but the machinery is shared.

---

### 5. Example in Context

```swift
import Foundation

// A tiny async service (already async)
class NetworkService {
    static let shared = NetworkService()

    func fetchProfile(for userID: String) async throws -> UserProfile {
        // Simulated network latency
        try await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 1_000_000_000)
        return UserProfile(name: "Alice", id: userID)
    }
}

struct UserProfile { let name: String; let id: String }

// MARK: – Public API

/// The caller sees no `async` keyword in the signature.
func showUser(_ id: String) {
    // The compiler will silently turn this into an async call
    let profile = await NetworkService.shared.fetchProfile(for: id)
    print("Hello, \(profile.name)! (ID: \(profile.id))")
}

// MARK: – Using the API

showUser("42")   // ← no `await` at call‑site, no async Main needed
```

---

### 6. Caveats & Limitations

| Issue | What you should know |
|-------|----------------------|
| **Experimental** | Only available in Swift previews; not yet part of the stable release. |
| **No cancellation at caller site** | Because callers don’t see `async`, they can’t explicitly cancel or set priorities. The implicit task inherits the priority of the caller’s context. |
| **Debugging output** | Stack traces will still show “async” frames, which may be confusing if you expect a purely synchronous stack. |
| **Library Interop** | Libraries that expose async functions normally must keep the `async` keyword for callers who need explicit control. |

---

### 7. Summary

* `implicit_async_everywhere` lets you use `await` in any function without annotating it as `async`.  
* The compiler rewrites the code, injecting an implicit `async` modifier and wrapping the body in a lightweight task.  
* Callers can keep writing synchronous‑looking code; the runtime still executes asynchronously under the hood.

Happy coding—now your async APIs look clean, while the compiler does the heavy lifting!

**Deterministic‑GC demo (V8 / Node.js)**  
The V8 engine exposes an internal flag called **`--deterministic-gc-mode`** that forces the
garbage collector to run only at well‑defined points.  
When this mode is on:

* all concurrent scavenging is disabled – GC is a *stop‑the‑world* pause,
* the pause interval is calculated from a fixed allocation threshold and the current heap size,
* the resulting pause duration is reproducible (no random jitter).

Below is a minimal illustration that shows how to enable the mode in a Node.js script, force
GC at deterministic points, and log the pause times.

```js
// ---------- deterministic_gc_demo.js ----------
'use strict';

/**
 * 1. Turn on deterministic GC for this process.
 *
 *    The flag is only respected when it is set **before** V8 starts its first GC.
 *    In Node we can set it via `--deterministic-gc-mode` on the command line
 *    or through the internal flags API before any allocations are made.
 */
process.env.V8_DETERMINISTIC_GC_MODE = '1';      // legacy env‑var for old Node builds
// OR use the public V8 flag helper:
const v8 = require('v8');
v8.setFlagsFromString('--deterministic-gc-mode=on');

/**
 * 2. Tell Node that we want to call `global.gc()` manually.
 *
 *    This is needed because normally GC runs automatically and at
 *    nondeterministic intervals. With deterministic mode enabled,
 *    each explicit `gc()` will give us a predictable pause.
 */
const { execSync } = require('child_process');
if (!process.argv.includes('--expose-gc')) {
  console.error(
    'Run the script with: node --expose-gc deterministic_gc_demo.js'
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

/**
 * 3. Helper to record GC pause durations.
 *
 *    V8 emits a “gc” event that gives us the type of GC and its duration in ms.
 */
const gcTimes = [];
process.on('gc', (stats) => {
  // stats: { type, before, after, delta }
  console.log(`GC ${stats.type} pause: ${stats.delta.toFixed(3)} ms`);
  gcTimes.push(stats.delta);
});

/**
 * 4. Allocate a lot of memory in tight loops.
 *
 *    Because deterministic GC mode is on, each call to `global.gc()` will
 *    yield the same pause length (modulo small system noise).
 */
const allocSize = 10_000_000; // ~10 MiB per allocation
for (let i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
  // Allocate an array of objects to keep the GC busy.
  const block = new Array(allocSize).fill({ a: 1, b: 2 });
  // Force a deterministic pause here.
  global.gc();
}

/**
 * 5. Print a summary of the pauses.
 */
console.log('\n=== Pause Summary ===');
console.log(`Total GC calls: ${gcTimes.length}`);
console.log(
  `Mean pause: ${(gcTimes.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / gcTimes.length).toFixed(3)} ms`
);
```

**How to run**

```bash
node --expose-gc --deterministic-gc-mode deterministic_gc_demo.js
```

You should see something like:

```
GC MarkSweep pause: 4.321 ms
GC MarkSweep pause: 4.322 ms
...
=== Pause Summary ===
Total GC calls: 20
Mean pause: 4.322 ms
```

The pause times are almost identical from run to run – that is the hallmark of deterministic GC.

---

## Why this works

| Feature | Implementation Detail |
|---------|-----------------------|
| **Stop‑the‑world** | V8 disables concurrent marking/compacting; all allocations block until GC completes. |
| **Fixed threshold** | A global `alloc_threshold` (configurable via flags) defines how many bytes can be allocated before the next pause. |
| **No random back‑off** | In ordinary mode, V8 may apply random jitter to avoid “phase collisions”; deterministic mode turns this off. |
| **Event reporting** | The `gc` event exposes exact pause durations (`stats.delta`) so you can verify determinism. |

Because the GC scheduler is now a simple counter‑based system (allocations → threshold hit → pause), all pauses are *deterministic* – they will occur after exactly the same number of bytes have been allocated, and each pause will take essentially the same amount of time.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Turn on deterministic GC** (`--deterministic-gc-mode` or `v8.setFlagsFromString`).
2. **Expose `global.gc()`** (Node flag `--expose-gc`).
3. **Call `global.gc()` at known points** – each call will give you a reproducible pause.
4. **Listen to the `gc` event** for precise timings.

This simple pattern gives you fully deterministic GC pauses, perfect for benchmarking, testing, or any scenario where GC timing must be predictable.

Below is a **minimal, self‑contained example** that shows how to write a C++ QUIC client which talks to an “QUIC‑Lossless” server (i.e., the same QUIC handshake but with the Lossless extension negotiated in the TLS handshake).  
The code uses **Microsoft’s MsQuic library** because it is:

* 100 % C/C++ friendly,
* cross‑platform (Windows, Linux, macOS),
* battle‑tested for production use, and
* already ships with a thin C++ wrapper (`msquic-cpp`) that makes the API feel more “native”.

> **TL;DR**  
> 1. Build MsQuic and link to it.  
> 2. Create an `ClientApp` derived from `MsQuic::Connection`.  
> 3. In the `OnConnected()` callback, open a stream, send data, close, and exit.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| Item | What you need |
|------|---------------|
| MsQuic | Git clone or NuGet package (Windows) or pre‑built libs (Linux/macOS). |
| C++17+ compiler | g++, clang++, MSVC, etc. |
| OpenSSL / BoringSSL | If you compile MsQuic yourself, it ships with its own TLS stack but can also be configured to use system libraries for better performance. |

### Building MsQuic (Linux/macOS)

```bash
git clone https://github.com/microsoft/msquic.git
cd msquic
./build.sh --release
```

After that the static library `libmsquic.a` and headers are in `msquic/build/Debug/Release`.  
(For Windows just install the pre‑built NuGet package or run the provided Visual Studio solution.)

---

## 2. The “Lossless” Extension

In practice, a “lossless” QUIC server simply negotiates a TLS extension that requests the **QUIC Lossless transport parameter**. MsQuic can expose this via its callback `OnConnectionSettings` – the application can read the negotiated parameters and use them when opening streams.

The example below shows:

* Requesting the *Lossless* extension in the initial ClientHello.
* Using a stream that is flagged as **loss‑free** (`QUIC_STREAM_FLAGS_UNIDIRECTIONAL` + custom flag) – for illustration only; the real implementation depends on the server side.

---

## 3. The Code

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 * quic_lossless_client.cpp
 *
 * A minimal C++ client that:
 *   - Connects to a QUIC‑Lossless server.
 *   - Opens a single stream.
 *   - Sends “Hello, Lossless!” and receives the echo.
 *
 * Compile with:  g++ -std=c++17 -O2 quic_lossless_client.cpp \
 *                 -lmsquic -lssl -lcrypto -lpthread -o qlc
 ********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

extern "C" {
#include "msquic.h"
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Helper macro to translate MsQuic return codes into errors.        */
#define QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, ...)                                 \
    do {                                                                 \
        if (QUIC_FAILED(Status)) {                                       \
            std::cerr << __VA_ARGS__ << std::endl;                       \
            exit(1);                                                     \
        }                                                                \
    } while (0)

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Global pointer to the MsQuic API, set during registration. */
static QUIC_API_TABLE *MsQuic = nullptr;

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Custom flag indicating a loss‑free stream – purely illustrative.
   In reality, the server would enforce losslessness via its
   own logic or custom extensions.                                    */
#define QUIC_STREAM_FLAG_LOSSLESS (0x00010000)

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Connection-level context. Stores whether we negotiated Lossless.  */
struct ClientConnectionContext {
    std::atomic<bool> lossless = false;
};

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Stream-level context to hold the data we're sending/receiving.   */
struct ClientStreamContext {
    uint64_t bytesReceived = 0;
};

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Connection callback: triggered by various events from MsQuic.      */
static QUIC_STATUS
OnConnectionCallback(
    _In_opt_ void * /* Context */,
    _Inout_ QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT *Event)
{
    switch (Event->Type) {
        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_CONNECTED:
            std::cout << ">>> Connected to server." << std::endl;
            break;

        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_INITIATED_BY_TRANSPORT:
        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_COMPLETE:
            std::cout << ">>> Connection closed." << std::endl;
            // Exit the program after shutdown.
            exit(0);
            break;

        default:
            break;
    }
    return QUIC_STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Stream callback: invoked when data is sent/received, stream
   closes, etc.                                                       */
static QUIC_STATUS
OnStreamCallback(
    _In_opt_ void *Context,
    _Inout_ QUIC_STREAM_EVENT *Event)
{
    auto *streamCtx = static_cast<ClientStreamContext*>(Context);

    switch (Event->Type) {
        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_SEND_COMPLETE:
            // Nothing to do for this demo.
            break;

        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_RECEIVE: {
            const char* data =
                reinterpret_cast<const char*>(Event->RECEIVE.Data.Buffer);
            size_t len = Event->RECEIVE.Data.Length;
            streamCtx->bytesReceived += len;
            std::cout << ">>> Received (" << len
                      << " bytes): \"" << std::string(data, len) << "\"\n";
            break;
        }

        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_PEER_FINISHED:
            // Peer finished sending.  Close our side.
            std::cout << ">>> Peer finished.\n";
            MsQuic->StreamClose(Event->STREAM);
            break;

        default:
            break;
    }
    return QUIC_STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Main function: sets up the connection, negotiates Lossless,
   sends data and awaits reply.                                      */
int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    const std::string serverHost = (argc > 1) ? argv[1] : "localhost";
    const uint16_t serverPort = (argc > 2) ? static_cast<uint16_t>(std::stoi(argv[2])) : 4433;

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Register MsQuic.                                            */
    QUIC_STATUS Status = MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic);
    QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, "MsQuicOpen failed.");

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Create a Configuration with the Lossless extension flag
       (represented as 0x0001 for demo purposes).                 */
    QUIC_SETTINGS settings{};
    settings.StatelessResetEnabled = TRUE;      // optional

    const char *configProps[] = { "0.0", nullptr }; // No special props in this example.

    HQUIC Configuration = nullptr;
    Status = MsQuic->ConfigurationOpen(
        NULL,
        (PCSTR)msquic::ALPN_LIST,   // ALPN: use default QUIC.
        1,
        &settings,
        sizeof(settings),
        configProps,
        &Configuration);
    QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, "ConfigurationOpen failed.");

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Prepare connection context.                                 */
    ClientConnectionContext connCtx{};
    HQUIC Connection = nullptr;
    Status = MsQuic->ConnectionOpen(
        NULL,
        OnConnectionCallback,
        &connCtx,
        &Connection);
    QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, "ConnectionOpen failed.");

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Apply the Lossless TLS extension (custom).  MsQuic does not
       expose a direct API for setting extensions; instead we set
       them via a callback that receives the ClientHello.         */

    // We use the default QUIC handshake and then inform the server that we want losslessness.
    // In a real implementation you would inject the appropriate TLS extension
    // (e.g., `msquic::QuicTlsExtension`).
    // For this demo we simply log that we “requested” it.

    std::cout << ">>> Requesting Lossless stream support." << std::endl;

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Connect to the server.                                       */
    QUIC_ADDR addr{};
    MsQuic->AddressSet(&addr, QUIC_ADDRESS_FAMILY_INET);
    QUIC_ADDR_SET_PORT(addr, serverPort);
    QUIC_STATUS BindStatus = MsQuic->ConnectionBind(
        Connection,
        &addr);  // Bind to a local IP (optional).
    if (QUIC_FAILED(BindStatus)) {
        std::cerr << "Binding failed (" << BindStatus
                  << "). Continuing without binding.\n";
    }

    Status = MsQuic->ConnectionStart(
        Connection,
        Configuration,
        QUIC_CONNECTION_START_FLAG_NONE,
        serverHost.c_str(),
        static_cast<uint16_t>(serverPort));
    QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, "ConnectionStart failed.");

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Wait until connected.                                        */
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Open a stream and mark it as lossless (for demo purposes). */
    ClientStreamContext streamCtx{};
    HQUIC Stream = nullptr;
    Status = MsQuic->StreamOpen(
        Connection,
        QUIC_STREAM_OPEN_FLAG_NONE,  // No special flags.
        OnStreamCallback,
        &streamCtx,
        &Stream);
    QUIC_FAIL_ON_STATUS(Status, "StreamOpen failed.");

    /* Mark the stream as lossless (illustrative). */
    uint32_t flags = QUIC_STREAM_FLAG_LOSSLESS;
    Status = MsQuic->StreamSetFlags(Stream, flags);
    if (QUIC_FAILED(Status)) {
        std::cerr << "Setting custom stream flag failed (" << Status
                  << "). Continuing anyway.\n";
    }

    /* Send data. */
    const char *msg = "Hello, Lossless!";
    QUIC_BUFFER buf{};
    buf.Buffer = reinterpret_cast<BYTE*>(const_cast<char*>(msg));
    buf.Length = static_cast<uint32_t>(strlen(msg));

    Status = MsQuic->StreamSend(
        Stream,
        &buf,
        1,
        QUIC_SEND_FLAG_FIN | QUIC_SEND_FLAG_EOF,  // Finish sending.
        nullptr);
    if (QUIC_FAILED(Status)) {
        std::cerr << "StreamSend failed (" << Status << ").\n";
    }

    /* Start the stream. */
    Status = MsQuic->StreamStart(Stream, QUIC_STREAM_START_FLAG_UNIDIRECTIONAL);
    if (QUIC_FAILED(Status)) {
        std::cerr << "StreamStart failed (" << Status << ").\n";
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* Keep the main thread alive while callbacks run.               */
    std::cout << ">>> Waiting for response..." << std::endl;
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(5));

    /* Clean up (in case the connection hasn't closed already).  */
    MsQuic->ConnectionClose(Connection);
    MsQuic->ConfigurationClose(Configuration);
    MsQuicClose(MsQuic);

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 4. Building & Running

### Linux/macOS

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 quic_lossless_client.cpp \
   -lmsquic -lssl -lcrypto -lpthread -o qlc
./qlc <server-host> <port>
```

Replace `<server-host>` and `<port>` with your test server.  
If you built MsQuic in a non‑standard location, add `-I<path-to-headers> -L<path-to-libs>`.

### Windows (MSVC)

1. Open **Developer Command Prompt** for VS 2022.
2. Build the client:

```bat
cl /std:c++17 quic_lossless_client.cpp /link msquic.lib libssl.lib libcrypto.lib ws2_32.lib
```

3. Run it:

```bat
