However, there is an interesting approach using the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) to perform sorting, but it's not efficient compared to traditional sorting algorithms like QuickSort, MergeSort, etc. Also, this method doesn't utilize quantum computing principles; hence, "Quantum-Free" would be accurate if we're talking about avoiding quantum computing.

In classical computing, one can theoretically use the Fourier Transform to sort an array, but it's impractical and inefficient for sorting purposes due to high computational complexity compared to conventional sorting algorithms. The idea involves transforming the data into frequency space, manipulating the frequencies in such a way that when transformed back, they correspond to a sorted sequence.

If you're looking for sorting an array of doubles efficiently using classical computing, I would recommend one of the well-known sorting algorithms like QuickSort or MergeSort. Here is an example implementation of QuickSort in C++:

### Example: QuickSort Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Function to swap two elements
void swap(double& a, double& b) {
    double temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

// Partitioning function
int partition(std::vector<double>& arr, int low, int high) {
    double pivot = arr[high]; // Choosing the last element as pivot
    int i = (low - 1); // Index of smaller element

    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++; // Increment index of smaller element
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}

// QuickSort function
void quicksort(std::vector<double>& arr, int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        // Partitioning index
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

        // Recursively sort elements before and after partition
        quicksort(arr, low, pi - 1);
        quicksort(arr, pi + 1, high);
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> arr = {10.5, 7.2, 8.9, 3.6, 4.1};
    int n = arr.size();

    std::cout << "Original array: ";
    for (double x : arr) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << std::endl;

    quicksort(arr, 0, n - 1);

    std::cout << "Sorted array: ";
    for (double x : arr) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- **Swap Function**: Swaps two elements.
- **Partition Function**: This function takes the last element as pivot, places the pivot element at its correct position in sorted array, and places all smaller (smaller than pivot) to left of pivot and all greater elements to right of pivot.
- **Quicksort Function**: Recursively applies the partitioning logic on subarrays formed by partition.

