Parsing SWIFT MT940 (Machine Readable Financial Message) files can be quite complex due to their structured yet often irregular format. Below is a simplified Java example using regex patterns and basic string manipulation to extract some information from an MT940 file.

This example assumes that the MT940 message is in ASCII format, which is common for such files. Note that this is a basic implementation and may need adjustments based on the specific structure of your SWIFT MT940 messages.

```java
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class SwiftMT940Parser {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String mt940Content = "20C16738116517538688460514264261350916602630603902179990152\n" +
                "940 16738116517538688460514264261350916602630603902179990152\n" +
                "20C16738116517538688460514264261350916602630603902179990152\n" +
                "MT940 Example File\n" +
                "Your Name ABC Corp\n" +
                "Bank Code 123\n" +
                "Account Number 987654321\n" +
                "\n" +
                ":20:REFERENCE ID\n" +
                ":25:Narrative Header\n" +
                ":28C:Credited Amount EUR 100.00\n" +
                ":60F:Credited Account DE98ZZZ1234567890\n" +
                ":61:Payment details\n" +
                "   CREDitor ID: ABCDEFGHIJKL\n" +
                "   Reference: 1234567890\n" +
                ":86:Additional information\n" +
                "\n" +
                "End of message";

        parseMT940(mt940Content);
    }

    public static void parseMT940(String mt940Content) {
        // Define regex patterns for different parts of the MT940 message
        Pattern referencePattern = Pattern.compile(":20:(.*?)\\n");
        Pattern narrativeHeaderPattern = Pattern.compile(":25:(.*?)\\n");
        Pattern creditedAmountPattern = Pattern.compile(":60F:Credited Amount (.*?)\\n");
        Pattern accountNumberPattern = Pattern.compile(":97A:Account Number (.*?)\\n");

        // Find and print matches
        Matcher referenceMatcher = referencePattern.matcher(mt940Content);
        Matcher narrativeHeaderMatcher = narrativeHeaderPattern.matcher(mt940Content);
        Matcher creditedAmountMatcher = creditedAmountPattern.matcher(mt940Content);
        Matcher accountNumberMatcher = accountNumberPattern.matcher(mt940Content);

        if (referenceMatcher.find()) {
            System.out.println("Reference ID: " + referenceMatcher.group(1));
        }

        if (narrativeHeaderMatcher.find()) {
            System.out.println("Narrative Header: " + narrativeHeaderMatcher.group(1));
        }

        if (creditedAmountMatcher.find()) {
            System.out.println("Credited Amount: " + creditedAmountMatcher.group(1));
        }

        if (accountNumberMatcher.find()) {
            System.out.println("Account Number: " + accountNumberMatcher.group(1));
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
- **Regex Patterns**: The regex patterns are designed to match specific parts of the MT940 message. For example, `:20:` corresponds to a reference ID, `:60F:` corresponds to the credited amount.
- **Matcher Objects**: These objects are used to find matches in the input string based on the defined regex patterns.
- **Output**: The parsed information is printed to the console.

### Note:
This example is very basic and does not handle all possible variations of MT940 messages. For a more robust solution, consider using existing libraries that specialize in parsing SWIFT messages, such as Apache OFBiz or similar projects.

Certainly! Reading CAN (Controller Area Network) bus frames from a vehicle's interface involves several steps. Below is a simple example using the SocketCAN API in Linux, which is commonly used for accessing CAN devices.

First, make sure you have the necessary tools and libraries installed. On Ubuntu, you can install them with:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install iproute2 can-utils
```

Next, ensure your CAN interface is up and running:

```bash
sudo ifconfig can0 up bitrate 500000
```

Replace `can0` with the name of your CAN interface and adjust the bitrate as necessary.

Here's a simple C++ program to read CAN frames using SocketCAN:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <linux/can.h>
#include <linux/can/raw.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    int s;
    struct sockaddr_can addr;
    struct ifreq ifr;

    // Create a socket for the CAN interface
    if ((s = socket(PF_CAN, SOCK_RAW, CAN_RAW)) < 0) {
        perror("Socket");
        return 1;
    }

    // Bind to the CAN interface
    strcpy(ifr.ifr_name, "can0");
    ioctl(s, SIOCGIFINDEX, &ifr);
    addr.can_family = AF_CAN;
    addr.can_ifindex = ifr.ifr_ifindex;

    if (bind(s, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, sizeof(addr)) < 0) {
        perror("Bind");
        close(s);
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout << "CAN frame reader is running..." << std::endl;

    struct can_frame frame;
    while (true) {
        // Receive a CAN frame
        int nbytes = read(s, &frame, sizeof(struct can_frame));
        if (nbytes < 0) {
            perror("Read");
            close(s);
            return 1;
        }

        // Print the received CAN frame details
        std::cout << "Received CAN frame: ID=" << frame.can_id 
                  << ", DLC=" << (int)frame.can_dlc << ", Data=";

        for (int i = 0; i < frame.can_dlc; ++i) {
            printf("%02x ", frame.data[i]);
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }

    close(s);
    return 0;
}
```

To compile and run this program, save it to a file (e.g., `can_reader.cpp`), and then use the following commands:

```bash
g++ -o can_reader can_reader.cpp
sudo ./can_reader
```

Note: Running CAN applications often requires root privileges due to the nature of the interface.

This program creates a SocketCAN socket, binds it to the `can0` interface, and enters a loop where it reads and prints incoming CAN frames. Each frame includes an ID, data length code (DLC), and the actual data payload. Adjust the interface name (`can0`) according to your system configuration.

