Here's an example demonstrating how to use SIMD sort with a vector of `float`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> // For std::sort (for comparison)
#include <experimental/simd> // Include SIMD library header

namespace simd = std::experimental::simd;

int main() {
    // Create a vector of floats to sort
    std::vector<float> vec = {2.3f, 1.1f, 4.5f, 3.2f};

    // Check if the type is SIMD compatible
    using simd_type = simd::simd_type_t<float>;
    static_assert(simd::is_simd<simd_type>::value);

    // Convert vector to a SIMD-friendly container
    std::vector<simd_type> v(vec.size() / simd_size + 1); // +1 for potential remainder elements

    // Copy the data from vec to v using SIMD operations
    int i = 0;
    for (; i < vec.size(); i += simd_size) {
        v[i / simd_size] = simd::load<simd_type>(vec.data() + i);
    }
    if (i < vec.size()) { // Handle any remaining elements
        v[i / simd_size] = simd::load_partial<simd_type>(vec.data() + i, vec.size() - i);
    }

    // Sort the SIMD container
    std::experimental::simd_sort(v.begin(), v.end());

    // Convert back to original vector for output
    std::vector<float> sorted_vec(vec.size());
    int j = 0;
    for (; j < vec.size(); j += simd_size) {
        simd::store<simd_type>(v[j / simd_size], sorted_vec.data() + j);
    }
    if (j < vec.size()) { // Handle any remaining elements
        simd::store_partial<simd_type>(v[j / simd_size], sorted_vec.data() + j, vec.size() - j);
    }

    // Output the sorted vector
    std::cout << "Sorted vector: ";
    for (float f : sorted_vec) {
        std::cout << f << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we first check if `float` is a SIMD type using `std::experimental::simd::is_simd`. We then create a vector of `floats` and convert it into a SIMD-friendly container. The `std::experimental::simd_sort` function is used to sort the SIMD container. After sorting, we convert the sorted SIMD container back to a standard vector for output.

Remember that SIMD operations are most effective when they operate on larger data sets, as the overhead of managing multiple instructions per data element is reduced. This example demonstrates the basic process but may need adjustments based on specific compiler and platform optimizations.

