tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```

2. Then, you can create a simple struct with an `async drop` block:

```rust
use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};

#[derive(Debug)]
struct AsyncResource;

impl AsyncResource {
    async fn new() -> Self {
        println!("Resource created");
        AsyncResource
    }

    async fn cleanup(&self) {
        println!("Cleaning up resource");
        // Simulate some asynchronous cleanup work
        sleep(Duration::from_secs(1)).await;
        println!("Cleanup done");
    }
}

impl Drop for AsyncResource {
    async fn drop(&mut self) {
        println!("Dropping AsyncResource...");
        self.cleanup().await;
        println!("AsyncResource dropped");
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    println!("Starting the program");
    let resource = AsyncResource::new().await;
    // The `resource` will be dropped when it goes out of scope at the end of this block
}
```

In this example, the `AsyncResource` struct is defined with an async `drop` method. When a value of type `AsyncResource` is dropped (which happens automatically when it goes out of scope), the `async drop` block is executed asynchronously.

The `new` method simulates creating a resource and returns an instance of `AsyncResource`. The `cleanup` method simulates some asynchronous cleanup work.

When you run this program, you should see output indicating that the resource is created, cleaned up asynchronously, and finally dropped:

```
Starting the program
Resource created
Dropping AsyncResource...
Cleaning up resource
Cleanup done
AsyncResource dropped
```

This demonstrates how to use the `async drop` feature in Rust 2027 edition to perform asynchronous cleanup when a value is dropped.

