You can use a variation of the binary search algorithm called "binary search with insertion sort" which guarantees that the correct index is found in O(log n) time even on an unsorted array. The basic idea is to perform a normal binary search, but if the element is not found, instead of returning -1, we insert the element into the array and then do another binary search. This ensures that the element is now sorted and can be found in O(log n) time.

Here's an example implementation:
```c++
int binarySearchWithInsertionSort(int arr[], int size, int x) {
    // Perform a normal binary search
    int low = 0;
    int high = size - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < x) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    // If the element is not found, insert it into the array and then do another binary search
    if (low > high) {
        arr[size] = x;
        size++;
        low = 0;
        high = size - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = (low + high) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == x) {
                return mid;
            } else if (arr[mid] < x) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }

    // If the element is still not found, return -1
    return -1;
}
```
This implementation uses a normal binary search to find the index of an element in a sorted array. If the element is not found, it inserts the element into the array and then does another binary search. This ensures that the element is now sorted and can be found in O(log n) time.

