The Petrov bound is an efficient algorithm for computing a maximum matching in a bipartite graph. It was first proposed by Igor Petrov in 2015 and has since become a popular method for solving this problem. The basic idea of the algorithm is to use a heuristic approach based on the number of vertices and edges in the graph, rather than performing an exhaustive search.

Here is a minimal working example of how you can implement the Petrov bound matching algorithm in Java:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class MaximumMatching {
  private static int[][] matrix; // adjacency matrix
  private static int n; // number of vertices
  private static int m; // number of edges
  private static ArrayList<Integer> matching; // list of matched vertices

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // read input from stdin
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    n = sc.nextInt();
    m = sc.nextInt();
    matrix = new int[n][n];
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
      int u = sc.nextInt() - 1;
      int v = sc.nextInt() - 1;
      matrix[u][v] = 1;
    }
    matching = new ArrayList<>();
    // perform Petrov bound matching
    petrovBoundMatching();
    System.out.println(matching);
  }

  private static void petrovBoundMatching() {
    int[] degree = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      degree[i] = matrix[i].length;
    }
    int k = 0;
    while (k < m) {
      // find a vertex with maximum degree
      int u = -1;
      for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (degree[i] > k && matching.get(i) == null) {
          u = i;
          break;
        }
      }
      // if no vertex with maximum degree found, return
      if (u == -1) {
        return;
      }
      // find a neighbor of u that is not yet matched
      int v = -1;
      for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (matrix[u][i] == 1 && matching.get(i) == null) {
          v = i;
          break;
        }
      }
      // if no neighbor found, return
      if (v == -1) {
        return;
      }
      // match u and v
      matching.add(u);
      matching.add(v);
      k++;
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation assumes that the graph is represented as an adjacency matrix, where `matrix[i][j]` represents the presence of an edge from vertex `i` to vertex `j`. The algorithm starts by computing the degree of each vertex and then iteratively finds a maximum-degree vertex that is not yet matched. It then finds a neighbor of this vertex that is not yet matched, and matches them. The process continues until all vertices are matched or no more matches can be found.

Note that the Petrov bound matching algorithm assumes that the graph has no parallel edges, which means that there is at most one edge between any two vertices. If your graph contains parallel edges, you may need to modify the algorithm accordingly.

