A lock-free stack is a concurrent data structure that allows multiple threads to access and modify it without the need for locks. To implement a lock-free stack, we can use a linked list where each node represents an element in the stack. The head of the list points to the topmost element in the stack, and each node has a reference to its successor node.

Here's some sample code in C++ that implements a lock-free stack:
```
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

class LockFreeStack {
public:
    // Constructor
    LockFreeStack() : head_(nullptr) {}

    // Push an element onto the stack
    void push(int element) {
        Node* new_node = new Node(element);
        new_node->next = nullptr;

        std::atomic<Node*>* current_head = &head_;
        while (true) {
            // Try to atomically update the head pointer to point to the new node
            Node* old_head = current_head->load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
            if (current_head->compare_exchange_weak(old_head, new_node)) {
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Pop an element from the stack
    int pop() {
        std::atomic<Node*>* current_head = &head_;
        while (true) {
            Node* old_head = current_head->load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
            if (!old_head) {
                return -1;  // Stack is empty
            }

            Node* new_head = old_head->next;
            if (current_head->compare_exchange_weak(old_head, new_head)) {
                int element = old_head->data;
                delete old_head;
                return element;
            }
        }
    }

private:
    struct Node {
        int data;
        Node* next;
    };

    std::atomic<Node*> head_;
};
```
This implementation uses atomic variables to ensure that the head pointer is always updated consistently, even in the presence of concurrent access. The `push` method creates a new node and updates the head pointer atomically using the `compare_exchange_weak` function. The `pop` method retrieves the topmost element from the stack by repeatedly trying to update the head pointer until it points to a non-empty node.

