```
// Define the clock class
class Clock {
  constructor(timestamp) {
    this.timestamp = timestamp;
  }

  // Increment the clock by one
  tick() {
    this.timestamp++;
  }

  // Get the current time
  getTime() {
    return this.timestamp;
  }
}

// Define the message class
class Message {
  constructor(content, sender) {
    this.content = content;
    this.sender = sender;
    this.timestamp = new Clock(0); // Initialize the timestamp to zero
  }

  // Set the message's timestamp to the current time
  setTime() {
    this.timestamp = new Clock();
  }
}

// Define the total order function
function totalOrder(messages) {
  const clock = new Clock(); // Initialize the clock

  // For each message in the array, compare its timestamp to the current time
  for (const message of messages) {
    if (message.timestamp > clock.getTime()) {
      clock.tick(); // If the message is newer than the current time, increment the clock
    } else if (message.timestamp < clock.getTime()) {
      return false; // If the message is older than the current time, return false
    }
  }

  // If all messages are ordered correctly, return true
  return true;
}
```
This code defines a `Clock` class that represents a Lamport-clock, which is used to keep track of the ordering of messages. The `Message` class is defined to represent an individual message with a timestamp and a sender. The `totalOrder` function takes an array of messages as input and returns true if all messages are ordered correctly, false otherwise.

To use this code, you can create instances of the `Clock` and `Message` classes, set the timestamps for each message using the `setTime()` method, and then call the `totalOrder()` function with the array of messages as input. For example:
