The implementation of a comparison-based sort with a worst-case time complexity of O(n log n) is the merge sort algorithm.

Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that works by recursively breaking down the input array into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element, at which point it merges the sorted subarrays back together to produce the final sorted array. The key to the efficiency of merge sort is the way it uses a temporary buffer to store the merged subarrays, allowing it to avoid having to make a complete pass through the input data for each merge operation.

The basic steps of the merge sort algorithm are:

1. Divide the input array into two halves if it has more than one element. If it has only one element, return it (since it is already sorted).
2. Recursively apply step 1 to each half of the input array.
3. Merge the two sorted halves back together into a single, sorted array.

The time complexity of merge sort is O(n log n) in the worst case, because the number of comparisons required to sort an array is proportional to the number of elements in the array and the logarithm of that number. The reason for this is that each level of the recursive call only needs to consider a fraction of the input data, so the time it takes to complete the merge operation grows logarithmically with the size of the input data.

Here's an example implementation of merge sort in JavaScript:
```
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, mid);
  const right = arr.slice(mid);

  return merge(mergeSort(left), mergeSort(right));
}

function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];

  while (left.length > 0 && right.length > 0) {
    if (left[0] <= right[0]) {
      result.push(left.shift());
    } else {
      result.push(right.shift());
    }
  }

  while (left.length > 0) {
    result.push(left.shift());
  }

  while (right.length > 0) {
    result.push(right.shift());
  }

  return result;
}
```
This implementation uses the `slice()` method to divide the input array into two halves, and the `merge()` function to merge the sorted subarrays back together. The `merge()` function uses a temporary buffer to store the merged subarrays, allowing it to avoid having to make a complete pass through the input data for each merge operation.

In summary, merge sort is an efficient comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a divide and conquer approach to sort arrays in O(n log n) time. It works by recursively breaking down the input array into smaller subarrays until each subarray contains only one element, at which point it merges the sorted subarrays back together to produce the final sorted array using a temporary buffer to store the merged subarrays.

