Sure! A LinkedHashMap is a type of map that stores key-value pairs, but it also maintains the order in which the keys were added or accessed. This can be useful for building an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache, where you want to keep track of the most recently used items and remove them when necessary.

Here's an example of how you could use a LinkedHashMap to build an LRU cache:
```
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;

public class LRUCache {
  private final LinkedHashMap<String, Object> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
  private int capacity; // the maximum number of items to store in the cache

  public LRUCache(int capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }

  public void put(String key, Object value) {
    if (map.containsKey(key)) {
      map.remove(key); // remove any existing value for the given key
    }
    map.put(key, value); // add the new value to the end of the list

    // if the cache is full, remove the least recently used item
    if (map.size() > capacity) {
      Object oldestKey = map.removeFirst();
      System.out.println("Removing least recently used item: " + oldestKey);
    }
  }

  public Object get(String key) {
    return map.get(key);
  }
}
```
This code defines a `LRUCache` class that has a `put()` method to add an item to the cache, and a `get()` method to retrieve an item from the cache. The `map` field is a LinkedHashMap, which maintains the order in which items were added or accessed. When the cache is full, the least recently used item is removed and printed to the console using the `System.out.println()` method.

You can use this class like this:
```
LRUCache cache = new LRUCache(3); // create a cache with a maximum size of 3 items
cache.put("key1", "value1");
cache.put("key2", "value2");
cache.get("key1"); // prints "value1"
cache.put("key3", "value3"); // removes "key1" and adds "key3" to the end of the list
```
This code creates a cache with a maximum size of 3 items, adds two items to the cache, and retrieves one of the items using the `get()` method. The cache is full after adding the third item, so the least recently used item ("key1") is removed from the cache.

Here's an example of using `java.nio.channels.FileChannel` to copy a file efficiently in Java:
```
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileCopy {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Get the input and output files
        String inputFile = "input.txt";
        String outputFile = "output.txt";

        // Open the input file for reading
        FileChannel inChannel = FileChannel.open(Paths.get(inputFile), StandardOpenOption.READ);

        // Open the output file for writing
        FileChannel outChannel = FileChannel.open(Paths.get(outputFile), StandardOpenOption.WRITE, StandardOpenOption.CREATE);

        // Copy the contents of the input file to the output file
        inChannel.transferTo(0, inChannel.size(), outChannel);

        // Close the input and output channels
        inChannel.close();
        outChannel.close();
    }
}
```
This example uses `java.nio.channels.FileChannel` to copy the contents of an input file to an output file efficiently using the `transferTo()` method. The `StandardOpenOption.READ` and `StandardOpenOption.WRITE` options are used to open the files for reading and writing, respectively. The `StandardOpenOption.CREATE` option is also used to create the output file if it does not already exist.

The `transferTo()` method takes three parameters: the starting position of the data in the input channel, the size of the data to be transferred, and the destination channel. It returns the number of bytes that were actually transferred, which can be used to determine whether all of the data was successfully copied.

In this example, the input file is opened for reading using `FileChannel.open()`, and the output file is opened for writing using `FileChannel.open()`. The `transferTo()` method is then called on the input channel to copy its contents to the output channel. Finally, both channels are closed using `close()` methods.

This example demonstrates how to use `java.nio.channels.FileChannel` to efficiently copy data between files in Java.

