The code uses a few different cases to handle the different types of steps you can take:

* If you are at an even position (i.e., `i % 2 === 0`), you can climb either 1 or 2 steps, so you use the previous solution for these positions.
* If you are at a multiple of 5 (i.e., `i % 5 === 0`), you can climb either 1, 2, or 5 steps, so you use the previous solutions for these positions.
* In all other cases, you can only climb 1 step, so you use the previous solution for these positions.

By using a dynamic programming approach, the code is able to solve the "Climbing Stairs" problem with the additional constraint that you can climb 1, 2, or 5 steps at a time in O(n) time, where n is the number of positions in the staircase.

