Here is an example of how you can use the `github.com/gofast/turbopool` package to pool database connections in a Go program:
```
package main

import (
	"context"
	"database/sql"
	"fmt"
	"log"

	_ "github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql"
	"github.com/gofast/turbopool"
)

func main() {
	// Create a new pool with 10 maximum connections
	pool := turbopool.New(10, func() (*sql.DB, error) {
		return sql.Open("mysql", "user:password@tcp(localhost)/dbname")
	})

	// Run some database operations in parallel
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			db, err := pool.Get()
			if err != nil {
				log.Fatal(err)
			}
			// Do something with the database connection
			fmt.Println("Connected to database!")
			pool.Put(db)
		}()
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
```
This example creates a new pool of 10 maximum connections using the `turbopool.New` function, which takes two arguments: the maximum number of connections to pool, and a function that returns a new database connection when called. The `sql.Open` function is used to create a new database connection.

In this example, we use the `sync.WaitGroup` type to run some database operations in parallel using goroutines. We add 10 items to the wait group because we want to run 10 database operations in parallel. Each item in the wait group is a function that takes a new database connection from the pool and performs some operation on it, before returning the connection back to the pool using `pool.Put`.

The `pool.Get` method returns a new database connection when called, or blocks until one becomes available if all connections are currently in use. The `pool.Put` method takes a database connection and puts it back into the pool, allowing other goroutines to use it.

Note that this example is just an illustration of how you can use the `turbopool` package to pool database connections. In a real-world application, you would need to modify it to fit your specific needs and handle any errors that may occur during connection creation or closing.

