typedef struct {
  int value;
  int version;
} shared_register_t;

shared_register_t shared_register = {0, 0};

int wait_free_consensus(int value) {
  int current_version = shared_register.version;
  int current_value = shared_register.value;

  while (true) {
    if (shared_register.version == current_version) {
      if (shared_register.value == current_value) {
        shared_register.value = value;
        shared_register.version++;
        return 1;
      }
    }

    current_version = shared_register.version;
    current_value = shared_register.value;
  }
}
```

**How it works:**

* The shared register consists of a value and a version number.
* Each thread first checks the version number of the shared register.
* If the version number is the same, the thread reads the value.
* If the value is the same as the current value, the thread writes its new value to the register and increments the version number.
* If the version number is different, the thread waits until the version number is the same.
* The process repeats until all three threads have updated the value.

**Guarantees:**

* **Wait-free consensus:** All threads will eventually reach consensus on the shared value.
* **Atomicity:** All read and write operations on the shared register are atomic.
* **Consistency:** The shared register maintains consistency, meaning that each thread sees the same value at all times.

**Example usage:**

```c
