```java
import java.util.*;

public class KunduRahmanDijkstra {

    private static final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

    public static int[] dijkstra(int[][] graph, int start) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] distances = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(distances, INF);
        distances[start] = 0;

        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minDistance = INF;
            int minVertex = -1;

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (!visited[j] && distances[j] < minDistance) {
                    minDistance = distances[j];
                    minVertex = j;
                }
            }

            visited[minVertex] = true;

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (!visited[j] && graph[minVertex][j] != 0 && distances[minVertex] + graph[minVertex][j] < distances[j]) {
                    distances[j] = distances[minVertex] + graph[minVertex][j];
                }
            }
        }

        return distances;
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* The `dijkstra()` method takes a weighted graph represented as a 2D array `graph` and the starting vertex `start` as input.
* It initializes an array `distances` to store the shortest distances from the starting vertex to each vertex in the graph.
* A boolean array `visited` keeps track of visited vertices.
* The algorithm iterates until all vertices have been visited.
* In each iteration, it finds the vertex with the minimum distance that has not been visited.
* It updates the distances of its neighbors if a shorter path is found.
* The method returns the `distances` array, which contains the shortest distances from the starting vertex to each vertex in the graph.

