```java
public class OpenAddressingHashMap<K, V> {

    private static final int DEFAULT_SIZE = 17;
    private Entry[] table;
    private int size;

    public OpenAddressingHashMap() {
        this(DEFAULT_SIZE);
    }

    public OpenAddressingHashMap(int size) {
        this.table = new Entry[size];
        this.size = 0;
    }

    private static class Entry {
        K key;
        V value;

        public Entry(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        int hash = hash(key);
        int index = hash & (table.length - 1);

        while (table[index] != null) {
            if (table[index].key.equals(key)) {
                table[index].value = value;
                return;
            }
            index = (index + 1) & (table.length - 1);
        }

        table[index] = new Entry(key, value);
        size++;
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        int hash = hash(key);
        int index = hash & (table.length - 1);

        while (table[index] != null) {
            if (table[index].key.equals(key)) {
                return table[index].value;
            }
            index = (index + 1) & (table.length - 1);
        }

        return null;
    }

    private int hash(K key) {
        return key.hashCode();
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

* The hash map uses an array of entries called `table`.
* The `put()` method calculates the hash value of the key, calculates the index in the array using bitwise AND, and iterates until it finds an empty slot or an entry with the same key.
* The `get()` method calculates the hash value of the key, calculates the index in the array using bitwise AND, and iterates until it finds the entry with the same key or an empty slot.
* The worst-case lookup time is O(1) because the hash function guarantees that the key will be found in a constant number of steps.

**Note:**

* This hash map uses the modulo operator to calculate the index in the array.
