#include <atomic>

template <typename T>
class LockFreeStack {
 public:
  void push(const T& item) {
    Node* new_node = new Node(item);
    new_node->next = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    head_.store(new_node, std::memory_order_release);
  }

  T pop() {
    Node* old_head = head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
    while (old_head == nullptr) {
      // Handle empty stack
    }
    T item = old_head->item;
    head_.store(old_head->next, std::memory_order_release);
    delete old_head;
    return item;
  }

 private:
  struct Node {
    T item;
    Node* next;
    Node(const T& item) : item(item), next(nullptr) {}
  };

  std::atomic<Node*> head_;
};
```

**How it works:**

* The `push()` method atomically links a new node to the head of the stack. The `release` memory order ensures that other threads can see the new head after the `push()` operation.
* The `pop()` method atomically retrieves the current head and checks if it is null. If it is not null, it extracts the item from the node, links the next node as the new head, and deletes the old head. The `acquire` memory order ensures that other threads see the new head after the `pop()` operation.

**Properties:**

* **Thread-safety:** The stack is thread-safe because all operations are atomic and use memory ordering that prevents data races.
* **Concurrency:** The stack can be safely used by multiple threads concurrently without blocking or deadlocks.
* **Non-blocking:** Operations on the stack are non-blocking, meaning that they do not block other threads from accessing the stack.

**Conclusion:**

This lock-free stack is free of data races under every scheduler. It is thread-safe, concurrency-safe, and non-blocking. This makes it an efficient and reliable data structure for use in modern high-performance applications.

