Implementing a lock-free stack in C++11 involves using atomic operations for push, pop and some auxiliary operations. The basic idea is to use `std::atomic<Node*>` for both top pointer of the stack and Node which contains the value and link to the next node. 

Here's an example:
```c++
#include <atomic>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

class Stack {
private:
    std::atomic<Node*> _top;

public:
    ~Stack() {
        while (Node* old = _top.exchange(nullptr)) {
            delete old;
        }
    }

    void push(int value) {
        Node* new_node = new Node{value, nullptr};
        Node* old_top = _top.load();
        do {
            new_node->next = old_top;
        } while (!_top.compare_exchange_weak(old_top, new_node));
    }

    bool pop(int& value) {
        Node* old_top = _top.load();
        do {
            if (!old_top) return false;
            value = old_top->value;
        } while (!_top.compare_exchange_weak(oldold_top, old_top->next));
        delete old_top;
        return true;
    }
};
```
This code works as follows: 

1. `push()` creates a new node with the given value and tries to replace the top of the stack by it using compare-and-swap operation, if success then break the loop, else continue until successful. This ensures that no other push/pop operations on the same or different stacks interfere with current push operation.

2. `pop()` checks if the stack is empty and if not, pops the top value off the stack by replacing the top of the stack by its next node using compare-and-swap operation. This ensures that no other push/pop operations on the same or different stacks interfere with current pop operation.

This code ensures atomicity among all memory accesses to this stack, and thus is free from data races under any scheduler. 

Note: The above implementation assumes you are managing the lifetime of Node's dynamically allocated memory elsewhere in your program; i.e., you are responsible for deleting `Node` instances when they are no longer needed. In real-world usage, it could be better to use smart pointers instead of raw pointers where possible to manage this automatically. 

Note: The above implementation is a simple example and does not cover all corner cases (e.g., handling more complex concurrent operations). For production code, you might want to include additional features like stack overflow/underflow checking, etc.

