Building an ACID compliant system across two databases using the two-phase commit protocol and robust error handling involves multiple steps, which are typically done by a dedicated transaction manager. Here's a very brief example of how this could be achieved in pseudocode:

```pseudocode
TwoPhaseCommitCoordinator {
    Database db1;
    Database db2;

    Transaction currentTransaction;

    start_transaction() {
        if (currentTransaction != null) 
            throw new Exception("Another transaction is in progress.");

        currentTransaction = new Transaction();

        try {
            // Prepare each DB for the transaction.
            db1.prepare(currentTransaction);
            db2.prepare(currentTransaction);

            // All resources are ready, commit can start.
            do_commit();
          } 
        catch (Exception e) {
            // Error occurred during prepare phase, rollback all changes.
            currentTransaction.rollback();

            if (db1.isInTransaction()) db1.rollback();
            if (db2.isInTransaction()) db2.rollback();

            throw e;  // rethrow exception to caller
          }
      }

    do_commit() {
        try {
            // Commit changes in both databases.
            db1.commit(currentTransaction);
            db2.commit(currentTransaction);

            currentTransaction = null;  // transaction is now complete, clear it
          } 
        catch (Exception e) {
            // Error occurred during commit phase, rollback all changes.
            currentTransaction.rollback();

            if (db1.isInTransaction()) db1.rollback();
            if (db2.isInTransaction()) db2.rollback();

            throw e;  // rethrow exception to caller
          }      
    }  
}
```
This is a very high-level pseudocode representation and it's meant for illustrating the concepts, not a complete solution. A real implementation would include more error handling, transaction timeout management, network communication between database nodes etc. 

Also note that two-phase commit protocol requires a coordinator to manage transactions across multiple resources (databases in this case). This can be implemented with a distributed system or a separate service if necessary.

