The C++ standard library doesn't provide an atomic 64-bit integer, so you would have to implement this yourself using `std::atomic` and a struct of two `std::atomic<unsigned int>`. Here is an example implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>

struct AtomicU64 {
    std::array<std::atomic<uint32_t>, 2> value;
};

AtomicU64 counter = {{0, 0}};

void increment() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1'000'000; ++i) {
        AtomicU64 oldValue, newValue;
        do {
            oldValue.value[0] = counter.value[0].load();
            oldValue.value[1] = counter.value[1].load();
            ++newValue.value[0];
            if (oldValue.value[0] == 0xFFFF'FFFF) { // 2^32 - 1, the maximum value of a 32-bit unsigned int
                ++newValue.value[1];
                newValue.value[0] = 0;
            }
        } while (!counter.value[0].compare_exchange_weak(oldValue.value[0], newValue.value[0]) ||
                 !counter.value[1].compare_exchange_weak(oldValue.value[1], newValue.value[1]));
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); ++i) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread(increment));
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        if (thread.joinable()) {
            thread.join();
        }
    }

    std::cout << "Counter: " << counter.value[1].load() << " " << counter.value[0].load() << '\n';
}
```

This program creates multiple threads that increment the `counter` variable 1,000,000 times each, and then prints out the final value of the counter. The counter is a struct of two atomic integers because we want to be able to represent a 64-bit number, but C++ does not have built-in support for a 64-bit integer type, so it's split into two 32-bit parts and treated as if they were one 64-bit value.

The `compare_exchange_weak` function is used in the do-while loop to implement atomic operations that allow us to read and write a 64-bit number atomically. It reads the current values of the two atomic integers into `oldValue`, increments `newValue` by one (or rolls over when it reaches its maximum value), then attempts to replace the old values with the new ones if they were not changed in between the read and write operations. This operation is performed atomically and only proceeds if the values that were read are still current (i.e., no other thread has written a different value). The loop continues until we successfully increment the 64-bit number.

