A RingBuffer is an abstract data type that represents a FIFO (First In, First Out) data structure using a fixed-size byte array to hold its elements.

The single producer and consumer model means that there are two operations that can happen on the buffer: write (producer) and read (consumer). 

Here is a C++11 implementation of a Ring Buffer with appropriate memory fences, using std::atomic variables for safe concurrent access. 

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <atomic>
#include <cassert>

template<typename T, size_t N>
class RingBuffer {
    static_assert(N > 0, "Ring buffer must have a positive length");

public:
    explicit RingBuffer() : head_(0), tail_(0) {}

    bool empty() const noexcept {
        return head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == tail_.load(std::memory_order
orelease); 
    }

    void push(T value) {
        auto current_tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        buffer_[current_tail] = std::move(value);
        tail_.store((current_tail + 1) % N, std::memory_order_release); 
    }

    T pop() {
        auto current_head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        if (empty()) return nullptr; // or throw an exception

        T result = buffer_[current_head];
        head_.store((current_head + 1) % N, std::memory_order_release); 
        return result; 
    }

private:
    std::array<T, N> buffer_; // Ring Buffer data array.
    std::atomic<size_t> head_, tail_; // Head and Tail indexes of the ring buffer.
};
```

How it works?

- `std::memory_order_relaxed` means no synchronization or ordering constraints for operations on this atomic variable can be assumed - that is, it doesn't provide any synchronization guarantee but allows more flexibility in compiler/runtime optimization. 

- `std::memory_order_acquire`: ensures all writes to the memory locations in this function are visible to subsequent loads (reads) of this function and also prevent other reads from reordering around them. It’s used here because we want to make sure that tail_.load() always reflects the most recent write operation, i.e., before head_ is changed via push().

- `std::memory_order_release`: ensures all writes in this function are visible to subsequent loads of other variables (reads) and also prevent other reads from reordering around them. It’s used here because we want to make sure that tail_.store() reflects the most recent write operation, i.e., before head_ is changed via pop().

The atomic operations in C++ are compiler-provided low-level instructions for managing memory operations at the hardware level with full fence semantics. A memory fence ensures that all previous stores have completed before any subsequent loads commence - which makes these operations thread-safe and safe to use in a multi-threaded scenario.

