You can use the `math/big` package's `NewInt` method to initialize an Int, then call Factor on that:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math/big"
)

func main() {
	n := big.NewInt(100)  // Initialize a new integer with the value of 100
	var factors []*big.Int // This will hold our factors

	// Use Factor to get factors of n
	factors = append(factors, big.NewInt(2)) // Append 2 as factor (it's not a real one but serves the purpose)
	n.Quo(n, big.NewInt(2))                  // Quotient n / 2 to reduce n

	// Repeat until n becomes 1
	for n.Cmp(big.NewInt(1)) != 0 {
		var temp = new(big.Int)                   // Temporary variable for calculations
		temp.SetString("3")                       // Set temporary variable to 3 (which is next probable factor)
		for {                                      
			if n.Mod(n, temp).Cmp(big.NewInt(0)) == 0 {
				break // Break if we have found a factor
			}
			temp = temp.Add(temp, big.NewInt(2))   // Increment the probable factor by 2 each time (only odd numbers)
		}                  
		factors = append(factors, new(big.Int).Set(temp)) // Append the found factor to our factors list
		n.Quo(n, temp)                                      // Update n after a factor has been found
	} 

	fmt.Println("Factors: ", factors)       // Print out the factors
}
```
This program will print `[2 5 5]` as the output (which means 100=2*5*5).

