Sure, here is a basic example of using `axios-retry` in your application:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
require('axios-retry')(axios, { 
    retries: 3, // Number of retry attempts
    shouldRetry: (error) => {
        return error.response.status >= 500; // Retry on server errors only
    },
    retryDelay: (attemptCount) => {
        return Math.pow(2, attemptCount); // Exponential backoff
    } 
});

axios({
    method:'get',
    url:'http://example.com/some-endpoint'
}).then((response)=>{
   console.log("Response: ", response.data);
}).catch((error) => {
   console.error('Axios failed with error: ', error);
});
```
In this code, we are importing the `axios` package and then adding the retry functionality to it using `axios-retry`. We define that we want to retry 3 times when an HTTP request fails (server errors in our case). The `shouldRetry` function allows us to specify conditions under which a failed request should be retried, for example, only on server errors (status codes 500 and above).

The `retryDelay` option defines the delay before each retry. In this case, we use an exponential backoff strategy where the delay increases by a factor of 2 with each attempt (e.g., 1st retry after 2ms, 2nd after 4ms, 3rd after 8ms etc).

Finally, `axios` is used to perform the HTTP request that could potentially fail and we catch any errors in the final `catch()` block. If axios fails (by throwing an error), it will automatically retry if the conditions in our configuration are met. After all retries have been exhausted, the promise will still reject with the last caught error.

