Firstly, let's define what our linked list node looks like:

```cpp
struct ListNode {
  int val;
  ListNode *next;
  ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(NULL) {}
};
```

Now we will implement the merge function:

```cpp
ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {
    // create a dummy node for storing head pointer of resultant list
    ListNode dummy(INT_MIN); 

    // 'prev' always points to the last node in the answer (as we build it up)
    ListNode *prev = &dummy; 

    while (l1 && l2){
        if (l1->val > l2->val){  
            prev->next = l1;
            l1 = l1->next;
        } else {
            prev->next = l2;
            l2 = l2->next;
        }
        // Move 'prev' forward
        prev = prev->next; 
    }

    // At least one of the lists is null. Point it to the other list
    if (l1){
        prev->next = l1;
    } else {
        prev->next = l2;
    }

    return dummy.next; 
}
```
The function starts by creating a new node that will serve as the head of our merged list. This way, we don't need to change anything if both input lists are empty (return null). Then it simply iterates through each element in the two lists simultaneously and adds whichever one is greater to the final linked list. The process continues until you exhaust all elements from either of the two original linked lists.

Finally, we return the `next` node of our dummy node as this would be the head pointer for our newly merged list. 

