Sure, below is an implementation for the 'move zeroes' problem in C++. This program moves all the zeros to the front while maintaining the relative order of other elements. 

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<vector>

void moveZeroes(std::vector<int>& nums) {
    int count = 0;

    // Counting total number of zeros in the array
    for(auto n : nums){
        if (n == 0) 
            count++;
    }

    std::remove(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);

    while(count--) {
       // pushing zeros at the front of vector
       nums.insert(nums.begin() , 0);
    }
}

int main(){
    std::vector<int> nums = {1,0,2,0,3,4,5};

    moveZeroes(nums);

    for(auto n: nums)
        std::cout<<n<<" ";  // printing the vector.
}
```
This program works by first counting how many zeros are in the array and then removing all occurrences of zero from the vector. After that, it is inserting back those removed zeros at front while keeping their order with respect to other non-zero elements. 

Please note that standard `std::vector` doesn't have an `insert()` method, so you might need to use another data structure or a list if the speed of access and manipulation is crucial in your program. The above solution also assumes that we can afford space complexity for this operation which is O(n) where n is size of array.

