The Rust programming language includes a borrow-checking mechanism that helps prevent bugs related to memory safety. However, there are some situations where you want to opt out of this system and manage it yourself at runtime. This option comes under the `runtime_borrow_checker` feature in Rust. 

Here is a simple example:

```rust
#![feature(box_syntax)]
#! feature(box_patterns);
extern crate test;
use std::boxed::Box;

// Here we use the `Rc` (reference counting) type from the standard library.
// This is a general purpose reference-counting pointer, and it's designed to be as
// cheap for small numbers as possible but still provide strong guarantees about
// lifetime safety. The `RefCell` wrapper on top of this uses static analysis to
// ensure that you're the only one who can mutate the references into your data.
#! feature(box_syntax, box_patterns);
extern crate rc;
use std::rc::Rc;

fn main() { 
    let x = Box::new(5) as *mut i32;

    // Here's how to do a borrow check at runtime. This is essentially the same
    // thing you would have to do if you were just writing C++, but it has the added
    // advantage of catching errors when your program is being run, rather than at
    // compile time.
    let rc = Rc::new(x);
    unsafe {
        assert!(rc.borrow_checker::valid_slice((*rc as *mut i32)..(*(rc.offset(1)) as *mut i32)));
    }
}
```
In this code, we have to use the `unsafe` block due to runtime checks and you should note that this is generally not recommended in Rust for safety reasons, but in certain scenarios where this is the only way to achieve what you want it can be quite useful. 

Please understand that this feature is unstable and may change or even go away at any point. As always with Rust, it's important to read up on its documentation and follow its principles for writing safe code.

The `constexpr_garbage_collection` feature is not available in current versions of C++ (as of C++20). It has been proposed, but it's still on the standards committee's backlog to be implemented and decided. 

This means that you won't be able to use this feature in your current compiler. If you are using GCC or Clang as your compiler, they both don't currently support `constexpr_garbage_collection`.

If you want to do compile-time garbage collection, a common technique is to store the items on the heap and use smart pointers (like in C++11 with `std::shared_ptr` or later versions). This can be done at runtime, but you could potentially create classes that are only used at compile time and then have them stored on the stack which will allow for more efficient memory usage.

Here's an example:
```cpp
#include <memory>

struct Foo {
  constexpr Foo() {}    // Constructor
  ~Foo() = default;      // Destructor needed if this is a POD type

  int x = 10;            // Some data
};

int main() {
  alignas(Foo) char storage[sizeof(Foo)];
  Foo* ptr = new (storage) Foo();    // Placement new - construct object in preallocated memory.

  std::shared_ptr<void> sp(ptr, [](void*p){delete static_cast<Foo*>(p);}); // shared pointer that destroys the object when done
}
```
Please note: This does not work for objects with dynamic member variables as in this case, it won't call the destructor. 

In general, compile-time garbage collection is a complex problem and would likely need significant compiler support to be feasible even though C++ doesn't natively support such features. For most needs, runtime or smart pointers are more than adequate.

The "auto_memoize" decorator can be used in Python for automatic caching of results of function calls. Here's an example of how you could use it:

```python
from memoization import auto_memoize

@auto_memoize(maxsize=10)  # maximum cache size is 10
def some_function(args):
    return computation(args)
```
In the above code, `some_function` will automatically be wrapped with a memoization decorator that caches results of function calls. The cached values are stored in LRU (Least Recently Used) order up to 10 items. If more than 10 distinct function call arguments are provided, the least recently used ones will be removed from cache when new entries are added.

The `implicit_async_everywhere` feature is a proposal for Swift 5.1 or later which allows implicit asynchronous execution of non-asynchronous functions, meaning you can write code that looks synchronous and runs asynchronously without having to mark the function with the `async` keyword or use other special syntax.

Here's an example:
```swift
func doNetworkRequest() -> Int {
    // This is a mock network request that takes 1 second, returns random number between 1 and 50
    Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: 1)
    return arc4random_uniform(50) + 1
}

let result = doNetworkRequest()
print("Result: \(result)") // Prints "Result: <a random number>" after one second
```
In the above code, `doNetworkRequest` is a synchronous function that does a network request. However, we don't have to mark it asynchronous because of `implicit_async_everywhere` feature. The compiler automatically treats this function as an async function by wrapping its return in a promise and running it on the current queue after scheduling that task for execution when Swift’s runtime decides is appropriate (typically on the next run loop).

So, essentially `implicit_async_everywhere` changes the way you write Swift code to make it more efficient. It allows us to write asynchronous code in a more familiar synchronous style without having to explicitly use async/await syntax or mark functions with `async` keyword. However, please note that this feature is currently under discussion and not yet implemented in all Swift compilers.

The `deterministic_gc_mode` is not a recognized feature in most popular programming languages, such as Python, Java or C#. However, if you're using Rust, this language provides such an option through its unstable features. 

Here's how it works:
Rust's garbage collector (GC) does not run concurrently with the execution of user-space code. This means that while the GC is running, no other code runs. However, this can sometimes result in large pauses where your program doesn't have any significant progress being made for a long time.

The `deterministic_gc_mode` feature aims to change this behavior and make the GC run concurrently with user-space execution. This way, while the GC is running, other parts of the code can still be making progress and your program will not have large pauses for long periods of time.

Here's an example of how you might use it:
```rust
#![feature(deterministic_gc)]
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

fn main() {
    let _ = std::env::var("RUST_BACKTRACE"); // turn on backtraces in this scope

    println!("starting up...");

    thread::spawn(move || loop {
        println!("doing work");
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(1));

        if std::mem::discriminant(&1 as &dyn Any) == 0 { // force a GC cycle
            eprintln!("gc triggered; sleeping for 3 sec...");
            thread::sleep(Duration::from(3));
        }
    });

    loop {} // block forever, to keep the rust program running
}
```
This code will print "doing work" every second and then trigger a garbage collection cycle (which will take about 3 seconds in practice). This forces the GC to run, allowing other parts of your program to make progress. Be aware that this behavior is unstable as of Rust 1.50, and may change over time as more refined control over concurrency with the user-space code is added.

