Sure, here's an example of how you could set up a simple reliable one-time communication using the built-in `net` package in Golang:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net"
)

func handleConnection(conn *net.UDPConn, addr *net.UDPAddr, id int) {
	buffer := make([]byte, 1024)
	n, _, err := conn.ReadFromUDP(buffer)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error: ", err)
	} else {
		// We've received a message once and are about to close the connection
		err = conn.SetReadDeadline(time.Now())
		if err != nil {
			fmt.Printf("Failed to set read deadline on socket for #%d: %v\n", id, err)
		} else {
			fmt.Printf("#%d: Received message: %s\n", id, string(buffer[0:n]))
		}
	}
}

func main() {
	// Listen on port 12345
	addr, _ := net.ResolveUDPAddr("udp", ":12345")
	sock, err := net.ListenUDP("udp", addr)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Failed to open UDP port: ", err)
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Listening on 12345...")
	}
	// Handling incoming connections in separate goroutines, simulating the "exactly once" semantic
	for i := 0; ; i++ {
		go handleConnection(sock, addr, i)
	}
}
```

This example creates a server that listens on port 12345 and handles each connection in its own goroutine. Once it receives a message (read from the socket), it immediately closes the connection to ensure the client only gets one chance at reading. This is essentially what you'd get with UDP-based "exactly once" semantics: Acknowledgement-based system but on top of raw UDP sockets.

This code lacks error handling and other features that you would normally want, but it should give a basic idea of how to accomplish the task. Remember to add any missing functionality (like command line arguments for port number or better goroutine management).

