```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <boost/lockfree/perfect_queue.hpp>
using namespace boost::lockfree;

int main() {
    perfect_queue<int, boost::mpl::true_> q(10);  // Create a queue with capacity for 10 elements.

    int vals[] = {1,2,3,4,5};

    // Push some values into the queue.
    for (auto &val : vals) {
        q.push(val);
    }

    // Pop some values from the queue and print them out.
    int value;
    while (q.pop(value)) {  // pop() will return true if successful, false otherwise.
        std::cout << value << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, `perfect_queue` is a lock-free queue provided by Boost library. It takes two template parameters: the type of elements it will hold (in this case `int`) and whether or not to use atomic operations for push() and pop(). The constructor parameter sets the capacity for the queue, which means how many elements it can hold without needing to allocate more memory.

After creating the perfect_queue instance, you push some values into it using the `push()` method, and then pop them out with the `pop()` method. Note that the order in which items are pushed onto the queue is preserved when they're later popped off (FIFO semantics), making this an excellent choice for multi-threaded scenarios where you want to share data between multiple threads without the overhead of locks.

The Boost library provides a wide range of concurrent and lock-free containers that can be used effectively in multi-threaded applications, such as `lockfree::queue`, `lockfree::stack`, `lockfree::spsc_queue` etc. The choice of container depends on the specific use case and requirements of your application.

