To simplify this explanation, I assume that you are familiar with Intel's Threading Building Blocks (TBB) library, as `<tbb/auto_parallel.h>` is one of its APIs. The TBB library provides parallel programming features for shared memory multiprocessors and distributed-memory systems, including task scheduling, synchronization and memory management utilities.

Here's a simple implementation:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <tbb/blocked_range.h>
#include <tbb/parallel_for.h>

void parallelProcess(int begin, int end) {
    for (int i = begin; i < end; ++i) {
        // Replace this with your code
        std::cout << "processing: " << i << "\n"; 
    }
}

int main() {
    const int N = 100;  // The number of elements to process.

    tbb::parallel_for(tbb::blocked_range<size_t>(0,N), parallelProcess);

    return 0;
}
```
