Java's built-in Cache API provides different implementations of the `Cache` interface, such as `SoftReference`, `WeakReference`, and others but there is no specific implementation for LRU (Least Recently Used) like `AutoLRU`. 

However, you can create your own by using a combination of Java's built-in data structures or third-party libraries. One such library is Apache Commons Collections. Here's an example:

```java
import org.apache.commons.collections4.map.PassiveExpiringMap;

public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private PassiveExpiringMap<K, V> cache;  // underlying data structure

    public LRUCache(long ttl) {        // time to live in milliseconds
        this.cache = new PassiveExpiringMap<>(ttl);
   }  

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        return cache.get(key);
    }

    // Add other methods as needed...
}
```
In the above code, we use `PassiveExpiringMap` from Apache Commons Collections which maintains an LRU ordering of its keys based on access time (as opposed to insertion order for a standard LinkedHashMap). The map's entries expire after some amount of time specified in the constructor.

This is not exactly equivalent to `java.util.cache.AutoLRU` but it gives similar functionality with less complexity and more available options. 

Make sure you have added Apache Commons Collections library to your project if it's not already there. You can include it in Maven or Gradle build files, or download the jar and add it manually to the classpath.

