Java 8 introduced java.time classes which were redesigned from the old date-time API (Joda-Time) to replace it and provide unified time handling. The `SmartZone` class was not part of these new classes, so you can't directly use that class. However, we can create a similar result using java.util.Calendar and java.time.ZonedDateTime.

Please note that the results will vary based on your system settings for timezone, so to get reliable results always specify an explicit time zone or set system default timezone appropriately before running this code.

Here's a minimal example:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;  

public class Main {    
    public static void main(String[] args)  {        
        // Create an Instant (a point on the time-line in timezone independent format)
        Instant instant = Instant.now();      

        System.out.println("Instant: " + instant);  

        // For example, let's assume we want to convert it to GMT+2 (Eastern Standard Time in most parts of the world). 
        ZoneId newZone = ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata");        

        // Convert Instant to ZonedDateTime (a date-time with timezone)
        ZonedDateTime zdt = instant.atZone(newZone);  

        System.out.println("ZonedDateTime: " + zdt); 

        // Formatting the DateTime for a more readable output    
        DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm:ss z");  

        String formattedDate = zdt.format(formatter);      

        System.out.println("Formatted Date & Time (GMT+2): " + formattedDate );    
    }     
}
```
This code gets the current time instant and converts it to a ZonedDateTime object in a specified time zone ("Asia/Kolkata" for IST, GMT+5:30 in many parts of the world). The example then prints out both the original Instant (which doesn't have a timezone) and the new ZonedDateTime.

