The example below demonstrates how you might use a FileChannel for zero-copy I/O:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.channels.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Create source and destination files
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("sourceFile");
        FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream("destinationFile");

        try (FileChannel src = fis.getChannel(); 
             FileChannel dest = fos.getChannel()) {
            // Transfer all data from source to destination using zero-copy by passing a long value of Long.MAX_VALUE for the size, which means it will copy till EOF is reached
            dest.transferFrom(src, 0, Long.MAX_VALUE);
       	   }
       }
    }
}
```
This code copies all data from a file to another using `FileChannel` methods like `transferFrom()` and by passing a long value of `Long.MAX_VALUE` as the size argument, which means it will copy till EOF is reached. This process does not involve copying any intermediate buffers in memory, providing zero-copy I/O for performance optimization.

