The `async_drop` crate provides the functionality you need for this, allowing asynchronous cleanup during drop operations. Here's an example of how to use it:

Cargo.toml:
```toml
[package]
name = "my-program"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2027"

[dependencies]
async_drop = "0.3.0"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```

main.rs:
```rust
use async_drop::{AsyncDrop, AsyncInit};
use tokio::time::sleep;
use std::error::Error;

struct MyResource;

impl AsyncDrop for MyResource {
    type Error = Box<dyn Error + Send + Sync>;

    async fn async_drop(&mut self) -> Result<(), Self::Error> {
        println!("My resource is about to be dropped");
        sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(1)).await; // Simulate some cleanup work.
        println!("My resource has been dropped");
        Ok(())
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let resource = MyResource {};

    // The following line will wait for the async drop to finish before `resource` goes out of scope.
    std::mem::forget(resource); 
}
```

In this example, we have a `MyResource` struct that implements the `AsyncDrop` trait. This trait requires an `async_drop()` method which is called when the resource is about to be dropped. The method does some simulated work (in this case, it just waits for 1 second), then prints a message indicating that it's been dropped.

We call `std::mem::forget(resource)`, ensuring that we forget the `MyResource` instance immediately after creating it. This is important because the async drop operation will be performed when the variable goes out of scope instead of instantly when the resource is dropped. Without this, the cleanup would run only once all existing references to the resource are gone (which might never happen if you keep a reference elsewhere).

