## 1️⃣ Rust – `include_str!` (JSON example)

```rust
// file: src/main.rs
// (Assumes you have a file named "config.json" next to this source file.)

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize;

// ---------- the data structure we expect ----------
#[derive(Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Config {
    pub name: String,
    pub version: u32,
}

// ---------- embed the file at compile time ----------
// The compiler reads "config.json" once while building the binary.
// The resulting binary contains the whole JSON as a static string.
const FILE_CONTENT: &str = include_str!("config.json");

fn main() {
    // Parse the embedded JSON – no runtime file I/O whatsoever.
    let config: Config = serde_json::from_str(FILE_CONTENT)
        .expect("failed to parse embedded JSON");

    println!("Parsed config: {:?}", config);
}
```

### How it works

* `include_str!("config.json")` instructs the Rust compiler to treat the file as a UTF‑8 string literal (`&'static str`).  
* At runtime `FILE_CONTENT` is just a regular constant – no `open`, `read`, `bufio.Reader`, etc.  
* The rest of the code parses it with `serde_json`, exactly as if it had been read from disk.

### Build & run

```sh
# Put a sample config.json next to main.rs:
# { "name": "my‑app", "version": 42 }

cargo run
# Parsed config: Config { name: "my-app", version: 42 }
```

*Zero runtime I/O – the file is read once by the compiler.*

