## 2️⃣ Go (1.16+) – `//go:embed` (same JSON example)

```go
// file: main.go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"log"
)

// Config mirrors the JSON we will embed.
type Config struct {
	Name    string `json:"name"`
	Version uint   `json:"version"`
}

//go:embed config.json
var configJSON string

func main() {
	var cfg Config
	if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(configJSON), &cfg); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("failed to unmarshal embedded JSON: %v", err)
	}
	fmt.Printf("Parsed config: %+v\n", cfg)
}
```

### How it works

* The `//go:embed config.json` directive tells the Go toolchain to embed the file’s contents into the `configJSON` variable at compile time.  
* At runtime `configJSON` is just a plain string – no file‑system calls are made.  
* The rest of the program parses the JSON with `encoding/json`.

### Build & run

```sh
# Place a "config.json" in the same directory as main.go

go run .
# Parsed config: {Name:my-app Version:42}
```

