## 3️⃣ C – preprocessor `#include` (plain‑text example)

```c
/* file: main.c */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

/* Embed a text file as a null‑terminated char array.
   The file is included by the preprocessor, so no runtime I/O. */
static const char *file_content =
#include "myfile.txt"
;

/* A trivial “parser” that just prints the string. */
void parse_file(const char *data) {
    printf("%s\n", data);
}

int main(void) {
    parse_file(file_content);
    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

* The preprocessor treats `#include "myfile.txt"` as if you had pasted the file’s contents verbatim into the source.  
* Because the file is part of the translation unit, the resulting binary contains the whole text as a static array.  
* The `parse_file` function operates on that static array – no `fopen`, `fread`, etc., at runtime.

### Build & run

```sh
# myfile.txt might simply contain: Hello, compile‑time world!

gcc -Wall -Wextra -O2 -o parser main.c
./parser
# Hello, compile‑time world!
```

