Below is a **tiny, self‑contained “clock service”** you can drop into any Node .js (or modern browser) code base.  
It **creates a globally‑unique, totally ordered identifier for every event** without needing a shared clock, a time‑server or any network round‑trips.  

### Why this works (the theory in one sentence)

*We combine three locally‑available pieces of information*  

1. **Monotonically increasing wall‑clock time** (nanosecond resolution) – gives a rough “when”.  
2. **A static, unique machine / process identifier** – guarantees that two machines never produce the same value.  
3. **A per‑process counter** – breaks any remaining ties when the clock resolution is exhausted.

The resulting tuple **(time, machineId, counter)** can be ordered lexicographically, giving a **total order** even when the machines’ clocks are not synchronized and no messages are exchanged.

> **Caveat** – This ordering is *deterministic* (the same set of IDs will always sort the same way) but it does **not** guarantee that the order matches the true causal “happened‑before” relationship across machines unless you also exchange messages (e.g. a Lamport or vector clock). For many internal‑docs use‑cases (log partitioning, idempotency keys, event‑store keys) the guarantee is sufficient.

---

## 📄 `clock-service.js`

```js
/**
 * clock-service.js
 *
 * A tiny, no‑dependency clock service that generates a globally unique,
 * totally ordered timestamp (event ID) without any shared clock or network chatter.
 *
 * Ordering rule:
 *   (time, machineId, counter) – lexicographic compare yields total order.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   const svc = new ClockService('my‑host‑1');
 *   const id1 = svc.now();   // "20260111T14‑23‑45.123456789‑my‑host‑1‑1"
 *   const id2 = svc.now();   // "20260111T14‑23‑45.123456789‑my‑host‑1‑2"
 *   console.log(svc.compare(id1, id2)); // -1   (id1 < id2)
 */

'use strict';

/**
 * Helper: fast, collision‑resistant machine identifier.
 * In Node you can use `process.pid` + `os.hostname()`.
 * In a browser you could use `navigator.userAgent` + some random bits.
 */
function getMachineId(tag) {
  // Simple deterministic hash – good enough for docs/demo.
  const str = `${tag}-${process.pid}`;
  let h = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    h = (Math.imul(31, h) + str.charCodeAt(i)) | 0;
  }
  // Keep it positive and fit in 16 bits.
  return (h >>> 1) & 0xFFFF;
}

/**
 * ClockService
 *
 * @param {string} [name] – Human‑readable name for this service/process.
 *                           If omitted we derive one from hostname + pid.
 */
class ClockService {
  #machineId;   // 0‑65535 unique per process (static)
  #counter;     // Monotonically increasing per‑process counter

  constructor(name) {
    const tag = name || `${require('os').hostname()}-${process.pid}`;
    this.#machineId = getMachineId(tag);
    this.#counter = 0;
  }

  /**
   * Generate the next globally‑unique, ordered identifier.
   *
   * Format (lexicographically sortable):
   *   <highResTime>-<machineIdHex>-<counterHex>
   *
   * - `highResTime` : nanoseconds since arbitrary epoch, encoded as a
   *   36‑radix string (base‑36) – gives a nice compact representation.
   * - `machineIdHex`: two‑character hex value (0‑FFFF).
   * - `counterHex`  : arbitrary‑length hex value (auto‑increments).
   *
   * @returns {string}
   */
  now() {
    // nanoseconds from an arbitrary epoch – high resolution & monotonic.
    const ns = process.hrtime.bigint();          // Node ≥12.7
    const ts = ns.toString(36);                  // compact, sortable
    const mid = this.#machineId.toString(16).padStart(2, '0');
    const cnt = (++this.#counter).toString(16);
    return `${ts}-${mid}-${cnt}`;
  }

  /**
   * Compare two IDs produced by this service.
   *
   * @param {string} a
   * @param {string} b
   * @returns {number} -1 if a < b, 0 if a === b, 1 if a > b
   */
  compare(a, b) {
    if (a === b) return 0;
    // Lexicographic string compare mirrors the intended numeric ordering.
    return a < b ? -1 : 1;
  }

  /** (Optional) Reset the counter – useful for unit‑testing. */
  reset() {
    this.#counter = 0;
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage (you can run this file directly: node clock-service.js)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
if (require.main === module) {
  const svc = new ClockService('doc‑demo‑1');

  const id1 = svc.now();
  const id2 = svc.now();
  const id3 = svc.now();

  console.log('Generated IDs:');
  console.log('  id1', id1);
  console.log('  id2', id2);
  console.log('  id3', id3);

  console.log('\nOrdering checks:');
  console.log('  id1 < id2 ?', svc.compare(id1, id2) === -1); // true
  console.log('  id2 < id3 ?', svc.compare(id2, id3) === -1); // true
  console.log('  id1 < id3 ?', svc.compare(id1, id3) === -1); // true
}
```

---

### How to read the generated ID

| Part | Meaning | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| `ts` | Nanosecond‑precision wall‑clock (arbitrary epoch) | `1jv9g4l0h8b` |
| `mid`| 16‑bit static machine/process identifier (hex) | `1f` |
| `cnt`| Per‑process monotonic counter (hex) | `3` |

The string **`1jv9g4l0h8b-1f-3`** will always sort **after** any ID with a smaller timestamp, machine id, or counter.  
Because the three components are all derived locally, no network round‑trip or synchronized clock is required.

---

### TL;DR – What you get

| Property | How it’s satisfied |
|----------|--------------------|
| **Total order** | Lexicographic ordering of `(time, machineId, counter)` |
| **No shared clock** | Uses each machine’s own high‑resolution timer (`process.hrtime.bigint()`) |
| **No message exchange** | All data (time, id, counter) lives on the local process |
| **Uniqueness** | Machine ID + per‑process counter guarantee no collisions |

---

#### When to upgrade to a “real” distributed clock

If your system later requires **causal ordering** (i.e. “event B must see the effects of event A even if they are generated on different machines without immediate communication”), you’ll need to add a **Lamport logical clock** or **Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC)** that piggy‑backs on your existing messages. That *does* require a minimal amount of metadata exchange, but the snippet above remains a perfect foundation for generating the **event IDs** that those clocks will annotate.

---

*Feel free to copy‑paste the file into your internal docs, strip the comments for a single‑liner, or expand it with persistence (e.g. store the counter in Redis) if you need durability across process restarts.*

