## 2️⃣ CRDT‑based counter (eventual / monotonic consistency)

If you cannot use RDMA and you are okay with **eventual** (or monotonic) consistency, a *PN‑Counter* (Positive‑Negative Counter) is trivial to implement and needs **no coordination** whatsoever.

```go
// -------------------------------------------------------------
// crdt_counter.go
// -------------------------------------------------------------
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

// PNCounter implements a grow‑only positive and a grow‑only negative
// counter, yielding a counter that can both increase and decrease.
// It is a conflict‑free replicated data type (CRDT) and therefore
// eventually consistent: all replicas converge to the same value
// after they have exchanged their state, but reads may be stale
// for a brief period.
//
// Single‑round‑trip writes: a client can send its delta to any replica
// (or broadcast to all). The replica applies the delta locally and
// later gossips its state to the rest. No leader, no quorum.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
type PNCounter struct {
	mu    sync.RWMutex
	P, N  int64 // P = sum of all positive deltas, N = sum of all negative deltas
}

// Inc adds `v` (v >= 0) to the counter.
func (c *PNCounter) Inc(v int64) {
	if v < 0 {
		panic("Inc called with negative value")
	}
	c.mu.Lock()
	c.P += v
	c.mu.Unlock()
}

// Dec subtracts `v` (v >= 0) from the counter.
func (c *PNCounter) Dec(v int64) {
	if v < 0 {
		panic("Dec called with negative value")
	}
	c.mu.Lock()
	c.N += v
	c.mu.Unlock()
}

// Value returns the current effective value (P - N).  This read is
// locally linearizable (i.e., the caller sees the most recent write
// that it performed on the same replica), but other replicas may lag.
func (c *PNCounter) Value() int64 {
	c.mu.RLock()
	defer c.mu.RUnlock()
	return c.P - c.N
}

// Merge merges the state of another PN‑Counter into this one.
// It is the CRDT “join” operation and is idempotent, commutative,
// and associative – perfect for gossip dissemination.
func (c *PNCounter) Merge(other *PNCounter) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	other.mu.RLock()
	c.P = max64(c.P, other.P) // take the max of the positive components
	c.N = max64(c.N, other.N) // take the max of the negative components
	other.mu.RUnlock()
}

func max64(a, b int64) int64 {
	if a > b {
		return a
	}
	return b
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// A tiny gossip layer that periodically pushes state to a few peers.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
type Node struct {
	ID      int
	Counter PNCounter
	Peers   []*Node // known peer nodes (e.g., from config or discovery)
	mu      sync.Mutex
}

func (n *Node) ApplyDelta(isInc bool, v int64) {
	if isInc {
		n.Counter.Inc(v)
	} else {
		n.Counter.Dec(v)
	}
	// Asynchronously push the new state to peers.
	go n.gossip()
}

func (n *Node) gossip() {
	n.mu.Lock()
	peers := make([]*Node, len(n.Peers))
	copy(peers, n.Peers)
	n.mu.Unlock()

	for _, p := range peers {
		go func(peer *Node) {
			peer.Counter.Merge(&n.Counter)
		}(p)
	}
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// Example HTTP API for a single‑node replica (use a reverse proxy or
// service mesh for a real deployment).
// -------------------------------------------------------------
import (
	"encoding/json"
	"log"
	"net/http"
)

func (n *Node) httpAPI() {
	http.HandleFunc("/inc", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		var req struct {
			V int64 `json:"v"`
		}
		if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
			http.Error(w, err.Error(), 400)
			return
		}
		n.ApplyDelta(true, req.V)
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)
	})
	http.HandleFunc("/dec", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		var req struct {
			V int64 `json:"v"`
		}
		if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
			http.Error(w, err.Error(), 400)
			return
		}
		n.ApplyDelta(false, req.V)
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)
	})
	http.HandleFunc("/value", func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) {
		json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(map[string]int64{"value": n.Counter.Value()})
	})
	log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil))
}

func main() {
	// Simple demo: create three nodes and wire them as peers.
	n1 := &Node{ID: 1}
	n2 := &Node{ID: 2}
	n3 := &Node{ID: 3}
	n1.Peers = []*Node{n2, n3}
	n2.Peers = []*Node{n1, n3}
	n3.Peers = []*Node{n1, n2}

	go n1.httpAPI() // start HTTP server on port 8080 (first node only)

	// Simulate a client incrementing the counter once on node 1.
	n1.ApplyDelta(true, 42)
	fmt.Printf("Node1 value after inc 42: %d\n", n1.Counter.Value())

	// Give gossip a moment to propagate.
	// (In production, use a proper gossip tick or a time‑based scheduler.)
	// For now, just wait a second.
	time.Sleep(time.Second)

	fmt.Printf("Node2 value after gossip: %d\n", n2.Counter.Value())
	fmt.Printf("Node3 value after gossip: %d\n", n3.Counter.Value())
}
```

### Why this fits the “no leader / no quorum” requirement

* **No coordinator** – any replica can accept a `Inc`/`Dec` request.
* **Single‑round‑trip write** – the client sends the delta to *one* replica (or broadcasts to all). The replica applies the delta immediately and later gossips its state in the background. The client gets an acknowledgment after the local apply (i.e., a single round‑trip to the replica).
* **Eventual consistency** – all replicas converge after the gossip cycle (typically a few hundred milliseconds). If you need **read‑after‑write** guarantees for the *same* client, you can simply read from the replica you wrote to; that read is locally linearizable.

### Caveats

* **Stale reads** – other clients may see an older value until the gossip propagates.
* **Monotonicity** – the counter only grows/decreases; you cannot “reset” to an arbitrary value without breaking the CRDT property.
* **Security** – gossip messages should be authenticated (e.g., TLS) to prevent injection.

