Pick the one that matches your environment, fill in the missing pieces (QP exchange, TLS, gossip tick, service discovery, etc.), and you’ll have a replicated counter that satisfies the **exact** set of constraints you can realistically meet. Good luck, and happy coding!

Below is a complete, step‑by‑step answer that shows **why** you can’t get deterministic Byzantine‑agreement with \(n=2f\) in the ordinary “oral‑messages’’ model, **what** you need to change to make it work, and a **compact C++17 prototype** that implements a simple signed‑message Byzantine‑broadcast (the basis of any BFT service) that works when the system size satisfies the known lower bounds (\(n>2f\) in the authenticated model).

