## 1.  Theory‑check – why \(n=2f\) is not enough

| Model | Minimum \(n\) for deterministic BA |
|-------|--------------------------------------|
| **Oral messages** (no signatures) | \(n>3f\) (i.e. \(n\ge 3f+1\)) |
| **Signed messages** (authenticated) | \(n>2f\) (i.e. \(n\ge 2f+1\)) |
| **Partial synchrony** (the DLS style) | \(n>2f\) (i.e. \(n\ge 2f+1\)) |
| **Randomised (Ben‑Or)** | \(n>5f\) (improved protocols can go down to \(n>2f\) but only **with extra cryptographic help**) |

*Key point*: With plain oral messages a Byzantine adversary can make two honest parties disagree as soon as the number of total nodes is **≤ 3 f**.  Adding digital signatures eliminates the “equivocation’’ problem and lowers the bound to **\(n>2f\)**.  When \(n=2f\) you are *exactly* at the bound, which means the adversary can partition the honest nodes into two groups, each of size \(f\), and make each group see a different set of signatures – breaking agreement.

**What this means for your project**

* If you really need **deterministic safety**, you must increase the total number of nodes to at least \(2f+1\).  
* If you are fine with **probabilistic safety** (i.e., the protocol may, with negligible probability, violate agreement), you can use a randomised protocol such as *Ben‑Or* or a modern *cryptographic‑sortition* approach (Algorand, HoneyBadgerBFT). Those protocols also need more than half the nodes to be honest, so you still need \(n\ge 2f+1\).  
* If you want to keep the **exact** ratio \(n=2f\) you must **add a trusted component** (e.g. a trusted dealer that issues a threshold‑signature key‑share, a hardware security module, or a “common‑coin’’ service that is trusted to be non‑Byzantine).  

For the code below we will stick to the **authenticated model** (digital signatures) and **increase the system size to \(n = 2f+1\)**.  This satisfies the known lower bound and lets us illustrate a clean, textbook‑style double‑echo broadcast that is the heart of any BFT service (e.g. PBFT, HotStuff, Tendermint).

---

## 2.  High‑level protocol (signed double‑echo broadcast)

The protocol we implement is the classic **“signed Byzantine broadcast’’** (sometimes called **Bracha‑style double‑echo**).  
In the authenticated model it works for any \(n>2f\).

* **Roles**  
  * **Proposer** – the node that initially holds the value \(v\) to be agreed on.  
  * **Receiver** – the other \(n-1\) nodes.

* **Message types**  

| Type | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `PROPOSE` | Proposer broadcasts the candidate value. |
| `ACK` | A node acknowledges that it saw the `PROPOSE` and is willing to “echo’’ it. |
| `READY` | After enough `ACK`s, a node broadcasts a stronger “ready to commit’’ message. |
| `DECIDE` | Once a node has gathered enough `READY`s it decides on the value. |

* **Quorum size**  

\[
Q = f+1\;(= \text{ threshold } t)
\]

Why \(f+1\)?  With at most \(f\) Byzantine nodes, any set of \(f+1\) signatures **must contain at least one honest participant**.  Hence a message that carries \(f+1\) valid signatures can be trusted as “originating from an honest node”.

* **Message format (simplified)**  

```text
struct Message {
    uint32_t  type;                // PROPOSE, ACK, READY
    uint32_t  round;               // we use a single round in this toy implementation
    uint32_t  sender;              // node id
    std::string payload;           // value (or any binary data)
    std::vector<std::pair<uint32_t, std::string>> sigs; // (nodeId, signature)
};
```

* **Protocol flow**

```
Proposer:
    send PROPOSE(v) to everybody

All receivers:
    on PROPOSE(v):
        if not yet sent ACK for v:
            sign( PROPOSE(v) + myId ) → sig
            send ACK(v, sig) to everybody

    on ACK(v, signerId, sig):
        if sig is valid and signerId not already counted:
            add sig to ACK-set[v]
            if |ACK-set[v]| >= Q and not yet sent READY:
                sign( ACK(v) + myId ) → sig2
                send READY(v, sig2) to everybody

    on READY(v, signerId, sig):
        if sig is valid and signerId not already counted:
            add sig to READY-set[v]
            if |READY-set[v]| >= Q and not yet decided:
                DECIDE(v)   // final decision
```

The protocol terminates after at most one round of messages because the **signed‑message double‑echo** guarantees that once a node has \(Q\) `READY`s, all honest nodes will also eventually have \(Q\) `READY`s (due to message propagation) and therefore decide the same value.

