## 6.  Quick FAQ

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| **Can I keep the ratio exactly \(n=2f\)?** | In the *deterministic* authenticated model you **cannot** guarantee safety; you need at least one extra honest node (\(n\ge 2f+1\)).  If you accept a *probabilistic* guarantee (e.g., a randomized protocol with a negligible chance of violation), you still need more than half the nodes honest, so again \(n>2f\).  The only way to keep the exact 2 f ratio is to add a **trusted component** (e.g., a hardware security module that acts as a non‑Byzantine validator). |
| **Why does the code use HMAC‑SHA256 instead of real signatures?** | It is **only** for a self‑contained illustration.  In production you must replace it with a standard, audited signature scheme (Ed25519, ECDSA, BLS). |
| **Is this protocol tolerant to network partitions?** | Yes – the double‑echo broadcast guarantees **safety** even if the network splits, as long as at most `f` nodes are Byzantine.  **Liveness** requires the network to eventually become synchronous (the usual partial‑synchrony assumption). |
| **How do I extend this to a full BFT state‑machine replication (SMR) service?** | Replace the single‑value broadcast with a **replicated log** (as in PBFT/HotStuff).  Each log entry goes through the same signed‑message phases (PRE‑PREPARE → PREPARE → COMMIT).  After a log entry is committed you apply it to the service’s state machine. |
| **What about performance?** | The double‑echo protocol sends \(O(N^2)\) messages (each node echoes to everyone).  Production systems improve this by using **signature aggregation** (BLS) or **threshold signatures**, reducing the per‑round cost to \(O(N)\) messages and a single aggregated signature.  Many BFT libraries already do this. |

