Below is a compact, production‑ready implementation of the classic **Strassen algorithm** (the fast‑multiplication method that most people refer to when they talk about “Strassen” in the context of matrix multiplication).  
The code is written in Python, but the same ideas translate directly to C++/Java/C# if you need a native‑code path for a performance‑critical service.

---

## 1. How the algorithm works (high‑level)

1. **Divide & Conquer** – Split the two *n × n* matrices into four sub‑matrices each  
   \[
   A=\begin{pmatrix}A_{11}&A_{12}\\A_{21}&A_{22}\end{pmatrix},
   \qquad
   B=\begin{pmatrix}B_{11}&B_{12}\\B_{21}&B_{22}\end{pmatrix}
   \]
2. **Form 7 intermediate products** (instead of the naïve 8)  

   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   M_1 &:= (A_{11}+A_{22})(B_{11}+B_{22})\\
   M_2 &:= (A_{21}+A_{22})B_{11}\\
   M_3 &:= A_{11}(B_{12}-B_{22})\\
   M_4 &:= A_{22}(B_{21}-B_{11})\\
   M_5 &:= (A_{11}+A_{12})B_{22}\\
   M_6 &:= (A_{21}-A_{11})(B_{11}+B_{12})\\
   M_7 &:= (A_{12}-A_{22})(B_{21}+B_{22})
   \end{aligned}
   \]

3. **Combine the 7 M‑terms** to obtain the four quadrants of the result  

   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   C_{11} &= M_1+M_4-M_5+M_7\\
   C_{12} &= M_3+M_5\\
   C_{21} &= M_2+M_4\\
   C_{22} &= M_1-M_2+M_3+M_6
   \end{aligned}
   \]

4. **Recursive termination** – For tiny matrices (e.g. ≤ 64 × 64) the overhead of recursion outweighs the savings, so the algorithm falls back to the classic triple‑loop (`O(n³)`) multiplication.

The recurrence for the run‑time satisfies  

\[
T(n)=7\,T\!\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)+O(n^{2})\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
T(n)=O\!\bigl(n^{\log_{2}7}\bigr)\approx O(n^{2.807}),
\]

which is asymptotically faster than the naïve \(O(n^{3})\).

> **Why “quintic”?**  
> The name you mentioned (“Peele‑Strassen quintic multiply”) is not a standard term in the literature. It may be a blend of “Strassen” (the fast‑multiplication algorithm) and “Peele” (perhaps a recent paper or internal naming). The algorithm presented above implements the same ideas that make Strassen’s method fast; if you truly need a method whose exponent is 5, that would be *slower* than naïve multiplication and would never be used in practice. The implementation below is therefore the most useful “fast” algorithm that fits the description.

