Below is a **complete, minimal‑but‑runnable** example that implements a **replicated, in‑memory cache** with **read‑your‑writes consistency** across replicas.  
The trick is **synchronous replication** – every write is applied to **all** replicas before the caller gets an ACK.  
Because every replica has the same state at that moment, any subsequent read (even from a different node) is guaranteed to see the just‑written value.  
No session token, no version number, no “last‑seen‑index” needs to be stored on the client side.

---

## 1. Why this works – the contract you get

| Step | What happens | What the client sees |
|------|--------------|----------------------|
| **PUT key value** | The leader (or coordinator) sends the update to **all** replicas, each applies it to its local map, and each replica replies **OK**. Only after **all** replies does the leader return success. | The call returns *only* after every replica has persisted the entry. |
| **GET key** | The client talks to any replica. Because the replica’s map already contains the entry (it was applied synchronously), the value returned is the one just written. | No extra round‑trip for a “freshness check” is needed – the replica cannot be stale. |

If a replica is temporarily unreachable the PUT fails, so the client knows the write was **not** made anywhere. This gives you **linearizability** (a.k.a. *read‑your‑writes*) without any client‑side version bookkeeping.

---

## 2. High‑level architecture

```
┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐
│  Client A    │          │  Client B    │          │  Client C    │
└──────┬───────┘          └──────┬───────┘          └──────┬───────┘
       │ PUT/GET                 │ PUT/GET                 │ PUT/GET
       ▼                         ▼                         ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                         RPC / HTTP                           │
└──────┬──────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────┘
       │                      │                      │
       ▼                      ▼                      ▼
  ┌────────┐            ┌────────┐            ┌────────┐
  │Replica-1│◄────────►│Replica-2│◄────────►│Replica-3│
  │(leader) │  sync    │(follower)│  sync    │(follower)│
  └────────┘  write    └────────┘  write    └────────┘
```

*All replicas keep an identical `map[string]string` (the cache).  
*The **leader** (or a single coordinator) is the only node that **coordinates** the synchronous write‑to‑all step.  
*Read requests are served locally – no extra coordination needed after a successful PUT.

If you need **fault‑tolerance** and **leader election**, you can replace the “simple leader” with a Raft/etcd/ZooKeeper layer (see the optional extension at the bottom).

