That's an interesting advanced topic! The **Kundu-Rahman variant of Dijkstra's algorithm** is a theoretical optimization that claims **O(m + n log n)** time complexity (same as standard Dijkstra with a Fibonacci heap) but with **constant-time decrease-key operations**. However, in practice, implementing a proper Fibonacci heap in Java from scratch is complex, and most libraries don't provide it.

Let me clarify: The **Kundu-Rahman algorithm** is primarily of theoretical interest. For practical purposes in Java, you're usually better off using:

1. **Standard Dijkstra with a binary heap** (like `PriorityQueue`) - O(m log n)
2. **Dial's algorithm** (bucket-based) for integer weights - O(m + nC) where C is the max edge weight

Here's a practical implementation of **standard Dijkstra's algorithm** in Java that you can build upon:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DijkstraExample {

    // Graph representation using adjacency list
    static class Graph {
        int vertices;
        List<List<Edge>> adjacencyList;

        Graph(int vertices) {
            this.vertices = vertices;
            adjacencyList = new ArrayList<>(vertices);
            for (int i = 0; i < vertices; i++) {
                adjacencyList.add(new ArrayList<>());
            }
        }

        void addEdge(int source, int destination, int weight) {
            adjacencyList.get(source).add(new Edge(destination, weight));
            // For undirected graph, add reverse edge
            // adjacencyList.get(destination).add(new Edge(source, weight));
        }
    }

    static class Edge {
        int destination;
        int weight;

        Edge(int destination, int weight) {
            this.destination = destination;
            this.weight = weight;
        }
    }

    static class Node implements Comparable<Node> {
        int vertex;
        int distance;

        Node(int vertex, int distance) {
            this.vertex = vertex;
            this.distance = distance;
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(Node other) {
            return Integer.compare(this.distance, other.distance);
        }
    }

    public static int[] dijkstra(Graph graph, int source) {
        int n = graph.vertices;
        int[] dist = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        dist[source] = 0;

        // Priority queue for vertices to be processed
        PriorityQueue<Node> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.offer(new Node(source, 0));

        // Track processed vertices (optional optimization)
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = pq.poll();
            int u = current.vertex;

            if (visited[u]) continue;
            visited[u] = true;

            // Explore neighbors
            for (Edge edge : graph.adjacencyList.get(u)) {
                int v = edge.destination;
                int newDist = dist[u] + edge.weight;

                // Relaxation step
                if (newDist < dist[v]) {
                    dist[v] = newDist;
                    pq.offer(new Node(v, newDist));
                }
            }
        }

        return dist;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Graph graph = new Graph(6);

        // Example graph
        graph.addEdge(0, 1, 4);
        graph.addEdge(0, 2, 2);
        graph.addEdge(1, 2, 1);
        graph.addEdge(1, 3, 5);
        graph.addEdge(2, 3, 8);
        graph.addEdge(2, 4, 10);
        graph.addEdge(3, 4, 2);
        graph.addEdge(3, 5, 6);
        graph.addEdge(4, 5, 3);

        int source = 0;
        int[] distances = dijkstra(graph, source);

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from vertex " + source + ":");
        for (int i = 0; i < distances.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("To " + i + ": " + 
                (distances[i] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? "∞" : distances[i]));
        }
    }
}
```

