**Why it works**

On x86‑64 a store that fits completely inside a cache‑line (64 bytes) and whose address is 64‑byte aligned is guaranteed to be **atomic** and **globally visible** as soon as the instruction retires.  
The CPU does not need a `mfence`, `lock`‑prefix, or any other barrier – the coherence protocol makes the new value visible to every other core immediately.

The code below uses that guarantee to build a *single‑producer / single‑consumer* (SPSC) lock‑free ring‑buffer where each message is exactly 64 bytes.  
The producer writes the whole message with a single AVX‑512 store (`_mm512_store_si512`), which is exactly the atomic 64‑byte store we rely on.  The indices are only updated by one side each, but we still give them release / acquire semantics so that the compiler cannot reorder the data write past the index update.

---

## Implementation (C++17 + AVX‑512)

```cpp
#include <immintrin.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * 64‑byte message type – must be aligned on a 64‑byte boundary.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct Msg {
    alignas(64) uint8_t data[64];
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Simple SPSC queue with a power‑of‑two ring of 64‑byte slots.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
class SPSCQueue {
    static constexpr size_t SIZE = 1024;          // must be power of two
    static constexpr size_t MASK = SIZE - 1;

    /* The slots themselves are 64‑byte aligned */
    alignas(64) Msg slots[SIZE];

    /* Only the producer writes write_idx, only the consumer reads it */
    alignas(64) uint64_t write_idx = 0;
    alignas(64) uint64_t read_idx  = 0;

public:
    /* -------------------------------------------------
     * Producer side: enqueue a 64‑byte message.
     * ------------------------------------------------- */
    void enqueue(const void* msg) {
        uint64_t idx = __atomic_load_n(&write_idx, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
        /* Copy the whole message with one atomic 64‑byte store. */
        _mm512_store_si512(
            reinterpret_cast<__m512i*>(slots[idx & MASK].data),
            _mm512_load_si512(static_cast<const __m512i*>(msg))
        );
        /* Release the index – makes the data visible to consumers. */
        __atomic_store_n(&write_idx, idx + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------
     * Consumer side: dequeue a 64‑byte message.
     * Returns false if the queue is empty.
     * ------------------------------------------------- */
    bool dequeue(void* out) {
        uint64_t idx = __atomic_load_n(&read_idx, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
        uint64_t nxt = __atomic_load_n(&write_idx, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
        if (nxt == idx) return false;               // empty

        /* Atomic 64‑byte load */
        _mm512_store_si512(
            reinterpret_cast<__m512i*>(out),
            _mm512_load_si512(reinterpret_cast<const __m512i*>(slots[idx & MASK].data))
        );
        /* Release the read index – we have consumed the slot. */
        __atomic_store_n(&read_idx, idx + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
        return true;
    }
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 * Minimal example of use (producer in main, consumer in a thread)
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    SPSCQueue q;

    std::thread consumer([&q]() {
        Msg tmp;
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
            while (!q.dequeue(&tmp)) { /* spin */ }
            std::cout << "got: " << (int)tmp.data[0] << '\n';
        }
    });

    /* Produce 5 messages */
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        Msg m;
        std::memset(m.data, 0, 64);
        m.data[0] = static_cast<uint8_t>(i + 1);
        q.enqueue(&m);
    }

    consumer.join();
    return 0;
}
```

---

## How it works – step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it’s safe |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Alignment** | `Msg`, `slots[]`, `write_idx`, `read_idx` are all declared with `alignas(64)`. | Guarantees that any access to a slot or index is naturally aligned for the hardware’s atomic guarantees. |
| **Producer writes a slot** | `enqueue` does a single `_mm512_store_si512` of the whole 64‑byte message. | The store is a single instruction that writes exactly one cache line. On x86‑64 such a store is **atomic** and becomes visible to all cores instantly – no `mfence` needed. |
| **Producer updates the index** | The index is written with `__ATOMIC_RELEASE`. | This prevents the compiler (and the CPU’s memory‑ordering logic) from moving the index write *before* the data store. The release semantics guarantee that any later acquire of the same index will see the just‑written message. |
| **Consumer checks the index** | `dequeue` loads `write_idx` with `__ATOMIC_ACQUIRE`. | The acquire pairs with the producer’s release, ensuring the consumer sees the data that was stored before the index was released. |
| **Consumer reads the slot** | Again a single `_mm512_load_si512` + `_mm512_store_si512` copies the 64 bytes. | Because the producer’s store was atomic and globally visible, the consumer sees the fresh data without needing any extra fence. |
| **Consumer advances its own index** | `__ATOMIC_RELEASE` on `read_idx`. | Keeps the two‑phase “data‑then‑index” protocol sound. |

The critical observation is that **the 64‑byte store itself already provides the “instant visibility” property** that many lock‑free algorithms normally buy with a `std::atomic_thread_fence` or a `mfence`. By laying out the data so that each message occupies a whole cache line and is aligned, we can skip those heavyweight barriers while still guaranteeing that no other core can see a partially‑written message.

---

### When AVX‑512 is not available

If your target does not have AVX‑512 you can fall back to two 32‑byte stores (`_mm256_store_si256`) **plus** a `mfence` after the pair, or use the `movdir64b` instruction (Intel TBM) which performs a guaranteed 64‑byte atomic store even on older CPUs.  
The example above sticks to the pure “no‑fence” path that only exists when a single 64‑byte instruction can be used.

