Below is a **self‑contained shared‑library** that forces **every call to `malloc` (and also `realloc`/`calloc`) to return zero‑initialised memory**.  
It works by intercepting the allocation functions before the program starts (via `LD_PRELOAD`) and adding a thin wrapper that:

1. **allocates the raw block from the C library**,  
2. **stores the size of the block in a tiny header**,  
3. **zeroes the user‑visible part before returning the pointer**, and  
4. **for `realloc` copies the old data into a new zero‑filled block**.

Because the wrapper knows the size of every allocation (via the header) it can also safely `free` the block and correctly expand memory for `realloc`.

---

## `zero_malloc.c`

```c
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1.  Resolve the *real* C‑library functions once at load time.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------- */
static void *(*real_malloc)(size_t)  = NULL;
static void *(*real_calloc)(size_t, size_t) = NULL;
static void (*real_free)(void *)    = NULL;
static void *(*real_realloc)(void *, size_t) = NULL;

static void init(void) __attribute__((constructor));
static void init(void)
{
    real_malloc  = dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "malloc");
    real_calloc  = dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "calloc");
    real_free    = dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "free");
    real_realloc = dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "realloc");
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2.  Layout of our header – we keep the size of the block here.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------- */
typedef struct {
    size_t size;          /* user‑requested size */
    /* the actual user data follows immediately after this header */
} block_hdr;

/* helper to move from user pointer to header and back */
static inline block_hdr *hdr_from_ptr(void *ptr)
{
    return (block_hdr *)((char *)ptr - sizeof(block_hdr));
}
static inline void *ptr_from_hdr(block_hdr *hdr)
{
    return (char *)hdr + sizeof(block_hdr);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3.  Wrapped allocation functions.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* malloc – always zero‑initialised */
void *malloc(size_t size)
{
    if (!real_malloc) init();

    if (size == 0) return NULL;

    /* allocate header + user block */
    block_hdr *hdr = real_malloc(sizeof(block_hdr) + size);
    if (!hdr) return NULL;

    hdr->size = size;
    void *ptr = ptr_from_hdr(hdr);

    /* zero the user‑visible part */
    memset(ptr, 0, size);
    return ptr;
}

/* calloc – we can just forward to the real calloc (it already zeros)
 * but we still need the header for our size bookkeeping. */
void *calloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size)
{
    if (!real_calloc) init();

    size_t total = sizeof(block_hdr) + nmemb * size;
    block_hdr *hdr = real_calloc(1, total);
    if (!hdr) return NULL;

    hdr->size = nmemb * size;               /* store user size */
    return ptr_from_hdr(hdr);               /* user data is already 0 */
}

/* free – strip the header and hand the block back to the libc */
void free(void *ptr)
{
    if (!real_free) init();
    if (!ptr) return;

    block_hdr *hdr = hdr_from_ptr(ptr);
    real_free(hdr);
}

/* realloc – allocate a new zero‑filled block, copy old data,
 * then free the old block. */
void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t newsize)
{
    if (!real_realloc) init();

    if (!ptr)               /* behave like malloc */
        return malloc(newsize);

    if (newsize == 0) {
        free(ptr);
        return NULL;
    }

    block_hdr *old_hdr = hdr_from_ptr(ptr);
    size_t old_size    = old_hdr->size;

    /* allocate new block (already zero‑filled) */
    block_hdr *new_hdr = real_malloc(sizeof(block_hdr) + newsize);
    if (!new_hdr) return NULL;

    new_hdr->size = newsize;
    void *new_ptr = ptr_from_hdr(new_hdr);

    /* copy the smaller of old/new */
    size_t copy = (old_size < newsize) ? old_size : newsize;
    memcpy(new_ptr, ptr, copy);

    /* zero the remainder if the block grew */
    if (newsize > old_size)
        memset((char *)new_ptr + old_size, 0, newsize - old_size);

    /* free the old block (header included) */
    real_free(old_hdr);
    return new_ptr;
}
```

---

## How it works (quick walk‑through)

| Function | What it does |
|----------|--------------|
| **`init()`** | A *constructor* that runs **before** `main()`. It uses `dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, …)` to obtain the *real* libc symbols (`malloc`, `calloc`, `free`, `realloc`). |
| **`malloc`** | Calls the underlying `real_malloc` to get *header + user* memory, writes the user‑requested size into the header, then `memset`s the user part to zero before returning the pointer **after** the header. |
| **`calloc`** | Calls the underlying `real_calloc` (which already zeros memory) for *header + user* memory, stores the size, and returns the pointer after the header. |
| **`free`** | Converts the user pointer back to the real block start (by subtracting the header size) and hands it to `real_free`. |
| **`realloc`** | 1. Gets the old size from the header.<br>2. Allocates a new block (zero‑filled) via `real_malloc`.<br>3. Copies the smaller of old/new bytes.<br>4. Zeros any extra bytes if the block grew.<br>5. Frees the old block via `real_free`. |
| **Header** | A tiny (`sizeof(size_t)`) structure placed **immediately before** the pointer returned to the caller. It stores the allocation size so `realloc` and `free` can operate without relying on non‑standard extensions. |

Because every allocation is funneled through these wrappers, the program **never sees memory that has not been explicitly zeroed** – even if the C library’s internal allocator would normally leave it uninitialised.

---

## Building & using the library

```bash
# 1. Compile as a shared library
gcc -shared -fPIC -Wall -O2 -o libzero_malloc.so zero_malloc.c -ldl

# 2. Run any program with the library pre‑loaded
LD_PRELOAD=./libzero_malloc.so ./my_service
```

*All* calls to `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, and `free` made by `my_service` (including any libraries it links against) will go through the wrappers above, guaranteeing zero‑initialised memory.

