## 3. Tuning the detector

| Parameter | What it controls | Typical values |
|-----------|------------------|----------------|
| **phiThreshold** | Sensitivity – larger = *less* sensitive (more tolerant of delayed heartbeats). | `4.0` (very aggressive) … `12.0` (very tolerant) |
| **windowSize** | Number of recent intervals used for the statistical model. Larger = smoother, but slower to adapt. | `200` … `10 000` |
| **checkInterval** | How often we recompute φ. Smaller = faster detection, more CPU. | `50 ms` … `1 s` |
| **maxAcceptableHeartbeatPause** (implicit) | It is expressed via `phiThreshold`. If you want a hard real‑time pause bound, you can also enforce a *timeout* after which you treat the node as dead regardless of φ. | `Duration.ofSeconds(10)` |

> **Rule of thumb** – start with the defaults (`phiThreshold = 8.0`, `windowSize = 1000`, `checkInterval = 200 ms`).  
> Increase `phiThreshold` if you see many *false positives* (nodes marked down when they are just a bit slow).  
> Decrease it if you need *faster* detection and you can tolerate occasional false alarms.

If you have a **hard real‑time deadline** (e.g., “I must know within 2 seconds”), you can augment the detector with an additional safety timeout:

```java
if (Duration.between(lastHeartbeat, Instant.now()).compareTo(Duration.ofSeconds(2)) > 0) {
    // forced failure regardless of φ
    failureHandler.onFailure(nodeId);
}
```

