## 2️⃣ Production‑ready version – pinned memory + custom stream

The above program uses **regular host memory** (`std::vector`), which forces `cudaMemcpy` to be synchronous.  
If you want the host‑to‑device copy, kernel, and device‑to‑host copy to run **asynchronously** (and potentially overlap), you must:

* Use **pinned (page‑locked) host memory** (`cudaMallocHost`).
* Create a **CUDA stream** and pass it to every async call.

Below is a self‑contained, fully‑commented example.

```cpp
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   gpu_copy_and_sync.cu
   Demonstrates:
   • Pinned host allocation (cudaMallocHost)
   • Custom CUDA stream (cudaStreamCreate)
   • Async host‑to‑device copy (cudaMemcpyAsync)
   • Kernel launch in the same stream
   • Stream synchronisation (cudaStreamSynchronize)
   • Async device‑to‑host copy (cudaMemcpyAsync)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */

#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <iostream>

// --------------------------------------------------------------
// Error‑checking macro (adds line/file info on failure)
// --------------------------------------------------------------
#define CUDA_CHECK(call)                                                  \
    do {                                                                  \
        cudaError_t err = call;                                           \
        if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                         \
            std::cerr << "CUDA error at " << __FILE__ << ":" << __LINE__  \
                      << " – " << cudaGetErrorString(err) << '\n';        \
            std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                      \
        }                                                                 \
    } while (0)

// --------------------------------------------------------------
// Example kernel: double every integer
// --------------------------------------------------------------
__global__ void doubleElements(int *data, int N)
{
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (idx < N) data[idx] *= 2;
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    const int N = 1 << 20;                 // 1 048 576 ints
    const size_t bytes = N * sizeof(int);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Allocate *pinned* host memory for async transfers
    // -------------------------------------------------
    int *h_in = nullptr;   // input data on the host
    int *h_out = nullptr;  // output data on the host
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMallocHost(&h_in,  bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMallocHost(&h_out, bytes));

    // Fill the input vector (0,1,2,…)
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) h_in[i] = i;

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Allocate device memory
    // -------------------------------------------------
    int *d_data = nullptr;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_data, bytes));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Create a CUDA stream
    //    All async work submitted to this stream will execute
    //    in order, but can overlap with work in other streams.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    cudaStream_t stream;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamCreate(&stream));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Async copy: host → device
    //    The transfer is enqueued into `stream` and does not block.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpyAsync(d_data,          // destination
                               h_in,            // source
                               bytes,           // size in bytes
                               cudaMemcpyHostToDevice,
                               stream));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5. Launch the kernel inside the same stream
    //    Execution will start only after the previous copy finishes.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    int blockSize = 256;
    int gridSize  = (N + blockSize - 1) / blockSize;
    doubleElements<<<gridSize, blockSize, 0, stream>>>(d_data, N);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6. Synchronise the stream
    //    This blocks the host until the kernel (and any earlier
    //    operations in the same stream) have completed.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 7. Async copy: device → host
    //    Enqueue the copy, then synchronise again.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpyAsync(h_out,          // destination
                               d_data,         // source
                               bytes,
                               cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost,
                               stream));

    // Ensure the final copy is finished before we read `h_out`.
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 8. Use the result – here we just print a few values
    // -------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "First 5 doubled values: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) std::cout << h_out[i] << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 9. Clean up
    // -------------------------------------------------
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_data));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFreeHost(h_in));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFreeHost(h_out));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(stream));

    return 0;
}
```

### What’s happening here?

| Step | What you see in the code | Why it matters |
|------|--------------------------|----------------|
| **Pinned host memory** (`cudaMallocHost`) | `int *h_in = nullptr; cudaMallocHost(&h_in, bytes);` | Only page‑locked memory can be transferred asynchronously. Regular `malloc`/`new` would silently fall back to a synchronous copy. |
| **CUDA stream** (`cudaStreamCreate`) | `cudaStream_t stream; cudaStreamCreate(&stream);` | A stream is a queue of operations that execute in order. Using your own stream lets you have multiple independent pipelines (e.g., overlap copy‑in, compute, copy‑out). |
| **Async copy** (`cudaMemcpyAsync`) | `cudaMemcpyAsync(d_data, h_in, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream);` | Returns immediately; the actual transfer is scheduled in the stream. |
| **Kernel launch** (`<<<grid, blk, 0, stream>>>`) | `doubleElements<<<gridSize, blockSize, 0, stream>>>(d_data, N);` | The kernel also goes into the same stream, after the copy. |
| **Stream synchronisation** (`cudaStreamSynchronize`) | `cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);` | Blocks the host **only** until that particular stream finishes. It does **not** block other streams. |
| **Copy‑back** (`cudaMemcpyAsync`) + second sync | `cudaMemcpyAsync(h_out, d_data, ...); cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);` | Guarantees the output is ready before you read `h_out`. |
| **Resource release** (`cudaFree`, `cudaFreeHost`, `cudaStreamDestroy`) | In `main` cleanup section | Prevents memory leaks – important for long‑running applications. |

### Common pitfalls & tips

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|----------|--------------|-----|
| `cudaErrorInvalidValue` or weird results | Using regular `malloc` with `cudaMemcpyAsync` | Switch to pinned memory (`cudaMallocHost`) or use synchronous `cudaMemcpy`. |
| Host program finishes before the kernel finishes | Forgetting to call `cudaStreamSynchronize` (or `cudaDeviceSynchronize`) before reading results | Add the synchronise call before the copy‑back or before you read the host buffer. |
| Over‑subscription of GPU resources | Launching too many blocks/threads | Choose a reasonable block size (usually 128–256 threads) and enough blocks to fill the GPU (grid size = ⌈N / blockSize⌉). |
| No performance gain from streams | Running on the **default null stream** (`cudaStream = 0`) which serialises all work | Create a non‑null stream (`cudaStreamCreate`) and use it for both copies and kernel. |
| “Out of memory” errors | Not freeing device memory between runs | Call `cudaFree` when the data is no longer needed. |

---

## 3️⃣ Compile & run

Assuming the file is named `gpu_copy_and_sync.cu`:

```bash
