Below is a **ready‑to‑copy C++17 snippet** that gives you a tiny, thread‑safe “shared‑flags” wrapper built on `std::atomic` with explicit **`release`** writes and **`acquire`** reads.  
It’s deliberately kept simple so you can paste it straight into your project and move on.

---

## 1. The `SharedFlags` class

```cpp
// shared_flags.hpp
#pragma once

#include <array>
#include <atomic>
#include <cstddef>

/**
 * @brief A small collection of boolean flags that are safe to share between threads.
 *
 * All mutating operations use memory_order_release, and all observing operations
 * use memory_order_acquire. This gives you the classic “flag + data” synchronisation
 * pattern without heavier primitives.
 */
class SharedFlags
{
public:
    // You can change `MAX_FLAGS` to whatever you need (up to 64‑ish is fine).
    static constexpr std::size_t MAX_FLAGS = 8;

    SharedFlags()
    {
        // Initialise every flag to false with a relaxed order – no synchronisation needed.
        for (auto& f : flags_) {
            f.store(false, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        }
    }

    /** Set flag `i` to true. */
    void set(std::size_t i) noexcept
    {
        if (i < MAX_FLAGS) {
            // release‑write: this store “publishes” any data you wrote before it.
            flags_[i].store(true, std::memory_order_release);
        }
    }

    /** Reset flag `i` to false. */
    void reset(std::size_t i) noexcept
    {
        if (i < MAX_FLAGS) {
            // release‑write: same idea as set().
            flags_[i].store(false, std::memory_order_release);
        }
    }

    /** Test whether flag `i` is set. */
    bool test(std::size_t i) const noexcept
    {
        if (i < MAX_FLAGS) {
            // acquire‑read: sees the newest release‑write from any other thread.
            return flags_[i].load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        }
        return false;
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // Convenience helpers (optional, but handy when you only need a few flags)
    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // You can expose named constants instead of raw indices, e.g.:
    //   enum FlagIdx { DATA_READY = 0, SHUTDOWN = 1, ERROR = 2 };
    // then call set(DATA_READY) / test(SHUTDOWN) …
    // -------------------------------------------------------------

private:
    std::array<std::atomic<bool>, MAX_FLAGS> flags_;
};
```

### Why `release` on writes and `acquire` on reads?

* **`store(..., std::memory_order_release)`**  
  Guarantees that **all memory writes (e.g., your data structures) that happened before this store in the same thread are visible to any thread that later performs an `acquire` load of the same atomic variable**.

* **`load(..., std::memory_order_acquire)`**  
  Guarantees that **the reads you perform after this load will see any writes that were released by another thread**.

In other words, the flag acts as a **lightweight synchronisation barrier** for any data that you associate with it.

---

## 2. Tiny usage example (producer‑consumer)

```cpp
// main.cpp
#include "shared_flags.hpp"
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

// Create one global instance – adjust to your design (singleton, member, etc.)
SharedFlags g_flags;

// A flag index, just for readability.
enum FlagIdx : std::size_t { DATA_READY = 0, SHUTDOWN = 1 };

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Producer thread: does some work and then signals that data is ready.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
void producer()
{
    // Simulate some heavy computation…
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(200));

    // **Write your shared data first**
    // (In a real program you'd modify non‑atomic structures here.)

    // Then “publish” it by setting the flag with release semantics.
    g_flags.set(DATA_READY);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Consumer thread: waits until the flag is set (acquire semantics).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
void consumer()
{
    // Spin‑wait (you could also use std::atomic::wait() in C++20+).
    while (!g_flags.test(DATA_READY)) {
        std::this_thread::yield();           // cheap sleep, avoid busy‑loop.
    }

    // At this point the consumer is guaranteed to see any data that the
    // producer wrote before setting DATA_READY.
    std::cout << "Consumer observed data as ready!\n";

    // You could reset the flag later if you need to reuse it.
    // g_flags.reset(DATA_READY);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    std::thread prod(producer);
    std::thread cons(consumer);

    prod.join();
    cons.join();

    // Simple demonstration of a second flag (SHUTDOWN).
    g_flags.set(SHUTDOWN);
    std::cout << "Shutdown flag is " << (g_flags.test(SHUTDOWN) ? "set" : "clear") << '\n';
}
```

**What you get from the snippet**

| Thread | Action | Memory ordering |
|--------|--------|-----------------|
| Producer | Writes data, then `set(DATA_READY)` | `release` – data becomes visible to any later `acquire` read |
| Consumer | `test(DATA_READY)` loops | `acquire` – once the flag is true, all prior writes of the producer are visible |

The same pattern works for any additional flags (`SHUTDOWN`, `ERROR`, etc.) you may need.

---

## 3. Quick checklist for your deadline

