Below is a **minimal, ready‑to‑run** example that shows how to add fast, modular routing to an Express app using the built‑in `express.Router`. The code is kept short so it can be dropped into your internal documentation or a starter project.

---

## 1️⃣ Install Express (if you haven’t already)

```bash
npm init -y           # creates a package.json
npm install express   # installs the express package
```

---

## 2️⃣ `index.js` – the whole app

```js
// -------------------------------------------------
//  index.js
//  Quick example of "fast routing" with Express
// -------------------------------------------------

// 1️⃣ Load Express and create the app
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 2️⃣ Middleware – JSON body parsing (optional but common)
app.use(express.json());

// -------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Define a Router (the “fast routing” part)
// -------------------------------------------------
const apiRouter = express.Router();

/**
 * GET /api/users
 * Returns a list of users (simulated)
 */
apiRouter.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  const users = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Carol' }
  ];
  res.json(users);
});

/**
 * GET /api/users/:id
 * Returns a single user by ID (simulated)
 */
apiRouter.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = parseInt(req.params.id, 10);
  // In a real app you’d query a DB here
  const user = { id, name: `User #${id}` };
  res.json(user);
});

/**
 * POST /api/users
 * Creates a new user (simulated)
 */
apiRouter.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  const { name } = req.body;
  // Simulate a created resource
  const newUser = { id: Date.now(), name };
  res.status(201).json(newUser);
});

// -------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣ Mount the router under a common path
// -------------------------------------------------
app.use('/api', apiRouter);

// -------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣ Start the server
// -------------------------------------------------
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`🚀 Server listening on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

---

## 📖 Why this is “fast”

- **Router‑level middleware** (`express.Router`) groups related routes together, reducing the number of middleware layers the request has to pass through.
- Express’s routing engine is highly optimized (compiled regex‑based path matching) and runs in pure JavaScript, so route lookups are very fast.
- By mounting the router on a prefix (`/api`) you keep the main app clean and make it easy to scale or replace the routing logic later.

---

## 🔎 How to test

```bash
node index.js               # starts the server on port 3000

# In another terminal (or use curl):
curl http://localhost:3000/api/users
curl http://localhost:3000/api/users/42
curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/users \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -d '{"name":"Dave"}'
```

You should see the JSON responses as defined in the code.

---

Feel free to copy the `index.js` snippet into your internal docs or starter repository. It demonstrates the recommended way to add **fast, modular routing** to any Express application. Happy coding!

