Below is a tiny, self‑contained example that adds **Tokio** as a dependency and demonstrates how to spin up an async task with the runtime.

---

## 1️⃣  Add Tokio to `Cargo.toml`

```toml
[package]
name = "tokio_demo"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```

> **Tip** – `features = ["full"]` enables all the most common run‑time features (`rt-multi-thread`, `macros`, `sync`, `time`, etc.).  
> In a real project you can pick a smaller set if you only need a subset (e.g. `features = ["rt", "macros"]`).

---

## 2️⃣  Write the async program in `src/main.rs`

```rust
use std::time::Duration;

// The `#[tokio::main]` attribute creates a `main` function that sets up a
// Tokio runtime and runs the async `main` function inside it.
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣ Spawn a background task that does some async work.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    let background_task = tokio::spawn(async {
        println!("[spawned] I'm an async task, starting now…");
        // Simulate a little async work (e.g. a DB call, HTTP request, etc.)
        tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(500)).await;
        println!("[spawned] …and I'm done after 500 ms!");
        // You can also return a value from the spawned task.
        42
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Meanwhile, the main task can do other work.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    println!("[main] I can do other things while the task runs…");
    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(200)).await;
    println!("[main] Doing even more work…");

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Await the spawned task to retrieve its result.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    match background_task.await {
        Ok(result) => println!("[main] Spawned task returned: {}", result),
        Err(e) => eprintln!("[main] Spawned task panicked: {:?}", e),
    }

    println!("[main] All done!");
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Piece | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| `#[tokio::main]` | Generates the proper `fn main() { … }` that boots a Tokio runtime (multi‑threaded by default when using the `"full"` feature). |
| `tokio::spawn(async { … })` | Fires off a new task that runs concurrently with the rest of the program. `spawn` returns a `JoinHandle<T>` that you can `.await` later. |
| `tokio::time::sleep` | An async‑aware way to pause a task without blocking the thread. |
| `background_task.await` | Blocks until the spawned task finishes, giving you its return value or propagation of a panic. |

---

## 3️⃣  Run it

```bash
cargo run
```

You should see output similar to:

```
[main] I can do other things while the task runs…
[spawned] I'm an async task, starting now…
[main] Doing even more work…
[spawned] …and I'm done after 500 ms!
[main] Spawned task returned: 42
[main] All done!
```

---

### Going further (optional)

* **Multiple tasks** – use `tokio::join!` or `tokio::try_join!` to run several futures concurrently.
  ```rust
  let (r1, r2) = tokio::join!(task1(), task2());
  ```

* **Cancellation** – drop the `JoinHandle` or use `tokio::sync::broadcast`/`oneshot` channels to signal abort.

* **Selective features** – if you want a tighter binary, replace `"full"` with a minimal set:
  ```toml
  tokio = { version = "1", features = ["rt", "macros", "time"] }
  ```

---

That’s it! You now have a working Tokio‑powered async program ready to be extended with real I/O, timers, or any of the many primitives Tokio provides. 🚀

