Below is a **self‑contained, minimal C++ program** that merges two **already sorted linked lists** **in descending order**.  
It follows the same idea as the classic “merge two sorted lists” problem, only we always pick the **larger** of the two current nodes.

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  Merge Two Sorted Linked Lists – Descending Order
 *
 *  Build & run:
 *      g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra merge_desc.cpp -o merge_desc
 *      ./merge_desc
 *
 *  The program creates two simple ascending‑sorted lists, merges them
 *  into a single descending‑sorted list, and prints the result.
 ********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 *  Definition of a singly‑linked list node (LeetCode style)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next;
    explicit ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
};

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 *  Merge two sorted lists **in descending order**.
 *
 *  Algorithm:
 *      - Use a dummy head to simplify edge‑cases.
 *      - While both lists are non‑empty, compare the front values.
 *        Append the larger one to the result list and advance that list.
 *      - When one list is exhausted, just attach the rest of the other.
 *
 *  Complexity:
 *      Time  : O(n + m)   (n,m = lengths of the two lists)
 *      Space : O(1)       (only a few pointers, no extra containers)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
ListNode* mergeTwoListsDesc(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {
    // Dummy head makes handling of the very first node trivial.
    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode* tail = &dummy;

    // Merge step – always take the larger current node.
    while (l1 && l2) {
        if (l1->val >= l2->val) {          // >= keeps duplicates together
            tail->next = l1;
            l1 = l1->next;
        } else {
            tail->next = l2;
            l2 = l2->next;
        }
        tail = tail->next;                 // move the result pointer forward
    }

    // One list may still have nodes – attach the remainder.
    tail->next = l1 ? l1 : l2;

    // The merged list starts right after the dummy.
    return dummy.next;
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 *  Helper: build a linked list from a std::vector<int>
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
ListNode* buildList(const std::vector<int>& vals) {
    ListNode dummy(0);
    ListNode* cur = &dummy;
    for (int v : vals) {
        cur->next = new ListNode(v);
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    return dummy.next;
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 *  Helper: print a linked list (for verification)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
void printList(const ListNode* head) {
    for (const ListNode* cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
        std::cout << cur->val;
        if (cur->next) std::cout << " -> ";
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 *  Driver / demonstration
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main() {
    // Two **ascending** sorted lists (the classic LeetCode example)
    ListNode* list1 = buildList({1, 3, 5, 7});
    ListNode* list2 = buildList({2, 4, 6, 8});

    std::cout << "List A: "; printList(list1);
    std::cout << "List B: "; printList(list2);

    ListNode* merged = mergeTwoListsDesc(list1, list2);
    std::cout << "Merged (descending): ";
    printList(merged);

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // (Optional) Clean up allocated memory – not required for a tiny demo,
    // but good practice in real code.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // while (merged) {
    //     ListNode* tmp = merged;
    //     merged = merged->next;
    //     delete tmp;
    // }

    return 0;
}
```

### How the code works

| Step | What we do | Why it works |
|------|------------|--------------|
| **Dummy head** | `ListNode dummy(0);` | Eliminates special‑case handling for the first node we attach. |
| **Merge loop** | `while (l1 && l2)` – compare `l1->val` and `l2->val` and attach the larger one. | Because we always pick the larger value, the result list ends up in **non‑increasing** (descending) order. |
| **Attach remainder** | `tail->next = l1 ? l1 : l2;` | When one list is exhausted the other already holds the remaining nodes in descending order. |
| **Return** | `return dummy.next;` | The real head is right after the dummy. |

### Complexity analysis

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| **Time** | `O(n + m)` – each node of each list is visited once. |
| **Auxiliary Space** | `O(1)` – only a few pointers are used regardless of input size. |

### Alternative (if you already have an *ascending* merge)

If you already have a routine that merges two lists **in ascending order**, you can simply reverse the result:

```cpp
ListNode* reverseList(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* prev = nullptr;
    while (head) {
        ListNode* nxt = head->next;
        head->next = prev;
        prev = head;
        head = nxt;
    }
    return prev;
}

// Merge ascending, then reverse:
ListNode* mergedDesc = reverseList(mergeTwoListsAsc(l1, l2));
```

Both approaches run in the same O(n+m) time, but the **direct descending merge** shown above avoids an extra O(n+m) reverse pass.

---

**Feel free to copy‑paste the program, compile it, and run it.** It will print:

```
List A: 1 -> 3 -> 5 -> 7
List B: 2 -> 4 -> 6 -> 8
Merged (descending): 8 -> 7 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1
```

That’s a minimal, fully functional example for the “merge two sorted lists in descending order” problem. Happy coding!

