Below is a **minimal, self‑contained Java example** that shows how you might read a plain‑text MT 940 file, split it into the well‑known SWIFT “tags” (e.g. `:20:`, `:25:`, `:60F:`, `:61:`, `:86:`, `:62F:` …) and extract the most common pieces of information – account identifier, opening/closing balance and each individual statement line (date, amount, reference, etc.).

The code is deliberately kept short so it can be dropped into a utility class or test. In a production system you would normally lean on an existing library (see the “Optional libraries” section at the end) and add richer error‑handling, logging, ISO‑4217 currency handling, etc.

---

## 1. Data model (POJOs)

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

/** One statement line (the :61: … :86: block). */
public class Mt940Transaction {
    private final LocalDate valueDate;
    private final LocalDate entryDate;   // may be null
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final String debitCredit;    // "C" or "D"
    private final String reference;
    private final String description;    // content of :86:

    public Mt940Transaction(LocalDate valueDate,
                            LocalDate entryDate,
                            BigDecimal amount,
                            String debitCredit,
                            String reference,
                            String description) {
        this.valueDate = valueDate;
        this.entryDate = entryDate;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.debitCredit = debitCredit;
        this.reference = reference;
        this.description = description;
    }

    // getters – omitted for brevity
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return String.format(
            "Tx[date=%s, amount=%s %s, ref=%s, desc=%s]",
            valueDate, debitCredit, amount, reference, description);
    }
}

/** A whole MT940 statement (account + balances + all transactions). */
public class Mt940Statement {
    private final String accountId;
    private final BigDecimal openingBalance;
    private final String openingCurrency;
    private final BigDecimal closingBalance;
    private final String closingCurrency;
    private final List<Mt940Transaction> transactions;

    public Mt940Statement(String accountId,
                          BigDecimal openingBalance,
                          String openingCurrency,
                          BigDecimal closingBalance,
                          String closingCurrency,
                          List<Mt940Transaction> transactions) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.openingBalance = openingBalance;
        this.openingCurrency = openingCurrency;
        this.closingBalance = closingBalance;
        this.closingCurrency = closingCurrency;
        this.transactions = transactions;
    }

    // getters omitted

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return String.format(
            "Statement[account=%s, open=%s %s, close=%s %s, txCount=%d]",
            accountId, openingBalance, openingCurrency,
            closingBalance, closingCurrency, transactions.size());
    }
}
```

