Below is a **self‑contained example** that shows how a language (or a runtime built on top of a language) can expose a `deterministic_gc_mode` feature and how you would use it to obtain *fully deterministic* stop‑the‑world pauses.  

The implementation is deliberately kept simple so you can see the mechanics, but a production runtime would replace the placeholder `run_gc()` with the real collector logic.

---

## 1.  The API surface (what the programmer sees)

```cpp
// -------------------------------------------------
//  DeterministicGC.h  –  public interface
// -------------------------------------------------
#pragma once
#include <chrono>

namespace gc {

/* Enable deterministic GC.
 *
 *  `interval` – the desired pause interval (e.g. 10 ms).  The GC will be
 *                triggered exactly every `interval` milliseconds.
 *
 *  When the mode is active the runtime guarantees that a full
 *  stop‑the‑world collection occurs at each tick, regardless of
 *  allocation pressure.
 */
void enable_deterministic_mode( std::chrono::milliseconds interval );

/* Disable deterministic GC – return to the normal (non‑deterministic) mode. */
void disable_deterministic_mode();

} // namespace gc
```

A typical program would look like this:

```cpp
#include "DeterministicGC.h"
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

int main() {
    // ── Activate deterministic GC (pause every 10 ms) ──
    gc::enable_deterministic_mode( std::chrono::milliseconds(10) );

    // Do some work – all allocations will be reclaimed on schedule
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        // Simulate useful work
        std::this_thread::sleep_for( std::chrono::milliseconds(5) );
        // allocate temporary objects
        char* buf = new char[1024];
        // … use buf …
        delete[] buf;
    }

    // ── Switch back to the normal collector ──
    gc::disable_deterministic_mode();
}
```

---

## 2.  Internal implementation (the “runtime” part)

```cpp
// -------------------------------------------------
//  DeterministicGC.cpp  –  private implementation
// -------------------------------------------------
#include "DeterministicGC.h"
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>

namespace gc {

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Internal helpers – replace these with the real collector code
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
static void run_gc() {
    //  In a real language runtime this function would:
    //   1. Stop all mutator threads (safepoint).
    //   2. Walk the heap, reclaim unreachable objects,
    //      optionally compact memory, and update internal bookkeeping.
    //   3. Resume all mutators.
    // For the illustration we just simulate a short pause.
    static const int dummy = 42;   // make the call “useful”
    (void)dummy;
    std::this_thread::sleep_for( std::chrono::microseconds(200) );
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The deterministic GC engine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
static std::atomic<bool>   keep_running{ false };
static std::thread         gc_thread;

void enable_deterministic_mode( std::chrono::milliseconds interval ) {
    keep_running.store( true, std::memory_order_relaxed );

    gc_thread = std::thread( [interval]() {
        auto next_tick = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

        while ( keep_running.load( std::memory_order_relaxed ) ) {
            // Wait until the next scheduled tick.
            std::this_thread::sleep_until( next_tick );

            // Capture the start of the pause for logging / telemetry.
            auto pause_start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

            // ---------- The actual GC ----------
            run_gc();

            // ---------- End of pause ----------
            auto pause_end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
            auto pause_us  = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(
                                 pause_end - pause_start ).count();

            // (In production you would also measure latency jitter, etc.)
            std::cerr << "[DeterministicGC] pause = " << pause_us
                      << " µs (target interval = " << interval.count()
                      << " ms)\n";

            // Advance to the next tick.
            next_tick += interval;
        }
    });
}

void disable_deterministic_mode() {
    keep_running.store( false, std::memory_order_relaxed );
    if ( gc_thread.joinable() )
        gc_thread.join();
}

} // namespace gc
```

---

## 3.  How it works – a short walkthrough

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1. Compiler / library support** | The compiler emits a call to `gc::enable_deterministic_mode` when the program contains a `#pragma gc mode deterministic` (or the equivalent API call shown above). | The programmer only has to request the mode; the runtime does the rest. |
| **2. Background timer thread** | The runtime spawns a single, high‑priority thread (`gc_thread`). This thread never performs application work – its sole purpose is to **tick** at the requested interval. | Because the tick interval is fixed at runtime start, the pause moments become *predictable* (e.g., every 10 ms). |
| **3. Global safepoint** | When the tick fires, the GC thread forces **all mutator threads** to a *safepoint* (a well‑defined point where the GC can safely inspect/modify their stacks). In a real language runtime this is usually done via a signal (e.g., `SIGURGENT` in the HotSpot JVM) or a polling mechanism. | All threads are frozen at the same instant → a true *stop‑the‑world* pause, which is a prerequisite for deterministic timing. |
| **4. Full collection** | With all threads paused, the GC walks the entire heap, reclaims dead objects, possibly compacts memory, and updates any bookkeeping. The `run_gc()` placeholder in the sample corresponds to this phase. | Because the collector runs to completion before any mutator continues, the pause length is solely a function of the heap size and the algorithm, not of allocation patterns. |
| **5. Resume mutators** | After the collector finishes, the safepoint is released and every thread continues from where it was interrupted. | The program sees a clean, isolated pause that always ends before the next scheduled tick. |
| **6. Logging / telemetry** | The implementation prints the measured pause time on `stderr`. In production you would expose this via counters, metrics APIs, or a diagnostic file. | Provides visibility that the pauses are indeed deterministic (or that they exceed the target, in which case the interval must be widened). |
| **7. Disable** | Calling `gc::disable_deterministic_mode()` sets the `keep_running` flag to `false`. The timer thread exits gracefully, and the runtime returns to the default collector (which may be concurrent, incremental, or even disabled). | Gives the programmer a clean way to switch back to the normal GC policy when deterministic timing is no longer required. |

### Key guarantees

1. **Fixed cadence** – GC runs *exactly* every `interval` milliseconds (or as close as the OS scheduler permits).  
2. **Single‑phase stop‑the‑world** – The pause is a single contiguous block; no incremental work happens while the program runs.  
3. **No external interference** – Because the timer thread is the *only* trigger, external allocation pressure cannot cause a premature GC.  
4. **Deterministic pause length** – If the heap size and the collector’s algorithm are stable, the pause duration becomes a constant (or at least a known bound).  

### Caveats

* **Pause‑interval vs. pause‑duration** – You must choose an interval *larger* than the worst‑case pause of your collector; otherwise the ticks will back‑up and the mode stops being deterministic.  
* **Real‑time OS support** – To get true nanosecond‑level determinism you typically need a real‑time OS (or at least a patched Linux kernel with `SCHED_FIFO`/`SCHED_RR`). The code above uses standard C++ threads, which are subject to the normal OS scheduler.  
* **Thread‑local resources** – In languages that allocate thread‑local pools, the safepoint mechanism must also collect those pools. The placeholder `run_gc()` would have to handle that.  
* **Finalizers & weak references** – Any async finalization can introduce extra work after the pause. In deterministic mode you often disable finalization or treat it as a separate scheduled task.  

---

## 4.  Putting it into a real language

If you are building a language from scratch, the pattern is:

1. **Add a language pragma** – e.g. `deterministic_gc on;`.  
2. **Emit a call** to `gc::enable_deterministic_mode( … )` during program initialization.  
3. **Implement the safepoint** – compile each function to occasionally poll a flag; when the flag is set, block until the GC thread signals “resume”.  
4. **Wire the collector** – replace the placeholder `run_gc()` with the actual GC (mark‑sweep, copying, generational, etc.).  

The rest of the runtime (allocator, object headers, stack maps) stays the same; only the *scheduling* of collections changes.

---

### TL;DR

```cpp
// One‑line activation (or a pragma in a language that supports it)
gc::enable_deterministic_mode( std::chrono::milliseconds(10) );

// … your program runs with GC pauses exactly every 10 ms …
gc::disable_deterministic_mode();   // when you no longer need it
```

The implementation uses a dedicated timer thread that forces a global safepoint, runs a full collection, and then resumes the program. This makes the pause moments and lengths fully deterministic, at the cost of slightly higher average pause overhead.

