## 4.  Things to watch out for

| Issue | Why it matters | Mitigation |
|-------|----------------|------------|
| **Server support** | Not every TLS 1.3 server permits early‑data for a given PSK. If the server rejects it, the connection will succeed but the early‑data will be ignored (the request will be sent again in the normal post‑handshake flow). | Test with a server you control, or verify that the public host you target actually accepts early‑data for your PSK. |
| **Replay protection** | TLS 1.3 mandates that servers must not replay early‑data across multiple connections. Using a **static PSK** removes the per‑session randomness, but you still rely on the server’s anti‑replay logic. | Use a single‑use ticket on the server side, or accept that early‑data may be replayed only within the same session. |
| **Security of the PSK** | If the PSK leaks, anyone can impersonate the client and send deterministic early‑data. | Keep the PSK secret, rotate it periodically, and store it in a vault or environment variable rather than hard‑coding it. |
| **TLS 1.3 version mismatch** | The code forces TLS 1.3 only (`TLS1_3_VERSION`). Older OpenSSL builds may not define that constant; you can fall back to `TLSv1_2_METHOD` but then you lose 0‑RTT. | Ensure you compile against OpenSSL ≥ 1.1.1. |

