---

## 4.  Why the data is guaranteed to arrive

* **Reliability** – QUIC uses ACK‑based retransmission on a per‑stream basis. If a packet is dropped, the receiver will ask for a retransmission, and the sender will resend the missing bytes. The application never sees gaps.

* **Ordered delivery** – QUIC guarantees that a stream’s bytes are delivered in the same order they were sent. You can read the stream with `quiche_conn_stream_recv` and you will get a contiguous byte stream.

* **Flow control** – Each side advertises how much buffer it is willing to accept. QUIC will throttle the sender if the receiver’s buffer would be overrun, but it will never drop data; it will simply pause the sending side until the receiver acknowledges more buffer space.

* **Loss detection** – The protocol tracks packet numbers and times of transmission. After a timeout (or after enough duplicate ACKs), the lost packet is retransmitted automatically.

All of this is handled inside the library; you only need to call the stream send/receive functions.

---

## 5.  Building and running the example

### 5.1 Install quiche

```bash
# On a Debian/Ubuntu machine:
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y git cargo clang libssl-dev pkg-config

# Clone the repo and build the C library
git clone https://github.com/cloudflare/quiche
cd quiche
cargo build --release --lib
# The static library will be at target/release/libquiche.a
# The header files are under quiche/src/cbind.rs.h (you can copy it to /usr/local/include)
sudo cp target/release/libquiche.a /usr/local/lib/
sudo cp -r quiche.h /usr/local/include/   # if you have the header; otherwise include the generated one
```

> **Tip** – If you prefer a system package, many distros ship `libquiche-dev` (e.g., Arch Linux has it in AUR).  Adjust the compile line accordingly.

### 5.2 Compile

```bash
gcc -Wall -O2 quic_client.c -lquiche -lssl -lcrypto -lpthread -o quic_client
gcc -Wall -O2 quic_server.c -lquiche -lssl -lcrypto -lpthread -o quic_server
```

If the header is in a non‑standard place, add `-I/path/to/include`.

### 5.3 Run

```bash
./quic_server 127.0.0.1 4433   # keep this running
./quic_client 127.0.0.1 4433   # in another terminal
```

You should see the echoed line.  Try running the client while the server is down – you’ll see that `quiche` will eventually time out and report an error, but it **won’t** silently drop data.

---

## 6.  Next steps for a “real‑world” client

| Feature | How to add it (with quiche) |
|---------|-----------------------------|
| **Server certificate verification** | Set `quiche_config_verify_peer(config, true)` and supply a trusted CA bundle via `SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations` (quiche uses OpenSSL under the hood). |
| **Multiple streams** | Call `quiche_conn_stream_new` for each logical conversation; each stream is independent and fully reliable. |
| **Bidirectional communication** | Open a bidirectional stream (the third argument of `stream_new` = `true`). Write data with `stream_send`, read with `stream_recv`. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Call `quiche_conn_close(conn, true, 0, NULL, 0)` to send a CONNECTION_CLOSE; call `quiche_conn_free` afterwards. |
| **0‑RTT** | Enable `quiche_config_set_enable_0rtt(config, true)` – you can send data before the handshake finishes (risk of replay). |
| **Logging/Debugging** | Build quiche with `QUICHE_DEBUG=1`; the library will emit detailed logs to stderr. |
| **Performance tuning** | Increase `quiche_config_set_initial_max_stream_data_*` and `quiche_config_set_initial_max_data` if you expect high throughput; adjust `max_udp_payload_size` to match your MTU. |
| **Portability** | On Windows you can use Winsock2 (`WSASendTo`/`WSARecvFrom`). The same `quiche` API works; just compile with the platform’s SSL library (OpenSSL or BoringSSL). |

---

## 7.  TL;DR – Why QUIC gives you loss‑less streams

* **QUIC is a reliable, ordered, flow‑controlled transport** built on UDP.  
* **Loss detection + retransmission** guarantee that every byte you send will eventually arrive (or the connection will be aborted).  
* **Streams are independent** – you can open many parallel streams; each stream’s reliability is independent of the others.  
* **Implementation** – Using a mature QUIC library (e.g., quiche, ngtcp2, msquic) hides all the complexity; you just call `stream_send` / `stream_recv`.  

The tiny client (`quic_client.c`) and server (`quic_server.c`) above give you a fully working, **loss‑less** data exchange over QUIC.  Replace the hard‑coded payload with whatever your application needs, and you have a reliable, high‑performance communication channel that will never silently lose a byte. Happy coding!

