Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run Java example** that shows how a client application can talk to **Cognivault ESB 7** through its native REST API.  
The code is deliberately generic – it uses only the standard `java.net.http` client (Java 11+) so you can drop it into any Java 11+ project without extra dependencies.

---

## 1. High‑level view of the integration

```
+-------------------+      HTTPS POST (JSON)      +-------------------+
|  Your Application |  ------------------------>  |  Cognivault ESB 7 |
|  (Producer)       |  <------------------------  |  (REST Listener)  |
+-------------------+      HTTP 2xx + body        +-------------------+
        |                                                    |
        |  1. Serialize payload to JSON                      |
        |  2. Set Content‑Type & X‑API‑Key headers           |
        |  3. Fire POST to /messages endpoint                |
        |                                                    |
        |  4. ESB receives the payload, runs configured      |
        |     routing, transformation, enrichment, logging   |
        |  5. ESB optionally forwards to backend services    |
        |  6. ESB returns a JSON response (acknowledgement,  |
        |     correlation ID, result, etc.)                  |
        |                                                    |
        |  7. Client reads response, logs, proceeds          |
        +----------------------------------------------------+
```

*The ESB can also be used in an **asynchronous** (fire‑and‑forget) or **JMS‑based** manner, but the snippet below focuses on the most common synchronous REST pattern.*

---

## 2. Maven / Gradle dependency (optional)

If you want to use a richer JSON library (Jackson is shown in the example), add:

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
    <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
    <version>2.15.2</version>
</dependency>
```

Or with Gradle:

```groovy
implementation 'com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind:2.15.2'
```

*The code below works with the JDK‑built-in JSON support, but Jackson makes it trivial to serialize any POJO.*

---

## 3. The client implementation

### 3.1 Exception class (simple wrapper)

```java
package com.example.esb;

/**
 * Simple checked exception that carries a clear message and an optional cause.
 */
public class CognivaultIntegrationException extends Exception {
    public CognivaultIntegrationException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }

    public CognivaultIntegrationException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}
```

### 3.2 Core client

```java
package com.example.esb;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Thin client that sends JSON payloads to a Cognivault ESB 7 REST endpoint.
 *
 * <p>Typical usage:</p>
 * <pre>
 * CognivaultEsbClient client = new CognivaultEsbClient(
 *         "https://cognivault-host.internal:8443/api/v1",
 *         "my-api-key"
 * );
 *
 * Map&lt;String,Object&gt; order = Map.of(
 *         "orderId", "ORD-12345",
 *         "customerId", "CUST-001",
 *         "items", List.of(
 *                 Map.of("sku", "SKU‑001", "qty", 2),
 *                 Map.of("sku", "SKU‑002", "qty", 1)
 *         )
 * );
 *
 * String esbResponse = client.sendMessage(order);
