## 📄 `constant_time_demo.cpp`

```cpp
// constant_time_demo.cpp
//
// Compile with:
//
//   g++ -fconstant-time -O2 -o constant_time_demo constant_time_demo.cpp
//
// The -fconstant-time flag tells GCC to treat any code that looks
// constant‑time as if it were really constant‑time (no secret‑dependent
// branches or memory‑access patterns will be introduced by the optimizer).
//
// For Clang the same flag works (Clang 12+), otherwise you can use the
// attribute version shown below.

#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Helper: constant‑time comparison of two byte arrays.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// The function never uses an early‑exit or a branch that depends on the
// secret data.  It walks the whole array, XORs each byte, and OR‑s the
// results into a single accumulator.  The accumulator is 0 iff all
// bytes are equal.
//
// We also mark it with the `optimize` attribute so the flag is honoured
// even if the whole translation unit is not compiled with -fconstant‑time.
[[gnu::optimize("fconstant-time")]]
bool constant_time_compare(const uint8_t *a, const uint8_t *b, size_t len)
{
    uint8_t diff = 0;               // will stay 0 only if a == b
    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i)
        diff |= a[i] ^ b[i];        // bitwise OR – no early exit possible

    // Return true iff diff == 0.  This comparison is a single, simple
    // operation that the optimizer cannot turn into a secret‑dependent branch.
    return diff == 0;
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Example usage: verify a fixed “secret” key against user input.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE: In a real security‑critical program you would also avoid branching
// on the result of the comparison (otherwise the attacker could still learn
// the result from the taken path).  A common pattern is to keep the result
// in a mask and use it for constant‑time selection – see below.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    // Simulated secret key (e.g. a password hash or an API token)
    const uint8_t secret[16] = {
        0x2b, 0x7e, 0x15, 0x16, 0x28, 0xae, 0xd2, 0xa6,
        0xab, 0xf7, 0x15, 0x88, 0x09, 0xcf, 0x4f, 0x3c
    };

    // In a real program you would read this from the user or network.
    uint8_t user_input[16];
    std::memset(user_input, 0, sizeof(user_input));   // zero‑init for demo

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Constant‑time comparison – the only place where secret data is used.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    bool equal = constant_time_compare(secret, user_input, sizeof(secret));

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Avoid a secret‑dependent branch.  We turn the boolean into a mask
    //     and select the “grant” or “deny” values with bitwise ops.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    const uint8_t grant[2]  = {0x00, 0x01};   // index 0 = deny, 1 = grant
    const uint8_t deny[2]   = {0x01, 0x00};

    // mask = 0xFF if equal, else 0x00
    uint8_t mask = equal ? 0xFF : 0x00;

    // Choose result = (grant & mask) | (deny & ~mask) – no branch!
    uint8_t result = (grant[1] & mask) | (deny[1] & ~mask);

    // In a real application you would use `result` without any further
    // secret‑dependent control flow.
    return result;   // 0 = deny, 1 = grant (for demo)
}
```

---

## 📦 Why this is constant‑time

| What we wrote                              | Why it stays timing‑invariant                              |
|--------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| **No early‑exit loops** (`for` always runs `len` iterations) | The loop count is data‑independent, so no information leaks via the number of iterations. |
| **Bitwise OR accumulation** (`diff |= a[i] ^ b[i]`) | All bytes are processed, no conditional jumps based on secret data. |
| **Final single‑compare** (`diff == 0`)    | A simple, non‑branching equality test. |
| **Mask‑based selection** (`mask`, `&`, `|`) | The program never takes a different path based on the secret; both possible outcomes are computed and selected with `mask`. |
| **`[[gnu::optimize("fconstant-time")]]`** | Tells GCC (and Clang) to keep the function constant‑time even if you forget the command‑line flag for a particular translation unit. |

Because the **only** place secret data influences execution is the XOR‑OR loop and the mask, the overall execution time (including the loop and the mask selection) is **independent of the secret’s value**.  An attacker who measures the wall‑clock time of `constant_time_compare` or `main` cannot infer any bit of the secret.

---

## 🛠️ Build instructions

```bash
# GCC (or Clang 12+) with the flag:
g++ -fconstant-time -O2 -o constant_time_demo constant_time_demo.cpp

# If you prefer to rely solely on the attribute and omit the flag:
g++ -O2 -o constant_time_demo constant_time_demo.cpp

# To verify the binary is indeed constant‑time you can run it under a
# timing‑analysis tool (e.g. `perf`, `time`, or a custom micro‑benchmark)
# with various inputs – you should see no measurable difference.
```

> **Tip:** `-fconstant-time` only guarantees constant‑time behavior **if the source code itself is written in a constant‑time style**.  The flag prevents the optimizer from inserting secret‑dependent jumps, but it cannot fix code that already branches on secret data.

---

### 🎯 Take‑away for your docs

* Use `-fconstant-time` **and** write the function in a branch‑free manner.
* Prefer bit‑wise operations, full‑length loops, and mask selection over `if/else` that depends on secret data.
* Mark critical functions with `[[gnu::optimize("fconstant-time")]]` to make the intent explicit and survive translation‑unit‑wide flag changes.

Feel free to copy‑paste the snippet, adjust the secret/key size, and integrate it into your internal documentation or training material. Happy (secure) coding!

