| **Core call** | `zerocopyutil.Copy(dst, src)` is the heart of the program. It tries to move data from `src` to `dst` using a zero‑copy mechanism (e.g. Linux `splice(2)`) when the OS supports it, falling back to a classic buffered copy otherwise. The function signature mirrors `io.Copy` so it’s drop‑in compatible. |
| **Error handling** | Any I/O or syscall errors are propagated back to `main`, which prints a friendly message and exits with a non‑zero status. |
| **Usage** | • No arguments → reads from **stdin**, writes to **stdout**.<br>• Two arguments → treats them as source and destination file paths and copies the whole file. |

