Below is a **minimal, self‑contained example** that shows how to build a lock‑free, multiple‑producer / multiple‑consumer (MPMC) FIFO with **Boost.Lockfree’s `perfect_queue`**.  
The code is ready to be dropped into a C++ file (e.g. `main.cpp`) and compiled with a recent Boost (≥ 1.71) and a C++‑14/17 compiler.

```cpp
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//  perfect_queue_demo.cpp
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//  A tiny producer‑consumer demo that uses
//  boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int>.  The queue is created
//  with a fixed capacity of 128 elements, but you can also pass
//  the capacity at run‑time (see the comment in main()).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

#include <boost/lockfree/perfect_queue.hpp>   // the header you were asked to include

#include <atomic>
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  Helper: simple spin‑yield (used when the queue is temporarily
//  full / empty).  In a real‑world program you would typically
//  use a condition variable or a lock‑free “pop‑until‑empty” loop.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
inline void yield() { std::this_thread::yield(); }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  Producer: pushes 1 000 integers onto the queue.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
void producer(boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int>& q,
              std::atomic<bool>& done)            // signals that we are finished
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        // push() returns false only when the queue is full.
        // If it fails we just yield and retry – the loop is tiny.
        while (!q.push(i)) {
            yield();               // <-- wait a bit before retrying
        }
    }
    // After all values are written we signal “done”.
    done.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  Consumer: pulls values until the producer is done *and* the
//  queue is empty.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
void consumer(boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int>& q,
              std::atomic<bool>& done)
{
    int value = 0;
    while (true) {
        // pop() returns true when it successfully retrieved an item.
        if (q.pop(value)) {
            std::cout << "Got: " << value << '\n';
        } else if (done.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            // No more items and the producer is finished → we can quit.
            break;
        } else {
            // Nothing to read right now, give the CPU a chance to run the producer.
            yield();
        }
    }

    // Drain any leftovers that might have arrived after we broke the loop.
    while (q.pop(value)) {
        std::cout << "Got (leftover): " << value << '\n';
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  main()
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    //  Create the lock‑free queue.
    //  -----------------------------------------------------------------
    //  You have two options:
    //
    //  1) Compile‑time capacity (power‑of‑two, recommended for best perf):
    //       boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int, 128> q;   // capacity = 128
    //
    //  2) Run‑time capacity (the queue will allocate enough slots at
    //       construction time):
    //       boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int> q(128);
    //
    //  Uncomment the version you prefer.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // 2) Run‑time capacity (the version we use for the demo):
    constexpr std::size_t initial_capacity = 128;
    boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int> q(initial_capacity);

    // 1) Compile‑time capacity (uncomment if you prefer):
    // boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int, 128> q;   // capacity is 128

    std::atomic<bool> done{false};

    std::thread prod(producer, std::ref(q), std::ref(done));
    std::thread cons(consumer, std::ref(q), std::ref(done));

    prod.join();
    cons.join();

    std::cout << "All work finished – queue is empty: " << std::boolalpha << q.empty() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

### How it works (high‑level)

| Aspect | What the code does | Why it matters |
|--------|--------------------|----------------|
| **Queue type** | `boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int, …>` (MPMC). | Provides a bounded, *lock‑free* FIFO that can be accessed by many threads simultaneously without any mutex or other blocking primitive. |
| **Capacity** | Declared either as a compile‑time template argument (`128`) or passed to the constructor (`initial_capacity`). | The internal storage is a power‑of‑two sized ring buffer. Knowing the size at compile time lets the implementation use cheap bit‑masking for the modulo operation. |
| **Push** | `q.push(value)` returns `true` if the element was stored, `false` if the queue is full. | The push operation uses an atomic fetch‑add on a *tail* index combined with a version (tag) field to avoid the ABA problem. |
| **Pop** | `q.pop(value)` returns `true` if an element was retrieved, `false` if the queue is empty. | The pop side works symmetrically on a *head* index. Both sides are lock‑free: if one thread is delayed, others can still make progress. |
| **Memory ordering** | All atomic operations use at least `memory_order_relaxed` for the internal bookkeeping and `memory_order_acq_rel` for the actual data writes/reads. | This gives the correct visibility guarantees while keeping the fast path as cheap as possible. |
| **ABA avoidance** | Each slot in the ring buffer carries a *tag* that is incremented each time the slot is reused. The index + tag pair (packed into a 64‑bit word) is the true “pointer” used in the CAS loop. | Guarantees that a thread cannot mistakenly think a slot is free after it has been reused by another thread. |
| **No locks** | The only synchronization primitives are the atomic CAS/fetch‑add on the head/tail tags. | The queue stays *lock‑free* (and on a single‑producer/single‑consumer path even *wait‑free*). |
| **Back‑off** | When the queue appears full/empty the demo code yields the CPU (`std::this_thread::yield()`). In production you may replace this with a more sophisticated back‑off or a spin‑wait. | Prevents the producer/consumer from burning CPU cycles while waiting for the opposite side to make progress. |

#### Inside the perfect queue (simplified)

1. **Ring buffer** – an array of `Capacity` slots (`N` must be a power of two).  
2. **Head / Tail indices** – each is a 64‑bit value: `index` (the slot number) packed together with a `tag` (a version counter).  
3. **Push** –  
   * Atomically fetch‑add the tail (by 1) and mask the lower bits to obtain the slot index.  
   * Spin on a CAS of the (slot, tag) pair until the slot is marked “empty”.  
   * Write the element, then bump the slot’s tag to mark it “full”.  
4. **Pop** – symmetric: fetch‑add the head, locate the slot, spin until “full”, read the element, and mark the slot “empty”.  

Because the tag is increased on each wrap‑around of the index, the same physical slot will never look “empty” again until the tag has changed, thus completely eliminating the classic ABA problem.

### Quick checklist for integration

| ✔️ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✅ | Include `<boost/lockfree/perfect_queue.hpp>` (already in the demo). |
| ✅ | Choose a *power‑of‑two* capacity (e.g. 128, 256, 1024…) for best performance. |
| ✅ | Use `push()` / `pop()` – they are thread‑safe and do **not** throw. |
| ✅ | If you need to know when the queue is empty, call `empty()` (no locking required). |
| ✅ | Compile with `-std=c++14` (or newer) and link against Boost (`-lboost_system` is not required for lockfree). |
| ✅ | Remember that the queue is *bounded*: a producer that continually outruns the consumer will see `push()` returning `false`. |

Feel free to copy‑paste the demo into your project, adjust the capacity, or replace the simple `yield()` loop with a more elaborate back‑off strategy (e.g. exponential back‑off or a lock‑free semaphore) if you need even higher throughput. Happy coding!

