## How it works (in a nutshell)

| Step | What the macro does | Why it matters |
|------|---------------------|----------------|
| **Macro invocation** | `TBB_AUTO_PARALLEL_FOR(init, test, inc, body)` translates the plain C‑style loop into a call to `tbb::parallel_for`. | You keep the loop looking almost the same while the library takes care of the heavy lifting. |
| **Range creation** | The macro builds a `tbb::blocked_range<std::size_t>(0, N)` that represents the whole iteration interval. | `blocked_range` is the abstraction TBB uses to partition the work into chunks that can be executed concurrently. |
| **Automatic partitioning** | The call uses `tbb::auto_partitioner()` (the default for `parallel_for`). | The auto‑partitioner dynamically decides how large each sub‑range should be, balancing the load among the available threads. It starts with a coarse grain and refines it if work‑stealing shows that some threads are idle. |
| **Execution** | The TBB runtime spawns a pool of worker threads (by default equal to the hardware concurrency) and distributes the sub‑ranges among them. | Each thread runs the body on its own portion of the range, and the results are automatically merged because the loop body operates on independent array elements. |
| **Scalability** | Because the grain size is chosen at runtime, the same code scales from a laptop (few cores) to a many‑core server without any source‑code changes. | You get “automatic” parallelism – no manual thread creation, no explicit synchronization, and usually good load‑balance out of the box. |

### What you get
- **Simplicity** – just add the macro call where a normal `for` would be.
- **Portability** – works on any platform that TBB supports (Windows, Linux, macOS, etc.).
- **Performance** – the library handles thread creation, work‑stealing, cache‑friendly partitioning, and optional affinity hints for you.

> **Note** – `<tbb/auto_parallel.h>` (and the `TBB_AUTO_PARALLEL_FOR` macro) is a convenience wrapper that dates back to earlier TBB releases. Modern TBB code tends to call `tbb::parallel_for` directly, often with a lambda and the default `auto_partitioner`. Both approaches are functionally equivalent; the macro just hides the boiler‑plate for very simple loops.

