  // …
  simd_sort(data.data(), data.size());   // works for raw arrays
  ```

  The repository’s README explains the exact signature and shows how to enable AVX‑512/AVX2 builds.

* **Other forks** (e.g., `llvm-ml/llvm-project` “simd” branch) follow the same naming conventions.

---

## 5. Things to keep in mind

| Point | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **Experimental API** | The function signature, behaviour, and even the namespace (`std::experimental::` vs. `std::simd::`) may change in future releases. Pin the version you test against. |
| **Alignment & SIMD width** | The algorithm internally works on blocks of e.g. 256 bits (AVX) or 512 bits (AVX‑512). It works with any container whose iterators satisfy `std::random_access_iterator_tag`. |
| **Comparators** | The comparator must be a **strict weak ordering**. The SIMD implementation can handle `std::less<>` and `std::greater<>` efficiently; custom comparators may fall back to a scalar path if they can’t be vectorised. |
| **Stability** | The routine is **not** required to be stable (it may permute equal elements). |
| **Thread‑safety** | Each call works on a **single range**. If you want to sort multiple independent ranges in parallel, launch separate threads yourself – the routine itself does not spawn threads. |
| **Performance tips** | * Use `-O3` (or `-Ofast`). <br>* Enable the widest SIMD ISA your CPU supports (`-march=native` is the easiest). <br>* Avoid heavy branch mispredictions inside the comparator (e.g., use plain `float` comparisons). |

---

## 6. Full, ready‑to‑run “one‑liner” version (no timing, just sort)

If you just want the shortest possible demonstration:

```cpp
