Below is a quick overview of what “Auto‑LRU” actually is (it isn’t part of the JDK), followed by a **fully‑working, minimal LRU cache** that you can drop into any Java program.

---

## 1. There is no `java.util.cache.AutoLRU`

The standard JDK does **not** ship a class called `java.util.cache.AutoLRU`.  
If you’ve seen that name somewhere, it’s probably from a third‑party library or a mis‑named tutorial.  

The idiomatic way to build a tiny LRU cache in the JDK is to extend **`java.util.LinkedHashMap`** and override the `removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry)` method.  

* `LinkedHashMap` already maintains the **access‑order** (most‑recently‑used first) when you call the constructor with `true` as the third argument.
* When `removeEldestEntry` returns `true`, the map automatically discards the *eldest* (i.e., least‑recently‑used) entry.

That’s essentially what a simple “Auto‑LRU” does – the JDK just makes you implement the policy yourself.

---

## 2. Minimal LRU cache using `LinkedHashMap`

```java
import java.util.*;

/**
 * A tiny, thread‑unsafe LRU cache backed by a LinkedHashMap.
 *
 * @param <K> key type
 * @param <V> value type
 */
public class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {

    private final int capacity;

    /**
     * Creates a cache that holds at most {@code capacity} entries.
     *
     * @param capacity max number of entries before eviction kicks in
     */
    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        //   initialCapacity, loadFactor, accessOrder = true
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    /**
     * Returns true when the map has grown beyond the allowed capacity.
     * In that case the eldest entry (the least‑recently‑used one) will be removed.
     */
    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity;
    }

    // ---- demo -------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LRUCache<String, Integer> cache = new LRUCache<>(3);

        cache.put("a", 1);
        cache.put("b", 2);
        cache.put("c", 3);
        System.out.println("Initial cache: " + cache);
        // => {a=1, b=2, c=3}

        // Access 'a' so it becomes the most‑recently‑used.
        cache.get("a");                     // a is now the MRU entry

        // Adding a new entry forces eviction of the LRU entry (which is now 'b')
        cache.put("d", 4);
        System.out.println("After adding d: " + cache);
        // => {a=1, c=3, d=4}  (b evicted)

        // Overwriting an existing key does NOT trigger eviction.
        cache.put("a", 10);
        System.out.println("After updating a: " + cache);
        // => {a=10, c=3, d=4}  (still 3 entries)
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| `super(capacity, 0.75f, true);` | Creates a map that keeps entries **in the order they were accessed** (access‑order). | Allows the *eldest* entry to be the one that was accessed the longest time ago. |
| `removeEldestEntry` returns `size() > capacity` | Every time you `put` a new element (or update an existing one) the map checks whether it has grown beyond the limit. | If it has, the *eldest* entry is automatically removed. |
| `cache.get(k)` | Moves `k` to the most‑recently‑used position. | Guarantees the LRU ordering. |
| `cache.put(k, v)` | If the key already exists, the entry is simply refreshed (moved to the head). If it’s a brand‑new key, the map may evict the LRU entry. | Mirrors the typical LRU‑cache semantics. |

---

## 3. Making the cache **thread‑safe** (optional)

If you need a cache that can be safely shared between threads, wrap the `LRUCache` with `Collections.synchronizedMap`:

```java
Map<String, Integer> safeCache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new LRUCache<>(100));
```

`Collections.synchronizedMap` guarantees atomicity of the map’s operations, but note that **iteration** over a synchronized map is still *not* thread‑safe by default; you’d need external synchronization for that.

### A more powerful alternative: **Guava `CacheBuilder`**

If you’re open to a popular third‑party library (Google Guava), you get a lot more features (statistics, expiration, weak keys, etc.) with a very concise API:

```java
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;

public class GuavaLRUCache {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 3‑entry LRU cache (evicts the least‑recently‑used when the cap is reached)
        Cache<String, Integer> cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(3)
                .recordStats()               // optional, for hit/miss statistics
                .build();

        cache.put("a", 1);
        cache.put("b", 2);
        cache.put("c", 3);

        cache.getIfPresent("a");   // a becomes MRU
        cache.put("d", 4);         // evicts b (the LRU entry)

        System.out.println(cache.asMap()); // {a=1, c=3, d=4}
    }
}
```

The Guava cache also supports:

* **Time‑based expiration** (`expireAfterWrite`, `expireAfterAccess`)
* **Weight‑based eviction** (`maximumWeight`)
* **Automatic loading** (`CacheLoader`)
* **Statistics** (`cache.stats()`)

It’s a solid choice for production‑grade LRU caches.

---

## 4. Quick‑reference checklist

| ✅ | Item | Notes |
|---|------|-------|
| ✅ | `LinkedHashMap(capacity, 0.75f, true)` | Access‑order is crucial for LRU. |
| ✅ | Override `removeEldestEntry` | Returns `true` when you want eviction. |
| ✅ | `cache.get(key)` to touch an entry | Makes it the most‑recently‑used. |
| ✅ | `cache.put(key, value)` for updates | If the key exists, the entry is refreshed (MRU). |
| ✅ | `Collections.synchronizedMap` for basic thread‑safety | Sufficient for many use‑cases. |
| ✅ | Guava `CacheBuilder` if you need extras | Expiration, stats, loading, etc. |

---

## 5. TL;DR – One‑liner you can copy‑paste

```java
Map<String,Integer> lru = Collections.synchronizedMap(new LRUCache<>(3));
```

Just copy the `LRUCache` class shown in Section 2 into your project and you have a ready‑to‑use LRU cache.

---

### TL;DR

* **No `java.util.cache.AutoLRU`** – you build the LRU behavior yourself with `LinkedHashMap`.
* **Core idea:** keep a map in *access‑order* and evict the eldest entry when the size exceeds the limit.
* **Demo code** (Section 2) shows a tiny, clean implementation.
* **Thread‑safety** is optional: wrap with `Collections.synchronizedMap` or use Guava `CacheBuilder`.

Happy coding! If you have any follow‑up questions (e.g., how to add expiration, how to implement a custom eviction listener, or how to unit‑test the cache), just let me know.

