Below is the **shortest, self‑contained example** that shows how to turn an `Instant` (a point on the UTC timeline) into a *zoned* representation for any time‑zone you like.  

The example uses only the standard Java 8+ `java.time` API – there is no built‑in class called `java.time.SmartZone`.  
If you really have a third‑party `SmartZone` class you can simply replace the `ZoneId` line with a call to that class (the pattern is the same).

```java
import java.time.Instant;
import java.time.ZoneId;
import java.time.ZonedDateTime;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

/**
 * Demonstrates converting an {@code Instant} to a {@code ZonedDateTime}
 * for a given time‑zone.
 */
public class InstantToZonedExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1️⃣  The instant you want to convert (right now, in UTC)
        Instant utcInstant = Instant.now();

        // 2️⃣  The time‑zone you care about.
        //     Any IANA zone identifier works, e.g. "America/New_York",
        //     "Europe/Paris", "UTC", etc.
        ZoneId zone = ZoneId.of("America/New_York");

        // 3️⃣  Convert the instant to a ZonedDateTime.
        //     The simplest way: Instant.atZone(ZoneId)
        ZonedDateTime zonedDateTime = utcInstant.atZone(zone);

        // 4️⃣  (Optional) Print it nicely.
        DateTimeFormatter fmt = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss z (VV)");
        System.out.println("UTC instant : " + utcInstant);
        System.out.println("Local time  : " + zonedDateTime.format(fmt));
    }
}
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1** | `Instant.now()` → a point on the global timeline (`UTC`). | An `Instant` has no timezone information; it is “universal”. |
