Below is a **stand‑alone, ready‑to‑paste** example that uses the incubator HTTP client API (`jdk.incubator.httpclient`) to fire a simple HTTP/2 request.  
It works with any JDK that ships the **incubator module** (JDK 9‑11 with the separate incubator JAR, JDK 12+ where the module is built‑in).

---

## 1️⃣  Minimum Maven / Gradle snippet (optional)

If you use Maven, add the *incubator* dependency only for the compile scope – you don’t need it at runtime because the class lives in the JDK itself.

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- only needed for compilation; the runtime already contains it -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.httpcore</groupId>
        <artifactId>httpcore</artifactId>
        <version>5.0</version>          <!-- not needed for the example, just shown for completeness -->
        <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

*If you’re not using Maven just add the JDK’s `incubator` module to your `javac` / `java` command (see the “Compile & Run” section below).*

---

## 2️⃣  The complete Java source

Save the file as **`Http2Example.java`** (or any name you like).

```java
// -------------------------------------------------
//  Import the incubator HTTP client classes
// -------------------------------------------------
import jdk.incubator.http.HttpClient;
import jdk.incubator.http.HttpRequest;
import jdk.incubator.http.HttpResponse;
import jdk.incubator.http.HttpClient.Version;

import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URL;
import java.time.Duration;

/**
 *  Demo: send a synchronous HTTP/2 GET request using the incubator Http3Client.
 *
 *  The Http3Client class is just a thin wrapper that builds an HttpClient with
 *  HTTP/2 as the preferred protocol (HTTP/3 can be negotiated later, but the
 *  request will be HTTP/2 unless the server supports HTTP/3).
 */
public class Http2Example {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // -------------------------------------------------
        //  1️⃣  Build the HttpClient
        // -------------------------------------------------
        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                // Force HTTP/2 (if the server also supports HTTP/1.1 it will be used as fallback)
                .version(Version.HTTP_2)
                // Optional: set a timeout (useful when the server is slow)
                .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
                // Optional: follow redirects (default is NEVER)
                .followRedirects(HttpClient.Redirect.NORMAL)
                .build();

        // -------------------------------------------------
        //  2️⃣  Build the request
        // -------------------------------------------------
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create("https://httpbin.org/get"))   // <-- replace with your target URL
                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                .header("Accept", "application/json")
                // .GET() is the default, but we are explicit
                .GET()
                .build();

        // -------------------------------------------------
        //  3️⃣  Send the request synchronously (blocking)
        // -------------------------------------------------
        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandler.asString());

        // -------------------------------------------------
        //  4️⃣  Process the response
        // -------------------------------------------------
        System.out.println("Status Code : " + response.statusCode());
        System.out.println("HTTP Version: " + response.version().orElse(null));
        System.out.println("Response Body:\n" + response.body());
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | What you see in the code | Why it matters |
|------|--------------------------|----------------|
| **Create HttpClient** | `HttpClient.newBuilder().version(Version.HTTP_2).build()` | Forces HTTP/2 (or falls back to HTTP/1.1 if the server does not support HTTP/2). |
| **Build request** | `HttpRequest.newBuilder().uri(...).GET()` | Constructs a simple GET request with a custom header (`Accept: application/json`). |
| **Send** | `client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandler.asString())` | Performs a **synchronous** call, blocking until the response arrives. |
| **Read response** | `response.statusCode()`, `.body()`, `.version()` | Demonstrates how to extract the status, the actual HTTP version negotiated, and the payload. |

---

## 3️⃣  Compile & Run

### Using the JDK’s built‑in module (JDK 12+)

```bash
# Compile (add the incubator module)
javac --add-modules jdk.incubator.httpclient Http2Example.java

# Run (again add the module)
java --add-modules jdk.incubator.httpclient Http2Example
```

> **Tip:** If you are on JDK 9‑11 you must obtain the incubator JAR from the [OpenJDK “incubator” repository](https://jdk.java.net/jeps/320). Then compile/run with:
> ```bash
> javac --add-exports jdk.incubator.httpclient/jdk.incubator.http=ALL-UNNAMED \
>        --add-modules jdk.incubator.httpclient Http2Example.java
> java   --add-exports jdk.incubator.httpclient/jdk.incubator.http=ALL-UNNAMED \
>        --add-modules jdk.incubator.httpclient Http2Example
> ```

### Using Maven (optional)

```bash
mvn compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="Http2Example"
```

---

## 4️⃣  Quick “copy‑paste‑run” one‑liner (for the truly rushed)

If you just want a single command that downloads, compiles, and runs the example, you can use **jshell** (available in any JDK):

```bash
jshell --class-path <path-to-incubator.jar> <<'EOF'
import jdk.incubator.http.*;
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder().version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2).build();
var req = HttpRequest.newBuilder().uri(URI.create("https://httpbin.org/get")).GET().build();
var resp = client.send(req, HttpResponse.BodyHandler.asString());
System.out.println("Status: " + resp.statusCode());
System.out.println("Body:\n" + resp.body());
EOF
```

Replace `<path-to-incubator.jar>` with the actual path (or omit it on JDK 12+ where the module is built‑in).

---

## 5️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Problem | Why it happens | Fix |
|--------|----------------|-----|
| **“package jdk.incubator.http does not exist”** | The incubator module isn’t on the module path. | Add `--add-modules jdk.incubator.httpclient` (or the proper `--add-exports` for older JDKs). |
| **“Unsupported protocol: HTTP/2”** | The underlying SSL transport (ALPN) isn’t negotiated. | Make sure you run on JDK 9+ and that the server supports HTTP/2 over TLS (most modern servers do). |
| **Timeout on the first request** | DNS or SSL handshake is slow. | Set a reasonable `connectTimeout` (as shown) and a `timeout` on the request. |
| **Response body is empty** | Some endpoints return an empty payload for GET; try a POST or a different URL. | Use a known test endpoint such as `https://httpbin.org/get`. |

---

## 6️⃣  Need async?  Here’s a 2‑line variant

```java
client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandler.asString())
      .thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
      .thenAccept(System.out::println)
      .join();   // blocks until the future completes
```

---

### 🎉  That’s it!

Copy the `Http2Example` class, tweak the URL / headers as needed, compile/run with the module flag, and you should see a successful HTTP/2 response in a matter of seconds. Good luck with your deadline! If you run into anything else, just let me know.

