**Short answer:**  
`std::format_to_simd` does **not** exist in the C++23 (or later) standard.  
It is a *proposed* extension (see P0645 – “SIMD‑accelerated formatting”) and is only available in experimental / preview implementations of the `<format>` header.  

If you just want a fast way to turn a bunch of numbers into a string today, you have a couple of practical options:

---

## 1.  Using the **experimental** `std::format_to_simd` (when your library implements it)

```cpp
#include <format>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <span>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> values = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };

    std::string out;
    // The function signature is roughly:
    //   OutIt format_to_simd(OutIt out, std::format_string<...> fmt, const std::span<T>& data);
    std::format_to_simd(std::back_inserter(out), "{}", std::span(values));

    // out now holds "10 20 30 40 50" (or whatever separators you ask for)
}
```

*What you need:* a recent snapshot of a library that ships the proposal (e.g., a development build of libc++ or a preview of the MSVC STL). The API may still change, so keep an eye on the proposal and your vendor’s documentation.

---

## 2.  Using the **fmt** library’s `fmt::format_to_simd` (works today, portable)

```cpp
#include <fmt/format.h>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> values = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };

    std::string out;
    // fmt::format_to_simd writes the formatted numbers directly into `out`.
    fmt::format_to_simd(std::back_inserter(out), "{}", fmt::span(values));

    // out == "10 20 30 40 50"
}
```

`fmt` is header‑only, released under the MIT license, and its `format_to_simd` is the reference implementation of the proposal. It gives you SIMD‑accelerated formatting *right now* on any compiler that supports C++17 or later.

---

## 3.  “Manual” SIMD‑ish approach with `<charconv>` (standard, but not full‑SIMD)

If you prefer to stay in the standard library and only need integer → text conversion, `std::to_chars` is highly optimized (often using SIMD internally). A simple loop looks like:

```cpp
#include <charconv>
#include <array>
#include <vector>
#include <string_view>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> vals = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    std::array<char, 256> buf;               // fixed‑size scratch
    std::string out;

    for (int v : vals) {
        auto [ptr, ec] = std::to_chars(buf.begin(), buf.end(), v);
        out.append(buf.data(), ptr - buf.begin());
        out.push_back(' ');
    }

    // out == "10 20 30 40 50 "
}
```

This is already very fast, but it does not give you the convenience of a format string (e.g., “{:05d}”). For that you still need `fmt` or the experimental `<format>`.

---

### TL;DR

* No `std::format_to_simd` in the standard yet.  
* If you want SIMD‑accelerated formatting *today*: use `fmt::format_to_simd`.  
* If you only need numeric conversion and want to stay with the standard library, `std::to_chars` is the fastest route.  

Pick the option that fits your toolchain and constraints. Happy formatting!

