### Short answer
Node .js (including the upcoming v26) **does not ship with a built‑in ORM**.  
The module `node:orm` does not exist in the standard library.  
If you need an ORM you’ll have to pick an external package such as **Sequelize**, **TypeORM**, **Prisma**, **Knex**, etc.

Below is a minimal, working example using **Sequelize** – one of the most popular SQL‑based ORMs for Node.  It shows how to:

1. connect to a SQLite (or any other SQL) database,
2. define a model that maps to a table,
3. create, read, update, and delete rows (the classic CRUD operations).

---

## 1️⃣ Install the needed packages

```bash
npm install sequelize sqlite3   # for SQLite; replace sqlite3 with mysql2 or pg for other DBs
```

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal example (`index.js`)

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import Sequelize (the ORM) and the driver for your DB
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const { Sequelize, DataTypes } = require('sequelize');

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Open a connection
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Using SQLite – a file‑based DB.  For MySQL/Postgres just change the
// dialect and the connection options accordingly.
const sequelize = new Sequelize({
  dialect: 'sqlite',
  storage: './my_app.sqlite',   // path to the DB file (created automatically)
  logging: false,               // set to console.log to see the generated SQL
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Define a model – this becomes the “users” table
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
const User = sequelize.define('User', {
  // Each field becomes a column in the table
  id: {
    type: DataTypes.INTEGER,
    autoIncrement: true,
    primaryKey: true,
  },
  name: {
    type: DataTypes.STRING,
    allowNull: false,
  },
  email: {
    type: DataTypes.STRING,
    unique: true,
    validate: { isEmail: true },
  },
  age: {
    type: DataTypes.INTEGER,
    defaultValue: null,
  },
}, {
  // Optional model options
  tableName: 'users',
  timestamps: true,            // adds createdAt/updatedAt columns
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Sync the model with the DB (creates the table if it doesn't exist)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
async function init() {
  try {
    await sequelize.authenticate(); // verify connection
    await sequelize.sync();         // creates/updates tables
    console.log('✅  Database & tables are ready');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('❌  Unable to connect to the database:', err);
    process.exit(1);
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  CRUD helpers
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
async function createUser(data) {
  // Returns a User instance (the row that was just inserted)
  return await User.create(data);
}

async function getUserById(id) {
  return await User.findByPk(id);
}

async function updateUser(user, newData) {
  // user is an instance returned by findByPk / create …
  return await user.update(newData);
}

async function deleteUser(user) {
  return await user.destroy();
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣  Demo (you can run this file directly with Node)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
(async () => {
  await init();

  // --- Create ---------------------------------------------------
  const jane = await createUser({ name: 'Jane Doe', email: 'jane@example.com', age: 29 });
  console.log('✅  Created:', jane.toJSON());

  // --- Read ----------------------------------------------------
  const found = await getUserById(jane.id);
  console.log('🔍  Found:', found.toJSON());

  // --- Update --------------------------------------------------
  const updated = await updateUser(found, { age: 30 });
  console.log('✏️  Updated:', updated.toJSON());

  // --- Delete --------------------------------------------------
  await deleteUser(updated);
  console.log('🗑️  Deleted user', updated.id);

  // Clean up the connection when done
  await sequelize.close();
})();
```

**Running the script**

```bash
node index.js
```

You should see output similar to:

```
✅  Database & tables are ready
✅  Created: { id: 1, name: 'Jane Doe', email: 'jane@example.com', age: 29, createdAt: …, updatedAt: … }
🔍  Found: { id: 1, name: 'Jane Doe', email: 'jane@example.com', age: 29, createdAt: …, updatedAt: … }
✏️  Updated: { id: 1, name: 'Jane Doe', email: 'jane@example.com', age: 30, createdAt: …, updatedAt: … }
🗑️  Deleted user 1
```

The file `my_app.sqlite` will appear in the same directory – that’s the SQLite database that Sequelize created automatically.

---

## How it works (high‑level)

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1️⃣ Import & configure** | `new Sequelize({…})` reads the config (dialect, connection string, etc.) and creates a pool of connections. |
| **2️⃣ Model definition** | `sequelize.define('User', { … })` describes a JS class whose instances correspond to rows in the *users* table. `DataTypes` give column types and validation rules. |
| **3️⃣ Sync** | `sequelize.sync()` translates the model into SQL (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users …`) and runs it against the DB. |
| **4️⃣ ORM ↔ SQL** | Every call you make (`User.create`, `User.findByPk`, `user.update`, `user.destroy`) is turned into the appropriate SQL statement (INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE) by Sequelize. |
| **5️⃣ Return plain objects** | The ORM returns “model instances” – JavaScript objects that expose the row data as properties and also helper methods (`update`, `destroy`, etc.). |
| **6️⃣ Connection cleanup** | `sequelize.close()` ends the underlying connection pool, ensuring the Node process can exit cleanly. |

### Why there isn’t a `node:orm`

Node’s standard library intentionally stays small and focused on low‑level I/O (networking, file system, crypto, etc.).  
Higher‑level data‑access patterns (ORMs, query builders, migrations, etc.) are supplied by the community because:

* Different projects need different SQL dialects (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite, …).
* Performance, feature set, and API style vary widely.
* The Node team prefers to keep the runtime stable and minimal.

If you or your team still want a “first‑party” ORM, you would have to lobby for a new proposal at the [Node.js repository](https://github.com/nodejs/node) or watch the Node‑ ecosystem for future proposals. For now, the pattern shown above with Sequelize (or any other popular ORM) is the standard way to map JS objects to SQL tables.

---

### Quick cheat‑sheet for swapping to MySQL/PostgreSQL

```js
// MySQL example
const sequelize = new Sequelize('my_db', 'user', 'pass', {
  host: 'localhost',
  dialect: 'mysql',
  pool: { max: 5, min: 0, acquire: 30000, idle: 10000 },
});

// PostgreSQL example
const sequelize = new Sequelize('postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/my_db', {
  dialect: 'postgres',
});
```

Everything else (model definition, sync, CRUD) stays exactly the same – Sequelize abstracts the dialect differences for you.

---

**Take‑away:** Use a community‑driven ORM like Sequelize (or TypeORM, Prisma, etc.) – they give you the object‑relational mapping you’re looking for and will continue to work with future Node.js releases. If your tech lead truly wants something *built‑in*, you can point them to the Node.js issue tracker and suggest a new proposal. Happy coding!

Here’s a **stand‑alone, self‑contained example** that shows how to call the C++29 `std::simd_sort` algorithm.  
It creates a vector of random integers, sorts the vector with the new SIMD‑aware execution policy, checks that the result is sorted and prints the elapsed time.

```cpp
// simd_sort_demo.cpp
// Compile with a C++29 (or later) front‑end, e.g.:
//   g++ -std=c++2b -O3 -march=native simd_sort_demo.cpp -o simd_sort_demo
//   clang++ -std=c++2b -O3 -march=native simd_sort_demo.cpp -o simd_sort_demo
//
// The `simd_sort` function lives in <algorithm> and uses the new execution
// policy `std::execution::simd` that tells the implementation to emit SIMD
// instructions while sorting.

#include <algorithm>   // std::simd_sort, std::is_sorted
#include <chrono>      // std::chrono::high_resolution_clock
#include <execution>   // std::execution::simd
#include <iostream>    // std::cout, std::boolalpha
#include <random>      // std::random_device, std::mt19937, std::uniform_int_distribution
#include <vector>      // std::vector

int main()
{
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Generate a large array of random data
    // -------------------------------------------------
    constexpr std::size_t N = 1'000'000;          // 1 M elements – enough to show off SIMD
    std::vector<int>      data;
    data.reserve(N);

    std::random_device                rd;
    std::mt19937                      gen(rd());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> dist(0, 9'999'999);

    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i)
        data.push_back(dist(gen));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Sort using the SIMD‑enabled execution policy
    // -------------------------------------------------
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    // The call that does the heavy lifting:
    std::simd_sort(std::execution::simd,   // <-- SIMD execution policy
                   data.begin(),
                   data.end());

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Verify the result and report timing
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const bool ok = std::is_sorted(data.cbegin(), data.cend());

    std::cout << "Sorted correctly: " << std::boolalpha << ok << '\n';
    std::cout << "Elapsed time: "
              << std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(end - start).count()
              << " ms\n";

    return ok ? 0 : 1;   // non‑zero exit code if something went wrong
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Data generation** | A `std::vector<int>` is filled with 1 M random `int`s (uniformly distributed over a modest range). This gives the sort something non‑trivial to work on while keeping the data cheap to generate. |
| **SIMD sort call** | `std::simd_sort(policy, first, last)` is the new algorithm. `std::execution::simd` tells the implementation “use SIMD instructions (e.g. AVX2/AVX‑512) to process several elements in parallel within a single thread.” Because the algorithm is still allowed to run in multiple threads, you can also combine it with `std::execution::par` (e.g. `std::execution::par | std::execution::simd`). |
