Below is a **self‑contained, tiny example** that shows how a `List<int>` (and other primitive value types) can be used directly in **Java 30** thanks to the new *value‑type generics* support in `java.util`.  
No boxing/unboxing occurs – the primitives are stored inline, just like reference‑type elements.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Demonstrates the new value‑type generic lists that ship with Java 30.
 * Primitives can now be used as type arguments, eliminating the need for
 * wrapper classes (Integer, Long, …) and avoiding autoboxing overhead.
 */
public class PrimitiveValueListDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // ----- List<int> -------------------------------------------------
        List<int> ints = new ArrayList<>();
        ints.add(10);          // no Integer boxing
        ints.add(20);
        ints.add(30);

        System.out.println("=== int list ===");
        // classic for‑each (no boxing)
        for (int v : ints) {
            System.out.println(v);
        }

        // random access – also boxing‑free
        int second = ints.get(1);
        System.out.println("Second element: " + second);

        // Stream (still primitive streams, but no extra wrapper)
        int sum = ints.stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum();
        System.out.println("Sum of ints: " + sum);

        // ----- List<long> ------------------------------------------------
        List<long> longs = new ArrayList<>();
        longs.add(100L);
        longs.add(200L);

        System.out.println("\n=== long list ===");
        longs.forEach(System.out::println);   // no Long boxing

        // ----- List<double> ---------------------------------------------
        List<double> doubles = new ArrayList<>();
        doubles.add(3.14);
        doubles.add(2.718);

        System.out.println("\n=== double list ===");
        doubles.forEach(System.out::println); // no Double boxing
    }
}
```

### What the example shows

| Feature | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| `List<int>`, `List<long>`, `List<double>` | Direct primitive type arguments – the first‑class “value‑type generics” introduced in Java 30. |
| `add(E)` with primitive literals (`10`, `100L`, `3.14`) | Inserted directly into the backing array, **no autoboxing** to `Integer`, `Long`, `Double`. |
| `for‑each` loop (`for (int v : ints)`) | Iterates over the primitive values without wrapper objects. |
| `get(int)` | Returns the primitive value directly, no `intValue()` call. |
| Streams (`ints.stream().mapToInt(...).sum()`) | Still use primitive streams (`IntStream`), but the list itself stores primitive values, so there’s no intermediate boxing when filling the stream. |

### How to compile / run (preview mode)

```bash
# Compile with preview features enabled for Java 30
javac --enable-preview --release 30 PrimitiveValueListDemo.java

# Run (also using the preview runtime)
java --enable-preview PrimitiveValueListDemo
```

> **Note:**  
> *Value‑type generics* are a preview feature in Java 30. They are expected to become stable in a later release. The syntax (`List<int>`, `List<long>`, …) is exactly what the language spec defines for “primitive generic arguments”.

---

Feel free to copy‑paste the example into your internal documentation, adjusting the comments to match the naming conventions of your project. Happy coding!

