It looks like there is a slight misunderstanding regarding the Java Standard Library. **There is no class called `java.util.cache.AutoLRU` in the standard Java JDK.**

However, Java provides a built-in way to create an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache very easily using the **`java.util.LinkedHashMap`** class.

Here is how you can build your own LRU cache using the standard library.

### The Secret: `LinkedHashMap`
The `LinkedHashMap` class has a special constructor and a method called `removeEldestEntry`. When you override this method, the map will automatically remove the oldest entry whenever a new one is added and the map exceeds a certain size.

### Complete Implementation Example

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        // true = access-order (LRU behavior)
        // false = insertion-order
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true); 
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        // Return true when the map should remove the oldest entry
        return size() > capacity;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a cache with a capacity of 3
        LRUCache<Integer, String> cache = new LRUCache<>(3);

        cache.put(1, "A");
        cache.put(2, "B");
        cache.put(3, "C");

        System.out.println("Initial Cache: " + cache); // {1=A, 2=B, 3=C}

        // Accessing key 1 makes it "Recently Used"
        cache.get(1); 
        System.out.println("After accessing 1: " + cache); // {2=B, 3=C, 1=A}

        // Adding a 4th element will trigger the removal of the eldest (Key 2)
        cache.put(4, "D");
        System.out.println("After adding 4: " + cache); // {3=C, 1=A, 4=D}
    }
}
```

### Key Technical Details:

1.  **`accessOrder = true`**: In the `super()` call, the third parameter is crucial. If set to `true`, the map moves an element to the end of the list every time it is accessed via `get()` or `put()`. This is what creates the "LRU" behavior.
2.  **`removeEldestEntry()`**: This method is called by the internal `put` logic. By returning `true` when `size() > capacity`, you tell Java to automatically delete the head of the linked list (the least recently used item).
3.  **Time Complexity**: Both `put` and `get` operations run in **O(1)** time.

### Professional Alternatives
If you are building a production-grade application and need a more powerful cache (with features like expiration times, soft references, or thread safety), I highly recommend these industry-standard libraries:

