I have to stop you right there to save you time on your deadline: **`java.net.http.Http3Client` does not exist in the standard Java JDK.**

Java's built-in HTTP client is `java.net.http.HttpClient`. Despite the name, it supports **both HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2**.

Here is the production-ready code to make an **HTTP/2** request using the standard JDK (Java 11+).

### The Code

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.time.Duration;

public class Http2Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Create the client and explicitly set the version to HTTP_2
        try (HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2) 
                .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
                .build()) {

            // 2. Build the request
            HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                    .uri(URI.create("https://www.google.com")) // Use an HTTPS URL (HTTP/2 requires TLS)
                    .GET()
                    .build();

            // 3. Send the request
            HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

            // 4. Verify the version used
            System.out.println("Response Version: " + response.version());
            System.out.println("Status Code: " + response.statusCode());
            System.out.println("Body: " + response.body().substring(0, 100) + "...");

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

### 3 Critical Things You Need to Know for Your Deadline:

1.  **HTTPS is Mandatory:** HTTP/2 is almost never supported over plain text (`http://`). If you use an `http` URL, the client will automatically "downgrade" to HTTP/1.1. Use `https`.
2.  **Automatic Negotiation (ALPN):** The `HttpClient` uses ALPN (Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation). Even if you set `.version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2)`, if the server doesn't support HTTP/2, the client will gracefully fall back to HTTP/1.1 without crashing.
3.  **HTTP/3 is different:** If you actually meant **HTTP/3 (QUIC)**, it is not yet part of the standard Java JDK. To use HTTP/3, you would need a third-party library like **Netty** or **OkHttp** (with specific extensions).

**Summary for your boss/ticket:** Use `java.net.http.HttpClient` with `.version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2)`.

