First, a quick technical correction to set the stage: in the C++20/23 standard library, the algorithm is actually called **`std::ranges::stable_partition`**. 

The "inplace" and "fast" aspects are implementation details: the algorithm is designed to be "fast" (linear time) if extra memory is available to buffer elements, but it will automatically fall back to a slower, truly in-place algorithm (log-linear time) if memory allocation fails.

Here is the professional implementation.

### The Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> // for std::ranges::stable_partition
#include <ranges>    // for std::ranges::view
#include <string>

struct Task {
    int id;
    std::string name;
    bool is_priority;

    // For easy printing
    auto operator<=>(const Task&) const = default; 
};

std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Task& t) {
    return os << "[" << t.id << ": " << t.name << (t.is_priority ? " (P)" : " (N)") << "]";
}

int main() {
    // 1. Setup a dataset where relative order matters
    // We want priority tasks first, but we must preserve the original 
    // sequence of priority tasks relative to each other.
    std::vector<Task> todo_list = {
        {1, "Email Client", false},
        {2, "Fix Critical Bug", true},
        {3, "Update Docs", false},
        {4, "Security Patch", true},
        {5, "Coffee Break", false},
        {6, "Deploy to Prod", true},
    };

    std::cout << "Before partition:\n";
    for (const auto& t : todo_list) std::cout << t << " ";
    std::cout << "\n\n";

    // 2. Use std::ranges::stable_partition
    // This moves all elements satisfying the predicate to the front.
    // It returns a subrange (or iterator) pointing to the first element 
    // that does NOT satisfy the predicate.
    auto [first_non_priority, end_it] = std::ranges::stable_partition(todo_list, [](const auto& t) {
        return t.is_priority;
    });

    std::cout << "After stable_partition (Priority first):\n";
    for (const auto& t : todo_list) std::cout << t << " ";
    std::cout << "\n\n";

    // 3. Demonstrate the power of the return value
    // We can now easily create views of the two resulting groups
    auto priority_tasks = std::ranges::subrange(todo_list.begin(), first_non_priority);
    auto normal_tasks = std::ranges::subrange(first_non_priority, end_it);

    std::cout << "Priority Queue size: " << std::ranges::distance(priority_tasks) << "\n";
    std::cout << "Normal Queue size: " << std::ranges::distance(normal_tasks) << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

### Engineering Breakdown

#### 1. Time and Space Complexity
*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ if enough extra memory is available; $O(N \log N)$ if no extra memory is available.
*   **Space Complexity:** $O(N)$ worst-case for the internal buffer to achieve linear time.

#### 2. Why `stable_partition` instead of `partition`?
If we used `std::ranges::partition`, the "Fix Critical Bug" (ID 2) and "Deploy to Prod" (ID 6) might be swapped. In a task list or a UI list, jumping elements around randomly is jarring for the user. `stable_partition` guarantees that if Task A came before Task B in the original list, they will maintain that relative order in their new partition.

#### 3. The "Ranges" Advantage
By using the `std::ranges` version instead of the classic `std::stable_partition`:
*   **No more `.begin()`/`.end()` boilerplate:** We pass the container `todo_list` directly.
*   **Projection potential:** While I used a lambda here, `std::ranges` algorithms often support projections, allowing you to partition based on a specific member variable without writing a full predicate.

