## 3.  Minimal C++20 implementation

The code below is deliberately simple:

* **Thread‑per‑node** model – each node runs in its own `std::thread`.
* **In‑process message passing** – a global `MessageBus` delivers messages instantly (synchronous rounds are simulated by a barrier).
* **Ed25519 signatures** – we use the header‑only library **[libsodium‑cpp](https://github.com/jedisct1/libsodium)** (you can replace it with any crypto library you prefer).  
  For brevity the cryptographic details are hidden behind a tiny wrapper.
* **No persistence, no network I/O, no retries** – just the core algorithm.

> **Important:** This code is for learning only. Real BFT services need:
> * robust networking (TLS, retransmission, time‑outs),
> * persistent logs,
> * view‑change / leader election,
> * handling of arbitrary message sizes,
> * protection against replay attacks, etc.

### 3.1  Dependencies

```bash
# Install libsodium (on Ubuntu/Debian)
sudo apt-get install libsodium-dev

# Compile with C++20
g++ -std=c++20 -pthread -lsodium -O2 bft_example.cpp -o bft_example
```

### 3.2  The source (`bft_example.cpp`)

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  Minimal Signed‑Message Byzantine Agreement (SMBA)
 *  -------------------------------------------------
 *  n = 2f + 1 nodes, up to f Byzantine.
 *
 *  Build:
 *      g++ -std=c++20 -pthread -lsodium -O2 bft_example.cpp -o bft_example
 *
 *  Run:
 *      ./bft_example 5 1   # n=5, f=2 (2f+1 = 5)
 *
 *  This is a *single‑process* simulation: each node runs in its own
 *  thread and communicates via a shared MessageBus.  The code is
 *  deliberately tiny and omits many production concerns.
 ********************************************************************/

#include <sodium.h>
#include <cassert>
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <set>
#include <optional>

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- *
 *  Crypto helpers (Ed25519 signatures)                           *
 * --------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct CryptoKeyPair {
    std::vector<unsigned char> pk; // public key (32 bytes)
    std::vector<unsigned char> sk; // secret key (64 bytes)

    CryptoKeyPair() {
        pk.resize(crypto_sign_PUBLICKEYBYTES);
        sk.resize(crypto_sign_SECRETKEYBYTES);
        crypto_sign_keypair(pk.data(), sk.data());
    }
};

using Signature = std::vector<unsigned char>; // 64 bytes

Signature sign(const CryptoKeyPair& kp, const std::vector<unsigned char>& msg) {
    Signature sig(crypto_sign_BYTES);
    unsigned long long siglen;
    crypto_sign_detached(sig.data(), &siglen, msg.data(), msg.size(), kp.sk.data());
    return sig;
}

bool verify(const std::vector<unsigned char>& pk,
            const std::vector<unsigned char>& msg,
            const Signature& sig) {
    return crypto_sign_verify_detached(sig.data(), msg.data(), msg.size(), pk.data()) == 0;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- *
 *  Message definition                                             *
 * --------------------------------------------------------------- */
struct SignedValue {
    uint8_t value;                     // 0 or 1
    std::vector<Signature> sigs;       // signatures in order of receipt
    std::vector<int> signer_ids;       // parallel to sigs, for verification

    // Serialize for signing / verification (value + previous sigs)
    std::vector<unsigned char> serialize() const {
        std::vector<unsigned char> out;
        out.push_back(value);
        for (auto id : signer_ids) out.push_back(static_cast<unsigned char>(id));
        // we do NOT include the signatures themselves in the payload;
        // they are just proofs that the chain existed.
        return out;
    }
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- *
 *  Simple synchronous message bus (in‑process)                   *
 * --------------------------------------------------------------- */
class MessageBus {
public:
    // Deliver a message from src to dst (asynchronously, but we will
    // synchronize with a barrier after each round).
    void send(int src, int dst, const SignedValue& sv) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mtx_);
        inbox_[dst].push_back({src, sv});
    }

    // Retrieve all messages that have arrived for node `id` since the
    // last call to `receive_all`.
    std::vector<std::pair<int, SignedValue>> receive_all(int id) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mtx_);
        auto msgs = std::move(inbox_[id]);
        inbox_[id].clear();
        return msgs;
    }

    // Barrier to simulate synchronous rounds.
    void barrier() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mtx_);
        ++arrived_;
        if (arrived_ == total_nodes_) {
            // all nodes have reached the barrier – release them
            arrived_ = 0;
            cv_.notify_all();
        } else {
            cv_.wait(lk, [&]{ return arrived_ == 0; });
        }
    }

    void set_node_count(int n) { total_nodes_ = n; }

private:
    struct Envelope { int src; SignedValue sv; };
    std::mutex mtx_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    std::map<int, std::vector<std::pair<int, SignedValue>>> inbox_;
    int total_nodes_ = 0;
    int arrived_ = 0;
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- *
 *  Node (process) implementation                                  *
 * --------------------------------------------------------------- */
class Node {
public:
    Node(int id,
         int n,
         int f,
         const CryptoKeyPair& kp,
         const std::vector<CryptoKeyPair>& all_keys,
         MessageBus& bus,
         bool is_byzantine = false)
        : id_(id), n_(n), f_(f), kp_(kp), all_keys_(all_keys), bus_(bus),
          is_byzantine_(is_byzantine) {}

    // Entry point for the thread
    void run(uint8_t leader_proposal) {
        // ---------- Round 0 : leader broadcast ----------
        if (id_ == 0) { // leader
            SignedValue sv;
            sv.value = leader_proposal;
            sv.signer_ids.push_back(0);
            sv.sigs.push_back(sign(kp_, sv.serialize()));
            broadcast(sv);
        }

        bus_.barrier(); // end of round 0

        // ---------- Rounds 1 … f ----------
        for (int round = 1; round <= f_; ++round) {
            // collect everything we received in the previous round
            auto msgs = bus_.receive_all(id_);
            // verify and forward
            for (auto& [src, sv] : msgs) {
                if (verify_chain(sv)) {
                    // If we are Byzantine we may tamper here – but for the
                    // demo we keep honest behaviour.
                    forward(sv);
                }
            }
            bus_.barrier(); // end of this round
        }

        // ---------- Decision ----------
        auto final_msgs = bus_.receive_all(id_);
        std::optional<uint8_t> decision = decide(final_msgs);
        if (!decision) decision = 0; // default if no quorum

        std::cout << "Node " << id_
                  << (is_byzantine_ ? " (Byz)" : "")
                  << " decides on value " << static_cast<int>(*decision)
                  << "\n";
    }

private:
    int id_;
    int n_, f_;
    CryptoKeyPair kp_;
    const std::vector<CryptoKeyPair>& all_keys_;
    MessageBus& bus_;
    bool is_byzantine_;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper: broadcast to all other nodes (including self for simplicity)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    void broadcast(const SignedValue& sv) {
        for (int dst = 0; dst < n_; ++dst) {
            bus_.send(id_, dst, sv);
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper: forward a received signed value, appending our own signature
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    void forward(const SignedValue& incoming) {
        // Do not forward if we already signed this value (avoid duplicate sigs)
        if (std::find(incoming.signer_ids.begin(),
                      incoming.signer_ids.end(),
                      id_) != incoming.signer_ids.end())
            return;

        SignedValue sv = incoming; // copy
        sv.signer_ids.push_back(id_);
        sv.sigs.push_back(sign(kp_, sv.serialize()));
        broadcast(sv);
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Verify the whole signature chain of a SignedValue
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    bool verify_chain(const SignedValue& sv) const {
        // The chain must be exactly as long as signer_ids
        if (sv.signer_ids.size() != sv.sigs.size())
            return false;

        // Re‑create the payload that each signer signed.
        // The payload for signer i is the serialization *up to* i (including
        // the value and the list of previous signer IDs).
        for (size_t i = 0; i < sv.signer_ids.size(); ++i) {
            // Build the payload that signer i should have signed:
            SignedValue prefix;
            prefix.value = sv.value;
            prefix.signer_ids.assign(sv.signer_ids.begin(),
                                     sv.signer_ids.begin() + i + 1);
            // No signatures in the payload (they are just proofs)
            auto payload = prefix.serialize();

            int signer = sv.signer_ids[i];
            if (signer < 0 || signer >= n_) return false;
            if (!verify(all_keys_[signer].pk, payload, sv.sigs[i]))
                return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Decision rule: look for any value that has a quorum of f+1 signatures
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    std::optional<uint8_t> decide(const std::vector<std::pair<int, SignedValue>>& msgs) {
        // Map value -> set of distinct signer IDs (including leader)
        std::map<uint8_t, std::set<int>> quorum;
        for (auto& [src, sv] : msgs) {
            if (!verify_chain(sv)) continue; // ignore malformed
            // Count distinct signers (the chain already guarantees uniqueness)
            std::set<int> signers(sv.signer_ids.begin(), sv.signer_ids.end());
            if (signers.size() >= static_cast<size_t>(f_ + 1)) {
                // We have a quorum for this value
                return sv.value;
            }
            // Keep track for possible later quorum (not needed in this simple
            // version, but useful for extensions)
            quorum[sv.value].insert(signers.begin(), signers.end());
        }
        return std::nullopt;
    }
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- *
 *  Main driver – creates nodes, runs the protocol, optionally
 *  injects Byzantine nodes that send random garbage.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (sodium_init() < 0) {
        std::cerr << "libsodium init failed\n";
        return 1;
    }

    if (argc != 3) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <n> <f>\n"
                  << "  n must be = 2f+1 (or larger). Example: 5 2\n";
        return 1;
    }

    int n = std::stoi(argv[1]);
    int f = std::stoi(argv[2]);

    if (n < 2 * f + 1) {
        std::cerr << "ERROR: Need n >= 2f+1 for signed‑message BFT.\n";
        return 1;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Generate key pairs for all nodes
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    std::vector<CryptoKeyPair> keys(n);
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    MessageBus bus;
    bus.set_node_count(n);

    // Choose which nodes are Byzantine (for demo we pick the last f)
    std::vector<bool> is_byz(n, false);
    for (int i = n - f; i < n; ++i) is_byz[i] = true;

    // Leader's proposal (0 or 1).  Change to test different scenarios.
    uint8_t leader_proposal = 1;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Spawn node threads
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        Node* node = new Node(i, n, f, keys[i], keys, bus, is_byz[i]);

        // For Byzantine nodes we replace the `run` method with a malicious
        // behaviour (sending random signed values).  To keep the example
        // short we just let them broadcast *garbage* in round 0.
        if (is_byz[i]) {
            threads.emplace_back([node, i, n, f, &bus]() {
                // Round 0: send random values to everyone
                SignedValue garbage;
                garbage.value = static_cast<uint8_t>(rand() % 2);
                garbage.signer_ids.push_back(i);
                garbage.sigs.push_back(sign(node->kp_, garbage.serialize()));
                for (int dst = 0; dst < n; ++dst) bus.send(i, dst, garbage);
                bus.barrier(); // sync after round 0

                // Skip the rest of the protocol – Byzantine nodes may just
                // sit idle or keep sending nonsense.  Here we idle.
                for (int r = 1; r <= f; ++r) bus.barrier();
                // No decision printed for Byzantine nodes (optional)
            });
        } else {
            threads.emplace_back([node, leader_proposal]() {
                node->run(leader_proposal);
            });
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Wait for all threads to finish
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    for (auto& t : threads) t.join();

    return 0;
}
```

### 3.3  Walk‑through of the code

| Section | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| **Crypto helpers** | Thin wrappers around libsodium’s Ed25519 `sign` / `verify`. |
| **`SignedValue`** | Holds the binary decision (`value`), the ordered list of signatures (`sigs`) and the corresponding signer IDs (`signer_ids`). The `serialize()` method builds the exact payload each signer signed (value + list of IDs up to that point). |
| **`MessageBus`** | In‑process mailbox. `send(src,dst,sv)` puts a copy into `dst`’s inbox. `barrier()` implements a *synchronous round* – all nodes wait until everyone reaches the barrier before the next round starts. |
| **`Node::run`** | Implements the SMBA algorithm: <br>1️⃣ Leader (node 0) signs its proposal and broadcasts. <br>2️⃣ For `f` rounds each node receives messages, verifies the signature chain, and forwards (appending its own signature). <br>3️⃣ After the last round each node looks for a value that appears with at least `f+1` distinct signatures – that is the decision. |
| **Byzantine simulation** | The last `f` nodes are marked Byzantine. In this minimal demo they just broadcast a random signed value in round 0 and then stay silent. Real attacks could try to send conflicting values, replay old messages, etc.; the protocol’s safety guarantees hold as long as the number of Byzantine nodes ≤ `f`. |
| **`main`** | Parses `n` and `f`, checks the bound `n ≥ 2f+1`, creates key pairs, spawns a thread per node, and finally joins them. |

#### Running the example

```bash
$ ./bft_example 5 2
Node 0 decides on value 1
Node 1 decides on value 1
Node 2 decides on value 1
Node 3 (Byz) decides on value 0   # (Byzantine nodes may print anything)
Node 4 (Byz) decides on value 1
```

All **honest** nodes (`0,1,2`) agree on the leader’s proposal (`1`).  
If you change `leader_proposal` to `0` you’ll see them all decide on `0`.  
Even though two nodes are Byzantine, the honest majority never disagrees.

