## 4.  Extending the prototype toward a real service

| Feature | What you need to add | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------------|----------------|
| **Persistent log & state machine** | Write each decided value to durable storage (e.g., RocksDB) before applying it. | Guarantees crash‑recovery and prevents “lost decisions”. |
| **View change / leader election** | Detect a faulty leader (timeout) and rotate to a new leader (e.g., PBFT’s view‑change). | Keeps the system live when the current leader crashes or is malicious. |
| **Network layer** | Replace the in‑process `MessageBus` with a real RPC framework (gRPC, ZeroMQ, custom TCP/UDP). Add message authentication (TLS) and retransmission. | Works across machines, handles packet loss, and provides confidentiality. |
| **Batching & pipelining** | Group many client requests into a single consensus instance. | Improves throughput (hundreds/thousands of ops/sec). |
| **Dynamic membership** | Add/remove nodes without stopping the service (e.g., using a reconfiguration protocol). | Allows scaling and maintenance. |
| **Performance optimizations** | Use threshold signatures (BLS) to shrink the `f+1` signatures into a single aggregate signature. | Reduces bandwidth from O(n·f) to O(1). |
| **Testing & formal verification** | Model‑check the protocol (TLA+, Ivy) and run systematic fault‑injection tests (Jepsen). | Ensures the implementation truly satisfies safety/liveness under the assumed model. |

If you need a production‑grade library, consider **open‑source BFT frameworks** that already implement these pieces:

* **[BFT-SMaRt](https://github.com/bft-smart/library)** – Java, but the design is portable.  
* **[MirBFT / HotStuff](https://github.com/facebookarchive/mirbft)** – modern BFT with linear communication.  
* **[Tendermint Core](https://github.com/tendermint/tendermint)** – Go implementation of a BFT consensus used in many blockchains.  
* **[libp2p / libp2p‑cpp](https://github.com/libp2p/cpp-libp2p)** – networking stack you can pair with your own consensus.

---

## 5.  TL;DR – What you should take away

1. **`n = 2f` is impossible** for Byzantine agreement without signatures.  
   With **digital signatures** you need at least **`n = 2f + 1`**; without signatures you need **`n = 3f + 1`**.

2. The **Signed‑Message Byzantine Agreement** protocol (leader signs, everyone forwards, decide on a quorum of `f+1` signatures) works for `n = 2f + 1`.

3. The code above is a **minimal, single‑process simulation** that:
   * generates keys,
   * runs the protocol in synchronous rounds,
   * tolerates up to `f` Byzantine nodes,
   * prints the decision of each honest node.

4. Turning this into a **real service** requires many engineering layers (networking, persistence, view‑change, batching, testing, etc.). Use the prototype as a learning sandbox, then move to a battle‑tested library or framework for production.

Happy hacking, and may your consensus always be *byzantine‑resilient* (and your bugs be few)!

Below is a **complete, minimal, and runnable** example of a *distributed lock service* that

* guarantees **safety** (no two clients ever hold the same lock at the same time) and
* provides **liveness** (a client that keeps trying eventually acquires the lock)  

in an **asynchronous network with crash‑only failures**.

The trick is to **reduce the lock problem to consensus** – i.e. to a replicated state machine that is driven by a proven consensus algorithm (Raft).  Raft gives us:

| Property | What it gives us for the lock service |
|----------|----------------------------------------|
| **Safety** (no split‑brain) | At most one leader at a time, and all committed log entries are seen by every non‑faulty node.  Therefore the lock state that lives in the Raft log is *single‑source‑of‑truth*. |
| **Liveness** (eventual progress) | Under the *partial‑synchrony* assumption that Raft makes (there is some unknown bound on message delay after some unknown time), a leader will eventually be elected and log entries will be committed.  This is the weakest realistic assumption you can make in an asynchronous network with crashes (the FLP impossibility theorem tells us you cannot guarantee liveness in a *purely* asynchronous system). |

The example below uses the **HashiCorp Raft library** (`github.com/hashicorp/raft`) because it is tiny, pure‑Go, and already implements the Raft protocol correctly.  The lock service itself is only a few hundred lines of code:

* a **finite‑state machine (FSM)** that stores a map `lockName → holderID` and applies `Lock` / `Unlock` commands,
* a **gRPC API** (`Lock`, `Unlock`, `Renew`) that clients call,
* a **lease‑based** lock so that a client that crashes does not hold the lock forever (the lease expires unless the client periodically renews it).

---

## 1. High‑level design

```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Raft Node A     |  RPC/   |   Raft Node B     |  RPC/   |   Raft Node C     |
| (lock FSM + Raft) |<-------> | (lock FSM + Raft) |<-------> | (lock FSM + Raft) |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
        ^  ^                         ^  ^                         ^  ^
        |  | gRPC (Lock/Unlock)      |  | gRPC (Lock/Unlock)      |  |
        |  +--------------------------+  +--------------------------+
        |
        +--- Client processes (any number)
```

*Each node* runs the same binary.  The first node you start becomes the initial leader (or you can bootstrap a three‑node cluster with a static configuration).  All nodes expose the same gRPC service; a client can talk to any node – the node forwards the request to the current Raft leader (the library does this for us).

The **lock state machine** is deterministic: the only way to change the lock map is by committing a `LockCmd` or `UnlockCmd` entry to the Raft log.  Because Raft guarantees that all non‑faulty nodes apply the same entries in the same order, the lock map is *always* consistent.

### Lease semantics

* When a client successfully acquires a lock it receives a **lease TTL** (e.g. 5 s) and a **lease ID**.
* The client must periodically call `Renew(leaseID)` before the TTL expires.
* If the client crashes or stops renewing, the lease expires and the lock is automatically released by a background goroutine on each server (the lease expiration is *deterministic* because all servers see the same timestamps).

This gives us **liveness**: a crashed client cannot block the lock forever.

