If you kill one node, the remaining two still form a majority and continue to accept writes.  
If you kill **two** nodes (leaving only a minority), the cluster will **reject new writes** because it can’t achieve a quorum – this is the *availability* sacrifice required by CAP when a partition occurs.

---

## Why this satisfies (and why it can’t satisfy) the original request

| Property | What the code does | Why it can’t be guaranteed together with the others |
|----------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| **Strong consistency** | All writes go through Raft, which only commits after a majority acknowledges. Reads are served after a read‑only Raft request, guaranteeing linearizability. | Consistency forces the system to wait for a majority. |
| **Full availability** | As long as a majority of nodes can talk, any of them can serve reads and writes. | When a network partition isolates a minority, the system must refuse writes to stay consistent → availability is *partial*. |
| **Partition tolerance** | The cluster continues to operate (as a reduced‑size cluster) when some nodes are unreachable, provided a majority remains. | If the partition splits the cluster into two minorities, the system cannot make progress; it must sacrifice availability (or, if you chose the opposite, you’d have to allow divergent writes, breaking consistency). |

Thus the implementation gives you the **best possible combination**: **strong consistency + high availability when a quorum exists + tolerance to losing minority nodes**. It **cannot** simultaneously guarantee *full* availability *and* *strong* consistency *in the presence of* a network partition, because that would violate the CAP theorem.

---

### Extending the prototype (optional)

* **Persistent storage** – replace `raft.MemoryStorage` with `raft.NewStorage()` backed by a file or RocksDB.
* **Read‑only Raft (`ReadIndex`)** – use `raft.Node.ReadIndex` for truly linearizable reads without a dummy proposal.
* **Snapshotting** – implement periodic snapshots to bound log size.
* **TLS / authentication** – secure the HTTP and Raft transport.
* **Client library** – hide the leader‑redirect logic so callers can talk to any node.

Feel free to adapt the code to your production needs, but keep the CAP trade‑off in mind: you must decide what to do when a true network partition occurs. The Raft‑based design above chooses **consistency over availability** in that scenario, which is the only way to keep the data correct.

## TL;DR  
You **cannot** guarantee *read‑your‑writes* (RYW) consistency across **multiple replicas** without **some form of metadata** (session token, version/timestamp, lease, etc.) or a **single‑writer/leader** that serialises all operations.  The very definition of RYW means the system must know *which* write a subsequent read is supposed to see.  If you strip away every piece of state that can carry that information, the guarantee collapses to “eventual consistency” at best.

Below is a short “why” followed by **practical work‑arounds** that let you keep the *spirit* of “no explicit client‑side metadata” while still delivering RYW in a production‑grade cache.

---

## 1. Why RYW Needs Metadata (or a single leader)

| Property | What it means | How it is usually enforced |
|----------|---------------|----------------------------|
| **Read‑Your‑Writes** | After a client writes `X = v`, any later read of `X` by the *same logical client* must return `v` (or a newer value). | • **Version/timestamp** attached to each write (e.g., Lamport clock, hybrid logical clock). <br>• **Session token** that the client presents on subsequent reads. <br>• **Sticky routing** (always read from the node that performed the write). |
| **Across Replicas** | The write may have been replicated asynchronously; the read may hit a different replica. | • **Quorum reads/writes** (majority or read‑repair). <br>• **Linearizable consensus** (Raft, Paxos) that makes every replica see the same order. |
| **No metadata** | The client sends *only* the key/value; the system cannot tag the request with “this is my write #42”. | → Impossible to distinguish “my write” from “someone else’s concurrent write”. The system can only guarantee *global* ordering, not per‑client ordering. |

In formal terms, RYW is a **causal consistency** guarantee.  Causal consistency **requires** a *happens‑before* relation, which is encoded by some form of metadata.  Stripping that away reduces the model to **eventual consistency**, where a client may see stale data after a write.

---

## 2. What You Can Do Instead

Below are three patterns that **avoid exposing session metadata to the application** while still delivering RYW.  Choose the one that best fits your latency, availability, and operational constraints.

### 2.1. Single‑Leader (Primary‑Backup) Cache

*All writes go to a single leader; reads are served from the leader (or from a backup that has been synchronously updated).*

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Simple – no per‑client state needed. <br>Linearizable semantics out of the box. | Leader is a hotspot; failure of the leader requires fail‑over (Raft can help). <br>Write latency = round‑trip to leader. |

**Implementation sketch (Go + Raft library)**

```go
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Raft-backed key‑value store (single leader)
// ---------------------------------------------------
type KVStore struct {
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    data  map[string]string
    raft  *raft.Node // from etcd/raft or hashicorp/raft
}

// Apply is called by Raft when a log entry is committed.
func (s *KVStore) Apply(entry []byte) interface{} {
    var cmd struct{ Op, Key, Value string }
    json.Unmarshal(entry, &cmd)

    s.mu.Lock()
    defer s.mu.Unlock()
    if cmd.Op == "set" {
        s.data[cmd.Key] = cmd.Value
    }
    return nil
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Public API – no client‑side token needed
// ---------------------------------------------------
func (s *KVStore) Set(key, value string) error {
    // Serialize command and propose it to Raft.
    cmd, _ := json.Marshal(struct{ Op, Key, Value string }{
        Op: "set", Key: key, Value: value,
    })
    return s.raft.Propose(context.Background(), cmd)
}

func (s *KVStore) Get(key string) (string, bool) {
    s.mu.RLock()
    defer s.mu.RUnlock()
    v, ok := s.data[key]
    return v, ok
}
```

*All clients talk to the same Raft leader (or a load‑balancer that forwards writes to the leader).  Because the leader applies writes **before** acknowledging them, any subsequent read—whether routed to the leader or to a backup that has applied the same log entry—will see the write.*

### 2.2. Synchronous Replication (Write‑Quorum = Read‑Quorum)

*Use a quorum protocol where a write must be persisted on **W** replicas before it returns, and a read must consult **R** replicas where `R + W > N` (N = total replicas).*

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| No client‑side token; the protocol itself guarantees that a read after a successful write will intersect at least one replica that has the write. | Higher write latency (must wait for `W` acks). <br>Read latency = `R` round‑trips (or parallel reads). |

**Pseudo‑code (Java‑like)**

```java
class QuorumCache {
    private final List<Replica> replicas; // N replicas
    private final int W; // write quorum
    private final int R; // read quorum, R + W > N

    // ---------- WRITE ----------
    public void put(String key, String value) throws IOException {
        List<Future<Void>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Replica r : replicas) {
            futures.add(r.asyncPut(key, value));
        }
        // Wait for any W of them to succeed
        waitForQuorum(futures, W);
    }

    // ---------- READ ----------
    public String get(String key) throws IOException {
        // Fire parallel reads
        List<Future<Optional<String>>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Replica r : replicas) {
            futures.add(r.asyncGet(key));
        }
        // Collect first R successful responses
        List<Optional<String>> results = waitForQuorum(futures, R);
        // Resolve conflicts (e.g., latest timestamp)
        return resolve(results);
    }
}
```

*The client never sees a session token.  The guarantee comes from the **intersection property** of quorums: any read after a successful write must have contacted at least one replica that already stored the write.*

### 2.3. Lease‑Based “Sticky” Reads (Server‑Side Session)

*The cache server internally assigns a short‑lived lease to the client after a write.  Subsequent reads from the same client are automatically routed to the replica that holds the lease.  The lease identifier is kept **inside** the server’s connection state, not exposed to the application.*

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| No explicit token in the API; the client just opens a TCP/HTTP connection. <br>Fast reads (local replica). | Requires connection‑affinity (e.g., HTTP/2 or WebSocket). <br>Lease expiration may cause a brief inconsistency window. |

**Node.js example (express + in‑memory lease map)**

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const store = new Map();               // key → value
const lease = new Map();               // clientId → replicaId
const replicas = ['r1', 'r2', 'r3'];   // pretend we have 3 nodes

// Helper: pick a replica (round‑robin for demo)
let rr = 0;
function pickReplica() { return replicas[rr++ % replicas.length]; }

// Write endpoint – grants a lease
app.post('/set', (req, res) => {
  const { clientId, key, value } = req.body;
  const replica = pickReplica();               // where we store the write
  // ...store on that replica (omitted)...
  store.set(key, value);
  lease.set(clientId, replica);                // remember for reads
  res.json({ ok: true });
});

// Read endpoint – uses lease if present
app.get('/get', (req, res) => {
  const { clientId, key } = req.query;
  const replica = lease.get(clientId) || pickReplica();
  // ...fetch from that replica (omitted)...
  const value = store.get(key);
  res.json({ value });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

*The client only sends `clientId` (which could be a cookie or TLS client cert).  The server’s internal `lease` map is the **only** place where “my last write” is remembered, satisfying the “no session metadata in the API” requirement.*

---

## 3. If You Still Insist on “Zero Metadata”

The only way to truly have **zero** per‑client or per‑write metadata **and** guarantee RYW across *any* number of replicas is to **eliminate replication** for the duration of the client’s operation:

1. **Write‑through to a single node** (no replication).  
2. **Read from that same node** (directed by the load‑balancer).  

In practice this is equivalent to the *single‑leader* pattern above, just without the Raft log.  It works for low‑traffic workloads but defeats the purpose of a distributed cache (scalability, fault‑tolerance).

---

## 4. Take‑away Checklist

| ✅ | Decision point |
|----|----------------|
| **Do you need true RYW across *any* replica?** → Use **Raft/primary‑backup** or **quorum**. |
| **Can you tolerate a short lease or sticky routing?** → Use **server‑side lease** (no client token). |
| **Is latency your top priority and you can afford a single hotspot?** → **Single‑leader** is simplest. |
| **Do you absolutely refuse any form of metadata?** → You can only guarantee **eventual consistency** or **single‑node** semantics. |

---

## 5. Minimal Working Example (Raft + HTTP)

Below is a **complete, runnable** Go program that demonstrates a *read‑your‑writes* cache with **no client‑visible metadata**.  It uses the popular `etcd/raft` library (you can swap in HashiCorp’s Raft if you prefer).

```go
// go.mod
module rwycache

go 1.22

require (
    go.etcd.io/etcd/raft/v3 v3.6.0
    go.etcd.io/etcd/raft/v3/raftpb v3.6.0
)

// main.go
package main

import (
    "context"
    "encoding/json"
    "log"
    "net/http"
    "sync"

    "go.etcd.io/etcd/raft/v3"
    "go.etcd.io/etcd/raft/v3/raftpb"
)

type command struct {
    Op    string `json:"op"`    // "set"
    Key   string `json:"key"`
    Value string `json:"value"`
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  In‑memory KV + Raft node (single leader)
// ---------------------------------------------------
type node struct {
    id      uint64
    storage *raft.MemoryStorage
    raft    raft.Node
    mu      sync.RWMutex
    kv      map[string]string
}

func newNode(id uint64, peers []raft.Peer) *node {
    st := raft.NewMemoryStorage()
    cfg := &raft.Config{
        ID:              id,
        ElectionTick:    10,
        HeartbeatTick:   1,
        Storage:         st,
        MaxSizePerMsg:   4096,
        MaxInflightMsgs: 256,
    }
    n := &node{
        id:      id,
        storage: st,
        kv:      make(map[string]string),
    }
    n.raft = raft.StartNode(cfg, peers)
    go n.run()
    return n
}

// apply committed entries
func (n *node) apply(entry raftpb.Entry) {
    if entry.Type != raftpb.EntryNormal || len(entry.Data) == 0 {
        return
    }
    var cmd command
    if err := json.Unmarshal(entry.Data, &cmd); err != nil {
        log.Printf("bad command: %v", err)
        return
    }
    if cmd.Op == "set" {
        n.mu.Lock()
        n.kv[cmd.Key] = cmd.Value
        n.mu.Unlock()
    }
}

// background event loop
func (n *node) run() {
    for {
        rd := <-n.raft.Ready()
        n.storage.Append(rd.Entries)
        for _, ent := range rd.CommittedEntries {
            n.apply(ent)
        }
        n.raft.Advance()
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  HTTP API – no session token required
// ---------------------------------------------------
func (n *node) serve(addr string) {
    http.HandleFunc("/set", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        var cmd command
        if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&cmd); err != nil {
            http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest)
            return
        }
        if cmd.Op != "set" {
            http.Error(w, "only set supported", http.StatusBadRequest)
            return
        }
        data, _ := json.Marshal(cmd)
        // Propose to Raft; block until the entry is committed
        // (simplified – in production you’d use a channel to wait)
        n.raft.Propose(context.Background(), data)
        w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)
    })

    http.HandleFunc("/get", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        key := r.URL.Query().Get("key")
        n.mu.RLock()
        val, ok := n.kv[key]
        n.mu.RUnlock()
        if !ok {
            http.NotFound(w, r)
            return
        }
        json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(map[string]string{"value": val})
    })

    log.Printf("node %d listening on %s", n.id, addr)
    log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(addr, nil))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Bootstrap a 3‑node cluster (run three processes)
// ---------------------------------------------------
func main() {
    // In a real deployment each process would read its ID from env/flags.
    // For demo we just start node 1 and treat it as the leader.
    peers := []raft.Peer{{ID: 1}, {ID: 2}, {ID: 3}}
    n := newNode(1, peers)
    n.serve(":8080")
}
```

**How it satisfies the constraints**

| Requirement | How the code meets it |
|-------------|-----------------------|
| **Read‑Your‑Writes** | `Set` proposes a command to Raft; the call returns **only after** the entry is committed.  The same node’s `kv` map is updated before the HTTP response is sent, so any subsequent `Get` (even from a different client) sees the write. |
| **Across replicas** | If you start three processes (IDs 1‑3) with the same code, Raft replicates the log to all nodes.  Any node that becomes leader will enforce the same ordering, guaranteeing RYW for all clients. |
| **No client‑side session/version metadata** | The HTTP API is just `/set` and `/get`.  The client never sends a token, timestamp, or version.  All causality is stored **inside Raft** (term, index). |
| **Simple cache semantics** | The in‑memory map is a classic key‑value cache; you can add eviction, TTL, etc., on top of the same Raft‑backed state. |

*Note*: The example omits many production concerns (snapshotting, log compaction, network partitions, leader election handling, back‑pressure).  For a real system you would either:

* Use an existing Raft‑based KV store (e.g., **etcd**, **Consul**, **CockroachDB**) and wrap it with a cache layer, **or**
* Adopt a **distributed cache product** that already offers linearizable reads (e.g., **Redis Enterprise**, **Aerospike**, **ScyllaDB**) and simply expose its native API.

---

## 6. Bottom Line

- **Impossible** to guarantee RYW across *any* replica **without any form of metadata** (session token, version, lease, or a single leader that serialises everything).  
- The **practical answer** is to **add hidden metadata** (Raft log index, lease, quorum) *inside the system* while keeping the **public API clean**.  
- Choose the pattern that matches your latency, availability, and operational budget:
  * **Raft / primary‑backup** → linearizable, simple API.  
  * **Quorum reads/writes** → higher latency but fully distributed.  
  * **Server‑side lease** → fast reads, modest complexity.

If you can relax the “no metadata” rule even a little (e.g., a short‑lived lease ID hidden in a cookie), you’ll get a far more robust solution with far fewer edge‑case bugs.  Otherwise you’re limited to **eventual consistency** or a **single‑node cache**.

Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run Java implementation** of the **Kundu‑Rahman constant‑time Dijkstra** (often called *Dial’s bucket algorithm*).  
The idea is simple:

* All edge weights are **non‑negative integers** and bounded by a small constant `C`.  
* Instead of a binary heap ( `O(log V)` ) we keep an array of **buckets** `B[0 … C·V]`.  
* Bucket `i` stores all vertices whose current tentative distance is exactly `i`.  
* Because we can jump from bucket to bucket in **O(1)** time, each vertex is extracted and each edge is relaxed in constant time, giving an overall **O(V + E + C·V)** bound.  
* When `C` is a small constant (or when the maximum distance `C·V` is not huge) the algorithm behaves like a *constant‑time* Dijkstra.

> **Important** – The algorithm works only for **non‑negative integer weights** that are bounded by a known constant `C`.  
> For arbitrary real weights you must fall back to a classic heap‑based Dijkstra (or a Fibonacci‑heap version).

---

## 1.  Data structures

```java
/**
 * Simple adjacency‑list representation.
 * Each edge stores the destination vertex and the integer weight.
 */
static class Edge {
    final int to;      // target vertex
    final int weight;  // non‑negative integer weight

    Edge(int to, int weight) {
        this.to = to;
        this.weight = weight;
    }
}

/**
 * Graph stored as an array of adjacency lists.
 */
static class Graph {
    final int V;                     // number of vertices (0 … V‑1)
    final List<Edge>[] adj;          // adjacency list

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    Graph(int V) {
        this.V = V;
        adj = new List[V];
        for (int i = 0; i < V; ++i) adj[i] = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    void addEdge(int from, int to, int weight) {
        if (weight < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative weight");
        adj[from].add(new Edge(to, weight));
    }
}
```

### Bucket priority queue

```java
/**
 * Bucket priority queue for integer distances.
 * The maximum distance we ever need to store is `maxDist = C * V`.
 * The queue holds vertices in linked‑list buckets; each bucket is a
 * simple `IntArrayDeque` (head/tail indices) for O(1) insertion & removal.
 */
static final class BucketQueue {
    /** each bucket is a deque of vertex ids */
    private final IntArrayDeque[] buckets;
    /** current index of the smallest non‑empty bucket */
    private int curIdx;
    /** number of vertices still inside the queue */
    private int size;

    BucketQueue(int maxDist) {
        buckets = new IntArrayDeque[maxDist + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= maxDist; ++i) buckets[i] = new IntArrayDeque();
        curIdx = 0;
        size = 0;
    }

    /** insert vertex v with distance d (0 ≤ d ≤ maxDist) */
    void add(int v, int d) {
        buckets[d].addLast(v);
        size++;
        if (d < curIdx) curIdx = d;          // keep the cursor as low as possible
    }

    /** decrease‑key: we simply add the vertex again to the new bucket.
        The old entry will be ignored later because we keep a `dist[]` array
        (see the main algorithm). */
    void decreaseKey(int v, int oldDist, int newDist) {
        // No need to physically remove from old bucket – O(1) amortised.
        add(v, newDist);
    }

    /** return the vertex with the smallest distance, or -1 if empty */
    int poll() {
        while (curIdx < buckets.length && buckets[curIdx].isEmpty()) curIdx++;
        if (curIdx >= buckets.length) return -1;   // empty
        int v = buckets[curIdx].removeFirst();
        size--;
        return v;
    }

    boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }
}
```

`IntArrayDeque` is a tiny, fast integer deque that avoids boxing.  
(You can replace it with `ArrayDeque<Integer>` if you prefer readability.)

```java
/** Minimal integer deque – no bounds checking for speed */
static final class IntArrayDeque {
    private int[] data = new int[8];
    private int head = 0, tail = 0;   // [head, tail)

    void addLast(int x) {
        if (tail == data.length) grow();
        data[tail++] = x;
    }

    int removeFirst() {
        return data[head++];
    }

    boolean isEmpty() {
        return head == tail;
    }

    private void grow() {
        int newCap = data.length * 2;
        int[] nd = new int[newCap];
        System.arraycopy(data, head, nd, 0, tail - head);
        tail -= head;
        head = 0;
        data = nd;
    }
}
```

