## 2.  The Kundu‑Rahman (Dial) algorithm

```java
/**
 * Returns the array `dist` where dist[v] = shortest distance from source to v.
 * If a vertex is unreachable, its distance stays as Integer.MAX_VALUE.
 *
 * @param g          the weighted directed graph
 * @param source     source vertex (0 … V‑1)
 * @param maxWeight  the known upper bound C on any edge weight
 * @return           distance array
 */
static int[] kunduRahmanDijkstra(Graph g, int source, int maxWeight) {
    final int V = g.V;
    final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

    // 1️⃣  distance array
    int[] dist = new int[V];
    Arrays.fill(dist, INF);
    dist[source] = 0;

    // 2️⃣  bucket queue – the farthest distance we could ever need is C * V
    int maxDist = maxWeight * V;               // safe upper bound
    BucketQueue pq = new BucketQueue(maxDist);
    pq.add(source, 0);

    // 3️⃣  main loop
    while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
        int u = pq.poll();                     // vertex with smallest tentative distance
        int du = dist[u];                      // the *current* distance of u

        // Because we never physically delete the old entry when we do a decrease‑key,
        // we may pull a stale vertex out of the queue.  Skip it.
        if (du == INF) continue;               // should never happen, but safe
        if (du != pq.curIdx) {                 // stale entry – its bucket index no longer matches
            // The vertex will be processed later when its true distance bucket is reached.
            continue;
        }

        // 4️⃣  relax all outgoing edges of u
        for (Edge e : g.adj[u]) {
            int v = e.to;
            int w = e.weight;
            int nd = du + w;                    // new candidate distance

            if (nd < dist[v]) {                 // improvement found
                int oldDist = dist[v];
                dist[v] = nd;
                if (oldDist == INF) {
                    // first time we see v → just insert
                    pq.add(v, nd);
                } else {
                    // we already have v somewhere in a bucket → decrease‑key
                    pq.decreaseKey(v, oldDist, nd);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return dist;
}
```

### Why the “stale‑entry” test works

*When we call `decreaseKey` we **do not** remove the old copy of the vertex from its previous bucket.*  
That keeps the operation O(1).  
When the old copy later surfaces at the front of the queue we compare its stored distance (`du`) with the current `dist[u]`. If they differ, the entry is stale and we simply ignore it. The true (smaller) distance will be processed when the bucket that actually contains the vertex is reached.

