## 5. Why we **cannot** prove termination for *all* integers

The statement

> “For every positive integer `n`, the Collatz iteration eventually reaches 1”

is exactly the **Collatz conjecture** (also known as the 3 n + 1 problem). Despite massive computational verification (the conjecture has been checked for all starting values up to at least 2⁶⁸ ≈ 2.9 × 10¹⁹ and far beyond using distributed projects), a general mathematical proof remains elusive. The difficulty stems from the fact that the map mixes linear growth (`3n+1`) with division by two, creating a highly irregular trajectory that resists standard inductive or analytic techniques.

A few high‑level observations that are *known*:

| Observation | Reason |
|-------------|--------|
| **Parity cycles** | If a number ever becomes a power of two, it will descend to 1 in a finite number of halving steps. |
| **Total stopping time** | For many numbers the “total stopping time” (steps needed to reach 1) grows roughly like `log n`, but there are outliers with much larger stopping times. |
| **No known counterexample** | Exhaustive computer searches have found none up to astronomically large bounds. |
| **Partial results** | Certain infinite families (e.g., numbers of the form `2^k`, `2^k - 1`, etc.) are known to converge, and many analytic bounds on the “average” behavior have been proved. |

Nevertheless, **no one has yet shown that every possible integer must eventually fall into one of those convergent families**. Consequently, any claim of a complete proof would be a breakthrough of historic magnitude and would have to be vetted by the mathematical community.

