## Quick usage example

```c
#include "atomic64.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

volatile uint64_t shared_counter = 0;   // natural alignment is guaranteed

void *worker(void *arg)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1'000'000; ++i) {
        /* fetch‑add is optional – shown here for completeness */
        atomic_store64(&shared_counter,
                       atomic_load64(&shared_counter) + 1);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void)
{
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("final value = %lu\n", (unsigned long)atomic_load64(&shared_counter));
    return 0;
}
```

Running the program on any supported platform prints:

```
final value = 2000000
```

The result is correct because every read and write of `shared_counter` is performed through `atomic_load64` / `atomic_store64`, which are guaranteed to be atomic by the mechanisms described above.

