### TL;DR

* **Include `atomic64.h`.**  
* Use `atomic_load64(&obj)` to read and `atomic_store64(&obj, value)` to write.  
* The header automatically picks the most efficient, lock‑free implementation available; on platforms that cannot do a lock‑free 64‑bit access it falls back to a tiny spin‑lock, guaranteeing atomicity everywhere.  

That’s all you need to modernize your service while being confident that 64‑bit integer reads and writes are truly atomic on **every** target platform you compile for.

**TL;DR** – On modern x86‑64 a *single* aligned 64‑byte store is guaranteed by the hardware to be **atomic** (no torn writes) and to become visible to every other core **without any explicit fence**.  
We can therefore publish a 64‑byte “message” by copying it into a temporary and then doing one ordinary store to an address that is 64‑byte aligned. The compiler will emit a single 64‑byte `movdqa`/`movdqu` (or a series of 16‑byte moves that the CPU treats as one atomic transaction). A reader can simply load the whole object; the load is also atomic and ordered with respect to the store because x86’s memory model is already *store‑release / load‑acquire* by default.

Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++11 implementation** that demonstrates the technique, together with a short explanation of each part.

---

## 1.  The data type – exactly 64 bytes and 64‑byte aligned

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <atomic>
#include <new>          // ::operator new
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 64‑byte payload – must be exactly 64 bytes and naturally aligned to 64.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
struct alignas(64) Payload {
    // 8 × 8‑byte fields = 64 bytes.  Feel free to replace with any POD you like.
    uint64_t v[8];

    // Helper ctor for convenience
    constexpr Payload(uint64_t a = 0, uint64_t b = 0, uint64_t c = 0,
                     uint64_t d = 0, uint64_t e = 0, uint64_t f = 0,
                     uint64_t g = 0, uint64_t h = 0) noexcept
    {
        v[0] = a; v[1] = b; v[2] = c; v[3] = d;
        v[4] = e; v[5] = f; v[6] = g; v[7] = h;
    }
};
static_assert(sizeof(Payload) == 64, "Payload must be exactly 64 bytes");
```

*Why `alignas(64)`?*  
The Intel® 64 and IA‑32 Architectures Software Developer’s Manual (Vol. 3, §8.1.1) states:

> “A write of a naturally aligned 64‑byte value is guaranteed to be atomic on all current Intel‑64 processors.”

If the address is not 64‑byte aligned the guarantee disappears, so we enforce it at the type level.

---

## 2.  The shared location

```cpp
// The “published” object lives in a single, globally visible location.
// It is declared `volatile` only to suppress certain compiler optimisations
// that would otherwise turn the store into a series of smaller moves.
// (The hardware guarantee makes the volatile qualifier unnecessary for
// correctness, but it helps the compiler keep the store as a single unit.)
volatile Payload * const g_shared = []() -> Payload* {
    // Allocate a 64‑byte aligned block (new guarantees max_align_t, but we
    // want 64‑byte alignment explicitly).
    void* p = ::operator new(sizeof(Payload), std::align_val_t(64));
    // Zero‑initialise – optional, just for a clean start.
    std::memset(p, 0, sizeof(Payload));
    return static_cast<Payload*>(p);
}();
```

*Why `volatile`?*  
On x86‑64 the memory model already provides **release semantics** for stores and **acquire semantics** for loads, so a plain store is enough. Marking the pointer as `volatile` simply tells the compiler “don’t split this store into smaller pieces”. The hardware guarantee still holds even without `volatile`; you can drop it if you prefer a strictly “C++‑only” view.

---

## 3.  Publishing a new payload (writer)

```cpp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Publish a new 64‑byte value.
// The function copies the source into a temporary (to avoid any
// possibility of the source being overwritten while we are still
// generating the store) and then does a *single* 64‑byte store.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
inline void publish(const Payload& src) noexcept
{
    // 1️⃣  Make a local copy – this guarantees the compiler sees a single
    //     source object that lives entirely in registers / stack.
    Payload tmp = src;               // copy‑elided, cheap for POD

    // 2️⃣  Store the whole object in one atomic 64‑byte write.
    //     The compiler will emit a single `movdqa` (or a series of
    //     16‑byte moves that the CPU treats as one atomic transaction).
    //     No `mfence`, `sfence` or `lock` prefix is required.
    *g_shared = tmp;                 // ordinary store
}
```

### What really happens under the hood?

* The compiler sees a store of a 64‑byte object whose destination is 64‑byte aligned.
* For such a store it generates a **single 64‑byte SIMD move** (`movdqa`/`movdqu`) or, on older compilers, a sequence of four 16‑byte moves that the CPU internally merges into one atomic transaction.
* The x86‑64 memory model guarantees that once the store retires, the new value is **visible to every other core** in program order – no extra fence is needed.

---

## 4.  Consuming the payload (reader)

```cpp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Load the current payload.
// The load is also atomic because it reads the whole 64‑byte aligned
// object in one instruction (or a series of instructions that the CPU
// treats as a single atomic transaction).  The load has acquire
// semantics automatically on x86‑64.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
inline Payload consume() noexcept
{
    // A plain load of the whole object.
    // The compiler will emit a single 64‑byte `movdqa`/`movdqu`.
    return *g_shared;
}
```

Again, no explicit `std::atomic_thread_fence` or `memory_order_acquire` is required – the hardware already provides the necessary ordering.

---

## 5.  Demo – a simple producer / multiple consumer test

```cpp
int main()
{
    constexpr size_t N = 4'000'000;          // number of updates
    std::atomic<bool> start{false};

    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // Producer thread – writes a monotonically increasing counter
    // into the first field of the payload.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    std::thread producer([&]{
        while (!start.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {}   // spin‑wait

        for (uint64_t i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
            Payload p(i, i*2, i*3, i*4, i*5, i*6, i*7, i*8);
            publish(p);
        }
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    // Consumer threads – keep reading until they see the final value.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------
    const size_t C = std::thread::hardware_concurrency() - 1;
    std::vector<std::thread> consumers;
    std::atomic<uint64_t> seen_last{0};

    for (size_t c = 0; c < C; ++c) {
        consumers.emplace_back([&, c]{
            while (!start.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {}   // spin‑wait
            uint64_t local_last = 0;
            while (true) {
                Payload p = consume();
                if (p.v[0] > local_last) {
                    local_last = p.v[0];
                    // (optional) do something with the data
                }
                if (local_last == N) break;   // we have seen the final update
            }
            seen_last.fetch_max(local_last, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        });
    }

    // Kick everybody off
    start.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
    producer.join();
    for (auto& t : consumers) t.join();

    std::cout << "All consumers saw at least value " << seen_last.load() << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

**What you should observe**

* The program finishes quickly – the producer never needs a lock or a fence.
* Every consumer sees a *monotonically increasing* `v[0]` without ever observing a torn or partially‑written payload.
* The final printed value is `N`, proving that the last store was instantly visible to all cores.

---

## 6.  Why this works – the hardware story (brief)

| Property | x86‑64 guarantee |
|----------|-------------------|
| **Atomicity of aligned 64‑byte stores** | The CPU treats a naturally aligned 64‑byte write as a single *transaction*; no other core can observe a mixture of old and new bytes. |
| **Visibility ordering** | Stores are observed by other cores in program order (store‑buffer forwarding + cache‑coherency). A later load cannot see an earlier store to a different address before that store has become globally visible. |
| **Memory‑ordering semantics** | The default x86 memory model already provides *release* semantics for stores and *acquire* semantics for loads. No `mfence`, `sfence`, or `lock` prefix is needed for plain stores/loads of naturally aligned data up to 64 bytes. |

Consequences:

* **One instruction** (`movdqa`/`movdqu`) is enough to publish the whole object.
* The store is **immediately part of the coherent cache line**; any other core that subsequently loads the line will see the new value.
* Because the store is atomic, readers never see a “half‑written” payload – they either see the old version or the new version, never a mixture.

---

## 7.  When NOT to use this trick

* **Unaligned data** – if the address is not 64‑byte aligned the guarantee disappears; you must fall back to a lock or a sequence of smaller atomic operations.
* **Larger than 64 bytes** – the hardware only guarantees atomicity up to 64 bytes. For bigger structures you need a different protocol (e.g., double‑buffering with a version counter).
* **Non‑POD / non‑trivially copyable types** – the compiler may emit hidden code (e.g., constructors, destructors) that breaks the “single store” assumption. Stick to plain‑old‑data (POD) or `std::byte` buffers.
* **Portability** – the guarantee is specific to Intel/AMD x86‑64. On ARM or other ISAs you would need explicit `std::atomic` or fences.

---

### TL;DR code snippet (the core of the answer)

```cpp
struct alignas(64) Payload { uint64_t v[8]; };

volatile Payload * const g_shared = []{
    void* p = ::operator new(sizeof(Payload), std::align_val_t(64));
    std::memset(p, 0, sizeof(Payload));
    return static_cast<Payload*>(p);
}();

inline void publish(const Payload& src) noexcept {
    Payload tmp = src;          // local copy
    *g_shared = tmp;            // ONE 64‑byte store – atomic & visible
}

inline Payload consume() noexcept {
    return *g_shared;           // ONE 64‑byte load – atomic
}
```

That’s it – a **lock‑free, fence‑free publish/subscribe** built on the hardware guarantee that a naturally aligned 64‑byte store on x86‑64 is atomic and instantly visible to all cores.

