## 3️⃣  What if you *cannot* change the `execute` call?

If you truly cannot touch the line that sends the query to the DB (e.g. a legacy library that only accepts a raw string), you can still rely on **automatic escaping**:

```js
// Very simple escape function (compatible with MySQL)
function sqlEscape(value) {
  if (value == null) return 'NULL';
  if (typeof value === 'number') return value;
  return `'${value
    .toString()
    .replace(/[\0\n\r\\'"\x1a]/g, (s) => {
      switch (s) {
        case '\0':   return '\\0';
        case '\n':   return '\\n';
        case '\r':   return '\\r';
        case '\\':   return '\\\\';
        case "'":    return "\\'";
        case '"':    return '\\"';
        case '\x1a': return '\\Z';
      }
    })}'`;
}

// Middleware that *mutates* the request values in‑place
app.use((req, _res, next) => {
  const escapeAll = (obj) => {
    if (obj == null) return obj;
    if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(escapeAll);
    if (typeof obj === 'object') {
      for (const k of Object.keys(obj)) obj[k] = escapeAll(obj[k]);
      return obj;
    }
    // Primitive → escaped literal
    return sqlEscape(obj);
  };
  req.body   = escapeAll(req.body);
  req.query  = escapeAll(req.query);
  req.params = escapeAll(req.params);
  next();
});
```

Now any legacy code that does something like:

```js
const sql = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '${req.body.username}'`;
connection.query(sql);   // <-- raw string, no placeholders
```

will receive an **already‑escaped** literal, so the injected payload becomes harmless text inside the query.

> **Caveat** – Escaping works for most common DB engines, but it is **error‑prone** (different DBs have different quoting rules, binary data, Unicode, etc.). The placeholder‑binding approach shown in the first part is far safer.

