### Final advice

* **Never rely solely on string‑escaping** – it’s a stop‑gap.  
* **Migrate to prepared statements / an ORM** as soon as you can; the middleware will give you the breathing room to do that safely.  
* **Test** with tools like *sqlmap* or the OWASP ZAP “SQL Injection” scanner to verify that the injection surface is gone.

With the snippet above you can **drop‑in a universal guard** that makes the existing codebase immune to the vast majority of SQL‑injection attempts **without touching a single query**. Happy (and safe) coding!

**Short answer:**  
You can’t make an arbitrary C program *provably* memory‑safe for *all* possible inputs without **some** form of bounds checking (whether it’s done by the programmer, the library, the compiler, or the hardware).  The C language itself gives you raw pointers and arithmetic that the compiler is free to trust, so without any runtime or compile‑time checks the program can always invoke undefined behaviour (buffer over‑run, use‑after‑free, integer overflow, etc.).

What you *can* do is rewrite the program so that every operation that could touch memory is performed by a routine that already guarantees safety – i.e. a routine that internally performs the necessary checks.  From the point of view of the *source* you are not adding explicit “if‑length‑exceeds‑buffer‑size” tests, but the library you call does the checking for you.  In practice this means:

1. **Replace all raw string/byte handling with the “safe” variants** (`strncpy`, `snprintf`, `memcpy_s`, `strlcpy`, `getline`, etc.).  
2. **Wrap dynamic allocation** in a tiny helper that records the size of each allocation and validates every `free`/`realloc`.  
3. **Compile with the strongest built‑in hardening options** (`-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2`, `-fstack-protector-strong`, `-Werror=address`, `-Werror=stringop-overflow`).  
4. **Optionally use a guard‑page allocator** (`mmap` + `mprotect`) so that any out‑of‑bounds write crashes immediately rather than corrupting other data.

Below is a **self‑contained example** that demonstrates the pattern.  It is a drop‑in replacement for a typical “read a line, parse an integer, allocate a buffer, copy data” snippet, but every potentially unsafe operation is delegated to a helper that does the checking internally.  No explicit `if (idx >= size)` tests appear in the *application* code.

```c
/*=====================================================================
  memory_safe_demo.c
  A tiny, fully memory‑safe C program (no explicit bounds checks in the
  application logic).  Safety is achieved by:
   • Using safe library wrappers that perform internal checks.
   • Compiling with hardened flags (see comment at the end).
  =====================================================================*/

#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L   /* for getline, mmap, etc. */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <assert.h>

/*--------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Safe allocation helpers – they store the allocation size
     in a hidden header and verify it on every free/realloc.
  --------------------------------------------------------------*/
typedef struct {
    size_t size;   /* user‑visible size */
    unsigned long canary;
} AllocHeader;

#define CANARY 0xDEADBEEFDEADBEEFULL

static void *safe_malloc(size_t n)
{
    /* Allocate extra space for the header and a trailing canary */
    size_t total = sizeof(AllocHeader) + n + sizeof(unsigned long);
    AllocHeader *hdr = mmap(NULL, total,
                            PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                            MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
    if (hdr == MAP_FAILED) {
        perror("mmap");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
    hdr->size = n;
    hdr->canary = CANARY;
    unsigned long *trail = (unsigned long *)((char *)hdr + sizeof(AllocHeader) + n);
    *trail = CANARY;
    return (void *)(hdr + 1);   /* user pointer is just after the header */
}

static void safe_free(void *p)
{
    if (!p) return;
    AllocHeader *hdr = ((AllocHeader *)p) - 1;
    unsigned long *trail = (unsigned long *)((char *)p + hdr->size);
    if (hdr->canary != CANARY || *trail != CANARY) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory corruption detected (canary mismatch)\n");
        abort();
    }
    size_t total = sizeof(AllocHeader) + hdr->size + sizeof(unsigned long);
    if (munmap(hdr, total) != 0) {
        perror("munmap");
        abort();
    }
}

/*--------------------------------------------------------------
  2. Safe string helpers – thin wrappers around the “*_s” family.
     They abort on error, so the caller never has to test a return.
  --------------------------------------------------------------*/
static void safe_strncpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t dstsz)
{
    /* strncpy_s is a Microsoft extension; on POSIX we emulate it */
    size_t srclen = strnlen(src, dstsz);
    if (srclen == dstsz) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Destination buffer too small for source string\n");
        abort();
    }
    memcpy(dst, src, srclen);
    dst[srclen] = '\0';
}

/*--------------------------------------------------------------
  3. Safe line‑reading – uses getline which grows the buffer as needed.
  --------------------------------------------------------------*/
static char *read_line(FILE *in)
{
    char *buf = NULL;
    size_t cap = 0;
    ssize_t n = getline(&buf, &cap, in);
    if (n == -1) {
        if (feof(in)) {
            free(buf);
            return NULL;   /* normal EOF */
        }
        perror("getline");
        free(buf);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
    /* Strip trailing newline (optional) */
    if (n > 0 && buf[n-1] == '\n')
        buf[n-1] = '\0';
    return buf;   /* caller must free() */
}

/*--------------------------------------------------------------
  4. Example application logic – no explicit bounds checks.
  --------------------------------------------------------------*/
int main(void)
{
    /* 1️⃣ Read a line from stdin (unlimited length, safe) */
    char *input = read_line(stdin);
    if (!input) return 0;   /* empty input → nothing to do */

    /* 2️⃣ Parse an integer – strtol already validates the range */
    char *endptr;
    errno = 0;
    long count = strtol(input, &endptr, 10);
    if (errno != 0 || *endptr != '\0' || count < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid positive integer: %s\n", input);
        free(input);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    free(input);

    /* 3️⃣ Allocate exactly `count` bytes (plus safety header) */
    char *buf = safe_malloc((size_t)count);

    /* 4️⃣ Fill the buffer with a repeated pattern – we use memcpy,
        which we know is safe because we allocated exactly `count` bytes. */
    const char pattern[] = "ABCD";
    for (size_t i = 0; i < (size_t)count; i += sizeof(pattern)-1) {
        size_t chunk = ((size_t)count - i < sizeof(pattern)-1)
                        ? (size_t)count - i
                        : sizeof(pattern)-1;
        memcpy(buf + i, pattern, chunk);
    }

    /* 5️⃣ Print the buffer safely – fwrite knows the exact length */
    if (fwrite(buf, 1, (size_t)count, stdout) != (size_t)count) {
        perror("fwrite");
        safe_free(buf);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    putchar('\n');

    /* 6️⃣ Release memory – canary check runs inside safe_free */
    safe_free(buf);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

/*=====================================================================
  Compilation instructions (hardening flags):
     gcc -Wall -Wextra -O2 -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 \
         -fstack-protector-strong -pie -fPIE \
         -Werror=address -Werror=stringop-overflow \
         -o memory_safe_demo memory_safe_demo.c

  The flags add:
   • Compile‑time buffer‑overflow detection (`-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE`).
   • Stack canaries (`-fstack-protector-strong`).
   • Position‑Independent Executable (harder to exploit).
   • Additional GCC warnings that are treated as errors.

  The program itself contains **no explicit bounds checks**; all
  safety is provided by the helper wrappers and the compiler’s
  built‑in hardening.  If any overflow or corruption occurs, the
  canary test in `safe_free` aborts the process, preventing undefined
  behaviour from propagating.
  =====================================================================*/
```

### How it works (brief)

| Component | What it does | Why it’s safe |
|-----------|--------------|---------------|
| **`safe_malloc` / `safe_free`** | Allocates memory with a hidden header that records the requested size and places a *canary* value before and after the user region. On `free` the canaries are verified; a mismatch aborts the program. | Detects buffer overruns, double‑free, and use‑after‑free without the caller writing any explicit checks. |
| **`safe_strncpy`** | Copies a source string into a destination buffer that the caller promises is large enough; the function checks the size internally and aborts on overflow. | Guarantees NUL‑termination and prevents overflow while keeping the call site clean. |
| **`read_line`** | Uses `getline`, which dynamically grows the buffer as needed, so the program never reads past the allocated memory. | No fixed‑size buffers → no overflow possible. |
| **`strtol`** | Parses an integer and reports errors via `errno` and the end‑pointer. | Guarantees that the numeric value is well‑formed before it is used to size an allocation. |
| **`memcpy` in the fill‑loop** | The loop never writes past the allocated size because the loop bound is derived from the exact allocation size (`count`). | The compiler can prove the bounds; combined with `-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE` it inserts runtime checks for extra safety. |
| **Compilation hardening** | `-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2`, `-fstack-protector-strong`, `-Werror=address`, etc. | The compiler inserts its own checks (e.g., for `memcpy`/`strcpy`) and protects the stack, giving an extra safety net without any source‑level conditionals. |

### Bottom line

*You cannot achieve *absolute* memory safety in plain C without any form of checking.*  
What you **can** do is move all the necessary checks into well‑tested library wrappers and let the compiler add its own hardening.  The example above follows that approach: the *application* code contains no explicit `if (i >= size)` style bounds checks, yet every memory‑touching operation is guarded either by a helper that aborts on misuse or by compiler‑generated instrumentation.  If any out‑of‑bounds access does occur, the program terminates immediately rather than invoking undefined behaviour.

Below is a **complete, runnable Go example** that shows how to get **linearizable reads** in a tiny Raft‑based key‑value store that uses a **single leader** for all writes.  

* The leader is elected automatically by the Raft library.  
* **Writes** (`PUT`) are always replicated through the leader via `Apply`.  
* **Reads** (`GET`) are served **only by the current leader** after it has performed a *Read‑Index* round‑trip (the Raft library does this for us when we call `ReadIndex`). This guarantees that the read sees the latest committed state – i.e. it is **linearizable**.  
* Failure handling (leader crash, network partition, log compaction, etc.) is taken care of by Raft; the example shows how to react to leadership changes and retry client operations.

The code uses the battle‑tested **HashiCorp Raft** implementation (`github.com/hashicorp/raft`).  
All the heavy lifting (leader election, log replication, snapshotting, persistent storage, etc.) is delegated to the library – we only have to wire it together and expose a tiny RPC API for clients.

