## 7. How it works – a quick walkthrough

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Raft library** | Handles leader election, log replication, safety guarantees, snapshotting, and failure recovery. |
| **`Node.Put`** | Only the leader accepts a write. It marshals a `Command`, calls `Raft.Apply`, and blocks until the entry is **committed** (i.e. replicated on a majority). Because the client only returns after commit, the write is **linearizable**. |
| **`Node.Get`** | The leader first performs a **ReadIndex** round‑trip (`ra.ReadIndex()`). This forces the leader to confirm that its commit index reflects all prior writes. Only after that does it read from the FSM. If the node is not the leader, it returns an error containing the current leader’s address, so the client can retry. |
| **Failure handling** | If the leader crashes, Raft automatically elects a new one. The new leader will have all committed entries in its log, so subsequent reads/writes remain linearizable. The client code (see below) retries on “not leader” errors, following the `Leader()` address returned by Raft. |
| **Snapshotting** | The FSM implements `Snapshot`/`Restore`. The example uses a simple JSON snapshot stored on disk; this keeps the log from growing without bound. |

