Below is a **complete, runnable Go example** that implements a **total‑order broadcast** (also called *atomic broadcast*) on top of a Raft replicated log.  
The code uses the battle‑tested **HashiCorp Raft** library (`github.com/hashicorp/raft`) and a tiny HTTP front‑end that lets any client POST a message to *any* node. The node that receives the request forwards the command to the Raft leader, the command is appended to the Raft log, and once the entry is committed the leader (and all followers) invoke a user‑supplied `Apply` function that delivers the message to a local “delivery channel”. Because every Raft node applies log entries **in exactly the same order**, the delivery channel on every node sees the same total order – i.e. we have built a total‑order broadcast.

---

## 1. High‑level design

```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
|   Client (HTTP)   |  POST    |   Raft Node A     |  POST    |   Raft Node B     |
|  /broadcast       |--------->|  (maybe follower) |--------->|  (maybe follower)|
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
          ^                               |                               |
          |                               |  Raft replication (log)       |
          |                               v                               v
          |                     +-------------------+          +-------------------+
          |                     |   Raft Leader     |<-------->|   Raft Follower   |
          |                     +-------------------+          +-------------------+
          |                               |
          |   Apply(entry)                |   Apply(entry) (same order)
          |   (deliver to local chan)     |
          +-------------------------------+
```

* **Client API** – `POST /broadcast` with a JSON body `{"msg":"your text"}`.  
* **Leader redirection** – If the node that receives the request is not the leader it returns HTTP 307 with the leader’s address in the `Location` header. The client can retry automatically (most HTTP libraries do this).  
* **Raft command** – The leader creates a `raft.Log` entry whose `Data` field is the raw message bytes.  
* **Apply** – When a log entry becomes *committed* (majority of nodes have it) Raft calls the node’s `FSM.Apply` method. The implementation simply pushes the message onto a `chan string` that the rest of the program reads from. Because every node runs the same `Apply` on the same log entry, all nodes receive the messages **in the same order**.  
* **Failure handling** –  
  * **Leader election** is automatic; when the current leader crashes a new one is elected.  
  * **Client retries** – If a request hits a follower, the 307 redirect points to the new leader. If the leader crashes after the client has been redirected, the client will get a connection error; a simple exponential‑back‑off retry loop (shown in the README) will eventually succeed once a new leader appears.  
  * **Log compaction** – The example includes a very small snapshot implementation so the log does not grow forever.  
  * **Network partitions** – Raft guarantees safety: a minority partition can never commit entries, so no two nodes will ever diverge. When the partition heals the minority rejoins the leader’s log.

