## 5. Failure handling explained

| Failure scenario | How the code reacts | Guarantees |
|------------------|---------------------|------------|
| **Transient network error** (e.g. connection reset) | `PushState` retries with exponential back‑off (max 5 attempts). If still failing, the error is logged and the anti‑entropy ticker will try again on the next tick. | No lost updates – the state will eventually be delivered. |
| **Node crash / restart** | Counter state is persisted (optional `persist` hook). On restart the node reloads the last persisted state and resumes anti‑entropy. | No state loss across restarts. |
| **Permanent partition** (node unreachable for a long time) | Anti‑entropy continues to try; once the partition heals, the first successful push merges the full state. | **Eventual consistency** – once connectivity is restored, all replicas converge. |
| **Duplicate merge** (same state received multiple times) | `Merge` uses element‑wise `max`, which is **idempotent**. Duplicate merges are harmless. | No over‑counting. |
| **Clock skew / out‑of‑order delivery** | No timestamps are used; ordering is irrelevant because the merge rule is commutative. | Correctness independent of clocks. |
| **Partial state loss** (e.g. disk corruption) | The persistence hook can be swapped for a write‑ahead log or replicated storage (e.g. Raft, etcd). The library itself does not assume any particular durability mechanism. | You decide the durability level; the CRDT logic remains unchanged. |

