Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++17 example** that builds a 2‑D k‑d tree, inserts a set of points, and answers *nearest‑neighbour* (NN) queries in O(log N) average time.  
The code is deliberately written to be easy to read rather than ultra‑fast; you can later replace the raw recursion with an iterative stack, add balancing, or switch to a production‑ready library (e.g. **nanoflann**, **CGAL**, **FLANN**) if you need more features.

---

## 1.  What a 2‑D k‑d tree looks like

```
                depth = 0 (split on x)
                     ┌───────────────┐
                     │   (x0, y0)    │   <-- root
                     └───────┬───────┘
          split on y (depth = 1)   split on y (depth = 1)
          left subtree               right subtree
```

* At **even depths** we compare the **x‑coordinate**.  
* At **odd depths** we compare the **y‑coordinate**.  

The tree is a binary search tree in the chosen dimension, so each node stores:

```cpp
struct Node {
    Point pt;               // the point stored at this node
    std::unique_ptr<Node> left, right;
    // depth is not stored; it is passed down during recursion
};
```

---

## 2.  The point type

```cpp
struct Point {
    double x, y;                     // you can change to float/int if you like

    // convenience: squared Euclidean distance (no sqrt)
    double dist2(const Point& other) const {
        double dx = x - other.x;
        double dy = y - other.y;
        return dx*dx + dy*dy;
    }
};
```

---

## 3.  Building the tree (static construction)

If you already have all points, the fastest way is to **build the tree once** by recursively
splitting the *median* of the current point set.  
The median guarantees a balanced tree (≈ log N depth).

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

struct KDTree2D {
    struct Node {
        Point pt;
        std::unique_ptr<Node> left, right;
        Node(const Point& p) : pt(p) {}
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Node> root;          // owns the whole tree

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Public API ------------------------------------------------------
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    explicit KDTree2D(const std::vector<Point>& points) {
        std::vector<Point> pts = points; // copy because we will reorder
        root = buildRecursive(std::move(pts), 0);
    }

    // Returns the nearest point (by reference) and its squared distance.
    // If the tree is empty, the returned distance is +inf.
    std::pair<const Point*, double> nearest(const Point& query) const {
        const Point* best = nullptr;
        double bestDist2 = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
        nearestRecursive(root.get(), query, 0, best, bestDist2);
        return {best, bestDist2};
    }

private:
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Construction helpers --------------------------------------------
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Build a subtree from a vector of points, depth tells us which axis to split.
    static std::unique_ptr<Node> buildRecursive(std::vector<Point>&& pts, int depth) {
        if (pts.empty()) return nullptr;

        // Choose axis: 0 → x, 1 → y
        const int axis = depth % 2;

        // nth_element puts the median at position mid, all left < median, all right > median
        size_t mid = pts.size() / 2;
        auto comparator = [axis](const Point& a, const Point& b) {
            return (axis == 0) ? a.x < b.x : a.y < b.y;
        };
        std::nth_element(pts.begin(), pts.begin() + mid, pts.end(), comparator);

        // Create node from median
        std::unique_ptr<Node> node = std::make_unique<Node>(pts[mid]);

        // Build left/right sub‑vectors (move semantics, no extra copies)
        std::vector<Point> leftVec(pts.begin(), pts.begin() + mid);
        std::vector<Point> rightVec(pts.begin() + mid + 1, pts.end());

        node->left  = buildRecursive(std::move(leftVec), depth + 1);
        node->right = buildRecursive(std::move(rightVec), depth + 1);
        return node;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Query helpers ---------------------------------------------------
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    static void nearestRecursive(const Node* node,
                                 const Point& query,
                                 int depth,
                                 const Point*& best,
                                 double& bestDist2) {
        if (!node) return;

        // 1️⃣  Check distance to the point stored at this node
        double d2 = node->pt.dist2(query);
        if (d2 < bestDist2) {
            bestDist2 = d2;
            best = &node->pt;
        }

        // 2️⃣  Decide which side of the splitting plane we should explore first
        const int axis = depth % 2;
        double diff = (axis == 0) ? query.x - node->pt.x
                                  : query.y - node->pt.y;

        const Node* first  = diff < 0 ? node->left.get()  : node->right.get();
        const Node* second = diff < 0 ? node->right.get() : node->left.get();

        // 3️⃣  Recurse into the side that contains the query point
        nearestRecursive(first, query, depth + 1, best, bestDist2);

        // 4️⃣  If the hypersphere (circle) around the query point intersects the
        //     splitting line, we must also explore the other side.
        //     The distance from the query point to the line is |diff|.
        if (diff*diff < bestDist2) {
            nearestRecursive(second, query, depth + 1, best, bestDist2);
        }
    }
};
```

### How it works

| Step | What we do | Why it matters |
|------|------------|----------------|
| **Median split** (`nth_element`) | Guarantees roughly balanced tree → O(log N) depth. | Prevents worst‑case linear scans. |
| **Axis selection** (`depth % 2`) | Alternates between *x* and *y*. | Makes the tree a true 2‑D k‑d tree. |
| **Distance check** (`dist2`) | Uses squared distance → no costly `sqrt`. | Faster, and we can `sqrt` only once at the very end if needed. |
| **Pruning** (`diff*diff < bestDist2`) | If the current best sphere does not intersect the splitting line, we can skip the far subtree. | Gives the logarithmic average query time. |

