### TL;DR

*Copy `murmurhash3_128.h` into your project, include it, and call `murmur3::hash128(...)`.*
It gives you a fast, 128‑bit, avalanche‑good, low‑collision hash that satisfies the tech‑lead’s request. Happy coding!

Below is a **complete, self‑contained program** that shows the only two places where an
`std::atomic_thread_fence` is required when you want to synchronize a *non‑atomic* data
object with an *atomic* flag.  
The example is deliberately tiny – it compiles with any C++17 (or newer) compiler
and prints the same value on every run, proving that the fence really does the job.

---

## 1.  Why a fence is needed at all

```text
Thread A (producer)                Thread B (consumer)

data = 42;                         // 1. read flag
std::atomic_thread_fence(release); // 2. publish
ready.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed);
```

* `data` is **not** atomic.  
* `ready` *is* atomic, but we only use it as a *synchronisation flag* (no
  acquire/release semantics on the store itself).

If we only wrote `ready.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed)`, the compiler
or the CPU could reorder the store to `ready` **before** the store to `data`.
Thread B could therefore see `ready == true` while still seeing the old value
of `data`.  

The **release fence** placed **right before** the store to `ready` tells the
compiler/CPU: *“everything that happened before this point must become visible
to other threads before the store to `ready` becomes visible.”*

The symmetric **acquire fence** on the consumer side does the opposite:
*“once I have observed `ready == true`, I must see all writes that happened
before the release fence in the producer.”*

---

## 2.  Minimal Working Example (C++17)

```cpp
// atomic_fence_mwe.cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>
#include <cassert>

int main() {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Shared objects
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    int          data = 0;                 // non‑atomic payload
    std::atomic<bool> ready{false};       // atomic flag used for sync

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Producer thread
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    std::thread producer([&]{
        data = 42;                         // 1️⃣ write the payload

        // 2️⃣ Release fence – everything before the fence must become
        //    visible before the following store to `ready`.
        std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);

        ready.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed); // publish
    });

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Consumer thread
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    std::thread consumer([&]{
        // Spin‑wait until the flag becomes true.
        while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
            // (optional) pause / yield to avoid burning CPU
            std::this_thread::yield();
        }

        // 3️⃣ Acquire fence – after we have observed `ready == true`,
        //    we must see all writes that happened before the release fence.
        std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_acquire);

        // Now it is safe to read `data`.
        int observed = data;
        std::cout << "observed data = " << observed << '\n';
        assert(observed == 42); // will never fire if the fences are correct
    });

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Join & finish
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    producer.join();
    consumer.join();
    return 0;
}
```

### How to compile & run

```bash
# g++ (or clang++) –O2 is enough to see the reordering effect without fences
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -pthread atomic_fence_mwe.cpp -o atomic_fence_mwe
./atomic_fence_mwe
```

You should always see

```
observed data = 42
```

If you **remove either fence** (or replace them with `memory_order_seq_cst`
on the *store/load* instead of `relaxed`) the program may still appear to work,
but it is *undefined behaviour* – on a weak‑ordering CPU (e.g. ARM or PowerPC)
or with aggressive compiler optimisations you can get `observed data = 0`
or even a crash.

---

## 3.  Where exactly the fences belong

| Situation                               | Where to put the fence | Reason |
|----------------------------------------|------------------------|--------|
| **Publishing** a non‑atomic payload after you have written it | **Just before** the *store* that makes the payload visible (the flag) | Release fence guarantees that all prior writes become visible first |
| **Consuming** a payload after you have observed the flag | **Immediately after** the *load* that saw the flag | Acquire fence guarantees that all writes that happened before the producer’s release fence are now visible |
| **Full‑order (seq‑cst) barrier** between two unrelated atomic ops | `std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_seq_cst);` anywhere you need a global “stop‑the‑world” ordering point | Guarantees a single total order of all seq‑cst fences and seq‑cst atomic operations |

> **Tip:** If you *can* make the flag itself `std::memory_order_release` on the store
> and `std::memory_order_acquire` on the load, you *don’t* need the explicit fences.
> The fence version is useful when you want to keep the flag `relaxed` (e.g. for
> lock‑free algorithms that already embed acquire/release semantics elsewhere) or
> when you need a fence that orders *multiple* atomic operations at once.

---

## 4.  A tiny variant with `memory_order_seq_cst`

If you prefer a single‑line “global” barrier instead of the acquire/release pair,
just replace the two fences with one `seq_cst` fence **between** the store and the load:

```cpp
// Producer
data = 42;
ready.store(true, std::memory_order_seq_cst);   // seq‑cst store already acts as a release

// Consumer
while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_seq_cst)) {} // seq‑cst load already acts as an acquire
int observed = data;   // safe, because the seq‑cst load synchronizes with the seq‑cst store
```

The explicit fence version is still valuable when you want *relaxed* atomics
but need ordering for *non‑atomic* data.

---

## 5.  TL;DR – The recipe

```cpp
// Producer
payload = …;                     // non‑atomic write(s)
std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);
flag.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed);

// Consumer
while (!flag.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) { /* spin */ }
std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_acquire);
auto value = payload;            // now safe to read
```

That is the **minimal** pattern where an `atomic_thread_fence` actually matters.
Feel free to copy‑paste the full program above into your own project and adapt the
payload type, the flag, or the surrounding algorithm as needed. Happy threading!

**`fetch_add` in a nutshell**

`fetch_add` is an atomic operation provided by the C++ `std::atomic` (and similar primitives in other languages).  
When you call  

```cpp
int old = counter.fetch_add(delta, std::memory_order_seq_cst);
```

the following happens **atomically** (i.e., as a single, indivisible step that no other thread can interleave with):

1. **Read the current value** of `counter`.  
2. **Add** `delta` to that value and store the result back into `counter`.  
3. **Return** the value that was read *before* the addition (the “old” value).

Because the whole sequence is performed with hardware‑level atomic instructions (e.g., `LOCK XADD` on x86), there is no need for a mutex; concurrent threads can all call `fetch_add` on the same counter without race conditions.

**Key points**

| Aspect | What it means |
|--------|----------------|
| **Atomicity** | The read‑modify‑write is indivisible; other threads see either the old value or the new value, never a partially‑updated state. |
| **Return value** | The function returns the *previous* value, which is useful for “fetch‑and‑increment” patterns (e.g., generating unique IDs). |
| **Memory ordering** | The second argument (`std::memory_order_*`) controls visibility of the operation to other threads. The default `seq_cst` gives the strongest ordering guarantees. |
| **Performance** | Typically a single CPU instruction; much cheaper than a lock‑based approach, especially under low contention. |
| **Use cases** | Counters, reference counts, ticket locks, generating sequence numbers, any situation where you need to increment a shared integer safely. |

**Example**

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

std::atomic<int> counter{0};

void worker()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        // Atomically add 1 and get the previous value
        int prev = counter.fetch_add(1);
        // (optional) use prev, e.g., as a unique index
    }
}

int main()
{
    std::vector<std::thread> th;
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) th.emplace_back(worker);
    for (auto& t : th) t.join();

    std::cout << "Final counter value: " << counter.load() << '\n'; // should be 8000
}
```

Each thread increments the shared `counter` safely; after all threads finish, the final value equals the total number of increments performed. No locks are required, and the operation is guaranteed to be race‑free.

Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++ example** that shows the typical workflow you’ll use when you want to

1. **Copy data from the host (CPU) to the device (GPU)**  
2. **Launch a kernel on a non‑default CUDA stream**  
3. **Synchronise that stream** (so we know the kernel has finished)  
4. **Copy the results back to the host**

The example does a classic *vector‑add* (`C = A + B`).  It uses the **CUDA Runtime API**, which is the most common way to write CUDA code from C++.  The code is heavily commented so you can see exactly what each line does.

---

## 1️⃣  Full source file (`vector_add.cu`)

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  vector_add.cu
 *
 *  Demonstrates:
 *   • Host → Device copy (async)
 *   • Kernel launch on a user‑created stream
 *   • Stream synchronisation
 *   • Device → Host copy (async)
 *
 *  Build with:
 *      nvcc -O2 -arch=sm_70 vector_add.cu -o vector_add
 *
 *  Run:
 *      ./vector_add
 *
 *  Requires: CUDA Toolkit (>= 11.0) and a GPU that supports the
 *            compute capability you compile for (the example uses sm_70).
 ********************************************************************/

#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper macro for error checking (always a good habit!)
#define CUDA_CHECK(call)                                                       \
    do {                                                                        \
        cudaError_t err = (call);                                               \
        if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                               \
            fprintf(stderr,                                                   \
                    "CUDA error in %s:%d: %s\\n",                               \
                    __FILE__, __LINE__, cudaGetErrorString(err));              \
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                                 \
        }                                                                       \
    } while (0)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Simple kernel: C[i] = A[i] + B[i]
__global__ void vecAdd(const float* __restrict__ A,
                       const float* __restrict__ B,
                       float* __restrict__ C,
                       size_t N)
{
    size_t idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (idx < N) {
        C[idx] = A[idx] + B[idx];
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Problem size
    const size_t N = 1 << 24;               // ~16 million elements
    const size_t bytes = N * sizeof(float);

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Allocate host memory (pinned = page‑locked → async copies are fast)
    float *h_A, *h_B, *h_C;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMallocHost(&h_A, bytes));   // pinned host memory
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMallocHost(&h_B, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMallocHost(&h_C, bytes));

    // Initialise input vectors on the host
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        h_A[i] = static_cast<float>(i) * 0.5f;
        h_B[i] = static_cast<float>(i) * 2.0f;
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Allocate device memory
    float *d_A, *d_B, *d_C;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_A, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_B, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_C, bytes));

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Create a CUDA stream (non‑default)
    cudaStream_t stream;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamCreate(&stream));

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5. Asynchronously copy host → device on that stream
    //    (Because we used pinned memory, this copy can overlap with kernel exec)
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpyAsync(d_A, h_A, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpyAsync(d_B, h_B, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream));

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6. Launch kernel on the same stream
    const int threadsPerBlock = 256;
    const int blocksPerGrid   = static_cast<int>((N + threadsPerBlock - 1) / threadsPerBlock);
    vecAdd<<<blocksPerGrid, threadsPerBlock, 0, stream>>>(d_A, d_B, d_C, N);
    // Check launch errors immediately (they are asynchronous)
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 7. Asynchronously copy result back to host (still on the same stream)
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpyAsync(h_C, d_C, bytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost, stream));

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 8. **Synchronise the stream** – we now know that everything queued
    //    on this stream (copies + kernel) has finished.
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 9. Verify a few results (optional but useful while learning)
    bool ok = true;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        float expected = h_A[i] + h_B[i];
        if (fabs(h_C[i] - expected) > 1e-5f) {
            fprintf(stderr,
                    "Mismatch at %zu: got %f, expected %f\\n",
                    i, h_C[i], expected);
            ok = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    printf("%s\\n", ok ? "PASS – results are correct!" : "FAIL – results differ!");

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 10. Clean‑up
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(stream));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_A));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_B));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_C));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFreeHost(h_A));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFreeHost(h_B));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFreeHost(h_C));

    // Optional: reset the device (helps with profiling tools)
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaDeviceReset());

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 2️⃣  What the program does – step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1. Problem size** | Choose `N` and compute the byte size. | Determines how much memory we need. |
| **2. Host allocation** | `cudaMallocHost` → *pinned* memory. | Pinned memory allows **asynchronous** `cudaMemcpyAsync` without an extra staging buffer. |
| **3. Device allocation** | `cudaMalloc` → device buffers. | Needed for the kernel to read/write. |
| **4. Stream creation** | `cudaStreamCreate`. | A stream is a *queue* of work that executes in order, but **independently** of other streams. |
| **5. Host→Device copy** | `cudaMemcpyAsync(..., stream)`. | The copy is queued on the stream; it can overlap with kernel execution on the same or other streams. |
| **6. Kernel launch** | `vecAdd<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>`. | The kernel is also queued on the same stream, so it will start **after** the two copies finish. |
| **7. Device→Host copy** | `cudaMemcpyAsync(..., stream)`. | Queued after the kernel, so it will start only when the kernel is done. |
| **8. Stream synchronisation** | `cudaStreamSynchronize(stream)`. | Blocks the CPU until **all** work submitted to that stream (copies + kernel) completes. |
| **9. Verification** | Simple CPU check. | Confirms that the GPU did the right thing. |
| **10. Clean‑up** | Free memory, destroy stream, reset device. | Prevents leaks and leaves the driver in a clean state. |

---

## 3️⃣  Why use a **non‑default stream**?

* The default stream (`cudaStreamLegacy` / `0`) is *synchronising* with many other operations, which can unintentionally serialize work.
* By creating your own stream you gain **explicit control**:
  * You can overlap copies with kernel execution (if you have enough hardware concurrency).
  * You can launch multiple independent kernels on different streams for true concurrency.
  * You can later **synchronise only the work you care about** (`cudaStreamSynchronize`) instead of the whole device (`cudaDeviceSynchronize`).

---

## 4️⃣  A few practical tips for beginners

| Tip | Explanation |
|-----|-------------|
| **Check errors after every CUDA call** | The `CUDA_CHECK` macro above aborts on the first error, making debugging far easier. |
| **Prefer `cudaMemcpyAsync` + pinned memory** | If you use regular `malloc`/`new`, `cudaMemcpyAsync` will *fallback* to a synchronous copy, losing the overlap benefit. |
| **Choose a reasonable block size** | 128‑256 threads per block works well on most GPUs.  Use `cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize` if you want to be fancy. |
| **Use `cudaGetLastError` after kernel launch** | Kernel launches are asynchronous; this call forces the runtime to report launch‑time errors immediately. |
| **Remember to free everything** | `cudaFree`, `cudaFreeHost`, `cudaStreamDestroy`, and optionally `cudaDeviceReset`. |
| **Compile with `-lineinfo` for better profiling** | `nvcc -lineinfo …` lets tools like Nsight Systems map kernels back to source lines. |
| **If you only need a quick copy, `cudaMemcpy` is fine** | It blocks the host until the copy finishes and also implicitly synchronises the default stream. Use it for simple scripts or debugging. |

---

## 5️⃣  How to compile & run

```bash
# 1️⃣  Compile (adjust -arch to match your GPU, e.g. sm_80 for RTX 3080)
nvcc -O2 -arch=sm_70 vector_add.cu -o vector_add

# 2️⃣  Run
./vector_add
```

You should see:

```
PASS – results are correct!
```

If you run the program under a profiler (e.g., `nsight-systems` or `nvprof`), you’ll see three distinct operations on the same stream:

1. `Memcpy HtoD` (host → device)  
2. `vecAdd` kernel execution  
3. `Memcpy DtoH` (device → host)

The `cudaStreamSynchronize` call guarantees that the CPU only proceeds after **all three** have completed.

---

## 6️⃣  Extending the example

* **Multiple streams** – allocate two streams and split the vectors in half; copies and kernels can then truly overlap.
* **Unified Memory** (`cudaMallocManaged`) – eliminates explicit copies, but you still need `cudaStreamSynchronize` (or `cudaDeviceSynchronize`) before reading results.
* **Thrust / C++20 ranges** – higher‑level abstractions that hide the low‑level copy/launch boilerplate.
* **Error‑propagation with exceptions** – wrap the macro in a function that throws `std::runtime_error` for a more C++‑ish style.

---

### TL;DR

* Allocate **pinned host memory** and **device memory**.  
* Create a **CUDA stream** (`cudaStreamCreate`).  
* Use **`cudaMemcpyAsync`** to copy data to the device **on that stream**.  
* Launch your kernel **on the same stream** (`<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>`).  
* Copy results back with another **`cudaMemcpyAsync`** on the same stream.  
* Call **`cudaStreamSynchronize(stream)`** to wait until everything queued on that stream is finished.  
* Finally, copy the data out of host memory, verify, and clean up.

That’s the canonical pattern you’ll use over and over when you start writing real‑world CUDA C++ programs. Happy hacking! 🚀

Below are a few short, self‑contained examples that show how to create and use a **64‑bit atomic counter** in the most common systems‑programming languages.  
All of them rely on the language‑provided atomic primitives, which map to a single hardware instruction (e.g. `LOCK XADD` on x86‑64, `LDXR/STXR` on ARM) when the target platform supports lock‑free 64‑bit atomics.  The compiler inserts the required memory‑ordering fences, so you never need a separate mutex.

---

## C++ (C++11 +)

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

using Counter = std::atomic<std::uint64_t>;

int main() {
    Counter counter{0};                     // start at 0 (default‑initialized)

    // Increment the counter from many threads
    const int NTHREADS = 8;
    const int INCR_PER_THREAD = 1'000'000;
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;

    for (int i = 0; i < NTHREADS; ++i) {
        workers.emplace_back([&]{
            for (int j = 0; j < INCR_PER_THREAD; ++j) {
                counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
                // ^‑‑ lock‑free 64‑bit add; relaxed ordering is enough for a pure counter
            }
        });
    }

    for (auto& t : workers) t.join();

    std::cout << "Final count = " << counter.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) << '\n';
}
```

**How it works**

* `std::atomic<std::uint64_t>` guarantees that every read‑modify‑write operation (`fetch_add`, `store`, `load`, …) is performed atomically.
* On a platform that provides lock‑free 64‑bit atomics (most 64‑bit CPUs), the implementation compiles to a single instruction (`lock xadd` on x86‑64, `ldxr/stxr` on ARM).  
* `memory_order_relaxed` tells the compiler/runtime that we only care about atomicity, not about ordering with respect to other memory accesses – perfect for a simple counter.

---

## C (C11 `<stdatomic.h>`)

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

#define NTHREADS 8
#define INCR_PER_THREAD 1000000

_Atomic uint64_t counter = 0;   // global atomic counter

void* worker(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < INCR_PER_THREAD; ++i) {
        atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&counter, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t th[NTHREADS];
    for (int i = 0; i < NTHREADS; ++i)
        pthread_create(&th[i], NULL, worker, NULL);

    for (int i = 0; i < NTHREADS; ++i)
        pthread_join(th[i], NULL);

    printf("Final count = %lu\n", atomic_load_explicit(&counter, memory_order_relaxed));
    return 0;
}
```

*The same principles as the C++ version apply – `atomic_fetch_add_explicit` is a lock‑free 64‑bit add when the target CPU supports it.*

---

## Rust

```rust
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let counter = AtomicU64::new(0);
    const NTHREADS: usize = 8;
    const INCR_PER_THREAD: u64 = 1_000_000;

    let mut handles = Vec::with_capacity(NTHREADS);
    for _ in 0..NTHREADS {
        // Clone a reference to the same atomic value
        let c = &counter;
        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
            for _ in 0..INCR_PER_THREAD {
                c.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
            }
        }));
    }

    for h in handles {
        h.join().unwrap();
    }

    println!("Final count = {}", counter.load(Ordering::Relaxed));
}
```

*`AtomicU64` is guaranteed to be lock‑free on any platform where the hardware can do a 64‑bit atomic RMW. `Ordering::Relaxed` is sufficient for a pure counter.*

---

## Java (JDK 8+)

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class CounterDemo {
    private static final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);
    private static final int NTHREADS = 8;
    private static final long INCR_PER_THREAD = 1_000_000L;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Thread[] workers = new Thread[NTHREADS];
        for (int i = 0; i < NTHREADS; ++i) {
            workers[i] = new Thread(() -> {
                for (long j = 0; j < INCR_PER_THREAD; ++j) {
                    counter.incrementAndGet();   // atomic 64‑bit increment
                }
            });
            workers[i].start();
        }

        for (Thread t : workers) t.join();

        System.out.println("Final count = " + counter.get());
    }
}
```

*`AtomicLong` uses `Unsafe` intrinsics that compile down to a single `compareAndSwapLong` (CAS) loop on the JVM. The JVM guarantees atomicity even on 32‑bit JVMs.*

---

## Go

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"sync/atomic"
)

func main() {
	var counter uint64 // plain uint64, accessed only via atomic ops
	const (
		nThreads        = 8
		incrPerThread   = 1_000_000
	)

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	wg.Add(nThreads)

	for i := 0; i < nThreads; i++ {
		go func() {
			for j := 0; j < incrPerThread; j++ {
				atomic.AddUint64(&counter, 1) // atomic 64‑bit add
			}
			wg.Done()
		}()
	}
	wg.Wait()
	fmt.Printf("Final count = %d\n", atomic.LoadUint64(&counter))
}
```

*`atomic.AddUint64` is implemented with a lock‑free CPU instruction on all supported 64‑bit architectures; on 32‑bit platforms the runtime falls back to a CAS loop.*

---

### TL;DR – What makes it “atomic”?

| Step | What happens under the hood |
|------|-----------------------------|
| **Read‑modify‑write** (`fetch_add`, `incrementAndGet`, `AddUint64`, …) | The compiler emits a single hardware instruction that reads the current 64‑bit value, adds the delta, and writes the new value back **without ever exposing an intermediate state to other threads**. |
| **Memory ordering** (`relaxed`, `seq_cst`, …) | The CPU may insert a lightweight fence to guarantee the requested visibility guarantees. For a pure counter, `relaxed` is enough because we only need atomicity, not ordering with other data. |
| **Lock‑free guarantee** | `std::atomic<T>::is_lock_free()`, `AtomicU64::is_lock_free()`, `AtomicLong` on the JVM, etc., report `true` on platforms that support a single‑instruction 64‑bit RMW. If the platform cannot do it lock‑free, the library falls back to a spin‑lock or mutex – still correct, just a bit slower. |

All the snippets above give you a ready‑to‑use 64‑bit atomic counter that can be safely shared across threads without any explicit mutexes. Choose the language that matches your service and drop the code in; the rest of the modernization work can focus on the business logic rather than on low‑level synchronization.

Below is a **complete, self‑contained, header‑only C++17 implementation** of a HyperLogLog (HLL) sketch that lets you estimate the number of distinct elements in a data stream while using only a few kilobytes of memory.  
The code is deliberately written to be easy to read and to modify, yet it follows the original HLL paper (Flajolet et al., 2007) and the later “HyperLogLog++” refinements (Google, 2017) for small‑range and large‑range corrections.

---

## 1.  What HyperLogLog does (in a nutshell)

| Step | What we store | Why it works |
|------|---------------|--------------|
| **Hash** each incoming element to a 64‑bit uniformly random value `h`. | – | A good hash makes the bits look like independent coin flips. |
| **Split** the hash: the first `p` bits select a *register* `j` (0 … `m‑1`, where `m = 2^p`). | `m` registers, each holds a small integer. | The register index distributes elements uniformly over the registers. |
| **Count leading zeros** in the remaining `64‑p` bits, add 1 → `ρ`. | Store `max(ρ, current_register_value)`. | The probability of seeing a long run of zeros drops exponentially; the longest run observed gives an estimate of the cardinality. |
| **Combine** the registers with the harmonic mean formula → raw estimate `E`. | – | The harmonic mean of `2^{-register}` is an unbiased estimator of `1/|U|`. |
| **Apply corrections** (small‑range, large‑range, bias‑correction). | – | Removes systematic bias for very small or very large cardinalities. |

The whole sketch is just an array of `m = 2^p` bytes (or 4‑bit nibbles) – e.g. with `p = 14` you need only **16 KB** and can count up to billions of distinct items with a typical relative error of **≈ 1.04/√m ≈ 0.8 %**.

---

## 2.  Header‑only implementation (`hyperloglog.hpp`)

```cpp
// hyperloglog.hpp
#pragma once
#include <cstdint>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>
#include <limits>
#include <cstring>   // for std::memcpy
#include <functional> // std::hash
#include <stdexcept>

namespace hll {

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
 *  MurmurHash3 (64‑bit version) – public domain, fast, good avalanche.
 *  We use it because std::hash is implementation‑defined and may be
 *  32‑bit on some platforms.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
static inline uint64_t murmurhash3_64(uint64_t key, uint64_t seed = 0xadc83b19ULL)
{
    key ^= seed;
    key *= 0xff51afd7ed558ccdULL;
    key ^= key >> 33;
    key *= 0xc4ceb9fe1a85ec53ULL;
    key ^= key >> 33;
    return key;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
 *  HyperLogLog sketch
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
class HyperLogLog
{
public:
    /**
     * @param p   Precision parameter (4 ≤ p ≤ 18 recommended).
     *            Number of registers m = 2^p.
     *            Memory ≈ m bytes (or m/2 if you pack 4‑bit registers).
     *            Relative error ≈ 1.04 / sqrt(m).
     */
    explicit HyperLogLog(uint8_t p = 14) : p_(p)
    {
        if (p < 4 || p > 18)
            throw std::invalid_argument("p must be in [4,18] for reasonable memory/error");

        m_ = 1u << p_;                     // number of registers
        registers_.assign(m_, 0);          // each register holds a 8‑bit counter
        alphaMM_ = getAlphaMM(p_, m_);     // pre‑computed constant α·m²
    }

    /** Add an element to the sketch.  The element can be any type that
     *  is hashable by std::hash (or you can call addHash() directly).   */
    template <typename T>
    void add(const T& item)
    {
        uint64_t h = murmurhash3_64(std::hash<T>{}(item));
        addHash(h);
    }

    /** Directly add a 64‑bit hash value (useful if you already have a fast
     *  hash function). */
    void addHash(uint64_t hash)
    {
        // 1) register index = first p bits
        const uint32_t idx = static_cast<uint32_t>(hash >> (64 - p_));

        // 2) remaining bits: count leading zeros + 1
        const uint64_t w = (hash << p_) | (1ULL << (p_ - 1)); // shift out index bits
        const uint8_t rho = static_cast<uint8_t>(clz(w) + 1); // clz counts leading zeros

        // 3) keep the maximum observed rho for this register
        if (rho > registers_[idx])
            registers_[idx] = rho;
    }

    /** Return the current cardinality estimate. */
    double estimate() const
    {
        // ---- harmonic mean of 2^{-register} ----
        double Z = 0.0;
        for (uint8_t reg : registers_)
            Z += std::ldexp(1.0, -static_cast<int>(reg)); // 2^{-reg}
        Z = 1.0 / Z;

        double rawE = alphaMM_ * Z; // α·m²·Z

        // ---- small‑range correction (linear counting) ----
        if (rawE <= 5.0 * m_) {
            uint32_t V = static_cast<uint32_t>(std::count(registers_.begin(),
                                                          registers_.end(),
                                                          0));
            if (V != 0) {
                // Linear counting:  m * ln(m / V)
                return static_cast<double>(m_) * std::log(static_cast<double>(m_) / V);
            }
        }

        // ---- large‑range correction (to avoid overflow) ----
        const double two64 = std::ldexp(1.0, 64); // 2^64 as double
        if (rawE > (two64 / 30.0))
            return -two64 * std::log(1.0 - (rawE / two64));

        // ---- otherwise raw estimate is fine ----
        return rawE;
    }

    /** Merge another sketch into this one (useful for parallelism). */
    void merge(const HyperLogLog& other)
    {
        if (p_ != other.p_)
            throw std::invalid_argument("Cannot merge HLLs with different precisions");

        for (size_t i = 0; i < registers_.size(); ++i)
            registers_[i] = std::max(registers_[i], other.registers_[i]);
    }

    /** Reset the sketch to the empty state. */
    void clear()
    {
        std::fill(registers_.begin(), registers_.end(), 0);
    }

    /** Number of registers (m = 2^p). */
    uint32_t registers() const noexcept { return m_; }

    /** Precision parameter p. */
    uint8_t precision() const noexcept { return p_; }

private:
    uint8_t p_;               // precision (bits used for index)
    uint32_t m_;              // number of registers = 2^p
    double alphaMM_;          // α·m² constant
    std::vector<uint8_t> registers_; // each register stores a small integer

    /** Count leading zeros of a 64‑bit word (portable version). */
    static inline int clz(uint64_t x)
    {
#if defined(_MSC_VER)
        unsigned long idx;
        if (_BitScanReverse64(&idx, x))
            return 63 - static_cast<int>(idx);
        return 64;
#elif defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)
        return x ? __builtin_clzll(x) : 64;
#else
        // fallback (slow) – shift until zero
        int n = 0;
        while (x) { x >>= 1; ++n; }
        return 64 - n;
#endif
    }

    /** α·m² constant for the given precision (see Flajolet et al.). */
    static double getAlphaMM(uint8_t p, uint32_t m)
    {
        // Pre‑computed α values for small m, otherwise 0.7213/(1+1.079/m)
        double alpha;
        switch (m) {
            case 16:   alpha = 0.673;  break;
            case 32:   alpha = 0.697;  break;
            case 64:   alpha = 0.709;  break;
            default:   alpha = 0.7213 / (1.0 + 1.079 / m); break;
        }
        return alpha * m * m;
    }
};

} // namespace hll
```

### How the code maps to the algorithm

| Part of the code | Algorithm step |
|------------------|----------------|
| `murmurhash3_64` | Uniform 64‑bit hash of the input |
| `idx = hash >> (64-p)` | Register selection (`j`) |
| `w = hash << p` + `clz(w)+1` | Count leading zeros (`ρ`) |
| `registers_[idx] = max(old, rho)` | Keep the maximum per register |
| `Z = Σ 2^{-register}` → `rawE = α·m²·Z` | Harmonic‑mean estimator |
| Small‑range (`rawE ≤ 5·m`) → linear counting | Correct bias when many registers are still zero |
| Large‑range (`rawE > 2^64/30`) → `-2^64·ln(1‑E/2^64)` | Avoid overflow when cardinality ≈ 2^64 |
| `merge()` | Union of two sketches (element‑wise max) |

---

## 3.  Minimal example (main.cpp)

```cpp
// main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include "hyperloglog.hpp"

int main()
{
    // 1) Choose precision.  p = 14 → 16384 registers → ~16 KB, ~0.8 % error.
    hll::HyperLogLog sketch(14);

    // 2) Feed a stream of values.
    const std::size_t N = 10'000'000;          // true distinct count
    std::mt19937_64 rng(12345);
    std::uniform_int_distribution<uint64_t> dist(0, std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max());

    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        uint64_t x = dist(rng);   // generate a random 64‑bit value
        sketch.add(x);
    }

    // 3) Query the estimate.
    double est = sketch.estimate();
    std::cout << "True cardinality: " << N << '\n';
    std::cout << "HLL estimate    : " << static_cast<std::uint64_t>(est) << '\n';
    std::cout << "Relative error  : " << std::abs(est - N) / N * 100 << " %\n";

    // 4) Demonstrate merging (optional)
    hll::HyperLogLog sketch2(14);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        uint64_t x = dist(rng) ^ 0xdeadbeefULL; // a different stream
        sketch2.add(x);
    }
    sketch.merge(sketch2);
    std::cout << "After merging two independent streams of size " << N
              << ", estimate = " << static_cast<std::uint64_t>(sketch.estimate()) << '\n';
}
```

**Compile**

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O3 -march=native main.cpp -o hll_demo
```

**Sample output (your numbers will vary a little because of randomness)**

```
True cardinality: 10000000
HLL estimate    : 10012345
Relative error  : 0.12345 %
After merging two independent streams of size 10000000, estimate = 19987654
```

The relative error is well inside the theoretical bound `≈ 1.04/√m ≈ 0.81 %` for `p=14`.

---

## 4.  Tweaking the sketch

| Parameter | Effect | Typical values |
|-----------|--------|----------------|
| `p` (precision) | Memory = `2^p` bytes, error ≈ `1.04/√(2^p)` | `10` → 1 KB, ~3 % error; `14` → 16 KB, ~0.8 %; `16` → 64 KB, ~0.26 % |
| Register type | We store a full `uint8_t` (max 255). The theoretical max `ρ` for 64‑bit hash is 64, so a byte is more than enough. | No change needed. |
| Hash function | Must be uniform. MurmurHash3 is fast and 64‑bit. | Replace with `xxHash64`, `CityHash64`, or a hardware CRC if you prefer. |
| **Bias correction** | The original paper suggests a small bias table for `m ≤ 2^12`. The implementation above uses the simple linear‑counting correction, which is sufficient for most practical `p ≥ 12`. | For production‑grade libraries (e.g., Google’s `hllpp`), you can embed the bias table from the HLL++ paper. |

---

## 5.  Why HyperLogLog is “approximate” and when it’s OK

* **Probabilistic guarantee** – The estimate is unbiased (after the small‑range correction) and its *standard deviation* is bounded by `1.04/√m`.  
* **No false negatives** – The sketch never under‑estimates the true cardinality by more than the statistical error.  
* **No false positives** – It never reports a cardinality larger than the true value by more than the error bound.  

If you need **exact** counts you must keep a hash set (O(N) memory). For most analytics, monitoring, or cardinality‑aware algorithms (e.g., distinct‑user counting, approximate joins, cardinality‑aware query planning) the HLL error is negligible while the memory savings are massive.

---

## 6.  Extending the implementation

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|---------------|
| **Serialization / deserialization** | Dump `p_` and the `registers_` vector to a binary file (`std::ofstream.write`). Load with `std::ifstream.read`. |
| **Thread‑safe updates** | Wrap `add()` in a `std::atomic<uint8_t>` per register, or use a per‑thread local sketch and merge at the end (the latter is usually faster). |
| **Compressed registers (4‑bit)** | Store two counters per byte (`uint8_t`). Adjust `addHash` to mask the low‑nibble. Saves a factor of 2 in memory, at the cost of a few extra bit‑twiddles. |
| **HyperLogLog++ bias table** | Copy the bias table from the original HLL++ paper (or from the open‑source `google/hll` repo) and apply `E' = E - bias(m, E)`. |
| **Cardinality of a union without merging** | Use the inclusion‑exclusion formula `|A∪B| = |A| + |B| - |A∩B|`. The intersection can be approximated by `|A∩B| ≈ Σ 2^{-max(rA_i, rB_i)}` – see the HLL++ “set operations” section. |

---

## 7.  Quick checklist for your own project

1. **Pick `p`** based on the memory budget you have.  
   *Example*: 8 KB → `p = 13` (8192 registers) → error ≈ 1.15 %.  
2. **Choose a fast 64‑bit hash** (MurmurHash3 is already in the header).  
3. **Create a single global `HyperLogLog` object** (or a thread‑local one).  
4. **Call `add(item)`** for every element you see.  
5. **Periodically call `estimate()`** (e.g., every minute) to emit the approximate distinct count.  
6. **If you need to combine results from multiple machines**, serialize each sketch, ship it, and `merge()` them on the collector node.

---

## 8.  References (for deeper reading)

1. **Flajolet, P., Fusy, É., Gandouet, O., & Meunier, F.** (2007). *HyperLogLog: the analysis of a near-optimal cardinality estimation algorithm*. *AOFA*.  
2. **Google** (2017). *HyperLogLog++: Improved Cardinality Estimation for Big Data*. (PDF: <https://research.google/pubs/pub40671/>).  
3. **MurmurHash3** – Austin Appleby, public domain, <https://github.com/aappleby/smhasher>.  

---

### TL;DR

*Copy the header `hyperloglog.hpp` into your project, include it, instantiate `hll::HyperLogLog sketch(p)`, feed it with `sketch.add(value)`, and read the estimate with `sketch.estimate()`. With just a few kilobytes you’ll get a distinct‑count estimate whose relative error is bounded by `≈ 1.04/√(2^p)`.*

Happy coding, and enjoy the power of probabilistic data structures! 🚀

## Quick‑look summary
* **What you get:** a tiny, self‑contained C program that measures the elapsed time of any code block using `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, …)`.
* **Why `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`?** It is a **monotonic** clock – it always moves forward at a constant rate and is never affected by NTP adjustments, daylight‑saving changes, or manual `date` changes. Perfect for measuring durations.
* **How it works:**  
  1. Call `clock_gettime` **before** the code you want to time → `start`.  
  2. Call it **after** the code → `end`.  
  3. Compute the difference (`end – start`) in seconds (or nanoseconds).  
  4. The result is the elapsed wall‑clock time.

---

## Complete, ready‑to‑compile example

```c
/* monotonic_timer.c
 *
 * Demonstrates timing a code section with CLOCK_MONOTONIC via clock_gettime().
 *
 * Build:
 *   gcc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 monotonic_timer.c -o monotonic_timer
 *   # On very old glibc you may need to link -lrt:
 *   # gcc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 monotonic_timer.c -lrt -o monotonic_timer
 *
 * Run:
 *   ./monotonic_timer
 */

#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 199309L   /* needed for clock_gettime() prototype */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <unistd.h>   /* for usleep() – just a dummy workload */

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Helper: return a double representing seconds from a struct timespec
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
static double timespec_to_sec(const struct timespec *ts)
{
    return (double)ts->tv_sec + (double)ts->tv_nsec / 1e9;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Helper: compute (a - b) where both are struct timespec.
   Result is placed in *out (always non‑negative because we use a
   monotonic clock and call it in the correct order).
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
static void timespec_sub(const struct timespec *a,
                         const struct timespec *b,
                         struct timespec *out)
{
    out->tv_sec  = a->tv_sec  - b->tv_sec;
    out->tv_nsec = a->tv_nsec - b->tv_nsec;
    if (out->tv_nsec < 0) {
        out->tv_sec  -= 1;
        out->tv_nsec += 1000000000L;
    }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Dummy workload – replace this with the code you really want to
   measure.  Here we just sleep for ~0.123 seconds.
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
static void do_some_work(void)
{
    /* Simulate work that takes ~123 ms */
    usleep(123000);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   Main – measures the time taken by do_some_work().
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(void)
{
    struct timespec start, end, diff;

    /* 1️⃣ Grab the start time */
    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &start) != 0) {
        perror("clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC) – start");
        return 1;
    }

    /* 2️⃣ Code you want to time */
    do_some_work();

    /* 3️⃣ Grab the end time */
    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &end) != 0) {
        perror("clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC) – end");
        return 1;
    }

    /* 4️⃣ Compute the elapsed interval */
    timespec_sub(&end, &start, &diff);

    /* 5️⃣ Print it */
    printf("Elapsed time: %.9f seconds  (%ld sec, %ld nsec)\n",
           timespec_to_sec(&diff), diff.tv_sec, diff.tv_nsec);

    return 0;
}
```

### How to run it

```bash
$ gcc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 monotonic_timer.c -o monotonic_timer
$ ./monotonic_timer
Elapsed time: 0.123001234 seconds  (0 sec, 123001234 nsec)
```

(Your exact numbers will differ slightly because `usleep` is not perfectly precise.)

---

## Brief technical explanation

| Concept | What it means | Why it matters for timing |
|---------|---------------|---------------------------|
| **`clock_gettime`** | POSIX function that fills a `struct timespec` (`tv_sec`, `tv_nsec`) with the current value of a specified clock. | Gives you a high‑resolution timestamp (nanosecond granularity). |
| **`CLOCK_MONOTONIC`** | A clock that starts at an unspecified point (usually system boot) and **never jumps backwards**. It is not affected by adjustments to the system’s wall‑clock time. | Guarantees that `end – start` is always the true elapsed duration, even if the user changes the date or NTP slews the clock. |
| **Resolution** | Typically 1 ns to a few hundred nanoseconds, depending on hardware and kernel. | Sufficient for most performance measurements; if you need even finer granularity on x86 you could use `rdtsc`, but `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` is portable and safe. |
| **Overflow** | `struct timespec` uses a signed 64‑bit `tv_sec`. At 2³¹‑1 seconds (~68 years) you would overflow on a 32‑bit `time_t`, but modern systems use a 64‑bit `time_t`. | Practically never a problem for normal program runs. |
| **Linking** | On glibc 2.17+ the function lives in `libc`. Older glibc required `-lrt`. | Keep `-lrt` in your build script if you need to support very old Linux distributions. |

### Step‑by‑step flow

1. **Capture start** – `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &start)`.  
   The kernel reads the monotonic hardware timer (e.g., TSC, HPET, or a high‑resolution kernel tick) and returns the current count as seconds + nanoseconds.

2. **Run the code** – any function or block you want to profile.

3. **Capture end** – same call, filling `end`.

4. **Subtract** – `end – start` yields the elapsed interval. Because the clock is monotonic, `end` is guaranteed to be ≥ `start`.

5. **Convert** – either keep the `struct timespec` (seconds + nanoseconds) or turn it into a `double` for easy printing.

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Pitfall | Symptom | Fix |
|---------|---------|-----|
| Using `CLOCK_REALTIME` instead of `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` | Measured time jumps forward/backward when the system clock is adjusted. | Always use `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` (or `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` on Linux if you need *exact* hardware ticks). |
| Forgetting to handle the nanosecond borrow when `end.tv_nsec < start.tv_nsec`. | Negative `tv_nsec` → wrong elapsed time. | Use the `timespec_sub` helper above (or the standard `timespecsub` macro from `<sys/time.h>` on BSD). |
| Not linking with `-lrt` on old glibc. | “undefined reference to `clock_gettime`”. | Add `-lrt` to the link line for glibc < 2.17. |
| Measuring a *very* short interval (few nanoseconds) and seeing zero. | The resolution of the underlying clock is coarser than the measured interval. | Run the code many times in a loop and divide, or use `perf`/`rdtsc` for sub‑nanosecond work. |
| Using the result after the program has been suspended (e.g., `SIGSTOP`). | Elapsed time includes the suspend period (which may be undesirable). | If you need “CPU time” instead, use `CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID`. |

---

## TL;DR code snippet you can paste anywhere

```c
struct timespec t0, t1, dt;
clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &t0);   // start

/* ---- code you want to time ---- */
do_some_work();
/* ------------------------------ */

clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &t1);   // end

/* dt = t1 - t0 */
dt.tv_sec  = t1.tv_sec  - t0.tv_sec;
dt.tv_nsec = t1.tv_nsec - t0.tv_nsec;
if (dt.tv_nsec < 0) {
    dt.tv_sec  -= 1;
    dt.tv_nsec += 1000000000L;
}
double elapsed = dt.tv_sec + dt.tv_nsec / 1e9;
printf("Elapsed = %.9f s\n", elapsed);
```

