## Single‑Producer / Single‑Consumer Ring Buffer  
**Goal** – a lock‑free circular queue that can be used by one thread that only *produces* items and another thread that only *consumes* them.  
The only synchronization needed is a pair of atomic indices (`head` for the producer, `tail` for the consumer) together with the proper **release / acquire** ordering so that the consumer sees the data that the producer has already written.

Below is a compact, production‑ready implementation in modern C++ (C++20).  
It works for any trivially copyable type `T` and for a capacity that is a power of two (the mask makes the wrap‑around cheap).  

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  SPSC ring buffer (single‑producer / single‑consumer)
 *
 *  • lock‑free, wait‑free
 *  • uses only two atomics (head, tail) with release/acquire ordering
 *  • capacity must be a power of two (checked at compile time)
 *
 *  Example:
 *      SpscRingBuffer<int, 1024> q;
 *      if (q.try_push(42)) { … }
 *      int v;
 *      if (q.try_pop(v)) { … }
 ********************************************************************/

#pragma once
#include <atomic>
#include <cstddef>
#include <type_traits>
#include <cassert>

template <typename T, std::size_t Capacity>
class SpscRingBuffer {
    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0,
                  "Capacity must be a power of two");
    static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<T>,
                  "SpscRingBuffer works only with trivially copyable types");

public:
    constexpr SpscRingBuffer() noexcept
        : head_(0), tail_(0)
    {}

    /** @brief  Try to enqueue an element.
     *  @param  value  Element to store.
     *  @return true  if the element was stored, false if the buffer is full.
     *
     *  The producer writes the element into the slot *first*,
     *  then publishes the new head index with a **release** store.
     *  This guarantees that the consumer, which reads the head with an
     *  **acquire** load, will see the fully‑written element.
     */
    bool try_push(const T& value) noexcept {
        const std::size_t head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        const std::size_t next_head = inc(head);
        // If next_head == tail, the buffer is full.
        if (next_head == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
            return false;                     // full

        // Write the element into the slot that belongs to the current head.
        buffer_[head & mask_] = value;

        // Publish the new head – release ensures the write to buffer_ happens
        // before the consumer can see the new head value.
        head_.store(next_head, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    /** @brief  Try to dequeue an element.
     *  @param[out] out  Destination for the popped value.
     *  @return true  if an element was retrieved, false if the buffer is empty.
     *
     *  The consumer reads the tail index, checks that it differs from the
     *  current head (acquire), then reads the element and finally advances
     *  the tail with a **release** store.  The release on the tail is not
     *  strictly required for correctness, but it pairs nicely with the
     *  acquire on the producer side should the producer ever read the tail
     *  (e.g. for statistics).
     */
    bool try_pop(T& out) noexcept {
        const std::size_t tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        // If tail == head, the buffer is empty.
        if (tail == head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
            return false;                     // empty

        // Read the element before we move the tail.
        out = buffer_[tail & mask_];

        // Publish the new tail – release pairs with the producer's acquire.
        tail_.store(inc(tail), std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    /** @brief  Number of elements currently stored (non‑atomic, for debugging). */
    std::size_t size() const noexcept {
        std::size_t h = head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        std::size_t t = tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        return (h >= t) ? (h - t) : (Capacity - (t - h));
    }

    /** @brief  Capacity of the buffer (always one slot less than the raw array). */
    static constexpr std::size_t capacity() noexcept { return Capacity; }

private:
    // Helper: increment an index modulo Capacity.
    static constexpr std::size_t inc(std::size_t i) noexcept {
        return (i + 1) & mask_;
    }

    static constexpr std::size_t mask_ = Capacity - 1;
    alignas(64) std::atomic<std::size_t> head_;   // written by producer, read by consumer
    alignas(64) std::atomic<std::size_t> tail_;   // written by consumer, read by producer
    // Pad to avoid false sharing between head and tail.
    alignas(64) unsigned char pad_[64 - sizeof(std::atomic<std::size_t>) * 2] = {};

    // The actual storage – not atomic because only one thread ever accesses a
    // given slot at a time (producer writes, consumer reads after the head
    // index has been published).
    T buffer_[Capacity];
};
```

### How it works – the memory‑ordering story

| Step | Thread | Action | Memory order | Why it matters |
|------|--------|--------|--------------|----------------|
| 1    | Producer | Write `value` into `buffer_[head & mask]` | **none** (ordinary store) | No other thread can read this slot yet because the head index still points to the *old* value. |
| 2    | Producer | `head_.store(next_head, std::memory_order_release)` | **release** | Guarantees that all writes that happened **before** the store (the element write) become visible to any thread that later does an **acquire** load of `head_`. |
| 3    | Consumer | `head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)` | **acquire** | Synchronizes with the producer’s release. After this load sees the new `head`, the consumer is guaranteed to see the element that was stored in step 1. |
| 4    | Consumer | Read `buffer_[tail & mask]` | ordinary load | Safe because of the acquire‑release pair. |
| 5    | Consumer | `tail_.store(next_tail, std::memory_order_release)` | **release** (optional) | Completes the consumer side; if the producer ever reads `tail_` (e.g., for statistics) it can use an acquire load to see the updated value. |

Because there is exactly **one** producer and **one** consumer, there is no contention on the same atomic variable: the producer only writes `head_` and reads `tail_`; the consumer only writes `tail_` and reads `head_`. This eliminates the need for any compare‑and‑swap loops or additional fences.

### Why the buffer size is “capacity‑1”

The ring buffer uses the classic “full‑vs‑empty” distinction:

* **Empty** when `head == tail`.
* **Full**  when `inc(head) == tail`.

Thus one slot is always left unused; the usable capacity is `Capacity‑1`. This makes the full/empty test a single equality comparison and avoids the need for a separate counter.

### Performance notes

* **Cache‑line padding** (`alignas(64)`) separates `head_` and `tail_` so that the producer and consumer do not cause false sharing on the same cache line.
* The mask (`Capacity‑1`) makes the wrap‑around a cheap bit‑wise `&` instead of a modulo operation.
* All operations are *wait‑free*: each `try_push` / `try_pop` does a bounded amount of work and never blocks.

### Usage example

```cpp
#include "spsc_ring_buffer.hpp"
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    SpscRingBuffer<int, 1024> q;

    std::thread producer([&]{
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
            while (!q.try_push(i)) { /* spin until space */ }
        }
    });

    std::thread consumer([&]{
        int value;
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
            while (!q.try_pop(value)) { /* spin until data */ }
            std::cout << value << '\n';
        }
    });

    producer.join();
    consumer.join();
}
```

The producer can push as fast as the consumer can pop; when the queue is full the producer simply retries, and when it is empty the consumer retries. No locks, no condition variables—just the two atomics with the correct release/acquire fences.

Below is a **stand‑alone, production‑ready** Go example that shows how to import the `io` package and copy data between two streams efficiently.  
It demonstrates three common patterns:

1. **`io.Copy`** – the simplest and fastest for most cases (it uses an internal 32 KB buffer).  
2. **`io.CopyBuffer`** – lets you supply your own buffer (useful if you want a different size or want to reuse a buffer across many copies).  
3. **`io.CopyN`** – copy only a limited number of bytes (handy for protocols that send a length prefix).

Feel free to copy‑paste the whole file into `main.go` and run it, or extract the helper functions you need.

```go
// main.go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"log"
	"os"
)

// copyUsingIOCopy copies everything from src to dst using io.Copy.
// It returns the number of bytes copied and any error that occurred.
func copyUsingIOCopy(dst io.Writer, src io.Reader) (int64, error) {
	// io.Copy uses an internal 32 KB buffer and is highly optimized.
	return io.Copy(dst, src)
}

// copyUsingIOCopyBuffer does the same thing but lets you control the buffer size.
// Re‑using the same buffer across many calls can reduce allocations.
func copyUsingIOCopyBuffer(dst io.Writer, src io.Reader, buf []byte) (int64, error) {
	if len(buf) == 0 {
		return 0, fmt.Errorf("buffer cannot be empty")
	}
	return io.CopyBuffer(dst, src, buf)
}

// copyN copies exactly n bytes (or fewer if src ends early) and returns the number copied.
func copyN(dst io.Writer, src io.Reader, n int64) (int64, error) {
	return io.CopyN(dst, src, n)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage: copy a file to another file, falling back to stdin/stdout.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
func main() {
	// Simple command‑line interface:
	//   go run main.go srcFile dstFile
	//   go run main.go -          -   // copies stdin → stdout
	if len(os.Args) != 3 {
		fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "usage: %s <src> <dst>\n", os.Args[0])
		fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "use \"-\" for stdin or stdout")
		os.Exit(1)
	}
	srcPath, dstPath := os.Args[1], os.Args[2]

	// Open source
	var src io.Reader
	if srcPath == "-" {
		src = os.Stdin
	} else {
		f, err := os.Open(srcPath)
		if err != nil {
			log.Fatalf("failed to open source %q: %v", srcPath, err)
		}
		defer f.Close()
		src = f
	}

	// Open destination
	var dst io.Writer
	if dstPath == "-" {
		dst = os.Stdout
	} else {
		f, err := os.Create(dstPath)
		if err != nil {
			log.Fatalf("failed to create destination %q: %v", dstPath, err)
		}
		defer func() {
			// Ensure data is flushed to disk.
			if err := f.Sync(); err != nil {
				log.Printf("warning: failed to sync %q: %v", dstPath, err)
			}
			f.Close()
		}()
		dst = f
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// 1️⃣  Fastest default copy (recommended for most cases)
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	n, err := copyUsingIOCopy(dst, src)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("copy failed: %v", err)
	}
	fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "✅ Copied %d bytes using io.Copy\n", n)

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// 2️⃣  If you need a custom buffer size, uncomment the block below.
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	/*
		const customBufSize = 64 * 1024 // 64 KB – tweak as needed
		buf := make([]byte, customBufSize)

		// Reset src/dst if you want to copy again (e.g. for benchmarking)
		// For a real program you would open the files again or seek back to 0.
		// n, err = copyUsingIOCopyBuffer(dst, src, buf)
		// if err != nil {
		//     log.Fatalf("copy with custom buffer failed: %v", err)
		// }
		// fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "✅ Copied %d bytes using io.CopyBuffer (buf=%dKB)\n", n, customBufSize/1024)
	*/

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// 3️⃣  Copy only a limited number of bytes (example: first 1 MiB)
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	/*
		const limit = 1 << 20 // 1 MiB
		n, err = copyN(dst, src, limit)
		if err != nil && err != io.EOF {
			log.Fatalf("copyN failed: %v", err)
		}
		fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "✅ Copied %d bytes (max %d) using io.CopyN\n", n, limit)
	*/
}
```

### How it works

| Function | What it does | When to use |
|----------|--------------|-------------|
| `io.Copy(dst, src)` | Reads from `src` and writes to `dst` using an internal 32 KB buffer. Returns total bytes written. | **Default** – you don’t need to think about buffers. |
| `io.CopyBuffer(dst, src, buf)` | Same as `io.Copy` but uses the supplied `buf`. | When you want a **different buffer size** (e.g., larger for high‑throughput disks or smaller for memory‑constrained environments) or you want to **reuse a buffer** across many copies to avoid allocations. |
| `io.CopyN(dst, src, n)` | Copies at most `n` bytes. | When the protocol tells you exactly how many bytes to read (e.g., length‑prefixed messages). |

### Why this is “efficient”

* **Zero‑copy where possible** – `io.Copy` delegates to the underlying `ReadFrom` / `WriteTo` methods of the concrete types (e.g., `*os.File`). If the OS supports `sendfile`‑style zero‑copy, Go will use it automatically.
* **Buffered I/O** – the internal 32 KB buffer is a sweet spot for most SSD/HDD workloads. You can tune it with `CopyBuffer` if you have special needs.
* **Error handling** – the example propagates any read/write error and logs a clear message, which is essential for production scripts.

