## 3️⃣ Node.js example – using DOMPurify on the server

```js
// file: sanitize.js
// -------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Load dependencies
// -------------------------------------------------
const { JSDOM } = require('jsdom');          // provides a fake window/document
const createDOMPurify = require('dompurify'); // the library itself

// -------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Create a DOM environment for DOMPurify
// -------------------------------------------------
const { window } = new JSDOM('');            // an empty document
const DOMPurify = createDOMPurify(window);   // bind DOMPurify to that window

// -------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Example user‑supplied HTML
// -------------------------------------------------
const dirty = `
  <p>Hello <strong>world</strong>!</p>
  <script>alert('XSS')</script>
  <img src="x" onerror="alert('XSS')">
  <a href="javascript:alert('XSS')">bad link</a>
`;

// -------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣ Sanitize (default configuration)
// -------------------------------------------------
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty);
console.log('--- Clean (default) ---');
console.log(clean);

// -------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣ Optional: tighten the whitelist
// -------------------------------------------------
const cleanStrict = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'strong', 'a'],          // only these tags survive
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href'],                     // only href attribute is kept
  // You can also add hooks, e.g. to force all links to open in a new tab:
  // RETURN_DOM: false,
});
console.log('\n--- Clean (strict whitelist) ---');
console.log(cleanStrict);
```

Run it:

```bash
node sanitize.js
```

**Typical output**

```
--- Clean (default) ---
<p>Hello <strong>world</strong>!</p>

--- Clean (strict whitelist) ---
<p>Hello <strong>world</strong>!</p>
```

All `<script>`, the dangerous `onerror` attribute, and the `javascript:` URL have been stripped.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick cheat‑sheet of the most useful options

| Option | What it does | Example |
|--------|--------------|---------|
| `ALLOWED_TAGS` | Whitelist of element names that may stay. Everything else is removed. | `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {ALLOWED_TAGS: ['b','i','a']})` |
| `ALLOWED_ATTR` | Whitelist of attribute names that may stay (applies to all tags). | `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href','title']})` |
| `FORBID_TAGS` | Blacklist – tags that **must** be removed even if they appear in `ALLOWED_TAGS`. | `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {FORBID_TAGS: ['style']})` |
| `FORBID_ATTR` | Blacklist for attributes. | `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {FORBID_ATTR: ['style','on*']})` |
| `SAFE_FOR_TEMPLATES` | When you plan to embed the result inside a template literal that later gets parsed again, set this to `true`. | `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {SAFE_FOR_TEMPLATES: true})` |
| `RETURN_DOM` | Return a **DOM node** instead of a string (useful when you need to manipulate the result further). | `const node = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {RETURN_DOM: true});` |
| `ADD_TAGS` / `ADD_ATTR` | Extend the default whitelist without replacing it. | `DOMPurify.addHook('uponSanitizeElement', (node, data) => { if (data.tagName === 'my-widget') node.removeAttribute('on*'); });` |

---

## 5️⃣ Common pitfalls & best‑practice checklist

| ✅ Good practice | ❌ What can go wrong |
|------------------|----------------------|
| **Never** concatenate raw user HTML into a string that later gets `innerHTML`‑injected **without** sanitising first. | Directly doing `element.innerHTML = userInput;` opens XSS. |
| Use the **same** DOMPurify instance throughout the request (or reuse the `window` you created) – it caches the configuration for speed. | Creating a new `JSDOM` + `DOMPurify` for every tiny string can be a performance hit. |
| When you need **different** policies (e.g. a comment box vs. a rich‑text editor), create **separate** sanitizer calls with custom options. | Re‑using a permissive config for a high‑privilege area can leak scripts. |
| If you allow **iframes** or **svg**, double‑check the default whitelist – they are **not** allowed by default because they can embed scripts. | Accidentally enabling `<iframe src="javascript:…">` leads to XSS. |
| In a **Node** environment, always create the `window` **once** and reuse it. | Forgetting to pass the same `window` to `createDOMPurify` will cause errors like “DOMPurify is not defined”. |
| When you need to **strip** *all* HTML (e.g. plain‑text usernames), you can simply use `DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {ALLOWED_TAGS: []})`. | Leaving the default whitelist will keep harmless tags like `<b>` that you might not want. |

---

