## 6. Common pitfalls & how the example avoids them

| Pitfall | What goes wrong | How the example handles it |
|---------|----------------|----------------------------|
| **Missing checksum** | `nmea.Parse` returns an error and you think the GPS is broken. | The error is logged, but the program continues reading the next line. |
| **Sentence split across reads** (serial ports can deliver partial lines) | `bufio.Scanner` would treat a partial line as a full sentence → parse error. | `Scanner` buffers until it sees a newline (`\n`). Most GPS modules terminate each sentence with `\r\n`. If you get raw bytes without line endings you can replace `Scanner` with a custom state machine that looks for `\r\n`. |
| **Mixed talker IDs** (`GP`, `GL`, `GN`, …) | You might filter on `$GPGGA` only and miss `$GNGGA`. | The parser ignores the talker prefix; `s.DataType()` returns just `GGA`, `RMC`, etc., so you get all talkers. |
| **Latitude/longitude in DMS vs. decimal** | Some libraries give you the raw `4807.038` string. | `go-nmea` already converts to **decimal degrees** (`48.11730`). |
| **Blocking forever on a dead serial port** | `Read` can block indefinitely. | The `serial.Config.ReadTimeout` is set to 5 s, so the scanner eventually returns an error and the program can decide what to do (re‑open, exit, etc.). |

