That’s it—you now have a **ready‑to‑run, production‑grade** NMEA‑0183 parser in Go. Happy coding! 🚀

Below is a **complete, step‑by‑step guide** for reading CAN‑bus frames from a vehicle in C++.  
I’ll cover the most common development environment (Linux + SocketCAN) because it is free, open‑source and works with virtually every USB‑to‑CAN adapter (PEAK‑PCAN‑USB, Kvaser, IXXAT, etc.). I’ll also give pointers for Windows (PCAN‑Basic) and a portable C++‑only wrapper (Boost.Asio) if you ever need to move off Linux.

---

## 1. What you need before you write code

| Item | Why it matters | Typical choice |
|------|----------------|----------------|
| **CAN hardware** | Provides the physical layer (transceiver, termination) | USB‑to‑CAN dongle (PEAK‑PCAN‑USB, Kvaser Leaf, etc.) |
| **Operating system support** | The OS must expose the CAN interface as a network device | Linux → *SocketCAN* (`can0`, `vcan0` …) <br> Windows → *PCAN‑Basic* DLL |
| **Permissions** | Raw sockets are privileged | On Linux add your user to the `dialout`/`can` group or use `sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep ./mycanapp` |
| **Development tools** | Compiler, build system | g++/clang++, CMake (or a simple Makefile) |
| **Testing aid** | You can develop without a car | Linux virtual CAN (`vcan`) or `candump`/`cansend` from `can-utils` |

---

## 2. Linux – SocketCAN “the native way”

### 2.1. Install the required packages

```bash
# Debian/Ubuntu
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y build-essential cmake libsocketcan-dev can-utils
```

*`libsocketcan-dev`* gives you the `struct can_frame` definition and a few helper functions.  
*`can-utils`* provides `cansend`, `candump`, `ip link` helpers for testing.

### 2.2. Bring up a CAN interface (real or virtual)

#### Real hardware (e.g. `can0`)

```bash
# Identify the device name (often can0, can1 …)
# Example for a PEAK‑PCAN‑USB that appears as can0:
sudo ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 500000   # 500 kbps, adjust to your vehicle
```

#### Virtual CAN (great for learning)

```bash
# Load the vcan kernel module
sudo modprobe vcan

# Create a virtual interface named vcan0
sudo ip link add dev vcan0 type vcan
sudo ip link set up vcan0
```

You can now send/receive frames with `cansend`/`candump`:

```bash
cansend vcan0 123#1122334455667788   # send a 8‑byte data frame
candump vcan0                       # watch it appear
```

### 2.3. Minimal C++ program – blocking read

Create a file `read_can.cpp`:

```cpp
// read_can.cpp --------------------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>      // memset, strerror
#include <cerrno>
#include <unistd.h>     // read, close
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <linux/can.h>
#include <linux/can/raw.h>
#include <net/if.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>

int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    const char* ifname = "can0";               // change to vcan0 for testing
    if (argc > 1) ifname = argv[1];            // optional CLI override

    // 1️⃣ Create a raw CAN socket
    int s = socket(PF_CAN, SOCK_RAW, CAN_RAW);
    if (s < 0) {
        std::perror("socket");
        return 1;
    }

    // 2️⃣ Locate the interface index (e.g. can0 → 2)
    struct ifreq ifr;
    std::strncpy(ifr.ifr_name, ifname, IFNAMSIZ - 1);
    if (ioctl(s, SIOCGIFINDEX, &ifr) < 0) {
        std::perror("ioctl(SIOCGIFINDEX)");
        close(s);
        return 1;
    }

    // 3️⃣ Bind the socket to the CAN interface
    struct sockaddr_can addr{};
    addr.can_family = AF_CAN;
    addr.can_ifindex = ifr.ifr_ifindex;
    if (bind(s, reinterpret_cast<struct sockaddr*>(&addr), sizeof(addr)) < 0) {
        std::perror("bind");
        close(s);
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout << "Listening on " << ifname << " …\n";

    // 4️⃣ Receive loop (blocking)
    struct can_frame frame{};
    while (true) {
        ssize_t nbytes = read(s, &frame, sizeof(frame));
        if (nbytes < 0) {
            if (errno == EINTR) continue;   // interrupted by signal, retry
            std::perror("read");
            break;
        }

        if (nbytes < sizeof(struct can_frame)) {
            std::cerr << "Incomplete CAN frame\n";
            continue;
        }

        // ----- pretty‑print the frame ------------------------------------
        std::cout << std::hex << std::uppercase;
        std::cout << "ID: 0x" << (frame.can_id & CAN_EFF_MASK);
        if (frame.can_id & CAN_EFF_FLAG) std::cout << " (EFF)";
        if (frame.can_id & CAN_RTR_FLAG) std::cout << " (RTR)";
        if (frame.can_id & CAN_ERR_FLAG) std::cout << " (ERR)";

        std::cout << "  DLC: " << static_cast<int>(frame.can_dlc) << "  Data: ";
        for (int i = 0; i < frame.can_dlc; ++i)
            std::cout << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0')
                      << static_cast<int>(frame.data[i]) << ' ';
        std::cout << std::dec << std::nouppercase << '\n';
    }

    close(s);
    return 0;
}
```

#### Build & run

```bash
g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -O2 read_can.cpp -o read_can
# Run (you may need sudo or proper caps)
sudo ./read_can can0          # or ./read_can vcan0
```

You should see a line printed each time a frame arrives, e.g.:

```
Listening on can0 …
ID: 0x123  DLC: 8  Data: 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88
```

### 2.4. Non‑blocking / select / poll (optional)

If you want to integrate CAN I/O into an event loop (GUI, ROS node, etc.) you can:

* set the socket to non‑blocking (`fcntl(s, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK)`);
* or use `select()`, `poll()`, or `epoll()` on the socket descriptor;
* or use **Boost.Asio** (see §4) which hides all that.

A tiny example with `select()`:

```cpp
fd_set readset;
FD_ZERO(&readset);
FD_SET(s, &readset);
int ret = select(s + 1, &readset, nullptr, nullptr, nullptr); // block until data
if (ret > 0 && FD_ISSET(s, &readset)) {
    // read() as above
}
```

### 2.5. Filtering frames (so you only get what you care about)

```cpp
struct can_filter rfilter[2];
rfilter[0].can_id   = 0x100;          // accept 0x100 … 0x1FF
rfilter[0].can_mask = CAN_SFF_MASK;  // standard IDs only
rfilter[1].can_id   = 0x18DAF110;    // accept a specific extended ID
rfilter[1].can_mask = CAN_EFF_MASK;

setsockopt(s, SOL_CAN_RAW, CAN_RAW_FILTER, &rfilter, sizeof(rfilter));
```

