## 4. Portable C++ with Boost.Asio (optional but nice)

Boost.Asio (since Boost 1.66) ships a **`boost::asio::generic::raw_protocol`** that can be used with any file descriptor, including a CAN socket. The benefit: you get an *asynchronous* API that works on Linux, macOS (for other protocols) and Windows (with the PCAN‑Basic wrapper you write yourself).

```cpp
// asio_can.cpp -------------------------------------------------------------
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <linux/can.h>
#include <linux/can/raw.h>
#include <net/if.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>

using boost::asio::generic::raw_protocol;

int main()
{
    const char* ifname = "vcan0";

    // 1️⃣ Create the raw CAN socket (POSIX)
    int raw_fd = ::socket(PF_CAN, SOCK_RAW, CAN_RAW);
    if (raw_fd < 0) { perror("socket"); return 1; }

    // 2️⃣ Bind to interface
    struct ifreq ifr{};
    std::strncpy(ifr.ifr_name, ifname, IFNAMSIZ - 1);
    if (ioctl(raw_fd, SIOCGIFINDEX, &ifr) < 0) { perror("ioctl"); return 1; }

    struct sockaddr_can addr{};
    addr.can_family = AF_CAN;
    addr.can_ifindex = ifr.ifr_ifindex;
    if (bind(raw_fd, reinterpret_cast<sockaddr*>(&addr), sizeof(addr)) < 0) {
        perror("bind"); return 1;
    }

    // 3️⃣ Wrap the fd in Boost.Asio
    boost::asio::io_context io;
    raw_protocol::socket socket(io, raw_fd);

    // 4️⃣ Async read handler
    auto read_handler = [&](const boost::system::error_code& ec,
                            std::size_t bytes_transferred,
                            std::shared_ptr<std::array<char, sizeof(can_frame)>> buf)
    {
        if (ec) {
            std::cerr << "Read error: " << ec.message() << '\n';
            return;
        }
        const can_frame* frame = reinterpret_cast<const can_frame*>(buf->data());

        std::cout << std::hex << std::uppercase
                  << "ID:0x" << (frame->can_id & CAN_EFF_MASK)
                  << " DLC:" << static_cast<int>(frame->can_dlc) << " Data:";
        for (int i = 0; i < frame->can_dlc; ++i)
            std::cout << ' ' << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0')
                      << static_cast<int>(frame->data[i]);
        std::cout << std::dec << std::nouppercase << '\n';

        // Re‑arm the async read
        auto new_buf = std::make_shared<std::array<char, sizeof(can_frame)>>();
        socket.async_read_some(boost::asio::buffer(*new_buf),
                               std::bind(read_handler,
                                         std::placeholders::_1,
                                         std::placeholders::_2,
                                         new_buf));
    };

    // Kick‑off the first async read
    auto buf = std::make_shared<std::array<char, sizeof(can_frame)>>();
    socket.async_read_some(boost::asio::buffer(*buf),
                           std::bind(read_handler,
                                     std::placeholders::_1,
                                     std::placeholders::_2,
                                     buf));

    std::cout << "Boost.Asio CAN listener started on " << ifname << "\n";
    io.run();   // blocks until the io_context is stopped
}
```

Compile (requires Boost ≥1.66):

```bash
g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -O2 asio_can.cpp -lboost_system -pthread -o asio_can
```

The same source works on any POSIX system that supports SocketCAN. On Windows you would replace the raw socket creation with the PCAN‑Basic `CAN_Initialize` call and then expose the resulting handle to Asio via `boost::asio::windows::stream_handle` (a bit more boilerplate, but doable).

