## 5. Quick sanity‑check checklist

| Step | What to verify |
|------|----------------|
| **Hardware** | `lsusb` (Linux) shows the dongle; `dmesg` shows `can0` created. |
| **Interface up** | `ip -details link show can0` → `state UP`, `bitrate 500000`. |
| **Permissions** | `sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep ./read_can` (or run with sudo). |
| **Test traffic** | `cansend can0 123#11223344` → your program prints the frame. |
| **Endianness** | CAN IDs are transmitted **big‑endian**; the kernel already presents them in host order, so you can treat `frame.can_id` as a normal integer. |
| **Extended vs. Standard** | `CAN_EFF_FLAG` indicates a 29‑bit ID; `CAN_SFF_MASK` masks the lower 11 bits. |
| **RTR frames** | `CAN_RTR_FLAG` – they carry no data, only a request. |
| **Error frames** | `CAN_ERR_FLAG` – useful for diagnostics (bus‑off, overrun, etc.). |

