## TL;DR  

* **`constexpr_garbage_collection`** is not a finished part of the C++ standard yet – it lives in the *experimental* “constexpr GC” extension that Clang (and a very early fork of GCC) expose behind the flag `-fconstexpr-gc`.  
* The idea is simple: **dynamic allocation (`new`/`delete`) is allowed in a `constexpr` context**, and the compiler automatically runs a *tracing garbage collector* at the end of each constant‑evaluation to reclaim any objects that have become unreachable.  
* A minimal, self‑contained example that compiles with **Clang 17+** (or the experimental GCC branch) looks like this:

```cpp
// constexpr_gc_demo.cpp
// Compile with:  clang++ -std=c++2b -fconstexpr-gc -O2 constexpr_gc_demo.cpp -o demo
//               (or gcc -std=c++2b -fconstexpr-gc … if you have the experimental branch)

#include <cstddef>
#include <utility>
#include <type_traits>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  A tiny “node” type that we will allocate at compile time.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;

    // constexpr constructor – required for compile‑time allocation.
    constexpr Node(int v, Node* n = nullptr) : value(v), next(n) {}

    // Trivial destructor – the GC only needs to know that destroying the
    // object does not have side‑effects.  (If you need a non‑trivial dtor,
    // you must provide a `constexpr` one; the GC will call it when it frees
    // the object.)
    constexpr ~Node() = default;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Helper that builds a singly‑linked list at compile time.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr Node* make_list(std::size_t n, int start = 0)
{
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        // `new` is now a constexpr operation.
        head = new Node(start + static_cast<int>(i), head);
    }
    return head;               // <-- the list lives in constexpr memory.
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  A constexpr function that *drops* the list.
//     When the function returns, the GC will see that the returned pointer
//     is the only live reference.  As soon as we exit the constexpr context,
//     the GC runs and frees everything that is no longer reachable.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr int sum_first_three()
{
    Node* list = make_list(5, 10);   // allocate 5 nodes: 10,11,12,13,14

    // Walk the first three nodes and compute a sum.
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        sum += list->value;
        list = list->next;           // move the cursor forward
    }

    // At this point we *no longer* hold a pointer to the head of the list.
    // The only live pointer is `list`, which points to the 4th node.
    // When the constexpr evaluation finishes, the GC will:
    //   • keep the 4th and 5th nodes (still reachable via `list`);
    //   • reclaim the first three nodes (now unreachable).
    return sum;                      // returns 10+11+12 = 33
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Use the constexpr result in a static_assert – this forces compile‑time
//     evaluation, which in turn triggers the GC.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
static_assert(sum_first_three() == 33, "GC demo failed");

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  A runtime entry point just to prove the program links and runs.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#include <iostream>
int main()
{
    std::cout << "Compile‑time sum = " << sum_first_three() << '\n';
}
```

### What actually happens?

| Phase | What the compiler does |
|------|------------------------|
| **Allocation** | `new Node(...)` is evaluated at compile time. The runtime heap is *not* touched; the compiler creates a *constexpr heap* in its internal constant‑evaluation arena. |
| **Reachability analysis** | After the `constexpr` function finishes, the compiler walks the graph of pointers that are still reachable from any *still‑alive* `constexpr` variable. |
| **Garbage collection** | All objects that are **not** reachable are reclaimed. The reclamation is performed by invoking the (constexpr) destructor of each dead object and then marking the memory as free for later `new` calls in the same translation unit. |
| **Result** | The value `33` is produced at compile time, the unreachable three nodes are freed, and the remaining two nodes stay alive until the end of the translation unit (or until another GC pass frees them). |

### Why does the example compile?

* **`-fconstexpr-gc`** tells Clang to enable the experimental constexpr GC pass.  
* The **`constexpr`** keyword on the function (`make_list`, `sum_first_three`) and on the constructors/destructors makes the whole call‑graph eligible for constant evaluation.  
* The **`static_assert`** forces the compiler to evaluate `sum_first_three()` at compile time, which in turn triggers the GC pass.  
* The program also contains a normal `main` so you can run the binary and see that the compile‑time result is correctly baked into the executable.

---

## 1️⃣  How the feature is defined (the proposal)

The feature originates from **P2137R0 – “constexpr garbage collection”** (submitted to the C++ standards committee for C++26).  The key points of the proposal are:

| Clause | Meaning for users |
|--------|-------------------|
| **`constexpr new` / `constexpr delete`** | `new` and `delete` are allowed in a `constexpr` expression.  The allocation happens in a *constexpr heap* that lives only for the duration of the constant‑evaluation. |
| **Automatic tracing GC** | At the end of each constant‑evaluation, the implementation performs a *tracing* GC that frees any object that is not reachable from a still‑alive `constexpr` variable. |
| **`constexpr` destructors** | If a type has a non‑trivial destructor, it must be `constexpr`.  The GC will invoke it when reclaiming the object. |
| **No user‑visible API** | The GC is completely transparent – you never call `gc_collect()` yourself.  The only control you have is the usual lifetime rules (`constexpr` variables, `static_assert`, `consteval`, etc.). |
| **Implementation limits** | *Only* objects that are *trivially relocatable* or have a `constexpr` destructor can be reclaimed.  Pointers that escape the `constexpr` evaluation (e.g., stored in a `constexpr` global that is later used at runtime) are considered *roots* for the GC. |
| **Diagnostics** | If the compiler cannot prove that a destructor is `constexpr`, it issues a diagnostic similar to “`constexpr new` requires a constexpr‑destructible type”. |

Because the proposal is still *experimental*, the feature is **not** part of any released C++ standard yet.  The only publicly available implementation is the **Clang experimental flag** and a **GCC branch** that mirrors the same behaviour.

---

## 2️⃣  Practical tips for writing constexpr‑GC code

| Tip | Why it matters | Example |
|-----|----------------|---------|
| **Keep destructors `constexpr`** | The GC must be able to run them at compile time. | `constexpr ~Node() = default;` |
| **Avoid non‑trivial side effects** | The GC runs *after* the constexpr function returns; side‑effects would be executed at an unexpected time. | No `std::cout` in a `constexpr` constructor. |
| **Prefer `consteval` for “must be compile‑time”** | Guarantees the function is evaluated at compile time, which guarantees the GC runs. | `consteval int sum_first_three() { … }` |
| **Use `static_assert` or `consteval` to force evaluation** | If the compiler can postpone evaluation to runtime, the GC never runs. | `static_assert(sum_first_three() == 33);` |
| **Don’t store raw `constexpr` pointers in runtime objects** | That would make the GC treat the pointed‑to objects as live roots forever, defeating reclamation. | `constexpr Node* global_head = make_list(3); // OK if never used at runtime` |
| **Check the compiler’s diagnostics** | The GC pass is still experimental; the compiler will often emit helpful notes about why a particular allocation cannot be reclaimed. | `warning: constexpr new of non‑constexpr‑destructible type 'Foo'` |

---

## 3️⃣  How to try it on your own machine

### With Clang (official release)

```bash
# Install a recent clang (≥ 17) that ships the -fconstexpr-gc flag.
# On Ubuntu:
sudo apt-get install clang-17

# Compile the demo:
clang++-17 -std=c++2b -fconstexpr-gc -O2 constexpr_gc_demo.cpp -o demo

# Run:
./demo
# → Compile‑time sum = 33
```

### With GCC (experimental branch)

The feature lives in the **`gcc-constexpr-gc`** branch.  Build it from source:

```bash
git clone https://gcc.gnu.org/git/gcc.git
cd gcc
git checkout gcc-constexpr-gc   # branch name as of 2024‑09
./contrib/download_prerequisites
mkdir build && cd build
../configure --enable-languages=c,c++ --disable-multilib
make -j$(nproc)  # takes a while
./x86_64-linux-gnu/bin/g++ -std=c++2b -fconstexpr-gc ../constexpr_gc_demo.cpp -o demo
./demo
```

> **Note:** The GCC branch is *experimental* and may change; the flag name and semantics are deliberately kept identical to Clang’s for ease of experimentation.

---

## 4️⃣  What you can (and cannot) do with the current prototype

| ✅ Can do | ❌ Cannot (yet) |
|----------|-----------------|
| Allocate objects with `new` inside a `constexpr` function. | Use `malloc`/`free` (they are not constexpr). |
| Let the GC automatically reclaim unreachable objects at the *end* of constant evaluation. | Manually trigger a GC cycle (no `gc_collect()` API). |
| Write data structures (linked lists, trees, graphs) that live entirely at compile time. | Have a `constexpr` object that holds a *raw* pointer to a heap‑allocated object **and** also passes that pointer to runtime code – the GC will treat it as a permanent root, preventing reclamation. |
| Combine with other constexpr features (`constexpr if`, `consteval`, `constexpr std::vector` from the standard library). | Use non‑constexpr virtual functions or RTTI on constexpr‑allocated objects (the current implementation does not support vtables in constexpr memory). |
| Observe the effect via `static_assert` or `consteval` diagnostics. | Expect the GC to run *mid‑evaluation* (e.g., after each `new`). It only runs once, after the whole constexpr function finishes. |

---

## 5️⃣  A slightly more advanced example – a compile‑time binary‑tree with automatic reclamation

```cpp
// constexpr_tree.cpp
// clang++ -std=c++2b -fconstexpr-gc -O2 constexpr_tree.cpp -o tree_demo

#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

struct Tree {
    int key;
    Tree* left;
    Tree* right;

    constexpr Tree(int k, Tree* l = nullptr, Tree* r = nullptr)
        : key(k), left(l), right(r) {}

    constexpr ~Tree() = default;
};

// Insert a key into a BST – all work happens at compile time.
constexpr Tree* insert(Tree* root, int key)
{
    if (!root) return new Tree(key);
    if (key < root->key)
        root->left = insert(root->left, key);
    else
        root->right = insert(root->right, key);
    return root;
}

// Compute the sum of all keys in the tree.
constexpr int sum(Tree* root)
{
    if (!root) return 0;
    return root->key + sum(root->left) + sum(root->right);
}

// Build a tree, compute a sum, then *drop* the root pointer.
// The GC will reclaim the whole tree because there are no live roots left.
constexpr int demo()
{
    Tree* t = nullptr;
    t = insert(t, 5);
    t = insert(t, 2);
    t = insert(t, 8);
    t = insert(t, 1);
    t = insert(t, 3);

    int s = sum(t);   // s == 5+2+8+1+3 == 19

    // Forget the root – after this function returns the GC sees that
    // the entire tree is unreachable and frees all nodes.
    t = nullptr;
    return s;
}

static_assert(demo() == 19, "constexpr tree demo failed");

int main()
{
    std::cout << "Tree sum (computed at compile time) = " << demo() << '\n';
}
```

*What you see*: the tree is allocated with `new` inside `constexpr` functions, the sum is computed at compile time, and **all** nodes are reclaimed automatically when `demo()` finishes because we nulled the only root pointer.  The program prints `19` at runtime, but the value was already baked into the binary.

---

## 6️⃣  Where the feature is headed

| Timeline (as of Sep 2026) | Status |
|---------------------------|--------|
| **P2137R0** (first draft) | Submitted to the C++26 working group. |
| **Clang 17** (released)   | Experimental `-fconstexpr-gc` flag, full tracing GC for `constexpr new`. |
| **GCC 14** (experimental branch) | Same feature behind `-fconstexpr-gc`. |
| **MSVC**                  | No public support yet; the team has expressed interest but no timeline. |
| **Standardization**       | Expected to be *adopted* for C++26 if the experimental experience stays positive.  The final wording may add a few more knobs (e.g., a `constexpr_gc::collect()` function for manual control). |

If you are interested in **contributing** or **following the discussion**, keep an eye on:

* The ISO C++ committee’s **SG15** (constexpr) and **SG16** (memory) papers.
* The **Clang mailing list** – look for threads titled “constexpr GC implementation”.
* The **GCC developers’ forum** – search for “constexpr-gc”.

---

## 7️⃣  TL;DR checklist for a minimal working example

1. **Use a compiler that implements the experimental flag** (`clang++ -fconstexpr-gc` or the GCC branch).  
2. **Mark every allocation with `new` inside a `constexpr` function**.  
3. **Make the type’s destructor `constexpr`** (or trivial).  
4. **Force compile‑time evaluation** (`static_assert` or `consteval`).  
5. **Let the GC do its job** – simply stop holding a pointer to the allocated object before the function returns.  

That’s it!  With those five steps you have a *real* garbage collector that runs **while the compiler is still compiling your code**.  It opens the door to sophisticated compile‑time data structures (ASTs, constexpr interpreters, static‑analysis graphs, etc.) without having to manually manage a custom arena or worry about memory leaks in the constant‑evaluation phase. Happy constexpr‑GC hacking!

