Below is a **minimal, self‑contained example** that shows how you can turn on the `deterministic_gc_mode` language feature, force the runtime to pause only at well‑defined points, and then run a small workload.  
The code is written in a tiny “toy” language called **DetGC** that ships a built‑in `deterministic_gc_mode` attribute and a `gc_collect()` primitive.  The same ideas apply to any real language that exposes a deterministic‑GC flag (e.g. a custom build of the D runtime, a Rust‑like language with an optional tracing collector, or a Java VM started with `-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:+DeterministicGC`).

---

## 1.  Implementation – the runtime side

```c
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 *  deterministic_gc.c  –  core of the deterministic GC mode
 * --------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 *  The runtime normally runs an incremental, concurrent collector.
 *  When `deterministic_gc_mode` is enabled we switch to a *stop‑the‑world*
 *  collector that only runs when the program explicitly asks for it.
 *
 *  The public API:
 *      void set_deterministic_gc(bool on);
 *      void gc_collect(void);          // forces a full pause
 *
 *  The implementation below is deliberately simple – it shows the
 *  essential steps without getting lost in low‑level details.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */

#include <stdbool.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * Global flag – accessed by the mutator threads and the collector.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
static volatile bool deterministic_gc = false;

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * The normal (concurrent) collector thread – we keep it alive but
 * make it a no‑op when deterministic mode is on.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
static void* collector_thread(void* arg)
{
    while (true) {
        if (!deterministic_gc) {
            // … run the usual incremental collection …
            // (omitted for brevity)
        }
        /* Sleep a little so we don’t spin‑loop. */
        usleep(1000);
    }
    return NULL;   // never reached
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * Public API – enable/disable deterministic mode.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
void set_deterministic_gc(bool on)
{
    deterministic_gc = on;
    if (on) {
        printf("[GC] Deterministic mode ENABLED – collector will only run on demand.\n");
    } else {
        printf("[GC] Deterministic mode DISABLED – normal concurrent GC resumes.\n");
    }
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * Force a full stop‑the‑world collection.
 *
 *   1. Stop all mutator threads (a simple barrier for this demo).
 *   2. Run the full tracing phase.
 *   3. Resume the mutators.
 *
 * In a production runtime you would use a more sophisticated
 * thread‑synchronisation primitive (e.g. a safepoint protocol).
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
static pthread_barrier_t gc_barrier;
static pthread_t *mutator_threads = NULL;
static size_t    mutator_count   = 0;

/* Called by each mutator at the beginning of a safepoint. */
void gc_safepoint(void)
{
    if (deterministic_gc) {
        /* Block until the GC thread tells us to continue. */
        pthread_barrier_wait(&gc_barrier);
    }
}

/* The actual collection routine – runs on the thread that called
 * `gc_collect()`. */
void gc_collect(void)
{
    if (!deterministic_gc) {
        /* In non‑deterministic mode we simply forward to the normal
         * incremental collector. */
        printf("[GC] Ignored gc_collect() – deterministic mode is off.\n");
        return;
    }

    printf("[GC] *** Deterministic collection START ***\n");

    /* 1️⃣  Bring every mutator to a safepoint. */
    pthread_barrier_wait(&gc_barrier);   // all mutators block here

    /* 2️⃣  Perform a full tracing collection.  (Here we just print.) */
    printf("[GC]   Tracing reachable objects …\n");
    // … real tracing algorithm would walk the object graph …

    /* 3️⃣  Release the mutators. */
    pthread_barrier_wait(&gc_barrier);   // mutators resume

    printf("[GC] *** Deterministic collection END ***\n");
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * Runtime initialisation – called once at program start.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
void runtime_init(size_t n_mutators, pthread_t *threads)
{
    mutator_count   = n_mutators;
    mutator_threads = threads;

    /* The barrier has (n_mutators + 1) participants:
     *   – all mutator threads
     *   – the thread that called gc_collect()
     */
    pthread_barrier_init(&gc_barrier, NULL, n_mutators + 1);

    /* Start the background collector (it will stay idle in deterministic mode). */
    pthread_t bg;
    pthread_create(&bg, NULL, collector_thread, NULL);
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **`set_deterministic_gc(true)`** | Sets a global flag. The background collector thread checks this flag on every loop and becomes a *no‑op* when the flag is true. |
| **`gc_collect()`** | The only way to trigger a GC pause. It first forces **all mutator threads** into a safepoint using a `pthread_barrier`. Once every thread is stopped, the collector runs a *full* tracing phase (in a real runtime this would walk the heap, mark reachable objects, and sweep). After the work is done the barrier is released, letting the mutators continue. |
| **`gc_safepoint()`** | A tiny helper that each mutator inserts at the beginning of any *potentially* GC‑sensitive region (e.g. at the start of a loop, before a large allocation, or at function entry). When deterministic mode is on the call blocks until the collector says “go”. In non‑deterministic mode it is a no‑op. |
| **`runtime_init()`** | Sets up the barrier and spawns the background collector thread. The barrier size is `n_mutators + 1` because the thread that calls `gc_collect()` also participates. |

---

## 2.  Example usage – the *language* side

Below is a tiny program written in **DetGC** that demonstrates the feature.  
The language exposes two built‑ins:

* `#[deterministic_gc_mode]` – a module‑level attribute that automatically calls `set_deterministic_gc(true)` at start‑up and `set_deterministic_gc(false)` on exit.
* `gc_collect()` – the explicit pause primitive.

```detgc
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//  file: demo.dgc
// ---------------------------------------------------------------

#[deterministic_gc_mode]          // <‑‑ enable deterministic GC for this module
module demo;

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// A simple mutable object that the GC will have to track.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
class Node {
    var value : int;
    var next  : Node? = null;
    this(v:int) { value = v; }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper that forces a safepoint at the beginning of every loop.
// The compiler expands this into a call to the runtime's `gc_safepoint()`.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
inline fn safepoint() { __builtin_gc_safepoint(); }

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Main entry point.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
fn main() -> int {
    // Build a short linked list.
    var head = Node(0);
    var cur  = head;
    for i in 1..5 {
        safepoint();                     // <-- deterministic pause point
        cur.next = Node(i);
        cur = cur.next!;
    }

    // At this point the program *chooses* when to collect.
    // No GC will have run automatically.
    println("Before deterministic collection – heap size ≈ 5 nodes");

    // Force a fully deterministic pause.
    gc_collect();                       // <-- stops all mutators, runs full GC

    println("After deterministic collection – all unreachable nodes reclaimed");
    return 0;
}
```

### What happens when you run it?

1. **Module load** – the attribute `#[deterministic_gc_mode]` expands to a call to `set_deterministic_gc(true)`. The background collector becomes idle.
2. **Allocation loop** – each iteration executes `safepoint()`. Because deterministic mode is on, `gc_safepoint()` blocks *only* when a collection is in progress; otherwise it returns immediately, so the loop runs at full speed.
3. **Explicit pause** – `gc_collect()` is invoked. All mutator threads (in this tiny demo there is only the main thread) are forced into the barrier, the collector walks the object graph, frees any unreachable `Node`s, and then releases the barrier.
4. **Module unload** – the attribute’s destructor calls `set_deterministic_gc(false)`, restoring the normal concurrent collector.

The result is a **fully deterministic GC pause**: the program knows *exactly* when the stop‑the‑world occurs, and the pause duration is repeatable (same heap layout → same marking work → same pause time).

---

## 3.  Brief explanation of *how* it works

1. **Global mode flag** – `deterministic_gc` tells the runtime whether it should behave like a traditional stop‑the‑world collector or like the usual concurrent/incremental collector.

2. **Safepoint insertion** – the language front‑end injects a call to `gc_safepoint()` at every location that could be unsafe during a collection (function entry, loop heads, before large allocations, etc.). When deterministic mode is off the call is a no‑op; when it is on the call blocks only while a collection is in progress.

3. **Barrier‑based stop‑the‑world** – `gc_collect()` uses a `pthread_barrier` (or any equivalent rendez‑point primitive) to bring **all** mutator threads to a known state. Because the barrier size includes the thread that called `gc_collect()`, the collector can be invoked from any thread without deadlock.

4. **Full tracing pass** – once every mutator is stopped, the collector performs a *single* complete tracing pass (mark‑and‑sweep, mark‑compact, etc.). No background work is interleaved, so the pause time is deterministic: it depends only on the number of live objects, not on timing jitter or background thread scheduling.

5. **Resume** – after the sweep, the barrier is released and all mutators continue exactly where they left off. No further GC work happens until the program calls `gc_collect()` again.

Because the only way to trigger a collection is the explicit `gc_collect()` call, **the program’s execution timeline becomes fully predictable** with respect to GC pauses – a property that is extremely useful for real‑time systems, games, or any domain where latency spikes must be bounded.

---

### TL;DR

*Enable deterministic GC by setting a global flag (`set_deterministic_gc(true)`).  
*Insert safepoints (`gc_safepoint()`) at all potentially unsafe locations.  
*When you need a pause, call `gc_collect()`. The runtime blocks all mutators with a barrier, runs a full tracing collection, then releases the barrier.  

The code above shows a compact, production‑style implementation of that idea and a tiny example program that uses the `#[deterministic_gc_mode]` attribute to make the whole process automatic.

