## 6. The C++ wrapper (`quic_client.hpp`)

> **Why a wrapper?**  
> MsQuic’s API is pure C and event‑driven. A thin C++ class hides the boilerplate and lets you write idiomatic C++ (RAII, `std::future`, etc.).

```cpp
#pragma once
#include <msquic.h>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <future>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cstring>

class QuicClient {
public:
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Construction / destruction
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    explicit QuicClient(const std::string& server_name,
                        uint16_t server_port,
                        const std::string& alpn = "hq-29"); // default ALPN for HTTP/3
    ~QuicClient();

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Public API
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    /// Connect to the remote endpoint (handshake + TLS). Throws on error.
    void connect();

    /// Send the whole buffer on a *new* stream. Returns when the remote
    /// side ACKs all bytes (i.e. loss‑less delivery). Throws on error.
    void send(const std::vector<uint8_t>& data);

    /// Receive data from the *first* inbound stream (optional helper).
    /// Returns when the stream is closed by the peer.
    std::vector<uint8_t> receive();

private:
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MsQuic handles
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    HQUIC registration_ = nullptr;
    HQUIC configuration_ = nullptr;
    HQUIC connection_   = nullptr;
    HQUIC stream_       = nullptr;

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Settings
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    const std::string server_name_;
    const uint16_t    server_port_;
    const std::string alpn_;

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Synchronisation helpers
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::promise<void> connect_promise_;
    std::promise<void> send_promise_;
    std::promise<std::vector<uint8_t>> recv_promise_;

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Static callbacks required by MsQuic
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    static
    _IRQL_requires_max_(DISPATCH_LEVEL)
    QUIC_STATUS QUIC_API
    ConnectionCallback(HQUIC, void* Context,
                       QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT* Event);

    static
    _IRQL_requires_max_(DISPATCH_LEVEL)
    QUIC_STATUS QUIC_API
    StreamCallback(HQUIC, void* Context,
                   QUIC_STREAM_EVENT* Event);
};
```

### 6.1. Implementation (`main.cpp`)

Below is a **single‑file** implementation that you can copy into `src/main.cpp`.  
All the heavy lifting lives in the `QuicClient` class; the `main()` function simply demonstrates usage.

```cpp
#include "quic_client.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: convert std::string to QUIC_BUFFER (no copy)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
static QUIC_BUFFER MakeQuicBuffer(const std::string& s) {
    QUIC_BUFFER buf{};
    buf.Length = static_cast<uint32_t>(s.size());
    buf.Buffer = reinterpret_cast<uint8_t*>(const_cast<char*>(s.data()));
    return buf;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// QuicClient implementation
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
QuicClient::QuicClient(const std::string& server_name,
                       uint16_t server_port,
                       const std::string& alpn)
    : server_name_(server_name), server_port_(server_port), alpn_(alpn) {

    // 1️⃣ Load the MsQuic API table (global singleton)
    const QUIC_API_TABLE* MsQuic = nullptr;
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("MsQuicOpen failed");
    }

    // 2️⃣ Create a registration (process‑wide context)
    QUIC_REGISTRATION_CONFIG regConfig = {
        "quic-lossless-client", // App name (used for tracing)
        QUIC_EXECUTION_PROFILE_LOW_LATENCY // Low latency = minimal buffering
    };
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->RegistrationOpen(&regConfig, &registration_))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("RegistrationOpen failed");
    }

    // 3️⃣ Create a configuration (TLS settings, ALPN, etc.)
    QUIC_SETTINGS settings = {0};
    settings.IsSetFlags = QUIC_SETTINGS_FLAG_MAX_STREAMS_UNIDIRECTIONAL |
                          QUIC_SETTINGS_FLAG_MAX_STREAMS_BIDIRECTIONAL |
                          QUIC_SETTINGS_FLAG_MAX_BYTES_PER_KEY;
    settings.MaxBytesPerKey = 0; // Unlimited (for demo)

    // Disable congestion control if you truly want “unlimited bandwidth”.
    // This is optional – QUIC is already loss‑less without it.
    settings.IsSetFlags |= QUIC_SETTINGS_FLAG_DISABLE_CID_UPDATE;
    settings.DisableCidUpdate = TRUE;

    // Build the ALPN buffer (MsQuic expects a length‑prefixed list)
    const uint8_t alpnBuf[] = { static_cast<uint8_t>(alpn_.size()), /* alpn bytes */ };
    // We'll allocate a temporary buffer that contains length+bytes.
    std::vector<uint8_t> alpnList;
    alpnList.reserve(alpn_.size() + 1);
    alpnList.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>(alpn_.size()));
    alpnList.insert(alpnList.end(), alpn_.begin(), alpn_.end());

    QUIC_BUFFER alpnBuffer = {
        static_cast<uint32_t>(alpnList.size()),
        alpnList.data()
    };

    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->ConfigurationOpen(
            registration_,
            &alpnBuffer,
            1,               // One ALPN entry
            &settings,
            sizeof(settings),
            nullptr,        // No custom context
            &configuration_))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("ConfigurationOpen failed");
    }

    // 4️⃣ Load a default TLS credential (skip verification for demo)
    QUIC_CREDENTIAL_CONFIG credConfig = {0};
    credConfig.Type = QUIC_CREDENTIAL_TYPE_NONE; // No client cert
    credConfig.Flags = QUIC_CREDENTIAL_FLAG_NO_CERTIFICATE_VALIDATION; // <‑‑ insecure, only for testing
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->ConfigurationLoadCredential(configuration_, &credConfig))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("ConfigurationLoadCredential failed");
    }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
QuicClient::~QuicClient() {
    const QUIC_API_TABLE* MsQuic = nullptr;
    MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic); // safe to call again

    if (stream_)      MsQuic->StreamClose(stream_);
    if (connection_)  MsQuic->ConnectionClose(connection_);
    if (configuration_) MsQuic->ConfigurationClose(configuration_);
    if (registration_)  MsQuic->RegistrationClose(registration_);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
void QuicClient::connect() {
    const QUIC_API_TABLE* MsQuic = nullptr;
    MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic);

    // 1️⃣ Open a connection object
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->ConnectionOpen(
            registration_,
            ConnectionCallback,
            this,               // Context passed to callbacks
            &connection_))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("ConnectionOpen failed");
    }

    // 2️⃣ Resolve the server address (IPv4 only for brevity)
    sockaddr_in serverAddr{};
    serverAddr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    serverAddr.sin_port   = htons(server_port_);
    if (inet_pton(AF_INET, server_name_.c_str(), &serverAddr.sin_addr) != 1) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Invalid IPv4 address: " + server_name_);
    }

    // 3️⃣ Start the handshake (TLS + QUIC version negotiation)
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->ConnectionStart(
            connection_,
            configuration_,
            QUIC_ADDRESS_FAMILY_INET,
            reinterpret_cast<const QUIC_ADDR*>(&serverAddr),
            server_port_))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("ConnectionStart failed");
    }

    // 4️⃣ Wait until the connection is up (or fails)
    auto fut = connect_promise_.get_future();
    if (fut.wait_for(std::chrono::seconds(10)) != std::future_status::ready) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Connection timeout");
    }
    // If the promise was set with an exception, rethrow it here:
    fut.get(); // will throw if connection failed
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
void QuicClient::send(const std::vector<uint8_t>& data) {
    const QUIC_API_TABLE* MsQuic = nullptr;
    MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic);

    // 1️⃣ Open a *new* unidirectional stream (you could also use bidirectional)
    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->StreamOpen(
            connection_,
            QUIC_STREAM_OPEN_FLAG_UNIDIRECTIONAL,
            StreamCallback,
            this,
            &stream_))) {
        throw std::runtime_error("StreamOpen failed");
    }

    // 2️⃣ Send the whole buffer in one go (MsQuic will split if needed)
    QUIC_BUFFER buf{};
    buf.Length = static_cast<uint32_t>(data.size());
    buf.Buffer = const_cast<uint8_t*>(data.data());

    if (QUIC_FAILED(MsQuic->StreamSend(
            stream_,
            &buf,
            1,                     // Buffer count
            QUIC_SEND_FLAG_FIN,    // FIN = end‑of‑stream
            nullptr))) {           // No send context
        throw std::runtime_error("StreamSend failed");
    }

    // 3️⃣ Wait for the SEND_COMPLETE event (means the peer ACKed everything)
    auto fut = send_promise_.get_future();
    if (fut.wait_for(std::chrono::seconds(30)) != std::future_status::ready) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Send timeout");
    }
    fut.get(); // rethrow if error
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
std::vector<uint8_t> QuicClient::receive() {
    // For a simple demo we just wait for the first inbound stream to close.
    // In a real app you would keep a map of stream IDs → buffers.
    auto fut = recv_promise_.get_future();
    if (fut.wait_for(std::chrono::seconds(30)) != std::future_status::ready) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Receive timeout");
    }
    return fut.get();
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Connection callback – runs on MsQuic’s internal thread
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
QUIC_STATUS QUIC_API
QuicClient::ConnectionCallback(HQUIC /* Connection */,
                               void* Context,
                               QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT* Event) {
    QuicClient* self = static_cast<QuicClient*>(Context);
    switch (Event->Type) {
        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_CONNECTED:
            // Handshake succeeded
            self->connect_promise_.set_value();
            break;

        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_INITIATED_BY_TRANSPORT:
        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_INITIATED_BY_PEER:
        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_COMPLETE:
            // Propagate error to any waiting futures
            self->connect_promise_.set_exception(
                std::make_exception_ptr(std::runtime_error("Connection shutdown")));
            break;

        case QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_PEER_STREAM_STARTED: {
            // Peer opened a stream toward us – we accept it and attach the same callback
            HQUIC stream = Event->PEER_STREAM_STARTED.Stream;
            MsQuic->SetCallbackHandler(stream, (void*)StreamCallback, self);
            break;
        }

        default:
            // Most events are not needed for this simple demo
            break;
    }
    return QUIC_STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Stream callback – handles SEND_COMPLETE, RECEIVE, etc.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
QUIC_STATUS QUIC_API
QuicClient::StreamCallback(HQUIC /* Stream */,
                           void* Context,
                           QUIC_STREAM_EVENT* Event) {
    QuicClient* self = static_cast<QuicClient*>(Context);
    const QUIC_API_TABLE* MsQuic = nullptr;
    MsQuicOpen(&MsQuic);

    switch (Event->Type) {
        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_SEND_COMPLETE:
            // All data we sent has been ACKed by the peer → loss‑less guarantee
            self->send_promise_.set_value();
            break;

        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_RECEIVE: {
            // Accumulate data from the peer
            std::vector<uint8_t> buffer;
            for (uint32_t i = 0; i < Event->RECEIVE.BufferCount; ++i) {
                const QUIC_BUFFER& buf = Event->RECEIVE.Buffers[i];
                buffer.insert(buffer.end(), buf.Buffer, buf.Buffer + buf.Length);
            }
            // If the peer set FIN, we resolve the receive promise
            if (Event->RECEIVE.Flags & QUIC_RECEIVE_FLAG_FIN) {
                self->recv_promise_.set_value(std::move(buffer));
            }
            break;
        }

        case QUIC_STREAM_EVENT_SHUTDOWN_COMPLETE:
            // Peer closed the stream without sending FIN – treat as error
            if (!self->send_promise_.get_future().valid()) {
                self->send_promise_.set_exception(
                    std::make_exception_ptr(std::runtime_error("Stream shutdown before send complete")));
            }
            break;

        default:
            break;
    }
    return QUIC_STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo `main()` – streams a few megabytes to a test server
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc != 3) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <server-ip> <port>\n";
        return 1;
    }
    const std::string server_ip = argv[1];
    const uint16_t    server_port = static_cast<uint16_t>(std::stoi(argv[2]));

    try {
        QuicClient client(server_ip, server_port);
        std::cout << "Connecting...\n";
        client.connect();
        std::cout << "Connected!\n";

        // Build a 5 MiB payload (all zeroes – you can fill with anything)
        const size_t payloadSize = 5 * 1024 * 1024;
        std::vector<uint8_t> payload(payloadSize, 0xAB);

        std::cout << "Sending " << payloadSize << " bytes (loss‑less)...\n";
        auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        client.send(payload);
        auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();

        std::chrono::duration<double> secs = end - start;
        std::cout << "Send completed in " << secs.count() << " s ("
                  << (payloadSize / (1024.0 * 1024.0) / secs.count()) << " MiB/s)\n";

        // Optional: receive a response (if the server echoes)
        // std::vector<uint8_t> reply = client.receive();
        // std::cout << "Received " << reply.size() << " bytes from server.\n";

    } catch (const std::exception& ex) {
        std::cerr << "Fatal error: " << ex.what() << "\n";
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Step | Code fragment | What it accomplishes |
|------|---------------|----------------------|
| **1** | `MsQuicOpen`, `RegistrationOpen` | Initializes the MsQuic library and creates a process‑wide registration (the “owner” of all connections). |
| **2** | `ConfigurationOpen` + ALPN | Sets up TLS parameters and tells QUIC which application protocol we intend to use (`hq-29` is the HTTP/3 draft ALPN; you can pick any string). |
| **3** | `ConfigurationLoadCredential` with `QUIC_CREDENTIAL_FLAG_NO_CERTIFICATE_VALIDATION` | Skips server‑certificate verification – **only for testing**. In production you would load a proper root store or pin the server cert. |
| **4** | `ConnectionOpen` + `ConnectionStart` | Starts the QUIC handshake (TLS + version negotiation). The `ConnectionCallback` receives `CONNECTED` or error events. |
| **5** | `StreamOpen` (unidirectional) | Creates a new stream for sending data. QUIC guarantees that data on a stream is delivered **in order and without loss**. |
| **6** | `StreamSend` with `QUIC_SEND_FLAG_FIN` | Sends the whole buffer and marks the end of the stream. The library may split the buffer into multiple packets internally. |
| **7** | `STREAM_EVENT_SEND_COMPLETE` → `send_promise_` | The callback fires only after the peer ACKs **every** packet, i.e. after the network has confirmed loss‑less delivery. |
| **8** | (optional) `STREAM_EVENT_RECEIVE` → `recv_promise_` | Shows how to read inbound data if you need a bidirectional exchange. |

> **Key point:** The moment `send_promise_` is fulfilled, you have **absolute assurance** that the remote endpoint has received *all* bytes you sent, regardless of packet loss, reordering, or retransmission. That is the “loss‑less” guarantee you asked for.

