## 5. Extending the example (optional but useful)

| Feature | How to add (few lines) |
|---------|------------------------|
| **Per‑client timeout / state cleanup** | Run a background goroutine that walks `clients` and removes entries whose `lastSeqSeen` is older than, say, 5 minutes. |
| **Windowed flow control** | Keep a map of *in‑flight* seq numbers and allow the client to send up to `N` packets before waiting for ACKs. |
| **Message ordering** | Store the highest *contiguous* seq seen and buffer out‑of‑order packets until the gap is filled. |
| **Authentication / integrity** | Add a 4‑byte HMAC (or a simple XOR checksum for a demo) after the payload and verify it before processing. |
| **Larger payloads** | Split a large payload into fragments, each with its own seq and a “fragment‑id” field; re‑assemble on the server side. |
| **Use a library** | If you prefer not to roll your own, the same pattern is implemented in the open‑source library `github.com/xtaci/kcp-go` (KCP) or `github.com/xtaci/udp-relay` – they both give reliable, ordered delivery over UDP. |

---

## 6. Why this is “exactly‑once”

* **Idempotent processing** – the server only calls the application layer *once* per unique `seq`. Duplicate packets are filtered out before the payload reaches your code.
* **ACK retransmission** – if the ACK itself is lost, the client will resend the DATA; the server will see the same `seq` again, recognise it as a duplicate, and resend the same ACK. This “ACK‑on‑duplicate” pattern guarantees that the client eventually sees the ACK **as long as the network eventually delivers at least one copy of each packet**.
* **No “at‑most‑once” loophole** – the client never proceeds to the next logical message until it has received the ACK for the current one, so the server never sees two different payloads with the same `seq`.

