## 8️⃣ REST Controller – `TransactionController.java`

```java
package com.example.cognivault.controller;

import com.example.cognivault.model.TransactionRequest;
import com.example.cognivault.model.TransactionResponse;
import com.example.cognivault.service.TransactionService;
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/transactions")
public class TransactionController {

    private final TransactionService transactionService;

    public TransactionController(TransactionService transactionService) {
        this.transactionService = transactionService;
    }

    /**
     * Public endpoint that forwards the request to Cognivault ESB.
     *
     * Example payload:
     * {
     *   "accountId": "ACC-12345",
     *   "type": "DEBIT",
     *   "amount": 250.75,
     *   "currency": "USD",
     *   "description": "Invoice #9876"
     * }
     */
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<TransactionResponse> createTransaction(
            @Valid @RequestBody TransactionRequest request) {

        TransactionResponse response = transactionService.submitTransaction(request);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
    }
}
```

*The controller is deliberately thin – all heavy lifting (serialization, authentication, retries) lives in the Camel route.*

