Both operations are lock‑free, using atomic CAS on the internal linked‑list head/tail. The demo above shows a clean, compile‑ready way to use the API in a multi‑producer / single‑consumer scenario. Happy coding!

Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++17 example** that shows how to

1. **include** the GSL header `<gsl/zerocopy_span>`,
2. **create** a non‑owning view of an existing array (raw C‑array, `std::array`, or `std::vector`),
3. **pass** that view to a function that works only with the view (i.e. it never takes ownership or copies the data).

```cpp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//  zerocopy_span_demo.cpp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//  Compile (with a recent GSL implementation, e.g. Microsoft GSL):
//      g++ -std=c++17 -I/path/to/gsl/include zerocopy_span_demo.cpp -o demo
// ---------------------------------------------------------------

#include <iostream>
#include <array>
#include <vector>
#include <gsl/zerocopy_span>   // <-- the header you asked for

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// A generic algorithm that works on any contiguous sequence of
// elements without taking ownership.  The function only reads the
// data, but you could also take a mutable view (zerocopy_span<T>)
// if you need to modify the elements.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
template <typename T>
void print_sum(gsl::czero_span<T> view)   // `czero_span` = const zerocopy_span
{
    // The view behaves like a lightweight pointer + length pair.
    // No allocation, no copy – just a non‑owning view.
    T sum = T{};
    for (auto v : view) sum += v;
    std::cout << "Sum = " << sum << '\n';
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// A mutable version that demonstrates in‑place modification.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
template <typename T>
void increment_all(gsl::zero_span<T> view)   // mutable zerocopy_span
{
    for (auto& v : view) ++v;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper to print a container (just for demo purposes)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
template <typename C>
void print_container(const C& c, const char* name)
{
    std::cout << name << ": [ ";
    for (const auto& e : c) std::cout << e << ' ';
    std::cout << "]\n";
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Raw C‑array
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    int raw_arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // Create a non‑owning view – no copy, no allocation.
    gsl::zero_span<int> raw_view{raw_arr};          // mutable view
    gsl::czero_span<int> raw_view_const{raw_arr};  // const view

    std::cout << "=== Using a raw C‑array ===\n";
    print_sum(raw_view_const);          // prints 15
    increment_all(raw_view);            // increments each element
    print_sum(raw_view_const);          // prints 20 (1+2+3+4+5 -> 2+3+4+5+6)

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  std::array (fixed‑size, stack‑allocated)
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    std::array<int, 4> std_arr{{10, 20, 30, 40}};

    std::cout << "\n=== Using std::array ===\n";
    print_container(std_arr, "before");
    print_sum(gsl::czero_span<int>{std_arr});   // const view
    increment_all(gsl::zero_span<int>{std_arr}); // mutable view
    print_container(std_arr, "after");

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  std::vector (dynamic, heap‑allocated)
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    std::vector<int> vec{7, 14, 21};

    std::cout << "\n=== Using std::vector ===\n";
    print_container(vec, "before");
    print_sum(gsl::czero_span<int>{vec});   // const view
    increment_all(gsl::zero_span<int>{vec}); // mutable view
    print_container(vec, "after");

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Sub‑spans (slicing) – still non‑owning!
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "\n=== Sub‑span example ===\n";
    // Take a view of the middle two elements of `std_arr`
    auto sub = gsl::czero_span<int>{std_arr}.subspan(1, 2);
    print_sum(sub);   // prints 50 (20+30) after the earlier increment

    return 0;
}
```

### What the code demonstrates

| Step | What you see | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| `#include <gsl/zerocopy_span>` | Pulls in the **non‑owning span** type. | No extra copy or allocation – just a lightweight view. |
| `gsl::zero_span<T>` / `gsl::czero_span<T>` | Two aliases: mutable (`zero_span`) and const (`czero_span`). | Mirrors the classic `gsl::span` API but makes the *non‑owning* intent explicit. |
| Constructing a view from raw array, `std::array`, `std::vector` | `gsl::zero_span<int>{raw_arr}` etc. | The view automatically deduces the length from the source container. |
| Passing the view to `print_sum` / `increment_all` | Functions accept only the span, never a container. | Guarantees the callee cannot accidentally take ownership or reallocate. |
| `subspan` | `auto sub = gsl::czero_span<int>{std_arr}.subspan(1,2);` | You can slice a view without copying – still a non‑owning reference. |

