### Quick cheat‑sheet for the most common `zerocopy_span` operations

| Operation | Syntax | Meaning |
|-----------|--------|---------|
| **Create mutable view** | `gsl::zero_span<T> view{container_or_ptr, length};` | Non‑owning, can modify elements. |
| **Create const view** | `gsl::czero_span<T> view{container_or_ptr, length};` | Read‑only view. |
| **From `std::array` / `std::vector`** | `gsl::zero_span<T>{my_vec}` | Length is inferred automatically. |
| **From raw pointer + size** | `gsl::zero_span<T>{ptr, n}` | Useful for C‑APIs. |
| **Slice** | `auto sub = view.subspan(offset, count);` | Returns another non‑owning view. |
| **Access** | `view[i]`, `*view.begin()`, `view.size()` | Same as `std::span`. |
| **Conversion to `std::span`** | `std::span<T> s = view;` | Implicit – `zerocopy_span` is just a thin wrapper. |

---

**Bottom line:** `gsl::zerocopy_span` gives you a *zero‑allocation, zero‑copy* handle to existing contiguous memory. It’s perfect for APIs that need to read or mutate data without taking ownership, and it works seamlessly with raw arrays, `std::array`, and `std::vector`. Feel free to copy‑paste the demo into your project and adapt the functions to your own algorithms!

**What the header does**

`<openssl/rsa_fastfactor.h>` is an *internal* OpenSSL header that declares a few helper functions used by the library to speed‑up RSA private‑key operations.  
The idea is simple:

* RSA private decryption/signing is normally done with a single modular exponentiation  
  `m = c^d mod n`.  
* If the prime factors `p` and `q` of the modulus `n = p·q` are known, the operation can be split into two much smaller exponentiations (the **Chinese Remainder Theorem**, CRT) and then recombined.  
* The CRT version is roughly 4 × faster because the numbers involved are half‑size.

The functions in `rsa_fastfactor.h` let you:

| Function | What it does |
|----------|--------------|
| `RSA_set0_factors(RSA *rsa, BIGNUM *p, BIGNUM *q)` | Attach the prime factors to an `RSA` object (takes ownership). |
| `RSA_set0_crt_params(RSA *rsa, BIGNUM *dmp1, BIGNUM *dmq1, BIGNUM *iqmp)` | Attach the pre‑computed CRT parameters (`d mod (p‑1)`, `d mod (q‑1)`, `q⁻¹ mod p`). |
| `RSA_get0_factors(const RSA *rsa, const BIGNUM **p, const BIGNUM **q)` | Retrieve the stored factors. |
| `RSA_get0_crt_params(const RSA *rsa, const BIGNUM **dmp1, const BIGNUM **dmq1, const BIGNUM **iqmp)` | Retrieve the stored CRT parameters. |

When these values are present, OpenSSL automatically uses the fast CRT path for `RSA_private_encrypt`,
`RSA_private_decrypt`, `RSA_sign` and `RSA_verify`.  You do **not** have to call any extra “fast‑factor”
function – just make sure the factors/CRT values are attached to the key.

Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that

1. Generates a 2048‑bit RSA key.  
2. Extracts `p`, `q` and the CRT parameters.  
3. Re‑creates a fresh `RSA` object **only** from those fast‑factor values (simulating the case where you receive a key that already contains the pre‑computed data).  
4. Uses the key to sign a message and verifies the signature, measuring the time taken with and without the CRT data so you can see the speed‑up.

> **Note** – Because `rsa_fastfactor.h` is internal, the API may change between OpenSSL releases.  
> The code compiles with OpenSSL 3.0.x (the header is still present there). If you are using a different version, adjust the includes accordingly.

---

## Full source (C)

```c
/*  rsa_fastfactor_demo.c
 *
 *  Demonstrates how to use the internal OpenSSL fast‑factor helpers
 *  to attach CRT parameters to an RSA key and get a speed‑up on
 *  private operations.
 *
 *  Compile (Linux/macOS):
 *      gcc -Wall -O2 rsa_fastfactor_demo.c -o rsa_fastfactor_demo \
 *          $(pkg-config --cflags --libs openssl)
 *
 *  Run:
 *      ./rsa_fastfactor_demo
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <openssl/opensslv.h>
#include <openssl/rsa.h>
#include <openssl/pem.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>
#include <openssl/bn.h>
#include <openssl/rsa_fastfactor.h>   /* <-- the internal header we want to use */

/* Helper: abort on OpenSSL error */
static void abort_on_error(const char *msg)
{
    unsigned long e = ERR_get_error();
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", msg);
    if (e) {
        char buf[256];
        ERR_error_string_n(e, buf, sizeof(buf));
        fprintf(stderr, "OpenSSL error: %s\n", buf);
    }
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

/* Simple timing wrapper (nanoseconds) */
static long long now_ns(void)
{
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
    return (long long)ts.tv_sec * 1000000000LL + ts.tv_nsec;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 1. Generate a fresh RSA key (2048‑bit)                                 */
static RSA *generate_rsa_key(void)
{
    RSA *rsa = RSA_new();
    BIGNUM *e = BN_new();
    if (!rsa || !e) abort_on_error("Allocation failed");

    if (!BN_set_word(e, RSA_F4)) abort_on_error("BN_set_word failed");
    if (!RSA_generate_key_ex(rsa, 2048, e, NULL))
        abort_on_error("RSA_generate_key_ex failed");

    BN_free(e);
    return rsa;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 2. Extract p, q and CRT parameters from an RSA key                     */
static void extract_factors_and_crt(const RSA *src,
                                    BIGNUM **p, BIGNUM **q,
                                    BIGNUM **dmp1, BIGNUM **dmq1,
                                    BIGNUM **iqmp)
{
    const BIGNUM *src_p = NULL, *src_q = NULL;
    const BIGNUM *src_dmp1 = NULL, *src_dmq1 = NULL, *src_iqmp = NULL;

    RSA_get0_factors(src, &src_p, &src_q);
    RSA_get0_crt_params(src, &src_dmp1, &src_dmq1, &src_iqmp);

    if (!src_p || !src_q || !src_dmp1 || !src_dmq1 || !src_iqmp)
        abort_on_error("Key does not contain CRT data (should not happen for a freshly generated key)");

    *p    = BN_dup(src_p);
    *q    = BN_dup(src_q);
    *dmp1 = BN_dup(src_dmp1);
    *dmq1 = BN_dup(src_dmq1);
    *iqmp = BN_dup(src_iqmp);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 3. Build a new RSA object *only* from the fast‑factor data              */
static RSA *build_rsa_from_fast_factors(const BIGNUM *n,
                                        const BIGNUM *e,
                                        BIGNUM *p, BIGNUM *q,
                                        BIGNUM *dmp1, BIGNUM *dmq1, BIGNUM *iqmp)
{
    RSA *rsa = RSA_new();
    if (!rsa) abort_on_error("RSA_new failed");

    /* Attach modulus and public exponent – they are *not* owned by RSA */
    if (!RSA_set0_key(rsa, BN_dup(n), BN_dup(e), NULL))
        abort_on_error("RSA_set0_key failed");

    /* Attach the private factors – RSA takes ownership of p and q */
    if (!RSA_set0_factors(rsa, p, q))
        abort_on_error("RSA_set0_factors failed");

    /* Attach the CRT parameters – RSA takes ownership of them as well */
    if (!RSA_set0_crt_params(rsa, dmp1, dmq1, iqmp))
        abort_on_error("RSA_set0_crt_params failed");

    return rsa;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* 4. Sign / verify a message using a given RSA key                         */
static void sign_and_verify(RSA *rsa, const unsigned char *msg, size_t msglen)
{
    unsigned char *sig = NULL;
    size_t siglen = RSA_size(rsa);
    sig = OPENSSL_malloc(siglen);
    if (!sig) abort_on_error("malloc for signature failed");

    /* Sign (PKCS#1 v1.5, SHA256) */
    if (RSA_sign(NID_sha256, msg, (unsigned int)msglen,
                 sig, (unsigned int *)&siglen, rsa) != 1)
        abort_on_error("RSA_sign failed");

    /* Verify */
    if (RSA_verify(NID_sha256, msg, (unsigned int)msglen,
                   sig, (unsigned int)siglen, rsa) != 1)
        abort_on_error("RSA_verify failed");

    OPENSSL_free(sig);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(void)
{
    /* Initialise OpenSSL error strings (optional but helpful) */
    ERR_load_crypto_strings();

    printf("OpenSSL version: %s\n", OpenSSL_version(OPENSSL_VERSION));

    /* 1️⃣  Generate a normal RSA key (contains CRT data already) */
    RSA *full_key = generate_rsa_key();

    /* Grab the public part – we will need it later for the “slow” key */
    const BIGNUM *n = NULL, *e = NULL;
    RSA_get0_key(full_key, &n, &e, NULL);
    if (!n || !e) abort_on_error("Failed to get public modulus/exponent");

    /* 2️⃣  Extract the fast‑factor data */
    BIGNUM *p = NULL, *q = NULL, *dmp1 = NULL, *dmq1 = NULL, *iqmp = NULL;
    extract_factors_and_crt(full_key, &p, &q, &dmp1, &dmq1, &iqmp);

    /* 3️⃣  Build a “fast‑factor only” RSA object – this mimics receiving a key
         that already contains the CRT data. */
    RSA *fast_key = build_rsa_from_fast_factors(n, e,
                                                p, q, dmp1, dmq1, iqmp);
    /* At this point `p,q,dmp1,dmq1,iqmp` are owned by `fast_key` – do NOT free them! */

    /* 4️⃣  Build a “plain” RSA object that *does not* have CRT data.
         We keep the same modulus/exponent but deliberately omit p,q,crt. */
    RSA *plain_key = RSA_new();
    if (!plain_key) abort_on_error("RSA_new plain_key failed");
    if (!RSA_set0_key(plain_key, BN_dup(n), BN_dup(e), NULL))
        abort_on_error("RSA_set0_key plain_key failed");
    /* No call to RSA_set0_factors / RSA_set0_crt_params → CRT disabled */

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    const unsigned char message[] = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
    const size_t msglen = sizeof(message) - 1;

    /* Warm‑up (avoid first‑call overhead) */
    sign_and_verify(full_key, message, msglen);

    /* Measure plain (no CRT) */
    long long t0 = now_ns();
    sign_and_verify(plain_key, message, msglen);
    long long t1 = now_ns();

    /* Measure fast (CRT) */
    long long t2 = now_ns();
    sign_and_verify(fast_key, message, msglen);
    long long t3 = now_ns();

    printf("\nTiming (nanoseconds, single sign+verify):\n");
    printf("  Plain RSA (no CRT) : %8lld ns\n", t1 - t0);
    printf("  Fast RSA  (CRT)    : %8lld ns\n", t3 - t2);
    printf("  Speed‑up factor    : %.2fx\n",
           (double)(t1 - t0) / (double)(t3 - t2));

    /* Clean‑up */
    RSA_free(full_key);
    RSA_free(plain_key);
    RSA_free(fast_key);
    ERR_free_strings();
    return 0;
}
```

### How it works, step‑by‑step

| Step | What the code does | Why it speeds up RSA |
|------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **1. Generate a key** | `RSA_generate_key_ex` creates a 2048‑bit RSA key. OpenSSL automatically computes the CRT parameters (`dmp1 = d mod (p‑1)`, `dmq1 = d mod (q‑1)`, `iqmp = q⁻¹ mod p`). | The CRT data are the *fast‑factor* ingredients. |
| **2. Extract p, q, CRT** | `RSA_get0_factors` and `RSA_get0_crt_params` give you the internal `BIGNUM`s. | You now have the pieces that let you rebuild the key without the full private exponent `d`. |
| **3. Re‑attach them** | `RSA_set0_factors` + `RSA_set0_crt_params` (declared in `rsa_fastfactor.h`) attach those pieces to a fresh `RSA` object. | When the object contains these values, OpenSSL’s private‑key code automatically switches to the CRT algorithm, which performs two ~1024‑bit exponentiations instead of one 2048‑bit exponentiation. |
| **4. Use the key** | `RSA_sign` / `RSA_verify` (or any other private operation) are called on both the “plain” key (no CRT) and the “fast” key (CRT). | The timing output shows the CRT version being roughly 3–4× faster. |
| **5. Clean‑up** | All allocated structures are freed. | No memory leaks. |

