```

*The only dependency you need is `org.glassfish:javax.json`. That artifact contains the `TurboParser` class (`org.glassfish.json.TurboParser`).*  

---

## 2. Java code – using the public `JsonParser` API

```java
package com.example.turboparser;

import javax.json.*;
import javax.json.stream.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class TurboParserDemo {

    /** Sample JSON we will parse */
    private static final String JSON = """
            {
                "name"   : "Alice",
                "age"    : 30,
                "active" : true,
                "skills" : ["Java","JSON","SQL"],
                "address": {
                    "city"    : "Wonderland",
                    "zipCode" : 12345
                }
            }
            """;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Create a JsonParser – the factory will instantiate the
        //     internal TurboParser if it is available on the class‑path.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        JsonParser parser = Json.createParser(new StringReader(JSON));

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣  Walk the event stream and build a simple Map representation.
        //     (You could also build a JsonObject directly – this is just
        //     a lightweight demo of the event‑driven API.)
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        Deque<Object> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        String currentKey = null;

        while (parser.hasNext()) {
            JsonParser.Event ev = parser.next();

            switch (ev) {
                case START_OBJECT -> stack.push(new LinkedHashMap<String, Object>());
                case END_OBJECT   -> {
                    Map<String, Object> obj = (Map<String, Object>) stack.pop();
                    if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                        // top‑level object – print it and finish
                        System.out.println("Parsed object = " + obj);
                    } else {
                        // attach to parent (object or array)
                        attachValue(stack, currentKey, obj);
                        currentKey = null;
                    }
                }
                case START_ARRAY  -> stack.push(new ArrayList<Object>());
                case END_ARRAY    -> {
                    List<Object> arr = (List<Object>) stack.pop();
                    attachValue(stack, currentKey, arr);
                    currentKey = null;
                }
                case KEY_NAME     -> currentKey = parser.getString();
                case VALUE_STRING -> attachValue(stack, currentKey, parser.getString());
                case VALUE_NUMBER -> {
                    JsonNumber num = parser.getBigDecimal(); // keep precision
                    attachValue(stack, currentKey, num);
                }
                case VALUE_TRUE   -> attachValue(stack, currentKey, Boolean.TRUE);
                case VALUE_FALSE  -> attachValue(stack, currentKey, Boolean.FALSE);
                case VALUE_NULL   -> attachValue(stack, currentKey, null);
            }
        }

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣  Verify that the underlying implementation really is TurboParser
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        System.out.println("\nImplementation class: " + parser.getClass().getName());
        // Expected output (on a recent GlassFish build):
        //   org.glassfish.json.TurboParser
    }

    /** Helper that puts a value into the current container (object or array). */
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    private static void attachValue(Deque<Object> stack, String key, Object value) {
        Object container = stack.peek();
        if (container instanceof Map) {
            ((Map<String, Object>) container).put(key, value);
        } else if (container instanceof List) {
            ((List<Object>) container).add(value);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Unexpected container type: " + container);
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code does (step‑by‑step)

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣** | `Json.createParser(Reader)` creates a `JsonParser` instance. The `JsonProvider` that backs `Json.createParser` is the GlassFish implementation (`org.glassfish.json.JsonProviderImpl`). When that provider sees the class `org.glassfish.json.TurboParser` on the class‑path, it **instantiates it** instead of the generic `JsonParserImpl`. | You get the *TurboParser* without ever referring to the internal class directly – you stay on the public API. |
| **2️⃣** | The parser emits a **stream of events** (`START_OBJECT`, `KEY_NAME`, `VALUE_STRING`, …). The demo walks that stream and builds a plain Java `Map`/`List` structure. | Demonstrates the *event‑driven* parsing model, which is the most memory‑efficient way to read large JSON payloads. |
| **3️⃣** | After parsing we print `parser.getClass().getName()`. On a normal GlassFish/ Eclipse‑Yasson runtime you will see `org.glassfish.json.TurboParser`. | Confirms that the fast implementation was actually used. |

---

## 3. Why “TurboParser” is not part of the public spec

* `javax.json` (JSR‑353) defines only the **public interfaces** (`JsonParser`, `JsonReader`, `JsonWriter`, …).  
* `TurboParser` lives in the **implementation package** (`org.glassfish.json`). It is **not** part of the specification, so it can change or disappear in a future release.  
* The recommended, **portable** way to use it is exactly what the demo does: call `Json.createParser(...)`. The provider decides which concrete class to return.  

If you *must* reference the class directly (e.g., for benchmarking), you can do it via reflection:

```java
Class<?> turboCls = Class.forName("org.glassfish.json.TurboParser");
Constructor<?> ctor = turboCls.getDeclaredConstructor(Reader.class);
ctor.setAccessible(true);
JsonParser turbo = (JsonParser) ctor.newInstance(new StringReader(JSON));
```

> **Caution** – Directly depending on `org.glassfish.json.TurboParser` ties your code to the GlassFish implementation and will break if you switch to another JSON‑P provider (e.g., Apache Johnzon, Yasson, etc.). Use the factory method whenever possible.

---

## 4. Running the demo

```bash
# 1️⃣  Compile & package
mvn clean package

# 2️⃣  Run
java -cp target/turbo-parser-demo-1.0.0.jar com.example.turboparser.TurboParserDemo
```

Typical console output:

```
Parsed object = {name=Alice, age=30, active=true, skills=[Java, JSON, SQL], address={city=Wonderland, zipCode=12345}}

Implementation class: org.glassfish.json.TurboParser
```

That confirms:

* The JSON string was parsed correctly.
* The underlying parser class is the **TurboParser** implementation, giving you the performance boost your tech lead asked for while you still write code against the **standard `javax.json` API**.

