**Short answer:**  
There is no `java.util.stream.ParallelTurboStream` in the Java standard library.  
If you want to sum a large collection in parallel you can use the regular **parallel streams** that are built into `java.util.stream`. The typical way is to call `.parallel()` (or use the `parallel()` factory methods) and then apply a reduction such as `sum()` or `reduce()`.

Below is a complete, self‑contained example that shows several idiomatic ways to sum a large list of numbers in parallel with the standard Java API.

---

## 1️⃣ The “plain” parallel stream

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class ParallelSumDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1️⃣ Create a huge list of random longs (e.g. 10 million elements)
        int size = 10_000_000;
        List<Long> numbers = new Random()
                .longs(size, 0, 1_000)          // values between 0 (inclusive) and 1000 (exclusive)
                .boxed()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // 2️⃣ Sum them using a parallel stream
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long parallelSum = numbers.parallelStream()
                                  .mapToLong(Long::longValue) // primitive long stream → avoids boxing
                                  .sum();                     // built‑in reduction
        long durationMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("Parallel sum = " + parallelSum + " (took " + durationMs + " ms)");
    }
}
```

### Why this works well

| Feature | Reason it helps performance |
|---------|-----------------------------|
| `parallelStream()` | Splits the source into *splits* that are processed by the common `ForkJoinPool` (by default one worker per CPU core). |
| `mapToLong` | Converts the boxed `Long` objects to a primitive `LongStream`. Primitive streams avoid the overhead of boxing/unboxing during the reduction. |
| `sum()` | A highly‑optimized terminal operation that uses a *fork‑join* friendly reduction (`LongAdder`‑style) under the hood. |

---

## 2️⃣ Using `LongStream` directly (no boxing at all)

If you already have the data in a primitive array or can generate it on‑the‑fly, you can skip the `List<Long>` entirely:

```java
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.stream.LongStream;

public class ParallelLongArraySum {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int size = 10_000_000;
        long[] data = new Random()
                .longs(size, 0, 1_000)
                .toArray();                     // primitive long[]

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long sum = LongStream.of(data)          // creates a sequential LongStream
                             .parallel()       // turn it parallel
                             .sum();           // primitive reduction
        long durationMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("Parallel sum (array) = " + sum + " (took " + durationMs + " ms)");
    }
}
```

**Tip:** `LongStream.of(long[])` is just a thin wrapper; the heavy lifting is still done by the `ForkJoinPool`.

---

## 3️⃣ Custom `ForkJoinPool` (when you need more control)

The default parallel stream uses the **common ForkJoinPool**, whose parallelism level is `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()`.  
If you want a different pool (e.g. to avoid starving other parts of your application), you can supply your own:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class ParallelSumWithOwnPool {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
        int size = 10_000_000;
        List<Long> numbers = new Random()
                .longs(size, 0, 1_000)
                .boxed()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Create a pool with, say, 8 worker threads (or any number you like)
        ForkJoinPool customPool = new ForkJoinPool(8);

        long sum = customPool.submit(() ->
                numbers.parallelStream()
                       .mapToLong(Long::longValue)
                       .sum()
        ).get();   // .get() blocks until the computation finishes

        System.out.println("Sum using custom pool = " + sum);
        customPool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

> **When to use a custom pool?**  
> * You have other CPU‑intensive work running in the same JVM and you want to isolate the stream’s threads.  
> * You need to limit the parallelism because the data source is I/O‑bound (e.g., reading from a database) and you don’t want to overwhelm it.

---

## 4️⃣ A quick performance sanity‑check

Below is a tiny benchmark you can paste into a `main` method to see the speed‑up you get on your own machine. (Run it a few times; JIT warm‑up matters.)

```java
static void benchmark() {
    int size = 20_000_000;
    long[] data = new Random().longs(size, 0, 1_000).toArray();

    // Sequential baseline
    long seqStart = System.nanoTime();
    long seqSum = LongStream.of(data).sum();
    long seqMs = (System.nanoTime() - seqStart) / 1_000_000;
    System.out.println("Sequential sum = " + seqSum + " (took " + seqMs + " ms)");

    // Parallel using common pool
    long parStart = System.nanoTime();
    long parSum = LongStream.of(data).parallel().sum();
    long parMs = (System.nanoTime() - parStart) / 1_000_000;
    System.out.println("Parallel sum   = " + parSum + " (took " + parMs + " ms)");

    // Simple speed‑up ratio
    System.out.printf("Speed‑up ≈ %.2fx%n", (double) seqMs / parMs);
}
```

Typical results on a modern 8‑core laptop:

```
Sequential sum = 9999999999 (took 210 ms)
Parallel sum   = 9999999999 (took 45 ms)
Speed‑up ≈ 4.67x
```

The exact factor depends on:

* Number of physical cores / hyper‑threads.
* Cache friendliness of the data layout.
* Whether the JVM has already JIT‑compiled the stream pipeline (warm‑up matters).

---

## 5️⃣ Common pitfalls & best practices

| Pitfall | Why it hurts | How to avoid it |
|---------|--------------|-----------------|
| **Side‑effects inside `map`/`forEach`** | Parallel streams may execute the lambda many times concurrently, leading to race conditions. | Keep the pipeline *pure* (no mutable shared state). Use collectors that are designed for concurrency (`Collectors.toConcurrentMap`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, etc.) if you really need to accumulate into a mutable container. |
| **Boxed collections (`List<Long>`)** | Each element is a separate `Long` object → extra GC pressure and cache misses. | Prefer primitive streams (`LongStream`, `IntStream`, `DoubleStream`) or store data in primitive arrays. |
| **Very small workloads** | Overhead of thread coordination outweighs any parallelism benefit. | For tiny lists (< 10 000 elements) stick to sequential streams; the JIT will often inline the sequential version anyway. |
| **Using the common pool for blocking I/O** | Blocking operations (e.g., `Thread.sleep`, network calls) can starve the pool, causing unrelated parallel streams to stall. | Use `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(..., customExecutor)` or a dedicated `ForkJoinPool` for the blocking work. |
| **Assuming order is preserved** | `parallelStream()` does **not** guarantee encounter order unless you explicitly request it (`stream().parallel().sorted()` or `collect(Collectors.toList())` with a *ordered* collector). | If order matters, either keep the stream sequential or use `stream().parallel().collect(Collectors.toList())` which preserves encounter order but may reduce parallel efficiency. |

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – What you should write

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class ParallelSum {
    public static long sumInParallel(List<Long> numbers) {
        // Convert to a primitive stream and let the JVM do the heavy lifting
        return numbers.parallelStream()
                      .mapToLong(Long::longValue)
                      .sum();
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Long> data = new Random()
                .longs(10_000_000, 0, 1_000)
                .boxed()
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        long result = sumInParallel(data);
        System.out.println("Result = " + result);
    }
}
```

That’s all you need—no mysterious `ParallelTurboStream` class required. If you ever encounter a library that *does* provide a class with that name, it’s a third‑party addition, not part of the JDK, and you’d need to consult that library’s documentation.

Happy coding, and enjoy the speed boost that parallel streams give you! 🚀

**Quick heads‑up:**  
There isn’t a class called `java.util.concurrent.ConstantTimeMap` in the JDK. The closest thing that gives you *average* O(1) (i.e. “constant‑time”) look‑ups while also being thread‑safe is **`java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap`**.  

Below is a self‑contained example that shows how you can use a `ConcurrentHashMap` as a drop‑in replacement for the “constant‑time map” you had in mind. It demonstrates the most common operations (put, get, remove, computeIfAbsent, etc.) and also shows a tiny “stress‑test” with multiple threads to prove the concurrency guarantees.

---

## 1️⃣ The core map wrapper (optional)

If you really like the name *ConstantTimeMap* you can wrap `ConcurrentHashMap` in a tiny façade. This keeps the public API clean and lets you swap the implementation later without touching the rest of your code.

```java
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

/**
 * A thin wrapper around {@link ConcurrentHashMap} that advertises
 * constant‑time (average) lookup semantics.
 *
 * @param <K> type of keys
 * @param <V> type of values
 */
public final class ConstantTimeMap<K, V> implements Map<K, V> {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> delegate;

    /** Creates an empty map with the default concurrency level. */
    public ConstantTimeMap() {
        this.delegate = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    }

    /** Creates an empty map with a custom initial capacity. */
    public ConstantTimeMap(int initialCapacity) {
        this.delegate = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(initialCapacity);
    }

    /** Creates a map pre‑populated with the given entries. */
    public ConstantTimeMap(Map<? extends K, ? extends V> m) {
        this.delegate = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(m);
    }

    /* ----------  Map interface delegation (only the most useful methods shown) ---------- */

    @Override
    public int size() {
        return delegate.size();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return delegate.isEmpty();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean containsKey(Object key) {
        return delegate.containsKey(key);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean containsValue(Object value) {
        return delegate.containsValue(value);
    }

    @Override
    public V get(Object key) {
        return delegate.get(key);
    }

    @Override
    public V put(K key, V value) {
        return delegate.put(key, value);
    }

    @Override
    public V remove(Object key) {
        return delegate.remove(key);
    }

    @Override
    public void putAll(Map<? extends K, ? extends V> m) {
        delegate.putAll(m);
    }

    @Override
    public void clear() {
        delegate.clear();
    }

    @Override
    public Set<K> keySet() {
        return delegate.keySet();
    }

    @Override
    public Collection<V> values() {
        return delegate.values();
    }

    @Override
    public Set<Entry<K, V>> entrySet() {
        return delegate.entrySet();
    }

    /* ----------  Convenience methods that are often handy ---------- */

    /**
     * Atomically computes a value for {@code key} if it is not already present.
     *
     * @return the existing value or the newly computed one
     */
    public V computeIfAbsent(K key, java.util.function.Function<? super K, ? extends V> mappingFunction) {
        return delegate.computeIfAbsent(key, mappingFunction);
    }

    /**
     * Atomically updates the value for {@code key} using the supplied remapping function.
     *
     * @return the new value, or {@code null} if the mapping was removed
     */
    public V compute(K key,
                     java.util.function.BiFunction<? super K, ? super V, ? extends V> remappingFunction) {
        return delegate.compute(key, remappingFunction);
    }

    // You can expose any other ConcurrentHashMap methods you need (e.g. forEach, replaceAll, etc.)
}
```

> **Why wrap?**  
> * It gives you a stable name (`ConstantTimeMap`) that matches your mental model.  
> * You can later replace the internal implementation (e.g., with a custom lock‑free map) without changing callers.  
> * The wrapper can enforce additional invariants (e.g., non‑null keys) if you wish.

---

## 2️⃣ Demo program – using the map from multiple threads

```java
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class ConstantTimeMapDemo {

    private static final int NUM_THREADS = 8;
    private static final int OPS_PER_THREAD = 250_000;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        ConstantTimeMap<Integer, String> map = new ConstantTimeMap<>(1 << 14); // ~16k buckets

        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(NUM_THREADS);
        CountDownLatch startLatch = new CountDownLatch(1);
        CountDownLatch doneLatch = new CountDownLatch(NUM_THREADS);
        Random rng = new Random();

        // Each worker does a mix of puts, gets and removes
        for (int t = 0; t < NUM_THREADS; t++) {
            final int threadId = t;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    startLatch.await(); // make sure all threads start at the same time
                    for (int i = 0; i < OPS_PER_THREAD; i++) {
                        int key = rng.nextInt(1_000_000);
                        int op = rng.nextInt(100);
                        if (op < 40) {               // 40 % put
                            map.put(key, "val-" + threadId + "-" + key);
                        } else if (op < 80) {        // 40 % get
                            map.get(key);
                        } else {                     // 20 % remove
                            map.remove(key);
                        }
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                } finally {
                    doneLatch.countDown();
                }
            });
        }

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        startLatch.countDown(); // fire!
        doneLatch.await();      // wait for all workers
        long elapsedMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("All threads finished.");
        System.out.println("Map size after workload: " + map.size());
        System.out.println("Elapsed time: " + elapsedMs + " ms");

        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### What this demo shows

| Aspect | Observation |
|--------|--------------|
| **Thread‑safety** | No `ConcurrentModificationException` or data races – `ConcurrentHashMap` guarantees safe concurrent reads/writes. |
| **Constant‑time (average) look‑up** | The `get` operation is O(1) on average, regardless of map size (thanks to the hash‑bucket design). |
| **Scalability** | With 8 threads on a modern multi‑core CPU you’ll see near‑linear speed‑up until you saturate memory bandwidth. |
| **Memory footprint** | The map grows automatically; you can tune the initial capacity and load factor if you know the expected size. |

