## 3️⃣ Quick cheat‑sheet of the most useful `ConcurrentHashMap`/`ConstantTimeMap` methods

| Method | What it does | Typical use‑case |
|--------|--------------|------------------|
| `V get(Object key)` | Constant‑time read (no locking for most reads) | Look‑up a cached value |
| `V put(K key, V value)` | Inserts or overwrites a mapping | Populate the cache |
| `V remove(Object key)` | Deletes a mapping | Eviction / cleanup |
| `V computeIfAbsent(K key, Function<? super K,? extends V> f)` | Atomically compute & store only if missing | Lazy initialization (e.g., memoization) |
| `V compute(K key, BiFunction<? super K,? super V,? extends V> f)` | Atomically recompute a value (can return `null` to delete) | Update‑in‑place counters, aggregates |
| `boolean replace(K key, V oldValue, V newValue)` | CAS‑style replace only if current value matches | Optimistic concurrency |
| `void forEach(long parallelismThreshold, BiConsumer<? super K,? super V> action)` | Parallel bulk operation (uses Fork/Join) | Dumping the map, bulk transformations |
| `int size()` | Approximate size (fast) – exact size may be slightly stale in highly concurrent scenarios | Monitoring / metrics |

