If you don’t need the wrapper and just want the simplest possible constant‑time concurrent map:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class SimpleDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Integer, String> constantTimeMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        // put
        constantTimeMap.put(1, "one");
        constantTimeMap.put(2, "two");

        // get
        System.out.println(constantTimeMap.get(1)); // → "one"

        // computeIfAbsent (lazy init)
        constantTimeMap.computeIfAbsent(3, k -> "three-" + k);
        System.out.println(constantTimeMap.get(3)); // → "three-3"

        // remove
        constantTimeMap.remove(2);
        System.out.println(constantTimeMap.containsKey(2)); // → false
    }
}
```

That’s all you need for a thread‑safe, average‑constant‑time lookup map in pure Java.

