## TL;DR  
*There is **no** `java.util.cache.AutoLRU` class in the JDK.*  
If you saw that name it belongs to a **third‑party library** (or it was a typo).  
You have three practical ways to get an LRU cache in Java:

| Option | What you need | When to use it |
|--------|---------------|----------------|
| **`LinkedHashMap` (JDK only)** | No external dependency | Simple, single‑threaded or manually synchronized caches |
| **Caffeine** (`com.github.ben-manes.caffeine`) | Add a Maven/Gradle dependency | High‑performance, thread‑safe, supports async loading, expiration, size‑based eviction |
| **Guava Cache** (`com.google.guava:guava`) | Add a Maven/Gradle dependency | Good for most cases, but a bit heavier than Caffeine and no longer the “fastest” option |

Below you’ll find **complete, ready‑to‑run examples** for each approach, plus a short guide on how to add the required libraries to your project.

---

## 1️⃣ Using `LinkedHashMap` (pure JDK)

`LinkedHashMap` has a special constructor that lets you keep entries in **access order**. By overriding `removeEldestEntry` you can automatically evict the least‑recently‑used entry when the map grows beyond a fixed size.

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Simple LRU cache based on LinkedHashMap.
 *
 * @param <K> key type
 * @param <V> value type
 */
public class LruCache<K, V> {

    private final Map<K, V> map;

    /**
     * Creates an LRU cache that holds at most {@code maxSize} entries.
     *
     * @param maxSize maximum number of entries; must be > 0
     */
    public LruCache(final int maxSize) {
        if (maxSize <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("maxSize must be > 0");
        }

        // true → access‑order (LRU). false → insertion‑order.
        this.map = Collections.synchronizedMap(
                new LinkedHashMap<K, V>(maxSize, 0.75f, true) {
                    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

                    @Override
                    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
                        // When size exceeds maxSize, remove the eldest (LRU) entry.
                        return size() > maxSize;
                    }
                });
    }

    /** Put a value in the cache. */
    public V put(K key, V value) {
        return map.put(key, value);
    }

    /** Retrieve a value; returns null if not present. */
    public V get(K key) {
        return map.get(key);
    }

    /** Remove a key from the cache. */
    public V remove(K key) {
        return map.remove(key);
    }

    /** Current number of entries. */
    public int size() {
        return map.size();
    }

    /** For debugging – prints the cache contents from LRU → MRU. */
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return map.toString();
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Demo
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
class DemoLinkedHashMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LruCache<Integer, String> cache = new LruCache<>(3);

        cache.put(1, "one");
        cache.put(2, "two");
        cache.put(3, "three");
        System.out.println(cache); // {1=one, 2=two, 3=three}

        // Access 1 → it becomes MRU
        cache.get(1);
        // Add a fourth entry → the LRU entry (2) is evicted
        cache.put(4, "four");
        System.out.println(cache); // {3=three, 1=one, 4=four}
    }
}
```

### Why `Collections.synchronizedMap`?
`LinkedHashMap` itself is **not thread‑safe**. Wrapping it with `Collections.synchronizedMap` gives you a simple, coarse‑grained lock that works for most single‑process use‑cases. If you need higher concurrency, move to Caffeine (see next section).

---

## 2️⃣ Using **Caffeine** – the modern, high‑performance cache library

Caffeine is the de‑facto successor to Guava’s cache. It’s:

* **Thread‑safe** out of the box.
* Extremely fast (benchmarks show it beats Guava and many hand‑rolled maps).
* Feature‑rich: size‑based eviction, time‑based expiration, refresh, async loading, statistics, etc.

### 2.1 Add the dependency

**Maven**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId>
    <artifactId>caffeine</artifactId>
    <version>3.1.8</version>   <!-- check for newer releases -->
</dependency>
```

**Gradle (Kotlin DSL)**

```kotlin
implementation("com.github.ben-manes.caffeine:caffeine:3.1.8")
```

### 2.2 Build an LRU cache

```java
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

/**
 * Simple wrapper around Caffeine that behaves like an LRU cache.
 * Caffeine uses a *window‑tiny‑LFU* policy internally, which is a
 * superset of LRU and works even better under realistic workloads.
 */
public class CaffeineLruCache<K, V> {

    private final Cache<K, V> cache;

    /**
     * Creates a cache that holds at most {@code maxSize} entries.
     *
     * @param maxSize maximum number of entries; must be > 0
     */
    public CaffeineLruCache(long maxSize) {
        this.cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(maxSize)               // size‑based eviction (LRU‑like)
                .expireAfterAccess(0, TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS) // disable time‑based eviction
                .recordStats()                      // optional: enables stats collection
                .build();
    }

    /** Put a value in the cache. */
    public void put(K key, V value) {
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    /** Retrieve a value; returns null if not present. */
    public V getIfPresent(K key) {
        return cache.getIfPresent(key);
    }

    /** Remove a key from the cache. */
    public void invalidate(K key) {
        cache.invalidate(key);
    }

    /** Current number of entries (approximate). */
    public long estimatedSize() {
        return cache.estimatedSize();
    }

    /** Print cache statistics (hits, misses, evictions, …). */
    public void printStats() {
        System.out.println(cache.stats());
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Demo
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
class DemoCaffeine {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CaffeineLruCache<Integer, String> cache = new CaffeineLruCache<>(3);

        cache.put(1, "one");
        cache.put(2, "two");
        cache.put(3, "three");
        System.out.println(cache.getIfPresent(1)); // "one" (makes 1 MRU)

        // Insert a fourth entry → the *least‑used* entry is evicted.
        cache.put(4, "four");

        System.out.println(cache.getIfPresent(2)); // null (2 was evicted)
        System.out.println(cache.getIfPresent(3)); // "three"
        System.out.println(cache.getIfPresent(1)); // "one"
        System.out.println(cache.getIfPresent(4)); // "four"

        cache.printStats(); // e.g. CacheStats{hitCount=4, missCount=1, …}
    }
}
```

#### Why “LRU‑like”?
Caffeine’s default eviction algorithm is **Window TinyLFU**. It approximates true LRU while also protecting frequently accessed items from being evicted too early. For most applications you’ll get *better* results than a naïve LRU.

If you *really* need strict LRU semantics, you can force it:

```java
Caffeine.newBuilder()
        .maximumSize(maxSize)
        .evictionListener((key, value, cause) -> {
            // optional: log evictions
        })
        .scheduler(Scheduler.systemScheduler()) // optional: for timed expiration
        .build();
```

Caffeine does not expose a “strict LRU” switch because the tiny‑LFU algorithm is provably superior in the majority of real‑world workloads.

