Below is a **complete, minimal‑working example** that shows how to:

1. **Install** the `@secure-io/sanitize` package.  
2. **Import** it in a Node‑JS script (or in a browser bundle).  
3. **Sanitize** a string of user‑supplied HTML.  
4. **Verify** that the dangerous parts are stripped while the allowed markup stays intact.

You can copy‑paste the code, run it, and see the output immediately.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the package

```bash
# From your project root
npm install @secure-io/sanitize
```

> `@secure-io/sanitize` has **zero runtime dependencies**, so the install is fast and the bundle size stays tiny.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal Node script (`sanitize-demo.js`)

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the sanitizer
// ------------------------------------------------------------
import { sanitize } from '@secure-io/sanitize';

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Define the HTML you want to clean
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const userSuppliedHtml = `
  <h1>Hello <em>world</em>!</h1>
  <script>alert('Gotcha!');</script>
  <img src="javascript:alert('XSS')" onerror="alert('XSS')" />
  <a href="https://example.com" onclick="stealCookies()">click me</a>
  <p style="color:red; position:fixed; top:0;">Styled text</p>
`;

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Call `sanitize()`
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// By default the sanitizer uses a safe‑default policy that
//   • allows common formatting tags (b, i, em, strong, u, p, h1‑h6, ul, ol, li, blockquote, code, pre, etc.)
//   • allows safe attributes (href on <a>, src on <img>, alt, title, etc.)
//   • strips everything else (script, style, event handlers, javascript: URLs, etc.)
const cleanHtml = sanitize(userSuppliedHtml);

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Show the result
// ------------------------------------------------------------
console.log('--- ORIGINAL ---------------------------------------------------');
console.log(userSuppliedHtml);
console.log('\n--- SANITIZED -------------------------------------------------');
console.log(cleanHtml);
```

### Run it

```bash
node sanitize-demo.js
```

**Expected console output**

```
--- ORIGINAL ---------------------------------------------------
<h1>Hello <em>world</em>!</h1>
<script>alert('Gotcha!');</script>
<img src="javascript:alert('XSS')" onerror="alert('XSS')" />
<a href="https://example.com" onclick="stealCookies()">click me</a>
<p style="color:red; position:fixed; top:0;">Styled text</p>


--- SANITIZED -------------------------------------------------
<h1>Hello <em>world</em>!</h1>
<img />
<a href="https://example.com">click me</a>
<p style="color:red;">Styled text</p>
```

*Notice what happened:*

| Original element | What the sanitizer did |
|------------------|------------------------|
| `<script>` tag   | **Removed entirely** |
| `<img src="javascript:…">` | Stripped the dangerous `src` value, left an empty `<img>` (you can configure a placeholder if you like) |
| `onerror`, `onclick` attributes | **Removed** (event handlers are never allowed) |
| `style="position:fixed; …"` | Kept only the safe CSS property (`color`) – the rest was stripped |
| `<a href="…">`   | Kept the `href` because it’s a safe URL scheme (`http`, `https`, `mailto`, etc.) |

