## 3️⃣ Using it in a **web server** (Express example)

If you want to sanitise incoming HTML before you store it or send it back to a client, you can do it right inside your route handler:

```js
import express from 'express';
import { sanitize } from '@secure-io/sanitize';

const app = express();
app.use(express.json()); // parse JSON bodies

app.post('/save-article', (req, res) => {
  const { title, body } = req.body; // `body` is user‑supplied HTML

  // Sanitize the HTML *once* before persisting it
  const safeBody = sanitize(body);

  // …store `title` and `safeBody` in your DB (omitted)
  // For demo we just echo it back:
  res.json({ title, safeBody });
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('🚀 server listening on http://localhost:3000'));
```

Now any POST like:

```json
{
  "title": "My article",
  "body": "<h2>Hi</h2><script>evil()</script>"
}
```

will be stored as:

```json
{
  "title": "My article",
  "safeBody": "<h2>Hi</h2>"
}
```

