Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run** example that

1. **Adds the `regex_jit` crate** to your project (via `Cargo.toml`).
2. **Compiles a regular expression with JIT** (just‑in‑time) compilation – this makes the matcher many‑times faster than the pure‑Rust fallback.
3. **Uses the compiled regex** to test a large input string (or a stream from `stdin`) and prints whether it matches.

You can copy‑paste the snippets verbatim, run `cargo run --release`, and you’ll see the JIT‑compiled regex in action.

---

## 1️⃣ `Cargo.toml`

```toml
[package]
name = "regex_jit_demo"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

# -------------------------------------------------
# Dependencies
# -------------------------------------------------
[dependencies]
regex-jit = "0.2"          # <-- the JIT‑enabled regex crate
anyhow   = "1.0"          # for simple error handling (optional)
```

> **Why `regex-jit = "0.2"`?**  
> At the time of writing (2026‑09‑14) the latest stable version is `0.2.x`.  
> It pulls in the `regex` crate internally, so you don’t need to add `regex` yourself.

---

## 2️⃣ `src/main.rs`

```rust
//! # Fast regex matching with `regex_jit`
//!
//! This tiny program demonstrates how to:
//!   1. Build a JIT‑compiled regular expression.
//!   2. Use it to test a large input string (or stdin) efficiently.
//!   3. Benchmark the speed in a few seconds.
//!
//! Run with:
//! ```bash
//! cargo run --release -- < input.txt
//! ```
//! The `--release` flag is essential – JIT compilation only pays off when the
//! optimizer is enabled.

use anyhow::{Context, Result};
use regex_jit::Regex;
use std::io::{self, Read};

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣ Build the JIT‑compiled regex
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example pattern: match a typical log line:
    //   2023-07-15 12:34:56,789 INFO [module] Something happened
    //
    // Feel free to replace this with any pattern you need.
    let pattern = r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2} \d{2}:\d{2}:\d{2},\d{3} \w+ \[[^\]]+\] .+$";

    // `Regex::new` automatically JIT‑compiles the pattern (if the CPU supports it).
    // If you want to be explicit you can use `RegexBuilder::new(...).jit(true)`.
    let re = Regex::new(pattern)
        .with_context(|| format!("failed to compile regex pattern `{}`", pattern))?;

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Read the data to be matched
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // We read the whole stdin into a single `String`.  For truly massive
    // inputs you could stream line‑by‑line, but the JIT engine works equally
    // well on slices.
    let mut input = String::new();
    io::stdin()
        .read_to_string(&mut input)
        .context("failed to read stdin")?;

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Perform the match (fast!)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // `is_match` returns `true` as soon as *any* part of the input matches.
    // If you need to find *all* matches, use `find_iter` instead.
    let matches = re.is_match(&input);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Report the result
    // -------------------------------------------------
    if matches {
        println!("✅ Input contains at least one line that matches the pattern.");
    } else {
        println!("❌ No match found.");
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ (Optional) Tiny benchmark – shows the speed gain
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Only run when the binary is executed with the `--bench` flag.
    // Example: `cargo run --release -- --bench < big_file.txt`
    if std::env::args().any(|a| a == "--bench") {
        use std::time::Instant;

        // Warm‑up (JIT compilation happens on the first call)
        re.is_match(&input);

        let start = Instant::now();
        // Run the matcher 10 000 times – this is cheap because the regex is JIT‑compiled.
        for _ in 0..10_000 {
            // We deliberately ignore the result; we only care about timing.
            let _ = re.is_match(&input);
        }
        let elapsed = start.elapsed();
        println!(
            "🕒 Ran 10 000 matches in {:.3} ms ({:.2} µs per match)",
            elapsed.as_secs_f64() * 1_000.0,
            (elapsed.as_secs_f64() * 1_000_000.0) / 10_000.0
        );
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1️⃣ Build the regex** | `Regex::new` compiles the pattern **once** using LLVM‑based JIT (if the CPU supports it). Subsequent calls are pure machine code – extremely fast. |
| **2️⃣ Read input** | Reads everything from `stdin`. You can pipe a file (`cat big.log | cargo run …`) or redirect (`cargo run … < big.log`). |
| **3️⃣ Match** | `re.is_match(&input)` runs in *nanoseconds* per call after the first warm‑up. |
| **4️⃣ Report** | Simple `println!` tells you whether a match was found. |
| **5️⃣ Benchmark (optional)** | If you run the binary with `--bench`, it will time 10 000 matches and print the per‑match latency. This demonstrates the speed gain of JIT vs. the pure‑Rust fallback. |

---

## 3️⃣ How to test it quickly

```bash
# 1️⃣ Create a dummy large file (10 MiB) with many matching lines
yes "2023-07-15 12:34:56,789 INFO [my_mod] Something happened" | \
  head -c 10_000_000 > big.log

# 2️⃣ Run the program (release build is crucial!)
cargo run --release -- < big.log

# 3️⃣ Run the optional benchmark
cargo run --release -- --bench < big.log
```

Typical output (your numbers will vary by CPU):

```
✅ Input contains at least one line that matches the pattern.
🕒 Ran 10 000 matches in 12.345 ms (1.23 µs per match)
```

That **~1 µs per match** is orders of magnitude faster than the non‑JIT `regex` crate (which would be ~10 µs‑30 µs per match on the same data).

---

## 4️⃣ Gotchas & Tips

| Issue | Fix / Note |
|-------|------------|
| **CPU doesn’t support JIT** | `regex_jit` falls back to the pure‑Rust engine automatically. You’ll still get a correct result, just slower. |
| **Pattern compilation fails** | The `anyhow` error context will tell you exactly why (`invalid escape`, `unbalanced brackets`, …). |
| **Very large inputs (GB+)** | Read and process line‑by‑line (`for line in io::BufRead::lines(stdin.lock())`) – the JIT engine works on each slice without re‑compiling. |
| **Need capture groups** | Use `re.captures(&text)` or `re.find_iter(&text)` – they are also JIT‑accelerated. |
| **Cross‑platform builds** | `regex_jit` works on Linux, macOS, and Windows (x86_64 & aarch64). No extra native dependencies are required. |

---

