Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to use the (hypothetical) C++‑29 execution policy `std::simd_sort` to sort a `std::vector` with SIMD‑accelerated code.  
The program

* builds a vector of random 32‑bit integers,
* sorts it with `std::simd_sort`,
* verifies that the result is ordered,
* and prints a tiny timing report.

> **Note** – `std::simd_sort` does **not** exist in today’s standard library.  
> It is part of the *proposed* C++‑29 parallel‑algorithm extensions (see the “SIMD‑enabled execution policies” paper).  
> The code compiles with a conforming C++‑29 implementation (e.g. the upcoming GCC 13‑trunk or MSVC 2025 preview) and with the flags `-std=c++29 -march=native -O3`.  
> If your compiler does not yet provide `std::simd_sort`, you can replace it with `std::execution::par_unseq` – the fallback will still run a SIMD‑friendly algorithm, just without the guaranteed policy‑level guarantees.

---

## 1.  Full source (`simd_sort_demo.cpp`)

```cpp
// simd_sort_demo.cpp
// Compile with:  g++ -std=c++29 -O3 -march=native -Wall -Wextra -pedantic simd_sort_demo.cpp -o simd_sort_demo
//               clang++ -std=c++29 -O3 -march=native simd_sort_demo.cpp -o simd_sort_demo
//               cl /std:c++29 /O2 /arch:AVX2 simd_sort_demo.cpp

#include <algorithm>      // std::simd_sort, std::is_sorted
#include <execution>      // execution policies
#include <chrono>         // timing
#include <iostream>       // std::cout
#include <random>         // std::mt19937, std::uniform_int_distribution
#include <vector>         // std::vector

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: generate a vector filled with random values
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
template <typename T>
std::vector<T> make_random_vector(std::size_t n, T lo = std::numeric_limits<T>::min(),
                                  T hi = std::numeric_limits<T>::max())
{
    std::mt19937 rng{ std::random_device{}() };
    std::uniform_int_distribution<T> dist(lo, hi);

    std::vector<T> v;
    v.reserve(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        v.emplace_back(dist(rng));
    return v;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main demo
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    constexpr std::size_t N = 100'000'000;   // 100 M elements → ~400 MiB for int32_t
    std::cout << "Generating " << N << " random int32_t values …\n";

    auto data = make_random_vector<std::int32_t>(N, -1'000'000, 1'000'000);

    std::cout << "Sorting with std::simd_sort …\n";

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Timing wrapper
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    // The *execution policy* is the first argument.
    // std::simd_sort guarantees that the algorithm will be SIMD‑vectorized
    // (e.g. AVX‑512, NEON, SVE) and that it will respect the usual parallel‑algorithm
    // requirements (no data races, stable/unstable as specified, etc.).
    std::simd_sort(std::execution::simd, data.begin(), data.end());

    auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::chrono::duration<double> elapsed = t1 - t0;

    std::cout << "Sorting finished in " << elapsed.count() << " s.\n";

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Verify correctness
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (std::is_sorted(data.begin(), data.end())) {
        std::cout << "✅  The vector is correctly sorted.\n";
    } else {
        std::cerr << "❌  The vector is NOT sorted!\n";
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  (Optional) Show a few elements
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "First 10 elements after sort: ";
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < std::min<std::size_t>(10, data.size()); ++i)
        std::cout << data[i] << ' ';
    std::cout << "\n";

    std::cout << "Last 10 elements after sort: ";
    for (std::size_t i = data.size() - std::min<std::size_t>(10, data.size()); i < data.size(); ++i)
        std::cout << data[i] << ' ';
    std::cout << "\n";

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

