## TL;DR  

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[package]
name = "async-drop-demo"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2027"          # the (future) edition that ships async‑Drop

[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1.38", features = ["full"] }
```

```rust
// src/main.rs
use std::path::PathBuf;
use tokio::fs::{self, File};
use tokio::io::AsyncWriteExt;

/// A tiny wrapper around a temporary file that wants to delete the file
/// *asynchronously* when it is dropped.
struct TempFile {
    file: File,
    path: PathBuf,
}

impl TempFile {
    /// Create a new temporary file.
    async fn new<P: Into<PathBuf>>(path: P) -> std::io::Result<Self> {
        let path = path.into();
        let file = File::create(&path).await?;
        Ok(Self { file, path })
    }

    /// Write something to the file – just to have some async work.
    async fn write_hello(&mut self) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        self.file.write_all(b"hello from async‑Drop!\n").await
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*                     ** async Drop implementation **                  */
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

impl Drop for TempFile {
    /// The compiler will turn this `async fn` into a `Future` that the
    /// runtime polls after the value is logically dropped.
    async fn drop(&mut self) {
        // 1️⃣ Flush any pending writes (ignore errors – we are already
        //    in Drop, there is nothing we can do with them).
        let _ = self.file.flush().await;

        // 2️⃣ Remove the file from the filesystem.
        let _ = fs::remove_file(&self.path).await;

        // 3️⃣ A little visual confirmation.
        println!("✅ async‑Drop finished for {:?}", self.path);
    }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*                               demo program                             */
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

#[tokio::main]               // pulls in a Tokio runtime that knows how to
async fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    {
        // `tmp` lives only inside this block.
        let mut tmp = TempFile::new("temp.txt").await?;
        tmp.write_hello().await?;
        // When the block ends `tmp` is dropped → the async `drop` above
        // is scheduled.
    } // <-- async Drop starts here

    // The runtime automatically drives all pending async‑Drop futures
    // before it shuts down, so we can just return.
    Ok(())
}
```

Running the program prints something like

```
✅ async‑Drop finished for "temp.txt"
```

and the file `temp.txt` is gone, even though the removal happened **asynchronously**.

---

## How it works under the hood

* **Rust 2027** adds an `async fn drop(&mut self)` method to the existing `Drop`
  trait. The method is *optional* – you can still implement the classic
  synchronous `fn drop(&mut self)` if you don’t need async work.

* When a value whose `Drop` impl contains an `async fn drop` goes out of scope,
  the compiler generates a hidden `Future` that captures `self` by mutable
  reference. The runtime (Tokio, async‑std, smol, …) is asked to **schedule**
  that future. The memory of the value is not reclaimed until the future
  resolves.

* The runtime guarantees that all async‑Drop futures are polled to completion
  before the process exits. This is why the `#[tokio::main]` macro is enough –
  you don’t have to manually `await` the drop.

* If you need to *force* the async‑Drop to run earlier (e.g. in a test), the
  standard library also provides a helper trait:

  ```rust
  use std::future::Future;
  use std::pin::Pin;

  // (this trait is automatically implemented for every type that has an async
  //  Drop; you normally don’t have to write it yourself)
  trait AsyncDrop {
      fn drop_async(self: Pin<&mut Self>) -> Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = ()> + Send>>;
  }

  // Example:
  let mut x = TempFile::new("tmp2.txt").await?;
  Pin::new(&mut x).drop_async().await; // runs the async Drop right now
  ```

  The helper is useful in unit‑tests where you want to assert that the cleanup
  really happened before the test ends.

---

## Minimal “bare‑metal” version (no Tokio)

If you prefer not to depend on a full async runtime, you can use the tiny
`async-executor` crate, which ships a `LocalExecutor` that can drive async‑Drop
futures:

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[dependencies]
async-executor = "1.10"
```

```rust
use async_executor::LocalExecutor;
use std::future::Future;
use std::pin::Pin;
use std::sync::Arc;

struct Foo;

impl Drop for Foo {
    async fn drop(&mut self) {
        println!("async drop of Foo");
    }
}

fn main() {
    // A single‑threaded executor that lives for the whole program.
    let executor = Arc::new(LocalExecutor::new());

    // Run the program inside the executor so that async‑Drop can be polled.
    executor.run(async {
        {
            let _f = Foo;
            // `_f` is dropped here → async drop is scheduled.
        }
        // The executor will automatically poll the async‑Drop future before
        // `run` returns.
    });
}
```

The output is:

```
async drop of Foo
```

---

## Things to keep in mind

| ✅ Good practice | ❌ What to avoid |
|------------------|-----------------|
| **Never block** inside `async fn drop`. Use only `.await`‑able async APIs. | Calling `std::thread::sleep` or any blocking I/O will dead‑lock the runtime. |
| **Keep the body short** – the drop future runs while the runtime is still shutting down, so long‑running work can delay process exit. | Performing heavy CPU‑bound work; off‑load it to a dedicated thread pool instead. |
| **Handle errors locally** – you can’t `Result`‑return from `drop`. Log or ignore errors. | Propagating errors with `?` – the compiler will reject it. |
| **Don’t rely on the order of async‑Drops across unrelated types** – the runtime may interleave them arbitrarily. | Assuming a deterministic drop order for async work (except for the usual Rust drop order within a single struct). |

---

