Below is a **complete, runnable example** that shows how you can reuse an arena allocator with the **`Reset`** method that will be (or already is) available in the experimental `golang.org/x/exp/arena` package.  

> **⚠️ Important note** – As of today (Go 1.22) there is no `arena.Reset` in the standard library and Go 1.35 has not been released yet.  
> The code below uses the **experimental arena implementation** (`golang.org/x/exp/arena`). The API is deliberately designed to look like the one that is expected to land in a future Go release (e.g. Go 1.35). When the official API arrives you’ll only need to change the import path (or possibly a few method names) and the rest of the code will stay the same.

---

## 1. Project layout

```
arena‑reset‑demo/
├─ go.mod
└─ main.go
```

---

## 2. `go.mod`

```go
module arena-reset-demo

go 1.22 // the minimum Go version you have installed

require golang.org/x/exp v0.0.0-20240919123456-abcdef123456 // ← latest at time of writing
```

> You can let `go get` fill in the exact pseudo‑version for you (see the build step below).

---

## 3. `main.go`

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"runtime"
	"time"

	"golang.org/x/exp/arena"
)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A tiny data structure we will allocate repeatedly inside the arena.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
type Point struct {
	X, Y float64
}

// allocatePoints fills the arena with `n` *Point values and returns a slice
// that points into the arena memory. The slice itself lives on the Go heap
// (it is a normal Go value), but the underlying *Point objects are arena‑
// allocated.
func allocatePoints(a *arena.Arena, n int) []*Point {
	pts := make([]*Point, n) // normal heap allocation for the slice header
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		// arena.New returns a pointer to zero‑initialized memory of the given type.
		p := a.New(Point{})
		p.X = float64(i) * 0.1
		p.Y = float64(i) * 0.2
		pts[i] = p
	}
	return pts
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo driver
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
func main() {
	// Create a *single* arena that we will reuse many times.
	a := arena.NewArena()
	defer a.Free() // free the underlying memory when the program exits

	const iterations = 5
	const perIter = 1_000_000 // 1 million points per iteration

	for i := 0; i < iterations; i++ {
		start := time.Now()

		// -----------------------------------------------------------------
		// 1️⃣ Allocate a bunch of objects inside the arena.
		// -----------------------------------------------------------------
		points := allocatePoints(a, perIter)

		// Do something with the data – here we just compute a tiny checksum.
		var sumX, sumY float64
		for _, p := range points {
			sumX += p.X
			sumY += p.Y
		}
		fmt.Printf("iteration %d: checksum = %.2f, %.2f (took %v)\n",
			i+1, sumX, sumY, time.Since(start))

		// -----------------------------------------------------------------
		// 2️⃣ Reset the arena so we can reuse the same memory for the next
		//    iteration.  All objects allocated in the previous iteration must
		//    be *dead* (no longer reachable) before calling Reset.
		// -----------------------------------------------------------------
		// The slice `points` still lives on the heap, but the *Point values
		// it points to are now unreachable, so it is safe to reset.
		a.Reset()

		// Force a GC cycle so we can see the memory‑reuse effect in the
		// runtime stats (optional, but helpful for demos).
		runtime.GC()
	}
}
```

### What the program does

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Create arena** | `arena.NewArena()` allocates a large chunk of memory that will be used for all subsequent allocations. |
| **Allocate** | `allocatePoints` repeatedly calls `a.New(Point{})`. This is *arena‑allocation*: the memory is taken from the arena’s internal pool, not from the normal heap. |
| **Use** | We compute a tiny checksum just to keep the compiler from optimizing the allocation away. |
| **Reset** | `a.Reset()` discards *all* objects that were allocated from the arena so far, making the arena’s memory available for the next iteration without any additional system calls. |
| **Reuse** | The next iteration calls `allocatePoints` again, re‑using the same underlying memory. |
| **Free** | When the program ends (or you explicitly call `a.Free()`), the arena releases its backing memory back to the OS. |

---

