## `useServerState` – a tiny “out‑of‑the‑box” hook for React 20  

Below is a **self‑contained TypeScript implementation** that works with the (still‑in‑proposal) React 20 server‑components / server‑actions model.  
It automatically:

1. **Hydrates** the initial value from the server on the first render.  
2. **Keeps the client in sync** with the server via a lightweight WebSocket channel (fallback to polling).  
3. **Sends optimistic updates** to the server and rolls‑back only if the server rejects them.  

> **Why a hook?**  
> React 20 introduces the `use` primitive that can suspend on any thenable. `useServerState` builds on that primitive, so the component can suspend while the first value is being fetched, and then continue as a normal client‑side hook.

---

### 1️⃣  The Hook (≈ 70 LOC)

```tsx
// useServerState.ts
import { use, useEffect, useRef, useState, useCallback } from "react";

/**
 * Shape of the payload that travels over the wire.
 */
type ServerStateMessage<T> =
  | { type: "init"; value: T }
  | { type: "update"; value: T }
  | { type: "error"; error: string };

/**
 * Options for the hook – you can plug‑in your own transport.
 */
export interface UseServerStateOptions<T> {
  /** URL that returns the initial state (GET) */
  fetchUrl: string;
  /** URL that receives updates (POST) */
  updateUrl: string;
  /** Optional custom WebSocket endpoint for push updates */
  wsUrl?: string;
  /** How often to poll when WS is unavailable (ms) */
  pollInterval?: number;
}

/**
 * Core hook – returns a tuple like `useState`.
 */
export function useServerState<T>(
  opts: UseServerStateOptions<T>
): [T, (next: T | ((prev: T) => T)) => void] {
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Suspend until the first value arrives from the server.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const initPromise = useRef(
    (async () => {
      const res = await fetch(opts.fetchUrl, { credentials: "include" });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to fetch ${opts.fetchUrl}`);
      const data = (await res.json()) as T;
      return data;
    })()
  ).current;

  // `use` will suspend the component until the promise resolves.
  const serverValue = use(initPromise) as T;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Local React state – starts with the server‑provided value.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const [local, setLocal] = useState<T>(serverValue);

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  WebSocket / polling subscription to keep the client in sync.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  useEffect(() => {
    let ws: WebSocket | null = null;
    let pollTimer: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;
    let isMounted = true;

    const handleMessage = (msg: MessageEvent) => {
      try {
        const payload: ServerStateMessage<T> = JSON.parse(msg.data);
        if (payload.type === "update") {
          setLocal(payload.value);
        }
      } catch (_) {
        // ignore malformed messages
      }
    };

    // Try WebSocket first
    if (opts.wsUrl) {
      ws = new WebSocket(opts.wsUrl);
      ws.addEventListener("message", handleMessage);
      ws.addEventListener("error", () => {
        // fallback to polling if WS fails
        ws?.close();
        ws = null;
      });
    }

    // Fallback polling
    if (!ws) {
      const interval = opts.pollInterval ?? 5_000;
      pollTimer = setInterval(async () => {
        try {
          const res = await fetch(opts.fetchUrl, { credentials: "include" });
          if (!res.ok) throw new Error("poll failed");
          const data = (await res.json()) as T;
          if (isMounted) setLocal(data);
        } catch (e) {
          console.error(e);
        }
      }, interval);
    }

    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
      ws?.close();
      if (pollTimer) clearInterval(pollTimer);
    };
  }, [opts.fetchUrl, opts.wsUrl, opts.pollInterval]);

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Updater – optimistic, then confirmed/reverted by the server.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const pendingRef = useRef<Set<number>>(new Set()); // track in‑flight request IDs
  const requestId = useRef(0);

  const setServerState = useCallback(
    (next: T | ((prev: T) => T)) => {
      // 4a – compute the new value locally (optimistic UI)
      setLocal(prev => {
        const newVal = typeof next === "function" ? (next as any)(prev) : next;
        // fire‑and‑forget the server request
        const id = ++requestId.current;
        pendingRef.current.add(id);

        // send to server
        fetch(opts.updateUrl, {
          method: "POST",
          credentials: "include",
          headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
          body: JSON.stringify({ id, value: newVal })
        })
          .then(async r => {
            if (!r.ok) throw new Error("update rejected");
            const payload: ServerStateMessage<T> = await r.json();
            if (payload.type === "update") {
              // server accepted – replace local with authoritative value
              setLocal(payload.value);
            } else if (payload.type === "error") {
              throw new Error(payload.error);
            }
          })
          .catch(err => {
            // rollback only if this request is still the latest pending one
            if (pendingRef.current.has(id)) {
              console.warn("Server rejected update, rolling back:", err);
              setLocal(prev); // revert to the value before this optimistic update
            }
          })
          .finally(() => pendingRef.current.delete(id));

        return newVal; // optimistic render
      });
    },
    [opts.updateUrl]
  );

  return [local, setServerState];
}
```

---

### 2️⃣  How It Works – Step‑by‑Step

| Phase | What Happens | Why It Matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Initial render** | `useServerState` calls `use(initPromise)`. The promise fetches the current value from `fetchUrl`. React 20 suspends the component until the promise resolves. | The UI never flashes an empty state – it starts with the server‑canonical value. |
| **Hydration** | Once the promise resolves, the returned value becomes the initial local state (`useState`). | Guarantees the client and server are in sync from the first paint. |
| **Live sync** | A `useEffect` opens a WebSocket (if `wsUrl` is supplied). Incoming `"update"` messages replace the local state. If the socket can’t be opened, a fallback poller repeatedly GETs `fetchUrl`. | The client automatically receives changes made elsewhere (e.g., another browser tab, a background job). |
| **Optimistic update** | The setter returned by the hook (`setServerState`) immediately updates the local state, then POSTs the new value (plus a request‑ID) to `updateUrl`. | UI feels instant; the user doesn’t wait for a round‑trip. |
| **Server confirmation** | The server replies with either `{type:"update",value:…}` (accepted) or `{type:"error",error:…}` (rejected). On success we replace the local value with the authoritative one; on error we roll back to the previous value. | Guarantees eventual consistency while still providing optimistic UX. |
| **Cleanup** | When the component unmounts the WebSocket is closed and any polling timer is cleared. | Prevents memory leaks and stray network traffic. |

---

### 3️⃣  Quick Example

```tsx
// Counter.tsx
import { useServerState } from "./useServerState";

export function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useServerState<number>({
    fetchUrl: "/api/counter",
    updateUrl: "/api/counter",
    wsUrl: "wss://example.com/counter",
    pollInterval: 10_000 // 10 s fallback
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Shared Counter</h2>
      <p>Current value: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+1</button>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c - 1)}>-1</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

*All instances of `<Counter />` (even in different tabs) will stay in sync automatically.*

---

### 4️⃣  What You Need on the Server

| Endpoint | Expected contract |
|----------|-------------------|
| **GET `fetchUrl`** | Returns JSON `T` – the current state. |
| **POST `updateUrl`** | Body `{ id: number, value: T }`. Responds with either:<br>• `{ type:"update", value: T }` (accepted) <br>• `{ type:"error", error: string }` (rejected) |
| **WebSocket `wsUrl`** (optional) | Sends `{ type:"update", value: T }` whenever the state changes (including changes made by other clients). |

Because the hook only cares about the shape of the messages, you can implement the server in Node, Go, Ruby, etc. – just follow the JSON contract.

