volatile int flag = 0;

void* worker_thread(void* arg) {
    printf("Worker: Starting, waiting for flag...\n");

    // Busy-wait for flag to become 1
    while (flag == 0) {
        // Empty loop - just waiting
    }

    printf("Worker: Flag changed to %d, proceeding...\n", flag);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t thread;

    printf("Main: Creating worker thread\n");
    pthread_create(&thread, NULL, worker_thread, NULL);

    // Simulate some work
    sleep(2);

    printf("Main: Setting flag to 1\n");
    flag = 1;

    // Wait for thread to finish
    pthread_join(thread, NULL);
    printf("Main: Worker thread completed\n");

    return 0;
}
```

## Important Caveats and Better Alternatives

### Why `volatile` Alone is Insufficient

1. **No atomicity guarantees**: Reading/writing `int` might not be atomic on all architectures
2. **No memory ordering guarantees**: Compiler/CPU can reorder operations
3. **No synchronization**: Other threads might see stale values from cache

### Better Approach: Use Proper Synchronization

```c
