Here's a Java solution for the Valid Parentheses problem that includes angle brackets as a fourth matching pair:

```java
import java.util.Stack;

public class ValidParentheses {
    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();

        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            // If it's an opening bracket, push to stack
            if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[' || c == '<') {
                stack.push(c);
            } 
            // If it's a closing bracket
            else if (c == ')' || c == '}' || c == ']' || c == '>') {
                // Check if stack is empty (no matching opening bracket)
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    return false;
                }

                char top = stack.pop();

                // Check if the closing bracket matches the most recent opening bracket
                if ((c == ')' && top != '(') ||
                    (c == '}' && top != '{') ||
                    (c == ']' && top != '[') ||
                    (c == '>' && top != '<')) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            // Optional: handle invalid characters (if needed)
            // else {
            //     return false; // if only brackets are allowed
            // }
        }

        // If stack is empty, all brackets were properly matched
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test cases
        String[] testCases = {
            "()",           // true
            "()[]{}<>",     // true
            "(<>)",         // true
            "([{}<>])",     // true
            "(]",           // false
            "([)]",         // false
            "<(>)",         // false
            "((())",        // false
            "",             // true (empty string)
            "({[<>]})"      // true
        };

        for (String test : testCases) {
            System.out.println(test + " -> " + isValid(test));
        }
    }
}
```

## Alternative approach using HashMap for cleaner matching logic:

```java
import java.util.Stack;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class ValidParentheses {
    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();
        Map<Character, Character> matchingPairs = new HashMap<>();

        // Define matching pairs
        matchingPairs.put(')', '(');
        matchingPairs.put('}', '{');
        matchingPairs.put(']', '[');
        matchingPairs.put('>', '<');

        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            // If it's a closing bracket
            if (matchingPairs.containsKey(c)) {
                // Check if stack is empty or top doesn't match
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != matchingPairs.get(c)) {
                    return false;
                }
            } 
            // If it's an opening bracket
            else if (matchingPairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            }
            // Optional: handle invalid characters
            // else {
            //     return false;
            // }
        }

        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test cases
        System.out.println("()[]{}<> -> " + isValid("()[]{}<>"));  // true
        System.out.println("(<>) -> " + isValid("(<>)"));
        System.out.println("([)] -> " + isValid("([)]"));          // false
        System.out.println("({[<>]}) -> " + isValid("({[<>]})"));  // true
    }
}
```

## Key Points:

1. **Stack Data Structure**: We use a stack to track opening brackets in LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) order.

2. **Algorithm Steps**:
   - Iterate through each character in the string
   - Push opening brackets onto the stack
   - When encountering a closing bracket:
     - Check if stack is empty (unmatched closing bracket)
     - Pop from stack and verify it matches the current closing bracket
   - At the end, check if stack is empty (all brackets matched)

3. **Time Complexity**: O(n) where n is the length of the string
4. **Space Complexity**: O(n) in worst case (all opening brackets)

## Common Edge Cases to Test:
- Empty string
- Single bracket
- Nested valid brackets
- Interleaved invalid brackets
- Only opening brackets
- Only closing brackets

The second approach with HashMap is more extensible if you need to add more bracket types later. Both solutions correctly handle the four bracket types: `()`, `{}`, `[]`, and `<>`.

