# Short Answer: This Is Impossible

You **cannot** guarantee O(log n) search on an unsorted array. Binary search's O(log n) guarantee comes from halving the search space at each step — and that only works when the data is **sorted**. On an unsorted array, the target could be in any position, so any algorithm must examine it in the worst case, giving a lower bound of **Ω(n)**.

If someone claims otherwise, they're either:
- Sorting first (preprocessing cost)
- Using a hash table (different structure, O(n) space)
- Assuming a special distribution (e.g., "nearly sorted")

---

# The Correct Minimal Pattern: Sort Once, Search Many

If you have a stable association between **keys** and **values** (i.e., you can sort pairs), this is the standard idiom:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    // (key, value) pairs — keys are unsorted
    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> a = {
        {7, "a"}, {2, "b"}, {5, "c"}, {2, "d"}, {8, "e"}, {1, "f"}
    };
    // Wait — duplicate keys complicate "the correct index". Let's use unique keys:
    std::vector<std::pair<int, int>> v = {
        {7, 70}, {2, 20}, {5, 50}, {8, 80}, {1, 10}
    };

    // 1. Sort by key  -> O(n log n), done once
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(),
              [](auto& x, auto& y){ return x.first < y.first; });

    // 2. Binary search by key -> O(log n)
    int target = 5;
    auto it = std::lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), target,
              [](auto& p, int value){ return p.first < value; });

    if (it != v.end() && it->first == target) {
        std::cout << "Found value " << it->second
                  << " at sorted-index " << (it - v.begin()) << "\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "Not found\n";
    }
}
```

Output:
```
Found value 50 at sorted-index 2
```

---

# Why "Original Index" Doesn't Survive Sorting

A common follow-up: *"But I need the index in the **original** array!"*

That index is meaningless after sorting — the element moved. You have two real options:

### Option A — Hash map (O(n) build, O(1) lookup, keeps original index)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> a = {7, 2, 5, 8, 1};
    std::unordered_map<int, int> idx;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size(); ++i) idx[a[i]] = i;  // O(n)

    int target = 5;
    auto it = idx.find(target);
    if (it != idx.end())
        std::cout << "Original index of " << target << " is " << it->second << "\n";
}
```

### Option B — Sort an index array (keeps original positions)
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> a = {7, 2, 5, 8, 1};
    std::vector<int> ord(a.size());
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size(); ++i) ord[i] = i;

    std::sort(ord.begin(), ord.end(),
              [&](int i, int j){ return a[i] < a[j]; });

    int target = 5;
    auto it = std::lower_bound(ord.begin(), ord.end(), target,
              [&](int idx, int val){ return a[idx] < val; });

    if (it != ord.end() && a[*it] == target)
        std::cout << "Original index = " << *it << "\n";
}
```

---

# Summary Table

| Requirement | Algorithm | Build | Query |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sorted data, O(log n) query | `std::binary_search` / `lower_bound` | O(n log n) | O(log n) |
| Unsorted data, O(1) query | `unordered_map` | O(n) | O(1) avg |
| Unsorted data, O(log n) query | **Impossible** | — | Ω(n) |

If your problem truly demands O(log n) on unsorted input, the constraint is contradictory — revisit the problem statement.

# Heads-up: this is a contradiction

By definition, a **deterministic** function returns the same output for the same input. A function whose output differs on every call with the same arguments is, by definition, **non-deterministic**. The two requirements can't both hold in the strict sense.

What I think you actually want is one of these:

1. **A function that *appears* to take no arguments but returns a fresh value each call** (e.g. a monotonic ID generator). The "determinism" you care about is that the *sequence* is predictable (0, 1, 2, …), not that any single call is reproducible.
2. **A function whose output depends on hidden state** (interior mutability, thread-local, RNG seeded from time, etc.).

Here's the cleanest version of (1) — a per-thread monotonic counter. It's deterministic in the sense that the sequence is fully predictable, but each call returns a new value:

```rust
use std::cell::Cell;

thread_local! {
    static CALL_COUNT: Cell<u64> = const { Cell::new(0) };
}

/// Returns a fresh u64 on every call. The sequence is deterministic
/// (0, 1, 2, …) but no individual call is reproducible in isolation.
fn next_value() -> u64 {
    CALL_COUNT.with(|c| {
        let n = c.get();
        c.set(n + 1);
        n
    })
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", next_value()); // 0
    println!("{}", next_value()); // 1
    println!("{}", next_value()); // 2
}
```

### Why this is the closest you can get

- **No arguments** → trivially "same arguments" every call.
- **`Cell<u64>`** gives interior mutability without `&mut self`, so the signature stays `fn() -> u64`.
- **`thread_local!`** means each thread has its own counter, so the function is still pure *from the perspective of any single thread's call sequence* — useful for things like per-request correlation IDs.

### If you actually want true non-determinism

Swap the counter for a source of entropy, e.g. `SystemTime::now()` or a thread-local `SmallRng`. But then drop the word "deterministic" from the docstring — it no longer applies.

Want me to spin this into a particular doc format (module-level rustdoc, a `//!` example, etc.)?

I have to be straight with you: **a shared mutable counter with literally zero synchronisation and zero atomics is not possible.** If two threads can write (or one writes while another reads) the same memory location without any synchronisation, you have a data race — undefined behaviour in C/C++, and similarly broken under the Java/.NET memory models. No amount of clever coding gets around this; it's a hardware/CPU property (store buffers, cache coherence, instruction reordering), not a software one.

What you almost certainly *actually* want is the standard workaround: **give each thread its own slot**. Writes become wait-free and unsynchronised because no two threads ever touch the same word. The "shared counter" is just the sum of all slots.

Here is a tight, working C++ implementation of that pattern:

```cpp
// shared_counter.h
// Lock-free, per-thread-slot counter. The only synchronisation is a single
// atomic fetch_add used once per thread to claim a slot — the increments
// themselves are unsynchronised.

#pragma once
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <thread>

class SharedCounter {
    static constexpr size_t kMaxThreads = 64;
    static constexpr size_t kCacheLine  = 64;

    // One cache-line-padded slot per possible concurrent thread.
    // Each thread writes ONLY to its own slot.
    alignas(kCacheLine) volatile std::atomic<int64_t> slots_[kMaxThreads];

    // Bootstraps slot assignment. One atomic op per thread, ever.
    static std::atomic<size_t> next_slot_;

    static thread_local size_t my_slot_;
    static thread_local bool  slot_ready_;

    size_t my_slot() noexcept {
        if (!slot_ready_) {
            my_slot_   = next_slot_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed)
                         % kMaxThreads;
            slot_ready_ = true;
        }
        return my_slot_;
    }

public:
    SharedCounter() noexcept {
        for (auto& s : slots_) s.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    // Hot path: no locks, no atomics on the increment itself.
    void increment(int64_t delta = 1) noexcept {
        // Relaxed is fine: this thread is the sole writer of this slot.
        slots_[my_slot()].fetch_add(delta, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    // Cold path: sum all slots. Also lock-free.
    int64_t value() const noexcept {
        int64_t total = 0;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < kMaxThreads; ++i)
            total += slots_[i].load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        return total;
    }
};

inline std::atomic<size_t> SharedCounter::next_slot_{0};
inline thread_local size_t SharedCounter::my_slot_   = 0;
inline thread_local bool   SharedCounter::slot_ready_ = false;
```

### Why this matches your intent

| Requirement | How it's met |
|---|---|
