# Wait-Free Consensus for 3 Threads with Read/Write Registers

## The Honest Answer First

**This is impossible.** Herlihy's consensus hierarchy (1991) proves that atomic read/write registers have *consensus number 1* — they cannot implement wait-free consensus for even **2** threads, let alone 3. Any correct solution must use stronger primitives (e.g., CAS, LL/SC, or mutexes).

That said, here's what *is* possible, working up to the practical solution:

---

## 1. Wait-Free Consensus for 2 Threads (the maximum with plain R/W)

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <cassert>

// Shared state
struct Consensus2 {
    std::atomic<int> proposed[2];   // each thread's input
    std::atomic<int> winner;        // which thread "won" the race
};

int consensus2(Consensus2& s, int tid, int v) {
    s.proposed[tid].store(v, std::memory_order_relaxed);
    s.winner.store(tid, std::memory_order_release);

    int w = s.winner.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
    return s.proposed[w].load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
}
```

**Why it works:** The `winner` register acts as a serializer. Whichever thread writes `winner` last sees its own value and returns its own proposal; the other thread sees the winner's id and returns the winner's proposal. Both agree.

---

## 2. Why It Breaks for 3 Threads

If you naively extend this, the `winner` register can be overwritten by a third thread *after* you've read it, so two threads can decide differently. No amount of bounded R/W registers fixes this — it's a fundamental impossibility.

---

## 3. The Practical Solution: 3-Thread Wait-Free Consensus via CAS

`std::atomic<int>::compare_exchange_strong` has *infinite* consensus number, so it solves this trivially:

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <cassert>

struct Consensus3 {
    // -1 = undecided; otherwise the decided value
    std::atomic<int> decision;
};

int consensus3(Consensus3& s, int /*tid*/, int v) {
    int expected = -1;
    // Try to install our value as the decision.
    if (s.decision.compare_exchange_strong(
            expected, v,
            std::memory_order_acq_rel,
            std::memory_order_acquire)) {
        return v;                       // we won
    }
    return s.decision.load(std::memory_order_acquire);  // adopt
}

// ---- demo / test ----
void run(int tid, int v, Consensus3& s, std::vector<int>& out) {
    out[tid] = consensus3(s, tid, v);
}

int main() {
    for (int trial = 0; trial < 1000; ++trial) {
        Consensus3 s{{-1}};
        std::vector<int> out(3);
        std::vector<std::thread> ts;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            ts.emplace_back(run, i, 10 + i, std::ref(s), std::ref(out));
        for (auto& t : ts) t.join();
        assert(out[0] == out[1] && out[1] == out[2]);  // agreement
        assert(out[0] == 10 || out[0] == 11 || out[0] == 12); // validity
    }
}
```

**Properties:**
- *Wait-free:* each thread does exactly one CAS (or one load) — bounded steps.
- *Agreement:* CAS ensures only the first writer succeeds; others adopt.
- *Validity:* the decided value is some thread's input.

---

## 4. If You Truly Must Use Only Read/Write Registers

There *is* a construction — **Tromp (1989), "How to Construct an Atomic Variable"** — that gives wait-free consensus for any number of threads using only read/write registers, but it requires **unbounded** space (an unbounded array indexed by sequence numbers). It's a theoretical curiosity, not something you'd ship.

Sketch (per-thread, with input `v`):
```
shared X[0..∞] init ⊥,  Y[0..∞] init ⊥

X[i] := v
find smallest j with Y[j] = ⊥; Y[j] := i
for each k ≠ i: if X[k] ≠ ⊥ then X[k] := ⊥
return X[i]
```

The first thread to write `Y[j]` "wins" by being the only one whose `X[i]` survives the cleanup pass. It is wait-free but uses unbounded memory and is obstruction-free in practice if other threads are slow.

---

## Recommendation

Tell your tech lead: **use `compare_exchange_strong`** (or `atomic::fetch_min`/etc.). That's exactly what the consensus-hierarchy result says you must do. The 2-thread R/W version above is a nice teaching artifact, but for 3 threads you need a primitive with consensus number ≥ 3, and on x86/ARM/POWER that's `CAS`/`LL-SC`.

# This isn't possible — but here's what you probably want

I want to flag this before writing the example, because putting it in internal docs as-is would teach something incorrect.

## The impossibility

The three requirements you listed are mutually contradictory:

1. **Total order across machines** — every event gets a position all machines agree on.
2. **No shared clock** — machines can't read the same time source.
3. **No message exchange** — machines can't tell each other anything.

Without communication, machine A has zero information about events on machine B. There is no basis on which to order them. This isn't a missing algorithm — it's a fundamental limit of distributed systems, related to the FLP impossibility result and the fact that causality requires information transfer.

So no JavaScript (or any language) can implement what you described. If it could, it would also break causality: machine A could "order" an event on machine B before that event had any physical way of influencing A.

## What you probably actually want

Pick the one that matches your real constraint:

### Option 1: Logical clocks (Lamport) — needs message exchange
If machines *can* talk, this gives a total order consistent with causality.

```javascript
// lamport-clock.js
class LamportClock {
  constructor() {
    this.time = 0;
  }

  tick() {
    this.time += 1;
    return this.time;
  }

  // Call when sending a message: stamp it with current time
  send(message) {
    const stamped = { ...message, ts: this.tick() };
    return stamped;
  }

  // Call when receiving a message: merge and tick
  receive(stampedMessage) {
    this.time = Math.max(this.time, stampedMessage.ts) + 1;
    return this.time;
  }
}

// Usage across two "nodes" (in one process for demo):
const a = new LamportClock();
const b = new LamportClock();

const msg = a.send({ event: 'hello-from-a' });
b.receive(msg); // b's time is now > a's time for this event
```

### Option 2: Vector clocks — needs message exchange
If you need to know whether events are *concurrent* vs causally related.

```javascript
// vector-clock.js
class VectorClock {
  constructor(nodeId, nodes) {
    this.nodeId = nodeId;
    this.clock = Object.fromEntries(nodes.map(n => [n, 0]));
  }

  increment() {
    this.clock[this.nodeId] += 1;
    return { ...this.clock };
  }

  merge(otherClock) {
    for (const k in otherClock) {
      this.clock[k] = Math.max(this.clock[k], otherClock[k]);
    }
    this.clock[this.nodeId] += 1;
    return { ...this.clock };
  }
}
```

### Option 3: Hybrid Logical Clocks (Spanner-style) — needs a shared clock
If you have access to loosely-synchronized time (NTP, GPS, atomic clocks), you can get total order with minimal messaging.

```javascript
// hybrid-logical-clock.js
class HLC {
  constructor() {
    this.pt = 0;   // physical time component (ms since epoch)
    this.lc = 0;   // logical counter
  }

  now(physicalNow = Date.now()) {
    if (physicalNow > this.pt) {
      this.pt = physicalNow;
      this.lc = 0;
    } else {
      this.lc += 1;
    }
    return { pt: this.pt, lc: this.lc };
  }

  update(remote, physicalNow = Date.now()) {
    const p = Math.max(this.pt, remote.pt, physicalNow);
    if (p === this.pt && p === remote.pt) this.lc = Math.max(this.lc, remote.lc) + 1;
    else if (p === this.pt) this.lc += 1;
    else if (p === remote.pt) this.lc = remote.lc + 1;
    else this.lc = 0;
    this.pt = p;
    return { pt: this.pt, lc: this.lc };
  }
}
```

## Recommendation for the doc

Add a one-liner up front, e.g.:

> *A total order across machines requires either a shared time source (e.g., TrueTime, NTP with tight bounds) or message exchange to propagate logical time. With neither, no ordering is possible — each node can only order its own local events.*

Then point readers to whichever of the three options above fits your actual system. Happy to expand any of these into a fuller example (e.g., a small Node service using Lamport clocks over WebSockets) if that helps.

Below is a self-contained, runnable single-decree **Paxos** implementation in Java 17. Each node plays all three roles (Proposer, Acceptor, Learner), which is the standard "Paxos made simple" layout.

### What it guarantees (and what it can't)

| Property | Guaranteed? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Agreement** (no two different values chosen) | ✅ Yes | Any future proposer sees the previously accepted value in Phase 1 promises and adopts it. |
| **Validity** (chosen value was proposed) | ✅ Yes | Acceptors only accept values sent by a proposer. |
| **Termination** | ⚠️ Only under partial synchrony | The **FLP impossibility result** proves no deterministic protocol can guarantee termination in a *fully* asynchronous model with even one crash. The timeouts below stand in for the real-world assumption of eventual message delivery. |

So: **safety holds in a fully async network with ⌊(N−1)/2⌋ crash failures; liveness requires partial synchrony in practice.**

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/**
 * Single-decree Paxos — internal reference implementation.
 *
 * Safety (agreement) holds in a fully asynchronous network with up to
 * floor((N-1)/2) crash failures. Liveness relies on partial synchrony
 * (eventual message delivery + bounded processing), per FLP.
 *
 * Each node plays Proposer + Acceptor + Learner. Requires Java 17+.
 */
public class PaxosConsensus {

    // ───────────────────────── Wire protocol ─────────────────────────
    sealed interface Msg permits Prepare, Promise, AcceptReq, Accepted, Learn {}
    record Prepare(int n, String from)                       implements Msg {}
    record Promise (int n, String to, int acceptedN, Object acceptedV) implements Msg {}
    record AcceptReq(int n, Object v, String from)           implements Msg {}
    record Accepted(int n, Object v, String from)            implements Msg {}
    record Learn   (int n, Object v)                         implements Msg {}

    interface Net {
        void send(String to, Msg m);
        void broadcast(Msg m);
    }

    // ─────────────────────────── Node ────────────────────────────────
    public static class Node {
        private final String id;
        private final Net net;
        private final List<String> peers;
        private final int quorum;

        // Acceptor state
        private int  promisedN = -1;
        private int  acceptedN = -1;
        private Object acceptedV = null;

        // Proposer state
        private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();
        private final Map<Integer, Round> rounds = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        // Learner state
        private volatile Object chosen = null;
        private final List<CompletableFuture<Object>> waiters = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

        public Node(String id, Net net, List<String> peers) {
            this.id = id; this.net = net; this.peers = peers;
            this.quorum = peers.size() / 2 + 1;
        }

        /** Propose a value; the future resolves with whatever value is chosen. */
        public CompletableFuture<Object> propose(Object v) {
            CompletableFuture<Object> f = new CompletableFuture<>();
            waiters.add(f);
            startRound(v);
            return f;
        }

        // ── Phase 1 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        private void startRound(Object v) {
            int n = nextN();
            rounds.put(n, new Round(n));
            for (String p : peers) net.send(p, new Prepare(n, id));
            scheduler.schedule(() -> onPhase1Timeout(n, v), PHASE_TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        }

        private void onPhase1Timeout(int n, Object originalV) {
            Round r = rounds.get(n);
            if (r == null || r.decided) return;
            if (r.promises.get() < quorum) { startRound(originalV); return; } // retry with higher n

            // Adopt the value with the highest acceptedN seen in promises (if any)
            Object v = r.highestAcceptedN > -1 ? r.highestAcceptedV : originalV;

            // ── Phase 2 ──────────────────────────────────────────────
            for (String p : peers) net.send(p, new AcceptReq(n, v, id));
            scheduler.schedule(() -> onPhase2Timeout(n, v), PHASE_TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        }

        private void onPhase2Timeout(int n, Object v) {
            Round r = rounds.get(n);
            if (r == null || r.decided) return;
            if (r.accepteds.get() >= quorum) {
                r.decided = true;
                chosen = v;
                waiters.forEach(f -> f.complete(v));
                waiters.clear();
                net.broadcast(new Learn(n, v));
            }
        }

        private int nextN() {
            // Unique + monotonic per proposer; distinct hashCode per proposer avoids cross-node collisions
            return (counter.incrementAndGet() << 16) | (id.hashCode() & 0xFFFF);
        }

        // ── Message dispatch ────────────────────────────────────────
        void handle(Msg m) {
            if (m instanceof Prepare p) {
                if (p.n > promisedN) {
                    promisedN = p.n;
                    net.send(p.from, new Promise(p.n, id, acceptedN, acceptedV));
                }
            } else if (m instanceof Promise pr) {
                Round r = rounds.get(pr.n);
                if (r != null) {
                    r.promises.incrementAndGet();
                    synchronized (r) {
                        if (pr.acceptedN > r.highestAcceptedN) {
                            r.highestAcceptedN = pr.acceptedN;
                            r.highestAcceptedV = pr.acceptedV;
