### How it works (the 30-second version)

1. **Phase 1 — Prepare/Promise.** A proposer picks a proposal number `n` strictly higher than any it has seen and asks a majority of acceptors: *"Promise me you won't accept anything numbered lower than `n`."* Each acceptor replies with the highest-numbered value it has already accepted (if any).
2. **Phase 2 — Accept/Accepted.** If the proposer gets promises from a majority, it picks the value with the highest `acceptedN` from those promises (or its own value if none), and asks acceptors to accept `(n, v)`. Once a majority accepts, the value is **chosen**.
3. **Learn.** Acceptors broadcast `Accepted` to all learners; any node that sees a quorum of accepts (or a `Learn` message) marks the value chosen.

### Why agreement holds even with crashes

- A value is only chosen when a **majority** of acceptors accept it.
- Any subsequent proposer, in Phase 1, will collect promises from a majority — and by the pigeonhole principle, at least one of those acceptors has seen the chosen value and will report it.
- The proposer is therefore **forced to adopt** the previously chosen value, so no two different values can ever be chosen.

### Caveats / next steps for production use

- **Single-decree only.** For a replicated log (the common use case), look at **Multi-Paxos** or **Raft** (which is essentially Multi-Paxos with a stronger leadership model and is usually easier to reason about).
- **Leader election.** Adding a stable leader (e.g., via heartbeats) eliminates the contention between competing proposers and lets Phase 1 be skipped in the common case.
- **Persistence.** Acceptor state (`promisedN`, `acceptedN`, `acceptedV`) must be fsync'd to stable storage before responding, otherwise a crash-recovery can violate safety.
- **Real networking.** Replace the in-memory `Net` with your RPC layer; the protocol itself makes no timing assumptions.

