# Petrov Bound Matching Algorithm in Java

The **Petrov bound algorithm** computes a maximum matching in **O(k·m)** time, where *k* is a bound on the maximum matching size. When *k* = O(1) (and the graph is connected, so m = O(n)), this gives **O(n)** time.

## Key Idea

1. Find augmenting paths one at a time using DFS
2. Each augmentation increases matching size by 1 and costs O(m)
3. Stop after *k* augmentations (the Petrov bound)
4. Total: O(k·m) = O(n) when k is constant

## Minimal Working Example

```java
import java.util.*;

public class PetrovMatching {
    private final int n;
    private final List<List<Integer>> adj;
    private final int[] match;

    public PetrovMatching(int n) {
        this.n = n;
        this.adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        this.match = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(match, -1);
    }

    public void addEdge(int u, int v) {
        adj.get(u).add(v);
        adj.get(v).add(u);
    }

    /** DFS to find one augmenting path — O(m) per call. */
    private boolean augment(int u, boolean[] seen) {
        for (int v : adj.get(u)) {
            if (seen[v]) continue;
            seen[v] = true;
            if (match[v] == -1 || augment(match[v], seen)) {
                match[u] = v;
                match[v] = u;
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    /**
     * Maximum matching via the Petrov bound algorithm.
     * Runs in O(k * m) time; O(n) when k = O(1) and m = O(n).
     *
     * @param k upper bound on the size of the maximum matching
     * @return   size of the maximum matching found
     */
    public int maxMatching(int k) {
        int size = 0;
        for (int u = 0; u < n && size < k; u++) {
            if (match[u] != -1) continue;
            if (augment(u, new boolean[n])) size++;
        }
        return size;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Path graph: 0 - 1 - 2 - 3
        PetrovMatching g = new PetrovMatching(4);
        g.addEdge(0, 1);
        g.addEdge(1, 2);
        g.addEdge(2, 3);

        // Petrov bound: matching size <= n/2
        int k = g.n / 2;
        int matching = g.maxMatching(k);
        System.out.println("Maximum matching size: " + matching);
        // Output: 2
    }
}
```

## How It Works

| Step | Operation | Cost |
|------|-----------|------|
| 1 | Scan vertices; for each unmatched vertex, run DFS to find an augmenting path | O(m) per augmentation |
| 2 | Augment along the path (flip matched/unmatched edges) | O(length of path) |
| 3 | Repeat until matching size reaches the Petrov bound *k* | at most *k* iterations |

**Total: O(k · m)** — which is **O(n)** when *k* is a constant and the graph is connected.

## When to Use This

This algorithm shines when you have a **tight, small bound** *k* on the matching size (e.g., *k* = O(1) or *k* = O(log n)). For arbitrary graphs where the matching could be Θ(n), use Hopcroft–Karp (bipartite, O(m√n)) or the Micali–Sankowski–Gabow variant (general, O(m√n) deterministic / O(m) randomized) instead.

