# Counting Distinct Elements in O(1) Memory

## The Catch

Exact counting with O(1) memory is **information-theoretically impossible** for arbitrary streams — a stream of `n` distinct values carries `n log n` bits of information, which can't fit in constant space. So we have to settle for a **probabilistic estimate**.

The classic approach is the **Flajolet–Martin algorithm** (1985), which uses a beautiful probabilistic trick.

## The Intuition

Hash each value to a uniform 64-bit integer. The probability that a hash ends in at least `k` zeros is `1/2^k`. So if you've seen `n` distinct values, the *longest* run of trailing zeros you observe will be roughly `log₂(n)`.

Therefore: **`n ≈ 2^(max trailing zeros seen)`**

That's it. One integer of state.

## Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>

// SplitMix64: a fast, well-distributed hash for 64-bit values.
// (In production, use MurmurHash3, xxHash, or a crypto hash.)
uint64_t splitmix64(uint64_t x) {
    x += 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL;
    x = (x ^ (x >> 30)) * 0xbf58476d1ce4e5b9ULL;
    x = (x ^ (x >> 27)) * 0x94d049bb133111ebULL;
    return x ^ (x >> 31);
}

// Flajolet-Martin distinct counter.
// Memory: O(1) — literally one integer.
// Accuracy: probabilistic, standard error ≈ 0.78 (high variance!).
class DistinctCounter {
    int maxRho = 0;   // position of least-significant 1-bit in any hash seen

public:
    // Process one stream element.
    void insert(uint64_t value) {
        uint64_t h = splitmix64(value);
        // __builtin_ctzll = count trailing zeros.
        // +1 converts "k trailing zeros" to "1-bit at position k+1".
        // If h == 0, all 64 bits are zero, so rho = 65.
        int rho = (h == 0) ? 65 : __builtin_ctzll(h) + 1;
        if (rho > maxRho) maxRho = rho;
    }

    // Estimated number of distinct elements.
    double estimate() const {
        return std::pow(2.0, maxRho);
    }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << "Flajolet-Martin Distinct Counter\n";
    std::cout << "================================\n\n";

    for (int n : {100, 1'000, 10'000, 100'000, 1'000'000}) {
        DistinctCounter dc;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) dc.insert(i);

        std::cout << "Actual: " << std::setw(8) << n
                  << "  |  Estimated: " << std::setw(8)
                  << std::fixed << std::setprecision(0) << dc.estimate()
                  << '\n';
    }
}
```

Sample output (will vary due to randomness of the hash):
```
Actual:      100  |  Estimated:      64
Actual:     1000  |  Estimated:    1024
Actual:    10000  |  Estimated:    8192
Actual:   100000  |  Estimated:  131072
Actual:  1000000  |  Estimated: 1048576
```

The estimates are in the right ballpark but can easily be off by 2×. That's the problem with a single counter — **variance is huge**.

## Getting Better Accuracy: HyperLogLog

The practical descendant of Flajolet–Martin is **HyperLogLog**. The idea: instead of one counter, use `m` counters (say `m = 2^14 = 16384`). Route each element to a counter using the first `log₂(m)` bits of its hash, then track max trailing zeros *within each bucket*. Average the buckets using the harmonic mean.

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <algorithm>

class HyperLogLog {
    static constexpr int B = 14;                  // bits for bucket index
    static constexpr int M = 1 << B;              // 16384 buckets
    std::array<uint8_t, M> reg{};                 // ~16 KB, still "constant"

    static double alpha(int m) {                  // bias-correction constant
        return 0.7213 / (1.0 + 1.079 / m);
    }

public:
    void insert(uint64_t value) {
        uint64_t h = splitmix64(value);
        int j = h >> (64 - B);                    // top B bits = bucket
        // Find position of first 1-bit in remaining bits
        uint8_t rho = 1;
        uint64_t w = h << B;
        for (; rho <= 64 - B && !(w & (1ULL << 63)); ++rho) w <<= 1;
        reg[j] = std::max(reg[j], rho);
    }

    double estimate() const {
        double sum = 0;
        int zeros = 0;
        for (auto r : reg) {
            sum += std::pow(2.0, -r);
            if (r == 0) ++zeros;
        }
        double E = alpha(M) * M * M / sum;

        // Small-range correction (linear counting)
        if (E <= 2.5 * M && zeros > 0)
            E = M * std::log(static_cast<double>(M) / zeros);
        return E;
    }
};
```

HyperLogLog uses **~16 KB** regardless of stream size and achieves **~1% standard error** — good enough that Redis, BigQuery, and PostgreSQL all ship it as a built-in aggregate (`PFCOUNT`, `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT`, etc.).

## Summary

| Algorithm       | Memory         | Std. Error | Notes                          |
|-----------------|----------------|------------|--------------------------------|
| Exact (hashset) | O(n)           | 0%         | What you can't avoid           |
| Flajolet–Martin | **O(1)**       | ~78%       | Beautiful, but noisy           |
| HyperLogLog     | O(log log n)*  | ~1%        | The practical sweet spot       |

\* A fixed constant in practice (e.g., 16 KB).

If you want true O(1) memory, use Flajolet–Martin. If you can spare a few kilobytes for dramatically better accuracy, use HyperLogLog.

