## Alternative: Try-Lock with Backoff

Another deadlock-free approach is to use `tryLock` and back off if you can't get both locks:

```javascript
transfer(fromId, toId, amount) {
  while (true) {
    const first = Math.min(fromId, toId);
    const second = Math.max(fromId, toId);

    this.#locks[first].lock();
    if (this.#locks[second].tryLock()) {
      try {
        // ... do the transfer ...
        return;
      } finally {
        this.#locks[second].unlock();
        this.#locks[first].unlock();
      }
    } else {
      // Couldn't get second lock — release first and retry
      this.#locks[first].unlock();
      // Optional: yield or sleep to reduce contention
    }
  }
}
```

This is also deadlock-free (a thread never holds a lock while waiting), but it can suffer from **livelock** under high contention (threads keep retrying without making progress). The lock-ordering approach avoids this problem.

## Summary

| Technique | Deadlock-free? | Livelock-free? | Notes |
|-----------|---------------|----------------|-------|
| Naive two-lock | ✗ | — | Can deadlock |
| Lock ordering | ✓ | ✓ (with fair lock) | Best general-purpose solution |
| Try-lock + backoff | ✓ | ✗ (possible livelock) | Needs random backoff to avoid livelock |

The lock-ordering technique is the workhorse of concurrent programming. Once you internalize it, you'll see it everywhere—from database transactions to distributed systems.

