# Portable 64-bit Atomic Integer Access (C11)

```c
/* atomic64.h -- portable atomic 64-bit integer reads/writes */
#ifndef ATOMIC64_H
#define ATOMIC64_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>

/*
 * We use uint_least64_t so the type exists on every platform C11 supports.
 * atomic_*_explicit lets us pick the memory order explicitly.
 *
 * Naturally aligned 64-bit loads/stores are atomic on every mainstream
 * architecture we care about (x86-64, ARMv8, POWER, MIPS64, RISC-V).
 * The C11 atomics layer guarantees this even on 32-bit platforms where
 * a 64-bit access would otherwise be two bus cycles.
 */

typedef _Atomic uint_least64_t atomic64_t;

/* Atomic read -- use when you need to see a value written by another thread
 * without any tearing. */
static inline uint64_t atomic64_load(const atomic64_t *a)
{
    return atomic_load_explicit(a, memory_order_relaxed);
}

/* Atomic write -- the new value becomes visible atomically. */
static inline void atomic64_store(atomic64_t *a, uint64_t v)
{
    atomic_store_explicit(a, v, memory_order_relaxed);
}

/* Full sequential-consistency variants -- use these when the access
 * participates in a happens-before chain with other atomics. */
static inline uint64_t atomic64_load_seq_cst(const atomic64_t *a)
{
    return atomic_load_explicit(a, memory_order_seq_cst);
}

static inline void atomic64_store_seq_cst(atomic64_t *a, uint64_t v)
{
    atomic_store_explicit(a, v, memory_order_seq_cst);
}

#endif /* ATOMIC64_H */
```

Example usage:

```c
#include "atomic64.h"
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static atomic64_t counter = 0;

static void *worker(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++)
        atomic64_store(&counter, atomic64_load(&counter) + 1);
    return NULL;
}

int main(void)
{
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    printf("counter = %llu\n",
           (unsigned long long)atomic64_load(&counter));
    return 0;
}
```

Compile with any C11-capable compiler:

```
gcc -std=c11 -O2 -pthread example.c -o example
```

## How it works

1. **`_Atomic uint_least64_t`** — the C11 `_Atomic` qualifier tells the compiler the object is accessed through the atomic operations library. The compiler is forbidden from merging, splitting, or reordering these accesses, and on platforms where a 64-bit access would not be naturally atomic (e.g. 32-bit ARM with MMU-disabled or older 32-bit x86 with misaligned data), the library emits the appropriate platform barrier / ldar-style instruction.

2. **`atomic_load_explicit` / `atomic_store_explicit`** — these are the only standard-guaranteed way to perform an atomic read or write. The compiler lowers them to a single MOV on 64-bit targets (where the architecture already guarantees atomicity) and to an OS-supplied lock-free routine or a small compare-and-swap loop on 32-bit targets.

3. **Memory order** — `memory_order_relaxed` is sufficient when you only need the access itself to be indivisible (no torn reads/writes). It imposes no ordering relative to other memory operations, so the compiler and CPU can still reorder surrounding loads and stores. If the atomic participates in a publication protocol (e.g. a flag guarding a data structure), use `memory_order_seq_cst` (or pair acquire/release) so the visibility is total.

4. **Fallback for pre-C11 toolchains** — if you must support compilers without `<stdatomic.h>`, the equivalent GCC/Clang builtins are `__atomic_load_n(&v, __ATOMIC_RELAXED)` and `__atomic_store_n(&v, x, __ATOMIC_RELAXED)`; on MSVC use `_InterlockedExchange64` for stores and a 64-bit read under `MemoryBarrier()` for loads. The C11 form above is the portable front-end for all of them.

The bottom line: by routing every read and write through `atomic_load_explicit` / `atomic_store_explicit`, you get a single, indivisible 64-bit access on every platform the C11 atomics library supports — which is effectively every platform modern services run on.

# The honest answer first

What you're describing — fully coherent shared memory with **zero** synchronisation — doesn't really exist, even in principle. Hardware cache coherency only guarantees that a write eventually becomes visible to other cores; it says nothing about **ordering** or **race conditions**. You'll always need *some* form of synchronisation (atomics, fences, or higher-level primitives) for correctness.

That said, several modern stacks get you as close as possible: the CPU and GPU see the same addresses, writes propagate in hardware, and you avoid explicit copies. Below are the main options with working code.

---

## 1. ROCm / HIP — closest to the HSA ideal (AMD APUs)

HSA was literally designed for this. On a supported AMD APU, `hipMallocManaged` returns a pointer that both CPU and GPU access coherently.

```cpp
// managed_mem.hip
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

#define HIP_CHECK(x) do { hipError_t e = (x); if (e != hipSuccess) { \
    std::printf("HIP error: %s\n", hipGetErrorString(e)); std::abort(); } } while(0)

struct Shared {
    int  data;
    std::atomic<int> ready{0};   // still need an atomic for ordering
};

__global__ void consumer(Shared* s) {
    // Spin until ready. atomic_load gives us the fence we need.
    while (s->ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == 0) { /* spin */ }
    std::printf("GPU saw data = %d\n", s->data);
}

int main() {
    Shared* s;
    HIP_CHECK(hipMallocManaged(&s, sizeof(Shared)));   // coherent on HSA APUs
    HIP_CHECK(hipMemset(s, 0, sizeof(Shared)));

    // Launch consumer first so it's already waiting
    hipLaunchKernelGGL(consumer, 1, 1, 0, 0, s);

    // CPU producer
    s->data = 42;
    s->ready.store(1, std::memory_order_release);       // <-- still needed

    HIP_CHECK(hipDeviceSynchronize());
    hipFree(s);
}
```

Build: `hipcc managed_mem.hip -o managed_mem`

**What you still need:** the atomic. Coherent memory ≠ ordered memory.

---

## 2. CUDA Managed Memory (NVIDIA, Pascal and later)

`cudaMallocManaged` gives a single pointer valid on host and device. Page faults migrate on access; on modern GPUs (Pascal+) accesses are largely coherent, but you still need fences/atomics.

```cpp
// managed_mem.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <atomic>

struct Shared {
    int data;
    int ready;   // plain int, but we use atomic ops on it
};

__global__ void consumer(Shared* s) {
    while (atomicAdd(&s->ready, 0) == 0) { /* spin */ }
    __threadfence();   // ensure we re-read s->data after seeing ready==1
    printf("GPU saw data = %d\n", s->data);
}

int main() {
    Shared* s;
    cudaMallocManaged(&s, sizeof(Shared));
    s->data = 0; s->ready = 0;

    consumer<<<1,1>>>(s);
    cudaDeviceSynchronize();   // not strictly required, but lets us set up

    s->data = 42;
    atomicExch(&s->ready, 1);   // release semantics on the GPU side

    cudaDeviceSynchronize();
    cudaFree(s);
}
```

Build: `nvcc managed_mem.cu -o managed_mem -arch=sm_60`

**Caveat:** managed memory is *not* zero-copy by default; pages migrate. For true zero-copy on integrated GPUs use `cudaSetDeviceFlags(cudaDeviceSetLimit, ...)` or, better, on Jetson/embedded, `cudaMallocHost` with the right flags.

---

## 3. SYCL / oneAPI USM — *shared* allocations (Intel GPUs, some CPUs)

SYCL's USM has three flavours: device, host, **shared**. `malloc_shared` returns a pointer both host and device can touch directly.

```cpp
// managed_mem.cpp  --  icpx -fsycl managed_mem.cpp
#include <sycl/sycl.hpp>
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdio>

struct Shared {
    int data;
    std::atomic<int> ready{0};
};

int main() {
    sycl::queue q{sycl::gpu_selector_v};   // try gpu_selector first
    Shared* s = sycl::malloc_shared<Shared>(1, q);
    *s = Shared{0, {0}};

    q.submit([&](sycl::handler& h) {
        h.single_task([=]() {
            while (s->ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == 0) {}
            std::printf("Device saw data = %d\n", s->data);
        });
    });

    s->data = 42;
    s->ready.store(1, std::memory_order_release);

    q.wait();
    sycl::free(s, q);
}
```

Build: `icpx -fsycl managed_mem.cpp -o managed_mem`

---

## 4. OpenMP 5 unified shared memory

If you already use OpenMP for offload, this is the tersest option:

```cpp
// managed_mem.cpp  --  clang -fopenmp -fopenmp-targets=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa managed_mem.cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <atomic>

struct Shared {
    int data;
    std::atomic<int> ready{0};
};

#pragma omp requires unified_shared_memory

int main() {
    Shared* s = new Shared{0, {0}};

    #pragma omp target parallel for
    for (int i = 0; i < 1; ++i) {
        while (s->ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == 0) {}
        std::printf("Device saw data = %d\n", s->data);
    }

    s->data = 42;
    s->ready.store(1, std::memory_order_release);

    #pragma omp barrier   // ensures host waits for device kernel
    delete s;
}
```

---

## What you can never escape

Even with perfect hardware coherency, three things remain your responsibility:

| Concern | Why coherency doesn't help | What you use |
|---|---|---|
| **Ordering** | Stores can be observed out of program order | `std::atomic` with `release`/`acquire`, `__threadfence()`, OpenCL/SYCL fences |
| **Races on the same location** | Two writers → torn/garbage values | Atomics (`fetch_add`, `compare_exchange`) or mutexes |
| **Happens-before edges** | You need to know *when* a write is visible | The same atomics/fences above |

A useful mental model:

> **Coherent memory** answers *"will I eventually see this write?"* — yes.
> **Synchronisation** answers *"am I guaranteed to see it **now**, and **before** I do this other thing?"* — only with explicit primitives.

If you genuinely have a problem where no synchronisation is needed (e.g. the GPU only reads data the CPU wrote *before* the kernel launch, and never touches it again until after `synchronize`), then you can drop the atomics — but that's a property of *your algorithm*, not of the memory system.

# Zero-Initialized Allocator Wrapper

> **Important caveat:** Standard `malloc(3)` does *not* guarantee zero-initialized memory — only `calloc(3)` does. If your code depends on this, the right fix is usually to audit call sites and use `calloc` (or explicit `memset`). The wrapper below is useful when you can't touch every call site (e.g. third-party code, or a staged migration).

## The code

```c
/* zmalloc.h */
#ifndef ZMALLOC_H
#define ZMALLOC_H

#include <stddef.h>

/* Drop-in replacement for malloc that returns zero-filled memory. */
void *zmalloc(size_t size);

/* Optional: also wrap realloc / calloc / free consistently. */
void *zcalloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size);
void *zrealloc(void *ptr, size_t old_size, size_t new_size);
void  zfree(void *ptr);

#endif /* ZMALLOC_H */
```

```c
/* zmalloc.c */
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include "zmalloc.h"

#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdint.h>

/* Saturating multiply — calloc-style overflow guard. */
static size_t sat_mul(size_t a, size_t b)
{
    if (a != 0 && b > SIZE_MAX / a) return 0; /* signal failure */
    return a * b;
}

void *zmalloc(size_t size)
{
    /* calloc(1, size) is the canonical "zero-initialized malloc":
     *   - it is allowed to (and on most OSes does) skip zeroing pages
     *     that the kernel already handed it as zero-filled,
     *   - but it MUST zero any pages it pulls from its own internal
     *     free lists (which may contain stale data). */
    return calloc(1, size);
}

void *zcalloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size)
{
    size_t total = sat_mul(nmemb, size);
    if (total == 0 && nmemb != 0 && size != 0) return NULL; /* overflow */
    return calloc(nmemb, size);
}

void *zrealloc(void *ptr, size_t old_size, size_t new_size)
{
    void *p = realloc(ptr, new_size);
    if (!p) return NULL;

    /* realloc may have moved the block and the new tail bytes are
     * uninitialized. Zero only the grown region. */
    if (new_size > old_size) {
        memset((char *)p + old_size, 0, new_size - old_size);
    }
    return p;
}

void zfree(void *ptr)
{
    free(ptr);
}
```

### Optional: redirect every `malloc` call site transparently

```c
/* At the top of exactly ONE translation unit, *after* including
   all standard headers: */
#define malloc  zmalloc
#define calloc  zcalloc
#define realloc(ptr, new)  zrealloc(ptr, /* old_size unknown */ 0, new)
#define free    zfree
```

Because this is a textual `#define`, every `malloc(n)` in that TU becomes `zmalloc(n)`. Don't use it in headers — it will leak into every includer.

## How it works

1. **`zmalloc` → `calloc(1, size)`.** `calloc` is the only standard allocator guaranteed to return zeroed memory. Implementations exploit two facts:
   - The kernel hands out fresh pages already zeroed (e.g. Linux's `MAP_ANONYMOUS`, Windows' zero-fill-on-demand), so no work is needed for brand-new pages.
   - For recycled pages from the allocator's own free lists, `calloc` must explicitly `memset` them — this is the part `malloc` skips.

2. **`zcalloc`** adds an overflow check on `nmemb * size` so a maliciously large count can't wrap around to a tiny allocation (a classic integer-overflow bug in hand-rolled `calloc` wrappers).

3. **`zrealloc`** is the subtle one. `realloc` preserves the first `min(old, new)` bytes but leaves any *new* bytes uninitialized. We zero only the grown tail — `old_size` must be supplied by the caller (the macro above passes `0`, which is safe but over-zeros; in a real migration, prefer explicit `zrealloc(p, known_old, new)`).

4. **`zfree`** is just `free`; included so the macro redirection is symmetric and you can swap allocators in one place.

## Performance note

On Linux/glibc, `calloc` already optimizes the common case (fresh pages are already zero, so it skips `memset`). The overhead is therefore mostly limited to recycled-memory paths. If profiling shows this matters, the long-term fix is to migrate call sites to `calloc` directly and drop the wrapper.

# Constant-Time Programming in Rust — and an Important Caveat

## First, the honest correction

The premise — "if both sides execute, timing side channels are physically impossible" — is **not quite right**, even on Apple Silicon. Modern CPUs leak through many side channels that don't depend on which path executes:

- **Cache state** (data touched, even speculatively)
- **Branch prediction history** (Spectre, Augury on Apple Silicon)
- **Port contention / execution unit pressure**
- **TLB state, memory bus arbitration**
- **Power / EM emanations**

So "execute both sides" is neither necessary nor sufficient. What you actually want is **data-independent control flow and memory access patterns** — the discipline called *constant-time programming*. Rust gives you good tools for this.

---

## The core toolkit

### 1. `subtle` — the canonical crate

[`subtle`](https://crates.io/crates/subtle) provides constant-time primitives explicitly designed to resist compiler optimizations that would reintroduce branches.

```toml
# Cargo.toml
[dependencies]
subtle = { version = "2.6", features = ["const-generics"] }
```

```rust
use subtle::{Choice, ConditionallySelectable, ConstantTimeEq, CtOption};

// --- Constant-time equality ---
fn ct_eq(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> Choice {
    a.ct_eq(b) // returns Choice (1 or 0), no short-circuit
}

// --- Constant-time conditional select ---
// Pick x or y based on a Choice, without branching.
let result = u32::conditional_select(&a, &b, flag);

// --- Constant-time option unwrap ---
// Returns `default` if `is_some` is 0, else the inner value.
let value: u32 = ct_opt.unwrap_or(default_value);
```

`Choice` is a wrapper around `u8` that the crate marks specially so the optimizer keeps it branchless. **Don't** unwrap it with `if choice.into() { ... }` — that defeats the purpose.

### 2. `constant_time_eq` — for byte slices

```rust
use constant_time_eq::constant_time_eq;

let equal: bool = constant_time_eq(b"hello", b"hello");
```

### 3. `core::hint::black_box` — fight the optimizer

The optimizer will happily delete "useless" work. `black_box` is an optimization barrier:

```rust
use core::hint::black_box;

// Prevents the compiler from constant-folding or eliminating the call.
let secret = black_box(secret_key);
let result = some_crypto_op(secret);
black_box(result);
```

Without this, the compiler may notice your secret never escapes and optimize away the whole computation — or worse, constant-fold it.

---

## A worked example: constant-time password check

```rust
use subtle::ConstantTimeEq;

fn verify_password(provided: &[u8], expected: &[u8]) -> bool {
    // ct_eq returns subtle::Choice; .into() converts to bool
    // but the comparison itself is branchless.
    provided.ct_eq(expected).into()
}
```

Compare with the **insecure** version:

```rust
// ❌ BAD: short-circuits on first mismatch → timing leak
fn verify_password_bad(provided: &[u8], expected: &[u8]) -> bool {
    provided == expected  // uses memcmp-like short-circuit
}
```

---

## A bigger example: constant-time AES-style table lookup

Table lookups with secret indices are a classic cache-timing leak. The fix is to *always* touch every entry and select:

```rust
use subtle::ConditionallySelectable;

const TABLE: [u8; 256] = [/* ... sbox ... */];

fn ct_table_lookup(index: u8) -> u8 {
    let mut result = 0u8;
    for i in 0u8..=255 {
        // For each i, conditionally select TABLE[i] when i == index.
        let mask = (i == index) as u8; // compiler must keep this branchless
        let mask = mask.wrapping_neg(); // 0x00 or 0xFF
        result |= TABLE[i as usize] & mask;
    }
    result
}
```

In practice, `subtle` and crates like `aes` / `chacha20` from RustCrypto already do this for you — **prefer vetted libraries over hand-rolled crypto**.

---

## What about Apple Silicon specifically?

A few notes:

1. **No microarchitectural timing side channel is fully eliminated by "executing both sides."** Apple has documented Augury (pointer chasing via DMP) and Spectre variants on M-series chips.
2. **Apple's pointer authentication (PAC)** and **memory tagging (MTE on A14+/M1+)** are hardware features that mitigate *some* classes of attack but don't make constant-time discipline unnecessary.
3. **Apple's `os_log` and friends are not constant-time** — never log secrets.
4. For high-assurance work, look at [**`faerie`**](https://github.com/aesophor/faerie), [**`elvish`](https://github.com/aesophor/elvish), or the [**HACL\***](https://github.com/hacl-star/hacl-star) verified crypto in Rust.

---

## Checklist for constant-time Rust

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| No `if`/`match` on secret data | Branches leak via prediction + cache |
| No array indexing by secret | Cache line loads leak the index |
| No early-return comparisons | Short-circuit leaks position of first diff |
| Use `subtle::Choice`, `CtOption` | Branchless selection primitives |
| Wrap secrets in `black_box` | Stops the optimizer from undoing your work |
| Use `#[inline(never)]` sparingly | Inlining can sometimes reintroduce branches |
| Audit with `cargo asm` / `cargo-show-asm` | Verify the compiler didn't branch |
| Run `dudect` or `ctgrind` | Statistical / dynamic timing-leak detection |

---

## TL;DR

You can't get "physically impossible" timing side channels just by executing both sides — but you *can* write **constant-time** Rust using `subtle`, `black_box`, and disciplined control flow. For real crypto, **use RustCrypto crates** rather than rolling your own; they've already done this work.

