## The Core Idea

A call to a function whose body is **not visible** to the compiler (external linkage, not inlined) acts as a **full compiler memory barrier**. The compiler must assume the callee could read or write any reachable memory, so it cannot hoist loads above the call or sink stores below it.

This is documented behavior in GCC, Clang, and MSVC, and is a foundational idiom in systems code (Linux kernel, etc.).

## Minimal Working Example

```cpp
