extern int payload, ready;

int consume() {
    while (ready == 0) {}   // spin
    return payload;          // guaranteed to observe 42
}
```

Verify with `g++ -O2 -S producer.cpp -o -` — you'll see `payload` and `ready` stores emitted **before** the call to `side_effect()`, in program order. Remove the call and the compiler is free to swap them.

## Explicit, Portable Alternatives

If the function might get inlined (defeating the barrier), or you want to be explicit:

```cpp
#include <atomic>

// (1) GCC/Clang inline assembly — the classic "memory clobber"
inline void barrier_asm() {
    asm volatile("" ::: "memory");
}

// (2) Standard C++ — compiler barrier only, no CPU fence
inline void barrier_signal() {
    std::atomic_signal_fence(std::memory_order_acq_rel);
}

// (3) Standard C++ — full barrier (compiler + CPU)
inline void barrier_full() {
    std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_seq_cst);
}
```

| Method | Compiler barrier | CPU barrier | Portable |
|---|---|---|---|
| External function call | ✅ | ❌ | de facto |
| `asm volatile("" ::: "memory")` | ✅ | ❌ | GCC/Clang |
| `atomic_signal_fence(acq_rel)` | ✅ | ❌¹ | C++11 |
| `atomic_thread_fence(seq_cst)` | ✅ | ✅ | C++11 |

¹ On most implementations; the standard doesn't require it.

## Important Caveats

1. **Compiler barrier ≠ CPU barrier.** A function call stops the *compiler* from reordering, but the CPU may still reorder. On x86 (TSO) this usually doesn't matter for loads/stores. On **ARM, RISC-V, or POWER**, you need an explicit CPU fence (`atomic_thread_fence`, atomic RMW, etc.) for cross-thread visibility.

