The honest answer is: **you can't, provably, in ordinary user space on a commodity CPU.** The premise doesn't quite hold together. Here's why, and here's what the best you *can* do looks like.

## Why "provably immune to all timing side channels" isn't achievable

Even if your code is perfect at the source level, you don't control:

- **Caches (L1/L2/L3/TLB).** Other code (yours, the kernel, other tenants) evicts your lines. Flush+Reload, Prime+Probe, Evict+Time all work against you even with no secret-dependent accesses in *your* code, because shared lines and prefetchers leak.
- **Branch predictors.** State persists across context switches and across calls. Even a perfectly branchless function can be preceded by attacker-trainable branches.
- **Speculative execution.** Spectre-class leaks happen *during* speculation, before any "constant-time" check resolves. `lfence`/`sb`/`isb` are mitigations, not proofs — they fence one known pattern, not all future microarchitectural discoveries.
- **Port contention, memory bus, DRAM row buffers, on-chip interconnect.** These are undocumented, model-specific, and observable.
- **The implementation itself.** "Constant-time" is a property of the *compiled and executing* code, not the source. Compilers (especially optimizing ones) introduce branches, table jumps, and variable-time sequences. CPU microcode revisions change timing.

Formal-verification tools like `ct-verif`, `Jasmin`, `FaCT`, `HACL*`, `dudect` verify the *absence of certain source-level patterns* (no secret-indexed loads/branches). They do **not** prove the absence of microarchitectural leakage. There is no tool that takes "ordinary user-space C on a commodity CPU" and emits a proof of timing-channel immunity, because the underlying machine model isn't strong enough to support one.

## What the best practical approximation looks like

This is what production crypto libraries (libsodium, BearSSL, BoringSSL, HACL*) actually do. It's *defense in depth*, not a proof:

```c
// Constant-time primitives (compare, select, multiply, mod).
// Compiled with -O3 -fvisibility=hidden -fwrapv, no LTO surprises,
// and ideally verified post-compilation (e.g., valgrind-based ct-checking
// or binary inspection with tools like Binsec/Relayer).

#include <stdint.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

// Constant-time byte comparison: no branches, no secret-indexed memory.
int ct_memcmp(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n) {
    const unsigned char *x = (const unsigned char *)a;
    const unsigned char *y = (const unsigned char *)b;
    unsigned int r = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        r |= (unsigned int)(x[i] ^ y[i]);
    }
    // Force a uniform data-independent sequence point.
    _mm_lfence();
    return (int)(-r);  // 0 iff equal
}

// Constant-time conditional copy: select = mask ? a : b, byte-wise.
void ct_select(uint8_t *out, const uint8_t *a, const uint8_t *b,
               size_t n, uint8_t mask) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // mask is 0xFF or 0x00; & and | are constant-time on every ISA.
        out[i] = (a[i] & mask) | (b[i & ~mask]);  // b[i & ~mask] is just b[i]
    }
    _mm_lfence();
}

// Constant-time conditional move of a *pointer* (e.g., for masked table lookups).
// Use this instead of `if (secret) p = a; else p = b;`.
static inline const void *
ct_ptr_select(const void *a, const void *b, uint8_t mask) {
    uintptr_t x = (uintptr_t)a;
    uintptr_t y = (uintptr_t)b;
    // Arithmetic on addresses is constant-time; no branch.
    return (const void *)((x & mask) | (y & ~mask));
}
```

And the discipline around it:

1. **No secret-indexed branches or memory accesses.** Every control-flow decision and every load address must be a public function of public data.
2. **Speculation fences around secret-dependent data paths.** `lfence` (x86), `sb`/`isb` (ARM), `csync` (POWER) before a secret is consumed by a load. Heuristic, not a proof.
3. **Blinding/masking.** Don't operate on the secret directly; randomize it, do the work, then unrandomize. Leaks become leaks of a one-time pad.
4. **Cache-aware data layout.** Align sensitive tables to cache-line boundaries; pad to avoid false sharing; consider page coloring if you control the allocator.
5. **Disable turbo/boost and pin cores** if you control the host (you said "ordinary user space," so usually you don't).
6. **Verify the binary, not just the source.** Tools: `dudect` (statistical), `microarchitectural leakage detection` papers, `Binsec/Relayer` for symbolic execution of the binary.
7. **Assume the rest of the system is hostile.** Co-resident VMs, kernel code, even your own other threads are potential timing attackers.

## The bottom line

If you need *provable* immunity, you need a stronger machine than a commodity CPU in ordinary user space: a formally verified ISA with a timing-contract model (e.g., the work on timing-attestable ISAs, or hardware enclaves like SGX/TrustZone/SEV — and even *those* have had repeated timing-channel breaks). On a normal CPU in normal user space, you can get *very good practical resistance* with constant-time coding, masking, and fences, but "provably immune to all timing side channels" is not a property you can establish — only one you can fail to falsify via testing.

