## Why a 32-bit key can't be "unbreakable"

The security of any cipher ultimately rests on **key space size**. A 32-bit key has only 2³² ≈ 4 billion possible values. A modern laptop can exhaust that keyspace in **seconds**. No amount of clever coding changes this — it's a fundamental mathematical limit, not an implementation problem.

To put it in perspective:

| Key size | Brute-force time (approx) |
|----------|---------------------------|
| 32 bits  | Seconds                   |
| 64 bits  | Days–weeks                |
| 128 bits | Heat death of universe    |

## AES itself won't even accept a 32-bit key

The AES standard (FIPS 197) defines key sizes of **128, 192, or 256 bits**. Libraries will reject anything smaller. Even if you forced one in (e.g., by zero-padding), you'd still only have 32 bits of entropy — the padding adds no security.

## What you should do instead

If your real situation is "I have a short secret and need to encrypt something," the right move is to **stretch that secret into a full-length key** using a password-based key derivation function. That way the security comes from the *secret*, not from the key length.

Here's a safe pattern using **libsodium** (the gold standard for modern C crypto):

