### How it stays memory-safe without bounds checks or sanitizers

The trick is to **never use an unbounded operation on untrusted data**. Every function that touches the request buffer takes an explicit length, and every output buffer is sized by the caller.

| Unsafe pattern (avoided) | Safe replacement (used) |
|---|---|
| `char buf[1024]; read(fd, buf, 99999);` | `realloc` grows a heap buffer to exactly fit the input; `read` is given `cap - len` |
| `strstr`, `strchr`, `index` on request bytes | `find_bytes` / `memchr` with an explicit max length |
| `strcpy`, `strcat`, `sprintf` | `memcpy` with source-derived length; `snprintf` with buffer size |
| `strlen` on attacker-controlled bytes | Lengths are tracked alongside the buffer; `strlen` is only used on string literals |
| Copying request fields into fixed structs | `request_t` holds `(pointer, length)` pairs that alias into the request buffer — no copies |
| `gets`, `scanf("%s")` | Not used; input is read as raw bytes with `read(2)` |

Because every read, search, and copy is length-bounded by construction, there is no place where an attacker can drive an index past an allocation. The only `if` statements left are genuine control-flow checks (malformed request → 400, allocation failure → abort, EOF → close), not bounds checks defending against memory errors.

The same recipe generalizes: **track lengths explicitly, alias instead of copying, and prefer length-bounded standard functions.** Done consistently, the program is memory-safe by construction — no ASan, no Valgrind, no language change required.

