## How it works

Total-order broadcast (TOB) needs two guarantees: **agreement** (every node delivers the same messages) and **order** (every node delivers them in the same sequence). Raft's replicated log already provides both — every committed entry appears at the same index on every replica, and entries are applied in log order. So TOB is just a thin wrapper:

- **Broadcast** = propose the message as a Raft log entry (`raft.Apply`).
- **Deliver** = read from a channel that the FSM pushes to as it applies committed entries.

Failure handling is layered:

| Failure | What happens |
|---|---|
| Leader crashes mid-broadcast | `Apply` returns `ErrLeadershipLost`; client retries. Raft guarantees the entry was either committed (and will be applied) or not — never half-applied. |
| Network partition (minority) | `Apply` returns `ErrNotLeader` or times out. Client redirects via `LeaderHint()`. |
| Node crash + restart | Raft replays committed entries through `FSM.Apply`. The dedup set discards already-delivered messages. |
| Duplicate broadcast (retry) | Idempotent via `Message.ID` — the FSM drops duplicates. |
| Lost response to client | Client retries with the same `Message.ID`; safe. |
| Slow consumer | Bounded delivery channel + per-apply timeout; entry is still committed and can be re-delivered on snapshot restore. |

