# k-d Tree for 2D Nearest-Neighbour Queries in C++

A **k-d tree** (k-dimensional tree) is a binary space-partitioning structure. At each level, it splits the points along one dimension (cycling through `x`, `y`, `x`, `y`, …). Using the **median** as the split point keeps the tree balanced, giving `O(log n)` average query time.

Below is a clean, self-contained implementation.

## The Code

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

// ---------- Point ----------
struct Point {
    double x, y;
};

// ---------- Node ----------
struct Node {
    Point pt;                              // the point stored here
    int dim;                               // splitting dimension: 0 = x, 1 = y
    std::unique_ptr<Node> left;            // "left" / "below" child
    std::unique_ptr<Node> right;           // "right" / "above" child

    Node(Point p, int d) : pt(p), dim(d) {}
};

// ---------- k-d Tree ----------
class KdTree {
public:
    // Build a balanced tree from a list of points.
    explicit KdTree(std::vector<Point> points) {
        root_ = build(std::move(points), 0);
    }

    // Return the point in the tree closest to `query`.
    // Throws if the tree is empty.
    Point nearest(const Point& query) const {
        if (!root_) throw std::runtime_error("nearest() on empty tree");
        const Node* best = nullptr;
        double best_d2   = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
        search(root_.get(), query, best, best_d2);
        return best->pt;
    }

private:
    std::unique_ptr<Node> root_;

    // ---- Build (recursive, median split) ----
    static std::unique_ptr<Node> build(std::vector<Point> pts, int depth) {
        if (pts.empty()) return nullptr;

        const int dim = depth % 2;                       // cycle x, y, x, y, ...
        const std::size_t mid = pts.size() / 2;

        // nth_element puts the median at `mid` in O(n) on average.
        auto cmp = [dim](const Point& a, const Point& b) {
            return dim == 0 ? a.x < b.x : a.y < b.y;
        };
        std::nth_element(pts.begin(), pts.begin() + mid, pts.end(), cmp);

        auto node = std::make_unique<Node>(pts[mid], dim);

        // Recurse on each half. We copy the halves because the
        // original vector is no longer needed after this call.
        std::vector<Point> left_pts(pts.begin(), pts.begin() + mid);
        std::vector<Point> right_pts(pts.begin() + mid + 1, pts.end());
        node->left  = build(std::move(left_pts),  depth + 1);
        node->right = build(std::move(right_pts), depth + 1);
        return node;
    }

    // ---- Squared distance (avoids a sqrt) ----
    static double dist2(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
        const double dx = a.x - b.x;
        const double dy = a.y - b.y;
        return dx * dx + dy * dy;
    }

    // ---- Recursive nearest-neighbour search ----
    static void search(const Node* node, const Point& query,
                       const Node*& best, double& best_d2) {
        if (!node) return;

        // 1. Consider the point at this node.
        const double d2 = dist2(node->pt, query);
        if (d2 < best_d2) {
            best_d2 = d2;
            best    = node;
        }

        // 2. Decide which child is the "near" side and which is the "far" side.
        //    diff > 0 means query is on the right/above the splitting plane.
        const double diff = (node->dim == 0)
                            ? (query.x - node->pt.x)
                            : (query.y - node->pt.y);

        const Node* near_child = (diff < 0) ? node->left.get() : node->right.get();
        const Node* far_child  = (diff < 0) ? node->right.get() : node->left.get();

        // 3. Always search the near side first.
        search(near_child, query, best, best_d2);

        // 4. Only search the far side if the splitting plane is closer
        //    than our current best — otherwise nothing over there can help.
        if (diff * diff < best_d2) {
            search(far_child, query, best, best_d2);
        }
    }
};
```

## How the Search Works

The trick is the **backtracking step** (step 4 above):

1. Walk down the tree, always going to the side of the splitting plane that contains the query point.
2. As you unwind the recursion, look at the point stored in each node you visited — it might be the closest so far.
3. Before recursing into the *other* subtree, check whether the splitting plane is closer than your current best. If not, that whole subtree can be pruned.

This is what makes k-d tree queries fast on average: large portions of the tree get eliminated without being visited.

## A Small Demo

```cpp
int main() {
    std::vector<Point> points = {
        { 2.0,  3.0},
        { 5.0,  4.0},
        { 9.0,  6.0},
        { 4.0,  7.0},
        { 8.0,  1.0},
        { 7.0,  2.0},
    };

    KdTree tree(points);

    Point query = {6.0, 4.0};
    Point ans   = tree.nearest(query);

    std::cout << "Query:        (" << query.x << ", " << query.y << ")\n"
              << "Nearest:      (" << ans.x   << ", " << ans.y   << ")\n"
              << "Distance:     " << std::sqrt(std::pow(ans.x - query.x, 2)
                                              + std::pow(ans.y - query.y, 2))
              << '\n';
}
```

Expected output:
```
Query:        (6, 4)
Nearest:      (5, 4)
Distance:     1
```

## Complexity

| Operation | Average | Worst case |
|-----------|---------|------------|
| Build     | `O(n log n)` | `O(n log n)` |
| Query     | `O(log n)`   | `O(n)` (degenerate data) |

The worst case happens when points are inserted in sorted order without median splitting — that's why we rebuild from scratch with `nth_element` rather than inserting one point at a time.

## Things to Try Next

- **k-NN queries**: keep a max-heap of size `k` instead of a single `best` point.
- **Templated `dim`**: replace the `dim == 0 ? x : y` checks with a generic `std::array<double, K>` and a compile-time dimension.
- **Build by inserting**: simpler to code but produces unbalanced trees; useful only if you need online updates.
- **Range queries**: report all points inside an axis-aligned rectangle — same recursive structure, just a different pruning rule.

