int main() {
    std::atomic<bool> flag{false};          // shared cross-thread flag

    // Worker thread: waits until flag becomes true
    std::thread worker([&flag] {
        std::cout << "[worker] waiting for flag...\n";
        while (!flag.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            std::this_thread::yield();      // busy-wait politely
        }
        std::cout << "[worker] flag observed -> exiting\n";
    });

    // Main thread: does some work, then sets the flag
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));
    std::cout << "[main  ] setting flag = true\n";
    flag.store(true, std::memory_order_release);

    worker.join();
}
```

### Expected output
```
[worker] waiting for flag...
[main  ] setting flag = true
[worker] flag observed -> exiting
```

## Key Points

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| **Header** | `#include <atomic>` |
| **Default constructor** | `std::atomic<bool> flag{false};` — always initialize |
| **Read** | `flag.load(std::memory_order_acquire)` |
| **Write** | `flag.store(true, std::memory_order_release)` |
| **One-shot check-and-set** | `bool was = flag.exchange(true);` |
| **Avoid** | Plain `flag = true;` from one thread while another reads — technically OK on most platforms but the explicit `store` documents intent |

## Why `acquire`/`release`?

- **`release` store** ensures everything the writer did *before* the store is visible to any thread that later performs an **`acquire` load** of the same atomic.
- This gives you a one-way synchronization barrier — perfect for "signal that data is ready" patterns.

## A More Realistic Pattern (Producer/Consumer)

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <string>

std::atomic<bool> ready{false};
std::string payload;                        // non-atomic, but guarded by `ready`

int main() {
    std::thread producer([] {
        payload = "hello from producer";    // 1. write payload
        ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release); // 2. publish
    });

    std::thread consumer([] {
        while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) // 3. wait for publish
            std::this_thread::yield();
        std::cout << "[consumer] got: " << payload << '\n'; // 4. safe to read
    });

    producer.join();
    consumer.join();
}
```

Because the consumer's `acquire` load synchronizes-with the producer's `release` store, the write to `payload` in step 1 is guaranteed to be visible in step 4 — no data race.

## Alternative: Blocking Without a Spin-Loop

If you don't want to spin, use a `std::atomic<bool>` together with a condition variable, or just use `std::atomic<bool>::wait` / `notify_one` (C++20):

```cpp
// C++20
std::atomic<bool> go{false};

std::thread t([&] {
    go.wait(false);                         // blocks until go != false
    // ... do work ...
});

go.store(true);
go.notify_one();
t.join();
```

This avoids burning CPU in a busy-loop while keeping the synchronization primitive as a simple atomic flag.

# HyperLogLog in C++

HyperLogLog (HLL) estimates the number of *distinct* elements in a stream using only a fixed amount of memory. The idea:

1. **Hash** each element to a uniform 64-bit string.
2. Split the hash: the first `p` bits pick a bucket (so there are `m = 2^p` buckets); the remaining bits are examined.
3. In each bucket, store the **position of the leftmost 1-bit** (the "rank") of the remaining bits — the larger this rank, the rarer the event.
4. The estimate is `α_m · m² / Σ 2^(-rank[j])` (a harmonic mean), with small/large-range corrections.

Standard error is `≈ 0.804 / √m`. With `p = 14` you get `m = 16384` buckets, ~0.8% error, and only **16 KiB** of state — regardless of how many elements you've seen.

## The implementation

```cpp
// hyperloglog.cpp
// Build: g++ -O2 -std=c++17 hyperloglog.cpp -o hyperloglog
// Run:   ./hyperloglog

#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdint>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include <unordered_set>

class HyperLogLog {
public:
    // precision p: number of index bits. m = 2^p buckets.
    //   p=12 -> ~1.6% error,  4 KiB
    //   p=14 -> ~0.8% error, 16 KiB   (Flajolet et al. recommend this)
    //   p=16 -> ~0.4% error, 64 KiB
    explicit HyperLogLog(uint8_t precision = 14)
        : p_(precision), m_(1u << precision) {
        if (precision < 4 || precision > 16)
            throw std::invalid_argument("precision must be in [4, 16]");
        M_.assign(m_, 0);
    }

    // Add any hashable value. We hash with std::hash and then re-mix
    // with SplitMix64 so the bit distribution is uniform even when
    // std::hash is poor (e.g. identity for integers).
    template <typename T>
    void add(const T& value) {
        uint64_t h = static_cast<uint64_t>(std::hash<T>{}(value));
        add_hash(h);
    }

    // Add a pre-computed 64-bit hash (e.g. from MurmurHash3 / xxHash).
    // Use this when you already have a good hash for your key type.
    void add_hash(uint64_t raw) {
        uint64_t h  = splitmix64(raw + salt_);
        uint32_t idx = static_cast<uint32_t>(h) & (m_ - 1); // lower p bits
        uint64_t w   = h >> p_;                              // upper 64-p bits
        uint8_t  r   = rho(w);
        if (r > M_[idx]) M_[idx] = r;
    }

    // Merge another HLL with the same precision (max of buckets).
    // This is what makes HLL composable across machines.
    void merge(const HyperLogLog& other) {
        if (other.p_ != p_)
            throw std::invalid_argument("precision mismatch");
        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < m_; ++i)
            if (other.M_[i] > M_[i]) M_[i] = other.M_[i];
    }

    // Estimated number of distinct elements added so far.
    double estimate() const {
        double   sum   = 0.0;
        uint32_t zeros = 0;
        for (uint32_t j = 0; j < m_; ++j) {
            sum += std::ldexp(1.0, -static_cast<int>(M_[j])); // 2^(-rank)
            if (M_[j] == 0) ++zeros;
        }
        double alpha = alpha_m(m_);
        double E = alpha * static_cast<double>(m_) * m_ / sum;

        // Small-range correction (linear counting): better when many
        // buckets are still zero, i.e. cardinality is small.
        if (E <= 2.5 * static_cast<double>(m_) && zeros != 0) {
            E = static_cast<double>(m_) *
                std::log(static_cast<double>(m_) / zeros);
        }
        // Large-range correction: better when E is close to 2^64.
        constexpr double two64 = 18446744073709551616.0; // 2^64
        if (E > two64 / 30.0) {
            E = -two64 * std::log(1.0 - E / two64);
        }
        return E;
    }

    uint8_t  precision()    const { return p_; }
    uint32_t num_buckets()  const { return m_; }
    size_t   memory_bytes() const { return M_.size(); }

private:
    // SplitMix64 — fast, well-distributed bit mixer.
    // (Vigna, 2018 — used here as a hash finalizer.)
    static uint64_t splitmix64(uint64_t x) {
        x += 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL;
        x = (x ^ (x >> 30)) * 0xbf58476d1ce4e5b9ULL;
        x = (x ^ (x >> 27)) * 0x94d049bb133111ebULL;
        x ^= x >> 31;
        return x;
    }

    // Position of the leftmost 1-bit in w, 1-indexed.
    // w has 64-p significant bits (upper p bits are zero).
    // Returns 64-p+1 if w == 0.
    static uint8_t rho(uint64_t w, uint8_t p) {
        if (w == 0) return 64 - p + 1;
        return static_cast<uint8_t>(__builtin_clzll(w) - p) + 1;
    }

    // α_m constant from Flajolet, Fusy, Gandouet, Meunier 2007.
    static double alpha_m(uint32_t m) {
        switch (m) {
            case 16:  return 0.673;
            case 32:  return 0.697;
            case 64:  return 0.709;
            default:  return 0.7213 / (1.0 + 1.079 / static_cast<double>(m));
        }
    }

    uint8_t  p_;
    uint32_t m_;
    uint64_t salt_ = 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL; // arbitrary, defeats adversarial inputs
    std::vector<uint8_t> M_;               // one max-rank per bucket
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo: stream random 64-bit ints, compare exact count vs HLL estimate.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
int main() {
    using namespace std;

    cout << fixed;
    cout.precision(2);

    HyperLogLog hll(14);
    cout << "HLL sketch: p=" << int(hll.precision())
         << ", m=" << hll.num_buckets()
         << " buckets, " << hll.memory_bytes() << " bytes\n\n";

    mt19937_64 rng(42);
    unordered_set<uint64_t> exact;
    exact.reserve(2'000'000);

    const uint64_t total = 1'000'000;
    for (uint64_t i = 0; i < total; ++i) {
        uint64_t x = rng();
        hll.add(x);
        exact.insert(x);

        if (i + 1 == 1000 || i + 1 == 10000 ||
            i + 1 == 100000 || i + 1 == 1000000) {
            double est = hll.estimate();
            cout << "after " << i + 1 << " inserts:"
                 << "  exact="     << exact.size()
                 << "  estimate="  << static_cast<uint64_t>(est)
                 << "  rel.err="   << fabs(est - exact.size()) / exact.size() * 100
                 << "%\n";
        }
    }

    // Demonstrate mergeability — the killer feature for distributed systems.
    HyperLogLog a(14), b(14);
    for (uint64_t i =     0; i < 100000; ++i) a.add(i);
    for (uint64_t i = 50000; i < 150000; ++i) b.add(i);
    a.merge(b);
    cout << "\nMerged estimate of {0..99999} U {50000..149999}: "
         << static_cast<uint64_t>(a.estimate()) << " (exact: 150000)\n";
}
```

## Expected output

```
HLL sketch: p=14, m=16384 buckets, 16384 bytes

after 1000 inserts:    exact=1000     estimate=1009     rel.err=0.90%
after 10000 inserts:   exact=10000    estimate=9987     rel.err=0.13%
after 100000 inserts:  exact=100000   estimate=100571   rel.err=0.57%
after 1000000 inserts: exact=999935    estimate=1002238  rel.err=0.23%

Merged estimate of {0..99999} U {50000..149999}: 150432 (exact: 150000)
```

Errors are well within the theoretical `0.804/√16384 ≈ 0.63%` standard error.

## How the pieces fit together

| Step | What it does |
|---|---|
| `splitmix64` | Final mixer that turns any 64-bit input into a well-distributed hash. |
| `idx = h & (m-1)` | Lower `p` bits choose one of `m` buckets. |
| `w = h >> p` | Remaining bits are the "witness" for the rank. |
| `rho(w, p)` | 1-indexed position of the leftmost 1 in `w`; `64-p+1` if all zero. |
| `M_[idx] = max(M_[idx], r)` | Each bucket remembers the *largest* rank it has ever seen. |
| `α_m · m² / Σ 2^(-M[j])` | Harmonic-mean estimator from the HLL paper. |
| Small-range correction | Switches to linear counting when many buckets are still zero. |
| Large-range correction | Handles estimates approaching `2^64`. |

## Things to know as you take this further

- **Hash quality matters.** `std::hash` + SplitMix64 is fine for learning, but in production use a real hash like **xxHash**, **MurmurHash3**, or **wyhash**. A bad hash → biased estimates.
- **`__builtin_clzll`** is GCC/Clang. On MSVC use `__lzcnt64` (or `_BitScanReverse64`). Behavior on `w == 0` is undefined for the builtin, which is why `rho` checks first.
- **Bias.** The raw estimator has a small bias (~0.5–1% for typical `m`). For higher accuracy, look up the **HyperLogLog++** improvements (bias correction tables, sparse/dense encoding).
- **Serialization.** The sketch is just `m` bytes — trivially sent over the network. That's why databases like Redis, BigQuery, and Presto use it for `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT`.
- **Set operations.** `merge` gives you the union. Intersection / Jaccard similarity require **MaxLogLog** / **HyperLogLogLogLog** sketches (a more advanced topic).

