# `deterministic_gc_mode` Implementation

Below is a runtime/GC implementation in C++ that exposes a `deterministic_gc_mode` feature. The idea is borrowed from real-world systems (e.g., Microsoft's `.NET` "no GC regions" and the JVM's `Epsilon`/deterministic modes), but packaged as a clean, self-contained feature.

## Header: `det_gc.hpp`

```cpp
#pragma once
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <atomic>
#include <functional>
#include <vector>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

namespace detgc {

// ---- Tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------
struct Config {
    std::size_t region_bytes   = 8 * 1024 * 1024; // total region budget
    std::size_t safepoint_budget = 64 * 1024;     // bytes allocatable per safepoint
    std::chrono::microseconds max_pause{50};      // hard ceiling per pause
};

// ---- Public API -------------------------------------------------------------
class GC {
public:
    static GC& instance();

    // Enter deterministic mode: GC will *only* run at explicit safepoints
    // or when the per-safepoint allocation budget is exhausted.
    void enter(const Config& cfg = {});

    // Leave deterministic mode: restore normal (possibly concurrent) GC.
    void leave();

    // Mark a deterministic safepoint. If a collection is pending, it runs
    // here, bounded by cfg.max_pause.
    void safepoint();

    // Allocation entry point used by the compiler-generated alloc fast-path.
    // Returns a non-null pointer or aborts if the region is exhausted.
    void* alloc(std::size_t bytes, std::size_t align = alignof(std::max_align_t));

    bool in_deterministic_mode() const { return mode_.load(); }

private:
    GC() = default;
    void collect_bounded();          // incremental, time-bounded collector
    void full_collect();             // fallback when budget is exceeded
    void* bump_alloc(std::size_t n, std::size_t a);

    struct Region {
        std::byte* base  = nullptr;
        std::byte* limit = nullptr;
        std::byte* next  = nullptr;
    };

    Config cfg_;
    Region region_;
    std::atomic<bool> mode_{false};
    std::atomic<std::size_t> since_last_{0}; // bytes allocated since last safepoint
    std::vector<void*> roots_;               // pinned roots registered by user
};

// ---- Scoped helper ----------------------------------------------------------
struct ScopedDeterministic {
    ScopedDeterministic(const Config& c = {}) { GC::instance().enter(c); }
    ~ScopedDeterministic()                   { GC::instance().leave();   }
};

} // namespace detgc
```

## Implementation: `det_gc.cpp`

```cpp
#include "det_gc.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <new>

namespace detgc {

GC& GC::instance() {
    static GC g;
    return g;
}

void GC::enter(const Config& cfg) {
    if (mode_.exchange(true)) return;          // already in mode
    cfg_ = cfg;

    // Carve out a contiguous region. In a real runtime this would come from
    // a dedicated arena; here we use new/delete for clarity.
    region_.base  = new std::byte[cfg_.region_bytes];
    region_.limit = region_.base + cfg_.region_bytes;
    region_.next  = region_.base;
    since_last_.store(0);

    // Disable background / concurrent GC threads here.
    // (In a real runtime: signal the concurrent collector to park.)
}

void GC::leave() {
    if (!mode_.exchange(false)) return;
    full_collect();                            // drain any leftover garbage
    delete[] region_.base;
    region_ = {};
}

void* GC::bump_alloc(std::size_t n, std::size_t a) {
    // Align the cursor.
    std::uintptr_t cur = reinterpret_cast<std::uintptr_t>(region_.next);
    std::uintptr_t aligned = (cur + a - 1) & ~(a - 1);
    std::byte* p = reinterpret_cast<std::byte*>(aligned);
    if (p + n > region_.limit) {
        // Region exhausted: deterministic mode forbids unbounded growth,
        // so we must collect now and retry.
        collect_bounded();
        return bump_alloc(n, a);
    }
    region_.next = p + n;
    return p;
}

void* GC::alloc(std::size_t bytes, std::size_t align) {
    // Fast path: bump-allocate inside the region.
    void* p = bump_alloc(bytes, align);
    since_last_.fetch_add(bytes);

    // If we've exceeded the per-safepoint budget, force a safepoint so the
    // pause is accounted for at a deterministic point, not asynchronously.
    if (since_last_.load() >= cfg_.safepoint_budget) {
        safepoint();
    }
    return p;
}

void GC::safepoint() {
    if (!mode_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return;
    if (since_last_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == 0) return;

    collect_bounded();
    since_last_.store(0, std::memory_order_release);
}

void GC::collect_bounded() {
    // Time-bounded incremental mark. We slice work into small units and
    // stop as soon as cfg_.max_pause is reached. Anything not finished is
    // picked up at the next safepoint.
    auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    std::size_t work_units = 0;

    // Pseudo: walk a small chunk of the heap graph per unit.
    // In a real collector this is a tri-color mark step.
    while (work_units++ < 1024) {
        // ... mark one object ...
        auto elapsed = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - start;
        if (elapsed >= cfg_.max_pause) break;
    }

    // If we finished early, do a tiny compaction of the bump region.
    // (Real impl: slide live objects down; here we just reset the cursor
    //  if no survivors exist — illustrative only.)
}

void GC::full_collect() {
    // Used on leave() and on region exhaustion. Not time-bounded.
    // ... standard mark-sweep-compact ...
    region_.next = region_.base;              // reset bump cursor
}

} // namespace detgc
```

## Example usage

```cpp
#include "det_gc.hpp"

struct Node { int v; Node* next; };

int main() {
    detgc::Config cfg;
    cfg.max_pause = std::chrono::microseconds(100);

    detgc::ScopedDeterministic region(cfg);   // enter deterministic mode

    for (int i = 0; i < 1'000'000; ++i) {
        auto* n = new (detgc::GC::instance().alloc(sizeof(Node))) Node{i, nullptr};
        (void)n;
        detgc::GC::instance().safepoint();    // deterministic pause point
    }
    // leaving scope runs a final non-bounded collection
}
```

## How it works

1. **Mode switch.** `enter()` allocates a contiguous *region* (arena) and disables the background/concurrent collector. From this point on, the only place GC work happens is at explicit `safepoint()` calls — never asynchronously.

2. **Bump allocation.** Allocations inside the region are pure pointer-bumps (`O(1)`, no per-object metadata). This is what makes the mode fast *and* predictable: allocation cost is constant and there is no write-barrier overhead.

3. **Budget-triggered safepoints.** Each allocation increments `since_last_`. When it crosses `safepoint_budget`, the runtime *forces* a safepoint so the pause is charged to a deterministic point in the program, not to an arbitrary moment chosen by a concurrent GC thread.

4. **Time-bounded collection.** `collect_bounded()` slices the mark phase into small work units and stops as soon as `cfg.max_pause` elapses. Unfinished work is resumed at the next safepoint, so no single pause ever exceeds the configured ceiling.

5. **Bounded region.** The arena has a fixed size (`region_bytes`). If it fills up, the runtime must collect — there is no fallback to "ask the OS for more memory" inside deterministic mode. This is what gives the *fully* deterministic guarantee: memory growth is bounded, so pause frequency is bounded.

6. **Exit.** `leave()` runs one unbounded collection to drain any remaining garbage and frees the arena, returning the runtime to its normal (possibly concurrent) GC policy.

The net effect: while the mode is active, every GC pause occurs at a `safepoint()` you control, and each pause is bounded by `cfg.max_pause`. That is what "fully deterministic GC pauses" means in this design.

## A quick clarification first

There isn't a separate protocol called "QUIC-Lossless." **QUIC is already lossless by design** — every QUIC stream is reliable and ordered, with retransmission, congestion control, and flow control built in (much like TCP, but with better latency behavior).

So what you actually want is just a normal QUIC client that uses **streams** — and that's what the example below does.

> If you ever see "QUIC" paired with "unreliable," that's the *unreliable datagram* extension (RFC 9221), which is the opposite of what you're asking for.

---

## Minimal QUIC client in C++ (using Cloudflare's **quiche**)

Quiche is a Rust implementation with a clean C API, which makes it ideal for C/C++ projects. Alternatives are **ngtcp2**, **MsQuic**, and **mvfst** — all viable, but quiche has the simplest example surface.

### Install quiche

```bash
# macOS
brew install quiche

# Or from source (needs Rust):
git clone --recursive https://github.com/cloudflare/quiche
cd quiche
cargo build --release --features ffi
# headers live in quiche/include/, lib in target/release/libquiche.{a,so}
```

### `quic_client.c`

```c
/*
 * Minimal QUIC client using quiche.
 *
 * QUIC streams are reliable + ordered, so any data you write() on a
 * stream is guaranteed to arrive at the peer intact and in order.
 *
 * Build:
 *   gcc quic_client.c -o quic_client -lquiche
 *
 * Usage:
 *   ./quic_client <server-ip> <port> <message>
 *
 * Try it against the public quiche server:
 *   ./quic_client 127.0.0.1 4433 "hello"
 *   (run: cargo run --release --example server --features ffi)
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>

#include <quiche.h>

#define LOCAL_CONN_ID_LEN 16
#define MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE 1350

static void die(const char *msg) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", msg, strerror(errno));
    exit(1);
}

/* Send any packets quiche wants to emit, return number of bytes sent or -1. */
static ssize_t flush_egress(int sock, quiche_conn *conn) {
    uint8_t out[MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE];
    ssize_t written = quiche_conn_send(conn, out, sizeof(out));
    if (written < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "quiche_conn_send failed: %zd\n", written);
        return -1;
    }
    if (written == 0) return 0;
    if (send(sock, out, written, 0) < 0) return -1;
    return written;
}

/* Drain the socket into quiche. */
static void drain_ingress(int sock, quiche_conn *conn) {
    uint8_t in[MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE];
    while (true) {
        ssize_t n = recv(sock, in, sizeof(in), 0);
        if (n <= 0) return;                 /* timeout or error -> stop */
        ssize_t done = quiche_conn_recv(conn, in, n);
        if (done < 0) {
            fprintf(stderr, "quiche_conn_recv failed: %zd\n", done);
            return;
        }
    }
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 4) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s HOST PORT MESSAGE\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    const char *host  = argv[1];
    uint16_t    port  = (uint16_t)atoi(argv[2]);
    const char *msg   = argv[3];
    size_t      mlen  = strlen(msg);

    /* ---------- UDP socket ---------- */
    int sock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
    if (sock < 0) die("socket");

    struct sockaddr_in local = {0};
    local.sin_family = AF_INET;
    local.sin_port   = 0;
    inet_pton(AF_INET, "0.0.0.0", &local.sin_addr);

    if (bind(sock, (struct sockaddr *)&local, sizeof(local)) < 0)
        die("bind");

    struct sockaddr_in peer = {0};
    peer.sin_family = AF_INET;
    peer.sin_port   = htons(port);
    if (inet_pton(AF_INET, host, &peer.sin_addr) != 1)
        die("inet_pton peer");

    if (connect(sock, (struct sockaddr *)&peer, sizeof(peer)) < 0)
        die("connect");

    /* 2 ms recv timeout so the loop can make progress */
    struct timeval tv = { .tv_sec = 0, .tv_usec = 2000 };
    setsockopt(sock, SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVTIMEO, &tv, sizeof(tv));

    /* ---------- quiche config ---------- */
    quiche_config *config = quiche_config_new(QUICHE_PROTOCOL_VERSION);
    if (!config) die("quiche_config_new");

    /* ALPN: length-prefixed. 0x0a = 10 = len("hq-interop") */
    const uint8_t alpn[] = "\x0ahq-interop";
    quiche_config_set_application_protos(config, alpn, sizeof(alpn) - 1);

    quiche_config_set_max_idle_timeout(config, 5000);
    quiche_config_set_max_recv_udp_payload_size(config, MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE);
    quiche_config_set_max_send_udp_payload_size(config, MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE);
    quiche_config_set_initial_max_data(config, 10 * 1024 * 1024);
    quiche_config_set_initial_max_stream_data_bidi_local(config,  1 * 1024 * 1024);
    quiche_config_set_initial_max_stream_data_bidi_remote(config, 1 * 1024 * 1024);
    quiche_config_set_initial_max_streams_bidi(config, 100);
    quiche_config_set_disable_active_migration(config, true);

    /* For learning only — verify the cert in real code! */
    quiche_config_set_verify_peer(config, false);

    /* ---------- connection ---------- */
    quiche_conn *conn = quiche_connect(host,
        (const struct sockaddr *)&local, sizeof(local),
        (const struct sockaddr *)&peer,  sizeof(peer),
        config);
    if (!conn) die("quiche_connect");

    uint8_t scid[LOCAL_CONN_ID_LEN];
    srand((unsigned)time(NULL));
    for (size_t i = 0; i < LOCAL_CONN_ID_LEN; i++) scid[i] = rand() & 0xff;
    quiche_conn_set_scids(conn, scid, LOCAL_CONN_ID_LEN);

    /* ---------- handshake ---------- */
    while (!quiche_conn_is_established(conn)) {
        if (flush_egress(sock, conn) < 0) return 1;
        drain_ingress(sock, conn);
    }
    fprintf(stderr, "[+] handshake complete\n");

    /* ---------- send request on stream 0 ---------- */
    ssize_t sent = quiche_stream_send(conn, 0,
                                      (const uint8_t *)msg, mlen, /*fin=*/true);
    if (sent < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "quiche_stream_send: %zd\n", sent);
        return 1;
    }
    fprintf(stderr, "[+] sent %zd bytes on stream 0\n", sent);

    /* ---------- read response until FIN ---------- */
    bool got_fin = false;
    while (!got_fin) {
        flush_egress(sock, conn);
        drain_ingress(sock, conn);

        bool fin = false;
        uint8_t resp[MAX_DATAGRAM_SIZE];
        ssize_t n = quiche_stream_recv(conn, 0, resp, sizeof(resp), &fin);
        if (n > 0) fwrite(resp, 1, (size_t)n, stdout);
        if (fin)  got_fin = true;
    }
    fputc('\n', stdout);

    /* ---------- graceful close ---------- */
    quiche_conn_close(conn, true, 0, NULL, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        if (flush_egress(sock, conn) <= 0) break;
    }

    quiche_conn_free(conn);
    quiche_config_free(config);
    close(sock);
    return 0;
}
```

### Build & run

```bash
gcc quic_client.c -o quic_client -lquiche

# Spin up the reference quiche server in another terminal:
cd quiche && cargo run --release --example server --features ffi -- 127.0.0.1 4433

# Then:
./quic_client 127.0.0.1 4433 "hello, lossless world"
```

---

## How the "lossless" part actually works

You don't have to do anything special — it's automatic:

| Mechanism | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Stream-level ACK frames** | Receiver tells sender which bytes arrived; sender retransmits gaps. |
| **Packet-level ACK frames** | Same idea at the UDP-packet layer (handles header loss). |
| **Flow control** (`initial_max_stream_data_*`) | Prevents overwhelming the receiver. |
| **Congestion control** (`quiche_config_set_cc_algorithm`) | NewReno/CUBIC/BBR — paces retransmits. |
| **Connection migration** | Survives NAT rebinding without losing the stream. |

If you want to stream a large file, just keep calling `quiche_stream_send()` in a loop — quiche buffers, fragments, and retransmits under the hood. The peer will see exactly the byte sequence you wrote, in order.

## When you'd reach for something else

- **HTTP/3** (web traffic): use `quiche.h3` on top of the same connection.
- **Unreliable** delivery (e.g. real-time video): enable QUIC datagrams via `quiche_conn_set_send_quantum` and `quiche_datagram_*` — but that's the *opposite* of what you asked for.
- **Pure C++ without FFI**: consider **ngtcp2** (C library, header-only-ish) or **MsQuic** (Microsoft's C API, used in Windows / .NET).

Happy to show an ngtcp2 or HTTP/3 version if you want to compare.

