#include <boost/lockfree/perfect_queue.hpp>

#include <atomic>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    // Bounded MPMC queue. Size must be a power of two (used as a ring mask).
    boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<int> q(1024);

    std::atomic<bool> done{false};

    // Producer: pushes 100k items, spinning if the queue is momentarily full.
    std::thread producer([&] {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100'000; ++i) {
            // push() returns false when the queue is full.
            while (!q.push(i)) {
                std::this_thread::yield();
            }
        }
        done.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
    });

    // Consumer: drains until producer is done AND queue is empty.
    std::thread consumer([&] {
        int v{};
        std::size_t seen = 0;
        while (!done.load(std::memory_order_acquire) || !q.empty()) {
            if (q.pop(v)) {
                ++seen;
            } else {
                std::this_thread::yield();
            }
        }
        std::cout << "consumed " << seen << " items\n";
    });

    producer.join();
    consumer.join();
}
```

Compile with (Boost.Lockfree is header-only):

```
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -pthread example.cpp -o example
```

## How `perfect_queue` works

`boost::lockfree::perfect_queue<T>` is a **bounded, multiple-producer / multiple-consumer (MPMC) FIFO** queue. The key properties:

1. **Ring buffer of fixed capacity.** Internally it allocates `capacity` slots in a circular buffer. The capacity you pass must be a power of two so the implementation can index with a bitmask instead of `%`, which keeps the hot path branchless.

2. **Lock-free via atomic head/tail indices.** Producers CAS a monotonically advancing `tail` to claim a slot; consumers CAS `head` to claim the right to read a slot. Because each slot is claimed independently, multiple producers and multiple consumers can make progress simultaneously without a global mutex.

3. **Bounded and non-blocking.** `push` returns `false` when full and `pop` returns `false` when empty — they never block. Callers typically spin (or back off) on `false`, as in the example.

4. **Why "perfect".** It is designed to be *wait-free* in the common case (a successful `push`/`pop` needs only a single CAS on the producer/consumer side), giving very predictable latency under contention compared to a generic CAS-based unbounded queue.

5. **Memory ordering.** The implementation uses acquire/release semantics on the slot publication so that the value written by a producer is visible to the consumer that later pops that slot, without extra fences in user code.

Useful API:

| Method | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `perfect_queue(size_t buffer_size)` | Construct with power-of-two capacity. |
| `bool push(const T&)` / `push(T&&)` | Enqueue; `false` if full. |
| `bool pop(T& dst)` | Dequeue into `dst`; `false` if empty. |
| `bool empty() const`, `bool full() const` | State queries (approximate under concurrency). |
| `size_t capacity() const` | Configured capacity. |

Caveats: it is **bounded** (you must choose capacity up front and handle `push` returning `false`), and it does require a power-of-two size. If you need an unbounded lock-free queue, use `boost::lockfree::queue<T>` instead.

