# There Is No `java.util.cache.AutoLRU` in Java

I want to gently flag a misconception before we proceed: **`java.util.cache.AutoLRU` does not exist** in the standard Java library (JDK), nor in any widely-used library I'm aware of. You may be thinking of one of the following:

| What you might be thinking of | Package |
|---|---|
| `LinkedHashMap` (the classic DIY LRU) | `java.util` |
| Guava's `CacheBuilder` | `com.google.common.cache` |
| Caffeine's `Cache` | `com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache` |
| JCache (`CacheManager`) | `javax.cache` |

Below are the three most common, idiomatic ways to build an LRU cache in Java.

---

## Option 1 — `LinkedHashMap` (no dependencies, in the JDK)

`LinkedHashMap` has a hidden gem: when constructed with `accessOrder=true`, it keeps entries in access order, and you can override `removeEldestEntry` to evict the oldest.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {

    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        // accessOrder = true  →  re-orders on get() and put()
        // initialCapacity, loadFactor are defaults; the 3rd "true" is the key
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        // Called automatically after each put; return true to evict
        return size() > capacity;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LRUCache<String, Integer> cache = new LRUCache<>(3);

        cache.put("a", 1);
        cache.put("b", 2);
        cache.put("c", 3);
        cache.get("a");          // "a" is now most-recently used
        cache.put("d", 4);       // evicts "b" (the least-recently used)

        System.out.println(cache); // {c=3, a=1, d=4}
    }
}
```

**Pros:** zero dependencies, simple, thread-unsafe (wrap with `Collections.synchronizedMap` or use `ConcurrentHashMap` patterns if needed).
**Cons:** not bounded by *size in bytes*, only by entry count; no expiration, no stats.

---

## Option 2 — Guava Cache (mature, widely used)

```java
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class GuavaExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cache<String, Integer> cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(3)                          // LRU by size
                // .maximumWeight(10_000).weigher(...)  // or by weight
                .expireAfterAccess(10, TimeUnit.MINUTES) // optional TTL
                .recordStats()                           // hit/miss stats
                .build();

        cache.put("a", 1);
        cache.put("b", 2);
        cache.put("c", 3);
        cache.getIfPresent("a");   // marks "a" as MRU
        cache.put("d", 4);         // evicts "b"

        System.out.println(cache.asMap()); // {c=3, a=1, d=4}
        System.out.println(cache.stats()); // hit/miss stats
    }
}
```

Add to Maven:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.google.guava</groupId>
    <artifactId>guava</artifactId>
    <version>33.x</version>
</dependency>
```

**Pros:** thread-safe, TTL, stats, weights, listeners.
**Cons:** Guava is in maintenance mode for caching — Caffeine is its recommended successor.

---

## Option 3 — Caffeine (modern, fastest)

Caffeine uses a **Window-TinyLFU** policy, which outperforms pure LRU in most workloads but is configured similarly.

```java
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class CaffeineExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cache<String, Integer> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(3)
                .expireAfterAccess(10, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
                .recordStats()
                .build();

        cache.put("a", 1);
        cache.put("b", 2);
        cache.put("c", 3);
        cache.getIfPresent("a");
        cache.put("d", 4); // evicts "b"

        System.out.println(cache.asMap()); // {c=3, a=1, d=4}
    }
}
```

Add to Maven:
```xml
