# Using `@secure-io/sanitize`

`@secure-io/sanitize` is a lightweight HTML sanitization library from Microsoft's `secure-io` scope. It strips dangerous constructs (like `<script>`, inline event handlers, etc.) while preserving safe HTML.

## Installation

```bash
npm install @secure-io/sanitize
```

## Minimal Example

```javascript
const sanitize = require('@secure-io/sanitize');

const dirty = '<script>alert("xss")</script><p>Hello <b>World</b></p><a href="javascript:bad()">click</a>';

const clean = sanitize(dirty);

console.log(clean);
// => '<p>Hello <b>World</b></p><a>click</a>'
```

Notice what got removed:
- The `<script>` tag and its contents
- The `javascript:` URL in the `<a>` href (the anchor is kept, but the dangerous attribute is stripped)

## With Options

The package accepts an options object as the second argument. Common options include configuring allowed tags/attributes:

```javascript
const sanitize = require('@secure-io/sanitize');

const dirty = '<p>Hello</p><img src="x" onerror="alert(1)">';

const clean = sanitize(dirty, {
  allowedTags: ['p', 'b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'a'],
  allowedAttributes: {
    a: ['href', 'title']
  }
});

console.log(clean);
// => '<p>Hello</p>'
// (the <img> is removed entirely, and onerror is stripped)
```

## In the Browser (via bundler)

```javascript
import sanitize from '@secure-io/sanitize';

document.querySelector('#output').innerHTML = sanitize(userInput);
```

> ⚠️ Always assign sanitized HTML via `innerHTML` (or equivalent) — never use `eval`, `document.write`, etc. on user input, even after sanitization.

## Tips

- **Don't roll your own sanitizer.** Use a maintained library like this one rather than trying to blacklist tags with regex.
- **Sanitize on the server too** if you render HTML server-side — client-side sanitization can be bypassed.
- **Check the README** on [npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@secure-io/sanitize) for the full list of supported options, as the API may have evolved beyond what's shown here.

