There is no `std::constant_time_sort` in the C++ standard library (or anywhere else). This function does not exist, and for good reason: **comparison-based sorting of `n` elements has a lower bound of Ω(n log n)**, so a true constant-time sort is mathematically impossible in the general case.

I'd recommend going back to your tech lead to clarify what they actually want. Some possibilities:

### 1. They meant `std::sort`
The default. O(n log n) average, introsort under the hood.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> v = {5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3};
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
```

### 2. They meant a *linear-time* sort (counting / radix)
If the integers are bounded or you want O(n) (not O(1)), counting sort works:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <ranges>

// Counting sort for non-negative ints with known max value M.
// Time: O(n + M), Space: O(n + M).
void counting_sort(std::vector<int>& a, int max_value) {
    std::vector<int> count(max_value + 1, 0);
    for (int x : a) ++count[x];

    std::vector<int> out(a.size());
    int idx = 0;
    for (int v = 0; v <= max_value; ++v)
        for (int c = 0; c < count[v]; ++c)
            out[idx++] = v;
    a = std::move(out);
}
```

### 3. They meant compile-time sorting (`constexpr`)
If the array is a compile-time constant, you can sort it with `constexpr` machinery — still O(n log n), but evaluated at compile time:

```cpp
#include <array>

constexpr auto sorted(std::array<int, 6> a) {
    for (auto& x : a)
        for (auto& y : a)
            if (x < y) { int t = x; x = y; y = t; } // bubble sort for brevity
    return a;
}

constexpr auto v = sorted({5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3}); // sorted at compile time
```

### 4. They were testing you
This is the most likely scenario. "Constant-time sort" is a classic trick question — recognizing that it doesn't exist (and why) is the point.

**Bottom line:** confirm with your tech lead. Don't ship code calling a function that doesn't compile.

