`std::pmr::turbo_pool_resource` doesn't exist in the C++ standard. You may be thinking of one of the two *actual* standard pool resources:

- **`std::pmr::unsynchronized_pool_resource`** — fastest, but not thread-safe
- **`std::pmr::synchronized_pool_resource`** — thread-safe, slightly slower due to internal locking

If "turbo" came from a third-party library (e.g., a blog post or a specific vendor library), let me know and I can adjust — but the standard answer for "fast memory pool" is `unsynchronized_pool_resource`.

---

# Using `unsynchronized_pool_resource`

The pool resource manages a set of "pools" for different block sizes. Small allocations come from these pools (very fast); large allocations fall through to an *upstream* allocator you supply.

## Minimal Example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory_resource>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

int main() {
    // 1. Choose an upstream allocator for "big" allocations.
    //    std::pmr::new_delete_resource() uses global new/delete.
    //    For real performance, you'd typically use a monotonic_buffer_resource
    //    or a huge pre-allocated buffer here.
    auto* upstream = std::pmr::new_delete_resource();

    // 2. (Optional) Tune the pool.
    //    pool_options { max_blocks_per_chunk, largest_required_pool_block }
    std::pmr::pool_options opts{
        /*max_blocks_per_chunk=*/32,
        /*largest_required_pool_block=*/4096
    };

    // 3. Create the pool resource.
    std::pmr::unsynchronized_pool_resource pool{opts, upstream};

    // 4. Use it via a pmr-aware container.
    std::pmr::vector<int> vec{&pool};
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) vec.push_back(i);

    std::pmr::pmr<std::string> greeting{&pool};
    greeting = std::pmr::string{"hello from the pool", &pool};

    std::cout << greeting << ", sum=" << vec.front() + vec.back() << "\n";
} // pool destroyed here; all its memory is released back to upstream
```

## How It Works

```
unsynchronized_pool_resource
├── Pool for size 8   ──┐
├── Pool for size 16    │  each pool grows in chunks
├── Pool for size 32    │  (sized by pool_options)
├── ...                 │
├── Pool for size 4096 ─┘
└── Anything larger ──► upstream allocator (e.g., new_delete_resource)
```

Allocations are bucketed by size, rounded up to the nearest pool. Each pool keeps a free-list, so `allocate`/`deallocate` are O(1) — typically just a pointer swap.

## Tuning Tips

| Option | Effect |
|---|---|
| `max_blocks_per_chunk` | How many blocks per chunk the pool keeps cached. Higher = fewer upstream calls, more memory held. |
| `largest_required_pool_block` | Sizes above this bypass the pool and go straight to upstream. Set to the largest object you expect. |

A common pattern is to put a `monotonic_buffer_resource` upstream so even "large" allocations are bump-allocated from a pre-allocated buffer:

```cpp
// Pre-allocate 1 MiB on the stack (or use a heap buffer / mmap)
std::byte buffer[1 << 20];
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource mono{buffer, sizeof(buffer)};

std::pmr::unsynchronized_pool_resource pool{&mono};

// All allocations — small (from pools) and large (from mono) —
// come from `buffer`. No heap calls at all after construction.
```

## When to Use Which

| Resource | Use when |
|---|---|
| `unsynchronized_pool_resource` | Single thread, you want max speed |
| `synchronized_pool_resource` | Multiple threads share the resource |
| `monotonic_buffer_resource` | Many short-lived allocations, free everything at once |

